window.ENTRY_PAGES = {
  "search": {
    "title": "Unstructured search",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Unstructured search</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Unstructured search)</span></h2>\n<p>Given coherent access to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>, or report that none exists, with probability at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>. Write <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span>. A candidate solution is represented by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> and checked by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p><p><span class=\"problem-anchor\" id=\"ideal-cipher-key-recovery\"></span><strong>Ideal-cipher key recovery.</strong> Given pairs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and a block cipher <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> with a uniformly random <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit key, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span> satisfying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> for every <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math></span>. Sufficiently many pairs isolate the key with high probability; the search space has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> elements.</p><p><span class=\"problem-anchor\" id=\"hash-preimages\"></span><strong>Hash preimages and second preimages.</strong> Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></span> in its image, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></span>. For a supplied <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, second-preimage search also requires <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math></span> counts the allowed preimages. The random-function comparison assumes a domain containing suitable preimages.</p><p><span class=\"problem-anchor\" id=\"oracle-np-search\"></span><strong>Oracle NP search.</strong> Given a verifier <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, decide whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02203;</mo><mi>w</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> and return a witness when one exists. This includes oracle access to feasibility predicates for SAT, exact cover and optimization thresholds, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>.</p><p>The comparisons count Boolean-, cipher-, hash- or verifier-oracle queries; explicit implementations additionally use quantum gate complexity and classical bit complexity.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case queries and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> expected queries for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>, Grover search and amplitude amplification.</strong> Randomized iteration counts handle unknown <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math></span>. A reversible predicate with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> gates and workspace <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math></span> gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>N</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math></span> logical qubits. A trial with success probability <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> is amplified in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> repetitions. Unique-key recovery takes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> cipher queries; random <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit hash outputs give the familiar <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> scale when the marked fraction has order <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. Oracle NP search has optimal worst-case complexity <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> verifier queries.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case queries and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries for a promised marked fraction, exhaustive search or random sampling.</strong> Independent trials cost <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The corresponding scales are <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for ideal-cipher key recovery, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for the stated random-hash regime and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for arbitrary verifier search. For an explicit bounded-fan-in verification circuit with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> gates and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span> witness bits, a count-preserving Boolean encoding and tensor contraction give\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>\nclassical search time. Set <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></math></span> or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> for the three applications. Linear-size verification circuits therefore give subexponential classical time in their witness length; for hash preimages, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gives subexponential time in output length as well. The best structural attack on an explicit cipher or hash function also enters its comparison.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Tight quadratic polynomial speedup in the oracle search-space ratio, entirely from Grover search and amplitude amplification. For a unique witness among <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> candidates, the exponent changes from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span> without power saving.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The upper and lower bounds match in the stated oracle models. Bennett–Bernstein–Brassard–Vazirani also establish their random-oracle NP separation. In the ordinary circuit model, a polynomial-time quantum algorithm for an NP-complete problem would imply <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">P</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">Q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math></span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Small-instance search circuits have been implemented. Large searches need fault tolerance, a candidate register and reversible-predicate workspace, with exponentially many coherent iterations in witness length. The Grassl–Langenberg–Roetteler–Steinwandt AES-128 construction uses 2,953 logical qubits and roughly <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>87</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> gates for full key search. Parallel search trades elapsed time for independently maintained processors.\n<small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, generic explicit-key and preimage searches used classical exhaustive-search bounds versus their square-root quantum bounds, including linear-size verification circuits.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9605043\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Grover, <em>A Fast Quantum Mechanical Algorithm for Database Search</em> (STOC 1996)</a>. Section 3, “Algorithm”, preprint p. 3, steps (i)–(iii).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0005055\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Brassard, Høyer, Mosca and Tapp, <em>Quantum Amplitude Amplification and Estimation</em> (2002)</a>. Section 2, Theorem 2 for known success probability; Theorem 3 and <span class=\"small-caps\">QSearch</span> for unknown success probability, preprint pp. 7–9.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9605034\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Boyer, Brassard, Høyer and Tapp, <em>Tight Bounds on Quantum Searching</em> (1998)</a>. Search procedure with an unknown number of marked items and its expected-query analysis.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9701001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bennett et al., <em>Strengths and Weaknesses of Quantum Computing</em> (1997)</a>. Section 3, Theorem 3.5, for the random-oracle separation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1512.04965\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Grassl, Langenberg, Roetteler and Steinwandt, <em>Applying Grover&#x27;s Algorithm to AES: Quantum Resource Estimates</em> (2016)</a>. Section 3.1: key uniqueness; Section 3.2: AES circuit; Section 3.4 and Table 5: full search resources.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1, Appendix C and witness self-reduction: explicit-circuit search and verification encodings.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "black-box-discrete-minimization": {
    "title": "Black-box discrete minimization",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Black-box discrete minimization</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Black-box minimum finding)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an oracle evaluating <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo /><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><msub><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. This is the optimization primitive used inside quantum dynamic programming and threshold-based exact optimization.\nComplexity is measured in value-oracle queries for both models, with a quantum gate bound parameterized by the evaluation circuit.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, Dürr–Høyer minimum finding.</strong> The algorithm returns a minimizing index with bounded error. Failure probability at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math></span> costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> evaluations. If reversible evaluation costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> gates, constant-error gate complexity is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Infeasible candidates can be represented by values above the feasible objective range.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, linear scanning.</strong> A scan retains the smallest value and its index, using constant additional words. The matching lower bound concerns arbitrary values accessed through the same oracle. This primitive is used inside the structured optimization algorithms below, whose classical comparisons exploit their explicit input representations.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Tight quadratic polynomial speedup in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span>, obtained through repeated amplitude amplification.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Fully proved in the value-oracle model; reduction from unstructured search supplies the quantum lower bound, and an unseen table entry supplies the classical lower bound.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The search and value registers need <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>N</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span> logical qubits plus reversible evaluation workspace. A compact objective can be evaluated arithmetically; a table supplied through coherent memory additionally requires storage for its entries. Useful large searches require fault tolerance and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> coherent evaluations.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9607014\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dürr and Høyer, <em>A Quantum Algorithm for Finding the Minimum</em> (1996/1999)</a>. Theorem 1 and the numbered minimum-finding procedure, preprint pp. 1–2; the linear scan is the classical comparison in the introduction.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "constraint-satisfaction-by-backtracking": {
    "title": "Constraint satisfaction by backtracking",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Constraint satisfaction by backtracking</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Constraint satisfaction)</span></h2>\n<p>For variables <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> and explicitly given constraints <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x022C0;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>, or decide unsatisfiability. Assume a classical branching rule and a predicate that rejects inconsistent partial assignments. Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></span> be the size of their backtracking tree and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> its depth.\nThe tree comparison counts branching and predicate-oracle calls, while the explicit bounded-arity comparison uses quantum gates and classical bit operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> detection queries and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> witness queries, Montanaro&#x27;s quantum backtracking.</strong> These are constant-error bounds for the supplied branching rule and pruning predicate. Reversible implementation costs multiply the query count. The quantum walk explores the same partial-assignment tree as the specified classical backtracking procedure; its depth dependence is explicit in the bounds.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> tree queries, depth-first backtracking; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> bit operations, Boolean tensor contraction for fixed domain and arity.</strong> For fixed domain <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span> and arity <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math></span>, the time bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. Encoding each variable in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> bits and forbidding disallowed local assignments produces a fixed-width CNF with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> variables. Exact counting and self-reduction decide feasibility and recover a witness in polynomial space. When <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, the exponent improves to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The tree-query comparison remains useful for an instance whose pruning rules yield a small <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></span>; the bounded-arity expression is the unrestricted explicit-input comparator in that regime.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Nearly quadratic in the size of a specified search tree, through a quantum walk. Fixed-domain, fixed-arity instances also admit the displayed subexponential classical bound, whose exponent has no power saving.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous acceleration of the supplied backtracking procedure; evidence against unrestricted classical solvers depends on the constraint language and instances.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>A path requires <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> qubits, together with reversible constraint-evaluation and phase-estimation workspace. The register count is polynomial, while a tree of exponential size entails exponentially many coherent walk operations. Useful large instances require fault tolerance.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Montanaro&#x27;s quantum backtracking</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1509.02374\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Montanaro, <em>Quantum Walk Speedup of Backtracking Algorithms</em> (Theory of Computing 2018)</a>. Theorems 1–2; Algorithm 1 defines classical backtracking, Algorithm 2 defines quantum detection, and the section on finding a marked vertex gives witness recovery.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendices D.1–D.4; applied to the fixed-width encoding described above.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/backtracking\">Quantum backtracking and branch and bound</a>: <a href=\"#entry/discrete-optimization-by-branch-and-bound\">Branch and bound</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/knapsack-and-binary-integer-programming\">Knapsack</a>, <a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-shortest-vector-problem\">Shortest vector problem</a>, <a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-closest-vector-problem\">Closest vector problem</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/backtracking\">→ Quantum backtracking and branch and bound</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "discrete-optimization-by-branch-and-bound": {
    "title": "Discrete optimization by branch and bound",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Discrete optimization by branch and bound</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Branch-and-bound optimization)</span></h2>\n<p>Minimize <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> over a finite feasible set represented by a rooted branching tree. Each node represents a subset of candidates and carries a computable lower bound on their objective values. Return an optimal feasible leaf and its value. Integer programming and scheduling commonly use this representation.\nComplexity is measured in branching, bounding and heuristic-oracle queries; quantum gate complexity additionally includes their reversible implementation costs.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>Q</mi></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, Incremental Quantum Branch and Bound.</strong> Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math></span> is the number of queries used by the specified classical branch-and-bound algorithm, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span> is the tree depth, and the heuristic satisfies the branch-local conditions of the theorem. Logarithmic factors include the objective range, heuristic range and failure probability. Montanaro&#x27;s construction gives an earlier near-quadratic bound in the truncated optimal-cost tree size. The incremental construction follows the portion of the tree explored by the chosen heuristic and returns an optimal leaf, or a leaf within a prescribed additive tolerance when that stopping criterion is supplied.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, the specified branch-and-bound procedure.</strong> The comparator uses the same branching rule, objective bounds and search heuristic. Its work can be far smaller than the size of the full search tree. For a concrete integer-programming or scheduling formulation, specialized algorithms and branch-and-cut methods supply additional classical comparisons; their costs depend on the input representation and bounding rules.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Nearly quadratic polynomial speedup in the work of the selected classical tree-search procedure, based on quantum tree search and subtree estimation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The query acceleration is rigorous under the stated heuristic conditions. Its significance relative to all classical algorithms is problem- and instance-dependent.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>A path, objective registers and reversible bounding workspace use polynomially many logical qubits. Long coherent tree searches require fault tolerance; an exponential <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math></span> leaves an exponential coherent running time. The implementation cost of the bound and heuristic is part of the resource estimate.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum branch and bound; Incremental Quantum Branch and Bound</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1906.10375\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Montanaro, <em>Quantum Speedup of Branch-and-Bound Algorithms</em> (Physical Review Research 2020)</a>. Algorithm 2 and Theorem 1.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2210.03210\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Chakrabarti, Minssen, Yalovetzky and Pistoia, <em>Universal Quantum Speedup for Branch-and-Bound, Branch-and-Cut, and Tree-Search Algorithms</em> (2022)</a>. Theorem 1.3 and Section 2.4, Algorithm 2, “Incremental-Quantum-Branch-and-Bound”; Section 1 defines the classical heuristic conditions.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/backtracking\">Quantum backtracking and branch and bound</a>: <a href=\"#entry/constraint-satisfaction-by-backtracking\">Constraint satisfaction by backtracking</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/knapsack-and-binary-integer-programming\">Knapsack</a>, <a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-shortest-vector-problem\">Shortest vector problem</a>, <a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-closest-vector-problem\">Closest vector problem</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/backtracking\">→ Quantum backtracking and branch and bound</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability": {
    "title": "Fixed-width Boolean satisfiability",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Fixed-width Boolean satisfiability</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT)</span></h2>\n<p>Fix an integer <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>. Given\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x022C0;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nwhere each <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> is a disjunction of literals, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> satisfying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>, or decide that none exists. The clause list is supplied explicitly. SAT represents Boolean feasibility in constraint programming and exact optimization.\nThe comparison uses quantum gate complexity and classical bit complexity on explicit formula inputs.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, quantum Schöning; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.1433</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for 3-SAT, amplified PPSZ.</strong> These are bounded-error trial-amplification bounds, with polynomial factors for formula evaluation suppressed. They assume coherent evaluation and reversal of the classical randomized trial. Running the classical counting algorithm below on a quantum computer also supplies a subexponential upper bound for explicit <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, exact counting by Boolean tensor contraction.</strong> The algorithm is deterministic and uses polynomial space. For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> with fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span>, the time is\n<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>.\nPositivity of the count decides satisfiability; conditional counts recover an assignment with polynomial overhead.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The subexponential classical bound is asymptotically smaller than the displayed amplified-trial bounds. Its exponent is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, so the reduction from exponential to subexponential time has no power saving.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The stated algorithms have rigorous upper bounds. The comparison supplies no asymptotic quantum-speedup claim for amplified Schöning or PPSZ.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The amplified-trial constructions require fault tolerance, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> assignment qubits, polynomial reversible workspace and exponentially many coherent trial evaluations.</p><p><small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, the known general classical 3-SAT bound was <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.30704</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, and amplified PPSZ claimed a quadratic improvement to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.1433</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Amplified Schöning and PPSZ algorithms</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cs.utep.edu/vladik/2005/tr05-26.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dantsin, Kreinovich and Wolpert, <em>On Quantum Versions of Record-Breaking Algorithms for SAT</em>, SIGACT News 36(4), 103–108 (2005)</a>. Section 1: “Ambainis&#x27; observation” and “The fastest algorithm for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT”, author-preprint pp. 2–3.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.eecs.harvard.edu/ michaelm/CS223/schoning99.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Schöning, <em>A Probabilistic Algorithm for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT and Constraint Satisfaction Problems</em> (FOCS 1999)</a>. Section 2, “The Algorithm”, p. 410. <a href=\"https://theoretics.episciences.org/13222/pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Scheder, <em>PPSZ Is Better Than You Think</em> (TheoretiCS 2024)</a>: Section 1.1, Algorithm 1, p. 3.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (25 October 2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1, p. 2; Appendices D.1–D.3, pp. 53–54; recursive contraction in D.4.1–D.4.7, pp. 57–64. Witness self-reduction follows Theorem 1.1.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments": {
    "title": "Approximate counting of feasible assignments",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Approximate counting of feasible assignments</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Approximate counting)</span></h2>\n<p>For an oracle <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mover><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math></span> with probability at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>. The count is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mover><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover></mrow></math></span>; this estimates solution density, feasible volume, or a probability under a reversibly samplable candidate distribution.\nThe black-box bounds count oracle queries, with gate costs obtained from the reversible oracle; the explicit-CNF comparator counts classical bit operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, quantum counting and amplitude estimation.</strong> For additive error <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math></span> and constant failure probability, coherent sampling and its inverse give the stated bound. Relative error <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></span> for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> calls. Gate complexity multiplies these counts by the state-preparation, oracle and reflection costs. The finite domain permits exact enumeration when that is cheaper than the displayed precision-dependent bound.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case sampling queries, Monte Carlo estimation.</strong> Independent samples give the additive-error bound, capped by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> evaluations of the entire domain. Relative error costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> samples. When <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math></span> is an explicit fixed-width CNF, deterministic tensor contraction computes the count exactly in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> bit operations and polynomial space; for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> the exponent is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. This exact method is an additional comparator at high precision or very small feasible density.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Quadratic polynomial improvement in inverse additive precision in the sampling-oracle model, from amplitude estimation. Relative-error improvement also depends on the feasible density <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Tight worst-case sampling-oracle separation in the precision range before finite-domain saturation. Explicit formula structure gives the additional classical comparison stated above.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Small amplitude-estimation circuits have been demonstrated. The input register needs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> qubits plus oracle workspace; phase-estimation implementations add <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> ancillas, while iterative variants reduce this register. Useful high precision requires long coherent oracle sequences and fault tolerance.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum counting and amplitude estimation</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0005055\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Brassard, Høyer, Mosca and Tapp, <em>Quantum Amplitude Amplification and Estimation</em> (2002)</a>. Section 4, <span class=\"small-caps\">Est Amp</span> and Theorem 12; Theorem 13 defines <span class=\"small-caps\">Count</span>, and Theorem 15 gives relative approximation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D: exact fixed-width counting.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>: <a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">→ Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "travelling-salesman": {
    "title": "Travelling salesman",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Travelling salesman</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Travelling salesman)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a weighted graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, find a Hamiltonian cycle <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></span> minimizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, or report infeasibility. Integer weights are binary encoded and have maximum magnitude <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span>. The problem models routing and cyclic sequencing.</p><p><span class=\"problem-anchor\" id=\"directed-feedback-arc-set\"></span><strong>Directed minimum feedback arc set.</strong> Given a directed graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, find a minimum-cardinality <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> is acyclic. Equivalently, minimize the number of backward arcs in a vertex order; nonnegative weights give the weighted objective. This models ranking with inconsistent pairwise preferences.</p><p>The general quantum bounds use QRAM time, bounded-degree travelling-salesman bounds use gate time, and classical bounds use RAM time with polynomial costs for weight arithmetic.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.728</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for both problems, quantum subset dynamic programming; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.110</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.301</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for degree-three and degree-four travelling salesman, quantum backtracking.</strong> The common dynamic-programming construction precomputes partial subset values and uses quantum minimum finding over their decompositions. For feedback arc set, an ordered partition fixes every crossing arc&#x27;s contribution and leaves two internal ordering costs. For travelling salesman, the state also records path endpoints. These constructions assume coherent access to exponential tables. The bounded-degree travelling-salesman algorithms use polynomial workspace and include factors <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span> for integer weights.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for travelling salesman, Held–Karp; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for feedback arc set, subset dynamic programming.</strong> Held–Karp stores the shortest path for each visited set and endpoint in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> words. The feedback-arc recurrence stores the best ordering cost for each vertex subset. Both use exponential memory. For degree-three and degree-four travelling salesman, sparse-graph path decompositions improve the bounds to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.1757</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.4214</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with exponential memory; a polynomial-space cubic-graph comparator takes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.232</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The weighted sparse-graph construction retains polynomial weight-bit costs. Restricted feedback-arc inputs, such as tournaments, select their own specialized classical algorithms.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedups through smaller exponential constants. The common subset dynamic program uses minimum finding and amplitude amplification; bounded-degree travelling salesman uses quantum backtracking in the specified graph and memory regimes.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous quantum upper bounds and partial evidence relative to the stated classical algorithms in each input regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The general algorithms combine polynomial active registers with exponential QRAM. Bounded-degree travelling salesman uses polynomial logical workspace and exponentially long coherent tree searches. Useful implementations require fault tolerance and reversible objective arithmetic.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum subset dynamic programming; quantum backtracking</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05209\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis et al., <em>Quantum Speedups for Exponential-Time Dynamic Programming Algorithms</em> (SODA 2019)</a>. Theorem 8 and Algorithm 2: travelling salesman; Section 7 and Corollary 9: directed feedback arc set and its split recurrence.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1612.06203\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Moylett, Linden and Montanaro, <em>Quantum Speedup of the Traveling-Salesman Problem for Bounded-Degree Graphs</em> (2017)</a>. Theorem 1; Theorems 4–5 give the degree-three and degree-four algorithms and their classical search subroutines.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://serggasp.github.io/pathwidth-site/papers/sparse-pathwidth/14-apps-connectivity.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gaspers, <em>Path Decompositions of Sparse Graphs via Gaussian Sweeps and Faster Exponential Algorithms</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 14.1(d): weighted TSP applications.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/backtracking\">Quantum backtracking and branch and bound</a>: <a href=\"#entry/constraint-satisfaction-by-backtracking\">Constraint satisfaction by backtracking</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-optimization-by-branch-and-bound\">Branch and bound</a>, <a href=\"#entry/knapsack-and-binary-integer-programming\">Knapsack</a>, <a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-shortest-vector-problem\">Shortest vector problem</a>, <a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-closest-vector-problem\">Closest vector problem</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/subset-dp\">Quantum subset dynamic programming</a>: <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition\">Set cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/backtracking\">→ Quantum backtracking and branch and bound</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/subset-dp\">→ Quantum subset dynamic programming</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition": {
    "title": "Set cover, set packing and set partition",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Set cover, set packing and set partition</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Set cover, set packing and exact set partition)</span></h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math></span> be a universe of size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>U</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> contain nonempty sets, and let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> be a positive integer. For an explicit family, write <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</p><p><strong>Set cover.</strong> Find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x022C3;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, or minimize the number of selected sets. This models facilities, tests or resources covering all requirements.</p><p><span class=\"problem-anchor\" id=\"set-packing\"></span><strong>Set packing.</strong> Find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> pairwise disjoint members of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math></span>, or maximize their number. The sets represent mutually compatible objects.</p><p><span class=\"problem-anchor\" id=\"exact-set-partition\"></span><strong>Exact set partition.</strong> Find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace=\"0.22em\" rspace=\"0.22em\"><mrow><mover><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo>&#x002D9;</mo></mover></mrow></mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo lspace=\"0.22em\" rspace=\"0.22em\"><mrow><mover><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo>&#x002D9;</mo></mover></mrow></mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>. This includes exact cover, clustering and assignment problems. Families may also be represented implicitly, with membership and restricted-enumeration routines supplied.</p><p>Quantum bounds count QRAM time and the supplied enumeration costs; classical subset algorithms use RAM time and explicit Boolean encodings use bit complexity.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.7274</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for explicit set cover, quantum dynamic programming; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for eligible families with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>, Gaspers–Li covering, packing and partition algorithms.</strong> For explicit set cover, polynomial factors include <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span>; subset preprocessing and recursive minimum finding use coherent table access. In the common enumeration framework, a simple branching routine must enumerate representative members on each restricted universe <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math></span> in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time, preserving a feasible nonstraddling packing or partition, or the prescribed projected cover, as stated in Theorem 4. Theorems 5–6 refine the bound when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1.147899</mn></mrow></math></span>; as <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></span> approaches one, the base approaches <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.7274</mn></mrow></math></span>. Membership, feasibility and preprocessing costs are retained.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, inclusion–exclusion; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for explicit set cover, subset methods or family enumeration.</strong> The inclusion–exclusion and subset-transform bounds include the represented family&#x27;s counting or membership costs. For explicit set cover and exact set partition with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, long-clause shortening and fixed-width counting give <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for every fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>. Selection variables, coverage clauses and cardinality constraints define the encodings; exact partition additionally enforces incompatibility of overlapping sets. Fixed element frequency with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for cover, or linear incidence count for exact partition, gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time. For explicit set packing, the conflict graph on its <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> sets gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time via maximum independent set. The applicable comparison therefore depends on both universe size and family representation.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedups through smaller exponential constants in the eligible large-family or implicit-family comparisons, from amplitude amplification and minimum finding. The stated linear-family regimes have stronger subexponential classical algorithms.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous quantum upper bounds in QRAM and under the representative-family hypotheses; partial evidence against the applicable classical subset methods. Set Cover Conjectures specify the universe-size and family-size regimes to which they refer.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial subset, feasibility and cardinality registers accompany exponential coherent preprocessing memory. Reversible enumeration, disjointness and coverage tests contribute to gate cost; useful depths require fault tolerance.\n<small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, the explicit set-cover comparison used <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classically versus <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.7274</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantumly, including the linear-family regime.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum set-cover dynamic programming; Gaspers–Li covering, packing and partition algorithms</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05209\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis et al., <em>Quantum Speedups for Exponential-Time Dynamic Programming Algorithms</em> (SODA 2019)</a>. Theorem 10 and Algorithm 3: Set Cover.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.08042\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gaspers and Li, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Graph Coloring and Other Partitioning, Covering, and Packing Problems</em> (ICALP 2024)</a>. Theorem 4: the representative-family conditions and the covering, packing and partition algorithms; Theorems 5–6 and Corollary 1: refined small-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></span> bounds.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/formal-math/tree/main/eth\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Claude (Anthropic), <em>A <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> Time Algorithm for 3-SAT</em> (2026)</a>. Long-clause shortening and Corollary 7: explicit set-cover and exact-partition encodings with a linear number of sets.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D: fixed-width counting, sparse incidence encodings and the conflict-graph reduction for set packing.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/subset-dp\">Quantum subset dynamic programming</a>: <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>: <a href=\"#entry/chromatic-number\">Chromatic number</a>, <a href=\"#entry/domatic-number\">Domatic number</a>, <a href=\"#entry/covering-by-maximal-induced-bicliques\">Maximal induced biclique cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/packing-maximal-induced-matchings\">Maximal induced matching packing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/vertex-arboricity\">Vertex arboricity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-into-hereditary-graph-classes\">Hereditary graph partition</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/subset-dp\">→ Quantum subset dynamic programming</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">→ Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "set-multicover-with-repeated-sets": {
    "title": "Set multicover with repeated sets",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Set multicover with repeated sets</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Set multicover with repeated sets)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> and a fixed demand <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>, minimize <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> over integers <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> satisfying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span> for every <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math></span>. Here a set may be selected repeatedly. This models redundant coverage or repeated resource provision.\nThe quantum algorithm is measured in QRAM time, classical lattice dynamic programming in RAM time, and the bounded-incidence encoding in bit operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span>, quantum lattice dynamic programming.</strong> For fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span>, the base satisfies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2.660</mn></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3.529</mn></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>4.421</mn></mrow></math></span>. The quasipolynomial prefactor is part of the bound. Coherent access to precomputed lattice states is assumed. Truncating coverage coordinates at <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span> retains all relevant states, and no set needs multiplicity above <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, demand-vector dynamic programming.</strong> The state space consists of capped coverage vectors in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>D</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span> be the incidence count. When <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span> is fixed, a linear-size bounded-width Boolean encoding gives the stronger deterministic bound <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> with polynomial space. This includes the multiplicity variables, capped local coverage sums and objective threshold.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup through a smaller exponential constant for fixed demand in the general lattice comparison, using quantum search. Linear-incidence instances have a subexponential classical comparator.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous QRAM upper bound and partial evidence against the best general classical lattice algorithm.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The active coverage register has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> qubits, with polynomial workspace and exponential coherent preprocessing storage. The dependence on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span> affects both the lattice size and reversible arithmetic.\n<small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, the lattice comparison used <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classically and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span> quantumly, including the linear-incidence regime now covered by the subexponential bound.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum dynamic programming on lattice graphs</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2104.14384\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Glos, Kokainis, Mori and Vihrovs, <em>Quantum Speedups for Dynamic Programming on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-Dimensional Lattice Graphs</em> (MFCS 2021)</a>. Algorithm 1 and Theorem 5: lattice search; Sections 6–7 and Theorem 9: QRAM implementation and repeated-set multicover.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D, applied to the bounded-demand, linear-incidence encoding.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/lattice-dp\">→ Quantum dynamic programming on lattice graphs</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "chromatic-number": {
    "title": "Chromatic number",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Chromatic number</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Graph coloring and chromatic number)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, find the smallest <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> admitting <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for every edge <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math></span>. The decision variant supplies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> and asks for such a coloring. Colors represent mutually incompatible time slots, frequencies, or resources.\nThe unrestricted quantum comparison uses QRAM time and classical RAM time, while fixed-color CNF algorithms are measured in bit operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.7956</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Gaspers–Li graph-coloring algorithm.</strong> Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span> and the number of colors is optimized without a fixed bound. The algorithm combines restricted-family preprocessing, maximal-independent-set enumeration and quantum minimum finding. Its table is accessed coherently in the QRAM model.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, inclusion–exclusion over independent sets; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>-colorability, Boolean tensor contraction.</strong> The first bound computes the unrestricted chromatic number. For a fixed number <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> of colors, one-hot vertex-color variables give <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> Boolean variables and fixed-width clauses for vertex and edge conditions, even on dense graphs. Exact counting and self-reduction recover a coloring in polynomial space. On sparse graphs the exponent becomes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup through a smaller exponential constant for unrestricted chromatic number, based on amplitude amplification. Fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> decision has the displayed subexponential classical comparator.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous quantum upper bound with partial evidence relative to the general classical chromatic-number algorithm. The fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> comparison follows the explicit bounded-width encoding.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial active subset and color registers coexist with exponentially many coherently accessible preprocessing values. Useful instances require fault tolerance; fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> decision and unrestricted optimization have different relevant time comparisons.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gaspers–Li graph coloring</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.08042\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gaspers and Li, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Graph Coloring and Other Partitioning, Covering, and Packing Problems</em> (ICALP 2024)</a>. Theorem 8 and the coloring construction following it; appendix Algorithms 1–3 give the preprocessing and recursive procedures. The preliminary covering formulation gives the classical inclusion–exclusion comparison.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendices D.1–D.4, applied to the one-hot coloring encoding.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>: <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition\">Set cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/domatic-number\">Domatic number</a>, <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/set-packing\">Set packing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/exact-set-partition\">Exact set partition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/covering-by-maximal-induced-bicliques\">Maximal induced biclique cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/packing-maximal-induced-matchings\">Maximal induced matching packing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/vertex-arboricity\">Vertex arboricity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-into-hereditary-graph-classes\">Hereditary graph partition</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">→ Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "minimum-steiner-tree": {
    "title": "Minimum Steiner tree",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Minimum Steiner tree</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Minimum Steiner tree)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an undirected graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, nonnegative edge weights <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span>, and terminals <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, find a connected subgraph containing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math></span> of minimum total weight. An optimum may be chosen as a tree. Steiner vertices permit cheaper network connections than restricting the tree to terminals.\nThe quantum bound uses QRAM time, while classical bounds count RAM operations with the stated weight-arithmetic costs.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.812</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, quantum Steiner-tree dynamic programming.</strong> The exponential parameter is the number <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> of terminals, while polynomial factors include graph size and weight arithmetic. The construction uses a recurrence based on splitting a Steiner tree, together with partial preprocessing and nested quantum minimum finding. Its table values must be coherently accessible.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, Mölle–Richter–Rossmanith; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for bounded integer weights, subset convolution.</strong> The subset-convolution bound includes the dependence on the weight range. These improve the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> Dreyfus–Wagner recurrence and the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2.684</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> splitting algorithm. For unit-weight sparse graphs, Gaspers&#x27; path-decomposition methods give vertex-parameter bounds <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.1661</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> on degree-three graphs and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.3100</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> on degree-four graphs, using exponential memory. On such inputs the appropriate comparator is the minimum of the applicable terminal- and vertex-parameter bounds.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup through a smaller exponential constant in terminal count in the general comparison, using amplitude amplification. Sparse unit-weight regimes also have the stated classical vertex-parameter bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous QRAM upper bound and partial evidence against the strongest displayed classical algorithms in the relevant weight and graph regimes.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial active registers encode terminal subsets, vertices, costs and reconstruction data, together with exponential coherent preprocessing memory. Useful terminal counts require fault tolerance; graph and weight parameters affect the polynomial register and arithmetic costs.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum Steiner-tree dynamic programming</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.03581\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Miyamoto, Iwamura, Kise and Le Gall, <em>Quantum Speedup for the Minimum Steiner Tree Problem</em> (2019/2020)</a>. Theorem 1; Section 3.1 defines Dreyfus–Wagner, Section 3.2 gives the splitting recurrence, and Section 4 gives the quantum algorithm. Table 1 and its cited classical papers specify the weight-dependent comparisons.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://serggasp.github.io/pathwidth-site/papers/sparse-pathwidth/14-apps-connectivity.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gaspers, <em>Path Decompositions of Sparse Graphs via Gaussian Sweeps and Faster Exponential Algorithms</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 14.1(a): sparse unit-weight Steiner tree.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/subset-dp\">Quantum subset dynamic programming</a>: <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition\">Set cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/subset-dp\">→ Quantum subset dynamic programming</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "graph-bandwidth": {
    "title": "Graph bandwidth",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Graph bandwidth</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Graph bandwidth)</span></h2>\n<p>For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> vertices, compute\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>bw</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>V</mi><mover><mrow><mi>&#x027F6;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x0007E;</mi></mrow></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nBandwidth minimizes the largest separation of adjacent vertices, equivalently the bandwidth of a symmetrically permuted adjacency matrix.\nThe quantum bound counts QRAM time and the classical bound counts RAM operations on the explicit graph.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2.946</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, quantum bandwidth dynamic programming.</strong> A decomposition into constrained vertex-ordering instances permits variable-time quantum search and partial subset preprocessing. The quoted time includes the search over decomposition choices and assumes coherent access to stored dynamic-programming values. Polynomial factors include graph processing and the objective threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>4.383</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Cygan–Pilipczuk exact bandwidth algorithm.</strong> The algorithm uses structural restrictions on feasible layouts to reduce the search space. The quantum construction also accelerates a simpler <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> formulation, while the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>4.383</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> bound supplies the stronger overall classical comparison. Both bounds use the number of vertices as their exponential parameter.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>4.383</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2.946</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>; quantum minimum finding and variable-time amplitude amplification are the principal acceleration tools.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The upper bound is rigorous in the stated memory model. The best-algorithm comparison has partial evidence from the established classical exact-algorithm literature.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial active subset, layout and objective registers require exponential QRAM for preprocessing. The nested searches and stored table make useful large instances a fault-tolerant target.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum bandwidth dynamic programming</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05209\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis et al., <em>Quantum Speedups for Exponential-Time Dynamic Programming Algorithms</em> (SODA 2019)</a>. Section 4, Theorem 6: bandwidth algorithm; the section specifies the classical recurrence and cites the Cygan–Pilipczuk <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>4.383</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> algorithm.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/vertex-ordering\">Quantum path finding in the Boolean hypercube</a>: <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/vertex-ordering\">→ Quantum path finding in the Boolean hypercube</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "exact-treewidth": {
    "title": "Exact treewidth",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Exact treewidth</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Treewidth)</span></h2>\n<p>For a graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span>, find a tree decomposition <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> minimizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>. Every vertex belongs to a bag, every edge has both endpoints in a bag, and the bags containing each vertex induce a connected subtree. Treewidth governs the complexity of many graph and constraint algorithms.\nThe polynomial-space quantum bound counts gate time, the exponential-memory quantum bounds use QRAM time, and classical bounds use RAM time.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.538</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with exponential QRAM, quantum treewidth dynamic programming.</strong> Kļevickis–Prūsis–Vihrovs also give <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.554</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.452</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> space, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.618</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time with polynomial space. The fastest variant uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.538</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> space. These are distinct memory tradeoffs obtained from separator-based structural decompositions and quantum search.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.7347</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with exponential space, potential-maximal-clique dynamic programming; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2.6151</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with polynomial space, Fomin–Villanger.</strong> The first bound enumerates potential maximal cliques and optimizes compatible decomposition blocks. The polynomial-space method enumerates connected sets with constrained neighborhoods and recursively assembles decompositions. The comparison with the polynomial-space quantum algorithm therefore uses the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2.6151</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> bound; the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>1.538</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> quantum algorithm uses the exponential-space comparison.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedups through smaller exponential constants in both displayed memory regimes. The quantum constructions combine structural enumeration with amplitude amplification and minimum finding.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous time-space bounds and partial evidence relative to the best classical algorithms for the same space regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The fastest quantum construction needs exponential coherent memory and polynomial active graph registers. The <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>1.618</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> variant reduces storage to polynomial logical workspace but retains exponential coherent running time. Fault tolerance is needed in either useful regime.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum treewidth dynamic programming</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.08186\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kļevickis, Prūsis and Vihrovs, <em>Quantum Speedups for Treewidth</em> (TQC 2022)</a>. Theorems 21, 22 and 24 give the three tradeoffs; Algorithm 4 gives the fastest construction. Algorithm 1 states the classical polynomial-space procedure.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1309.1559\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Fomin, Todinca and Villanger, <em>Large Induced Subgraphs via Triangulations and CMSO</em> (SIAM Journal on Computing 2015)</a>. Proposition 5 states the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.7347</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> potential-maximal-clique enumeration bound of Fomin–Villanger; the preceding triangulation preliminaries define the blocks used in classical treewidth dynamic programming.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/treewidth-dp\">→ Quantum treewidth algorithms</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "pathwidth-and-vertex-separation": {
    "title": "Pathwidth and vertex separation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Pathwidth and vertex separation</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Pathwidth)</span></h2>\n<p>Minimize <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> over path decompositions: bags <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> cover all vertices and edges, and each vertex occurs in consecutive bags. Equivalently, minimize over vertex orders the largest number of already placed vertices with a neighbor still unplaced. Pathwidth captures the live frontier in linear processing and memory allocation.\nThe quantum algorithm is measured in QRAM time and classical ordering algorithms in RAM operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.817</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, quantum vertex-ordering dynamic programming.</strong> The vertex-separation objective admits a subset recurrence: each state records an already placed prefix and its boundary. Partial preprocessing and quantum minimum finding optimize the largest boundary along a full ordering, using exponential coherent storage.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.89</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Kitsunai et al. directed-pathwidth algorithm applied to an undirected graph.</strong> Replace each edge by its two opposite arcs; this preserves the relevant vertex-separation objective. The algorithm exploits structural restrictions on orderings and improves the generic <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> subset recurrence. Its exponential parameter remains the original number of vertices.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup through a smaller exponential constant relative to the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>1.89</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> classical bound. The quantum acceleration comes from recursive minimum finding and amplitude amplification.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous QRAM upper bound with partial evidence from the classical exact-algorithm comparison.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The active subset, boundary-count and reconstruction registers use polynomially many logical qubits. Exponential QRAM and long coherent recursive searches dominate useful-instance requirements.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum vertex-ordering dynamic programming</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05209\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis et al., <em>Quantum Speedups for Exponential-Time Dynamic Programming Algorithms</em> (SODA 2019)</a>. Corollary 5 and the vertex-ordering recurrence, equation (3): pathwidth application.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00453-015-0015-9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kitsunai et al., <em>Computing Directed Pathwidth in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.89</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> Time</em> (Algorithmica)</a>. The directed-pathwidth algorithm and main running-time theorem; the symmetric-digraph specialization gives the undirected comparison.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/vertex-ordering\">Quantum path finding in the Boolean hypercube</a>: <a href=\"#entry/graph-bandwidth\">Bandwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/vertex-ordering\">→ Quantum path finding in the Boolean hypercube</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "minimum-linear-arrangement": {
    "title": "Minimum linear arrangement",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Minimum linear arrangement</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Cutwidth, minimum linear arrangement and interval completion)</span></h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> be an undirected graph on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> vertices and let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> be a bijection.</p><p><strong>Cutwidth.</strong> Minimize\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;or&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nThe objective measures the largest number of edges crossing a prefix cut, as in simultaneous communication or wiring requirements.</p><p><span class=\"problem-anchor\" id=\"minimum-linear-arrangement\"></span><strong>Minimum linear arrangement.</strong> Minimize <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span>, the total edge length in a linear layout.</p><p><span class=\"problem-anchor\" id=\"interval-completion\"></span><strong>Minimum interval completion.</strong> Minimize <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span> over sets of missing edges such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> is an interval graph: there exist intervals <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></math></span> exactly when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02229;</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The objective adds as few missing overlaps as possible.</p><p>The quantum comparisons use QRAM time; classical exact and parameterized comparisons use RAM time on the explicit graph.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.817</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for all three problems, quantum vertex-ordering dynamic programming.</strong> For cutwidth, the recurrence minimizes the maximum cut size over the prefixes of an ordering. Minimum linear arrangement uses the sum of the same prefix-cut sizes, which equals total edge length. Interval completion uses the local completion cost of appending a vertex to a prefix. Partial subset preprocessing and recursive minimum finding solve these ordering recurrences with exponential coherent memory; reconstruction returns the ordering or completion.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for all three problems, subset dynamic programming; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for interval completion with fill parameter <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> The subset recurrence processes prefixes by cardinality and stores the best accumulated value for each prefix. Its polynomial factors include objective evaluation; minimum linear arrangement admits an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> implementation for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The cutwidth divide-and-conquer alternative uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time and polynomial space. For interval completion, the parameterized algorithm decides and reconstructs a completion using at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> added edges; threshold search obtains the optimum. Small-cutwidth instances and restricted graph classes, including trees for linear arrangement, have their corresponding specialized classical algorithms.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The common unrestricted vertex-count comparison is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> versus <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.817</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, a polynomial speedup obtained from amplitude amplification and quantum minimum finding. Small-fill interval completion uses the stated parameterized comparator.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous quantum ordering bounds and partial evidence relative to the general classical subset algorithms.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial active subset, objective and reconstruction registers require exponential coherently accessible tables. Linear arrangement needs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> objective bits in addition to the subset register and workspace. Useful coherent searches require fault tolerance.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum vertex-ordering dynamic programming</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05209\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis et al., <em>Quantum Speedups for Exponential-Time Dynamic Programming Algorithms</em> (SODA 2019)</a>. Corollary 5 and equation (3): the common vertex-ordering recurrence for cutwidth, minimum linear arrangement and interval completion.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00224-011-9312-0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bodlaender et al., <em>A Note on Exact Algorithms for Vertex Ordering Problems on Graphs</em> (2012)</a>. The dynamic-programming and polynomial-space divide-and-conquer constructions for vertex-ordering objectives.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1402.3473\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bliznets et al., <em>A Subexponential Parameterized Algorithm for Interval Completion</em></a>. Theorem 1.1 states the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> result; the algorithmic construction combines the structural enumeration and dynamic-programming sections.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/vertex-ordering\">Quantum path finding in the Boolean hypercube</a>: <a href=\"#entry/graph-bandwidth\">Bandwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/vertex-ordering\">→ Quantum path finding in the Boolean hypercube</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "worst-case-subset-sum": {
    "title": "Worst-case subset sum",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Worst-case subset sum</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Subset sum)</span></h2>\n<p>Given integers <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span> in binary, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span>, or decide that none exists. This entry concerns arbitrary inputs; the random density-one regime has separate, faster heuristic algorithms.\nQuantum time is measured with coherent random-access memory and polynomial integer-arithmetic costs; classical bounds specify word-RAM operations and coefficient bit length.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Chukhin–Kulikov–Levitskii–Mihajlin; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Arvind–Schuler.</strong> The <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math></span> bound is a 2026 preprint result obtained from a worst-case seven-list sum algorithm, using quantum walks and coherent data structures. The <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span> construction uses a smaller precomputed sum list and quantum search over the remaining variables. Both bounds suppress polynomial arithmetic factors and require exponential accessible memory.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.49</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> word operations for polynomial-bit inputs, OpenAI&#x27;s worst-case subset-sum algorithm.</strong> For each fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></span>, the bound holds on all inputs of bit length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> for sufficiently large <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, with success probability at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span> and a running-time cap on every random execution. The word size is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. A lower-space alternative uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.199</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> writable words, in particular <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> words under a fixed polynomial-bit convention. For nonnegative inputs and small target <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span>, pseudopolynomial algorithms with near-linear dependence on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span> can be faster.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.49</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. The fastest quantum result uses quantum walks; the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> algorithm uses amplitude amplification.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Worst-case upper bounds with partial evidence for a speedup over classical algorithms. The strongest displayed quantum and classical bounds are 2026 preprints with their stated memory and bit-length assumptions.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial active registers hold subset indices and integer sums; exponentially large coherent list and search structures dominate storage. Long walk or amplification sequences require fault tolerance. The classical low-space tradeoff provides a separate memory-sensitive comparison.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Chukhin–Kulikov–Levitskii–Mihajlin subset sum; Arvind–Schuler search</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.07309\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Chukhin, Kulikov, Levitskii and Mihajlin, <em>Improved Quantum Algorithms for Subset Sum and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SUM</em> (2026)</a>. Corollary 1.3; Theorem 1.2 and Algorithm 1 give the underlying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SUM routine; the Subset Sum application gives the block reduction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0212048\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Arvind and Schuler, <em>The Quantum Query Complexity of 0-1 Knapsack and Associated Claw Problems</em> (ISAAC 2003)</a>. Section 2, Theorem 1 and Corollary 2; steps 1–6 define the search construction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>Subset Sum in Time <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.49</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span></em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and Section 6, especially Sections 6.1–6.3: full algorithm and bounded running time.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>A Low-Space Algorithm for Worst-Case Subset Sum</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1, Corollary 1.2 and Section 7: uniform low-space algorithm.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/arvind-schuler\">Arvind–Schuler binary integer-programming search</a>: <a href=\"#entry/knapsack-and-binary-integer-programming\">Knapsack</a>, <a href=\"#entry/knapsack-and-binary-integer-programming/binary-integer-programming\">Binary integer programming</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/arvind-schuler\">→ Arvind–Schuler binary integer-programming search</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/cklm-subset-sum\">→ Chukhin–Kulikov–Levitskii–Mihajlin subset-sum algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "knapsack-and-binary-integer-programming": {
    "title": "Knapsack and binary integer programming",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Knapsack and binary integer programming</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Knapsack and binary integer programming)</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Zero-one knapsack.</strong> Given positive integer weights <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, nonnegative profits <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> and capacity <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math></span>, maximize <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> subject to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math></span>. The decision form also supplies a profit threshold. This models budgeted selection and resource allocation.</p><p><span class=\"problem-anchor\" id=\"binary-integer-programming\"></span><strong>Binary integer programming.</strong> Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span>, or decide infeasibility. A linear objective is handled through a profit threshold. The quantum regime of interest has fixed or slowly growing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span> and binary-encoded coefficients; knapsack contributes capacity and profit-threshold inequalities.</p><p>The quantum bound uses coherent-memory time with bit-arithmetic costs; classical meet in the middle and capacity dynamic programming use RAM time, and Boolean encodings use bit complexity.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Arvind–Schuler binary integer programming; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>, including knapsack.</strong> Precomputed partial-sum vectors support coherent multidimensional range queries. The bound includes preprocessing and search; coefficient arithmetic contributes polynomial factors in bit length. For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, the time is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. Threshold search recovers the optimum with polynomial bit-length overhead. For knapsack, QuantumTreeGenerator additionally gives instance-dependent branch-and-bound estimates from classical solver trees. With <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit coefficients, the reversible arithmetic has cost polynomial in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>, meet in the middle with dominance searching; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for knapsack, capacity dynamic programming.</strong> The two half-assignment lists store partial-sum vectors and are matched by coordinatewise inequalities. Horowitz–Sahni knapsack removes dominated weight–profit pairs; capacity dynamic programming instead stores the best profit at each capacity. The smaller applicable bound supplies the comparison. A direct knapsack threshold circuit has size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, retained when applying Boolean tensor contraction. If every integer-programming row has support at most fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math></span>, forbidden local assignments give fixed-width clauses on the original <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> variables: polynomially many rows give <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mtext>input&#x000A0;size</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time and polynomial space, while linearly many rows improve the exponent to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>, dense, large-coefficient comparison improves the exponential constant from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>, a polynomial speedup based on Grover search and amplitude amplification. Small capacities and bounded row support select the stated stronger classical bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous quantum bounds in the coherent-memory model, with partial evidence against the applicable classical algorithms. The separate branch-and-bound estimates depend on solver trees and implementation costs.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Active registers contain <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> assignment qubits and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span> arithmetic accumulators; knapsack uses weight and profit registers of widths <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, with reversible workspace. Exponential coherent range-search data and long coherent searches dominate useful fault-tolerant implementations.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Arvind–Schuler binary integer programming; QuantumTreeGenerator</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0212048\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Arvind and Schuler, <em>The Quantum Query Complexity of 0-1 Knapsack and Associated Claw Problems</em> (ISAAC 2003)</a>. Section 2, Theorem 1 and steps 1–6, pp. 3–4: partial-sum preprocessing, range-tree queries and quantum search; Corollary 2 gives threshold optimization.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41534-025-01097-8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A Quantum Algorithm for Solving 0-1 Knapsack Problems</em> (npj Quantum Information 2025)</a>. The <span class=\"small-caps\">QuantumTreeGenerator</span> construction and the numerical comparison with COMBO search trees.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendices D.1–D.4, applied to the bounded-row-arity clauses.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/backtracking\">Quantum backtracking and branch and bound</a>: <a href=\"#entry/constraint-satisfaction-by-backtracking\">Constraint satisfaction by backtracking</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-optimization-by-branch-and-bound\">Branch and bound</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-shortest-vector-problem\">Shortest vector problem</a>, <a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-closest-vector-problem\">Closest vector problem</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/arvind-schuler\">Arvind–Schuler binary integer-programming search</a>: <a href=\"#entry/worst-case-subset-sum\">Worst-case subset sum</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/backtracking\">→ Quantum backtracking and branch and bound</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/arvind-schuler\">→ Arvind–Schuler binary integer-programming search</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover": {
    "title": "Independent set, clique and vertex cover",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Independent set, clique and vertex cover</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Maximum independent set, maximum clique and minimum vertex cover)</span></h2>\n<p>Find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math></span> of maximum size with no edge inside <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math></span>. Equivalent exact objectives are maximum clique in the complement graph and minimum vertex cover <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></math></span> in the original graph. These equivalences preserve vertex count and translate witnesses directly.\nThe comparison uses quantum gate complexity and classical bit complexity on explicit graph inputs.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.1488</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Dörn&#x27;s amplified branching algorithm.</strong> This bound applies to maximum independent set on an arbitrary <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-vertex graph. Complementation transfers it to maximum clique, and taking the complementary vertex set gives minimum vertex cover. Running the classical counting algorithm below on a quantum computer also supplies its subexponential upper bound.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, Boolean tensor contraction with cardinality constraints.</strong> Use one Boolean variable per vertex and a width-two clause for each forbidden selected edge. A linear-size Boolean cardinality circuit tests the target size, so the encoding uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> variables and fixed-width clauses even for a dense graph. Exact counting and self-reduction recover an optimum in polynomial space. For sparse graphs, maximum independent set and minimum vertex cover have the sharper exponent <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>; maximum clique has this sharper bound when its complement is sparse.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The subexponential classical bound is asymptotically smaller than the displayed amplified-branching quantum bound. The classical exponent is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, so its exponential-to-subexponential reduction has no power saving.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The stated algorithms have rigorous upper bounds. Dörn&#x27;s displayed bound supplies no asymptotic quantum-speedup claim against the current classical comparator.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The amplified branching method uses polynomial assignment and graph-evaluation workspace, with exponentially many coherent evaluations. Useful instances would require fault tolerance. The polynomial-space classical algorithm changes the relevant time comparison without adding an exponential memory requirement.\n<small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, the known general classical bound was <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.1996</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, and Dörn&#x27;s quantum algorithm claimed an improvement to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.1488</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Dörn&#x27;s amplified branching algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0510084\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dörn, <em>Quantum Complexity Bounds for Independent Set Problems</em> (2005)</a>. Algorithm 2 and Theorem 6: maximum independent set.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1312.6260\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Xiao and Nagamochi, <em>Exact Algorithms for Maximum Independent Set</em> (Information and Computation 2017)</a>. The main maximum-independent-set algorithm and its <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>1.1996</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> running-time result.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/formal-math/tree/main/eth\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Claude (Anthropic), <em>A <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> Time Algorithm for 3-SAT</em> (2026)</a>. Corollary 7: graph cardinality applications. <a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>, Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D, supplies the explicit fixed-width time and space bounds.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "domatic-number": {
    "title": "Domatic number",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Domatic number</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Domatic number)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, maximize <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math></span> can be partitioned into <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math></span> for every <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math></span>. Each class is a dominating set. The problem models disjoint teams of resources, each capable of providing coverage to the entire network.\nQuantum complexity is measured in QRAM time, classical inclusion–exclusion in RAM time, and the fixed-part-count Boolean algorithms in bit operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for some constant <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, Gaspers–Li domatic-number algorithm.</strong> The theorem obtains a strict improvement below base two through restricted enumeration of minimal dominating sets and the covering framework. The analysis establishes the existence of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math></span> rather than an optimized numerical base. The number of parts is optimized as part of the input problem, and preprocessing values are coherently accessible.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, inclusion–exclusion over dominating sets.</strong> For any fixed target <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>, the stronger bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003B7;</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for every fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>. One-hot membership variables and closed-neighborhood domination clauses produce <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> variables and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> possibly long clauses; long-clause shortening followed by fixed-width counting gives this bound. For fixed maximum degree, the same encoding has bounded width and linear size, yielding <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. These fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> bounds do not treat an unbounded number of parts as a constant.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup through a smaller exponential constant for unrestricted domatic number, based on amplitude amplification. Fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> decision has the subexponential classical comparison above.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>A rigorous quantum upper bound with partial evidence against the general classical exact algorithm. The fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> classical conclusion follows the explicit domination encoding.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial active subset and part-count registers accompany exponential coherent preprocessing memory. The small unspecified base improvement still entails exponentially long coherent searches on large graphs.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gaspers–Li domatic-number algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.08042\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gaspers and Li, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Graph Coloring and Other Partitioning, Covering, and Packing Problems</em> (ICALP 2024)</a>. Theorem 9: restricted minimal-dominating-set enumeration; Corollary 2: domatic number; Theorem 4 defines the quantum covering routine.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/formal-math/tree/main/eth\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Claude (Anthropic), <em>A <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> Time Algorithm for 3-SAT</em> (2026)</a>. Long-clause shortening; together with <a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em>, Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D</a>, applied to the domination clauses above.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>: <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition\">Set cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/chromatic-number\">Chromatic number</a>, <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/set-packing\">Set packing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/exact-set-partition\">Exact set partition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/covering-by-maximal-induced-bicliques\">Maximal induced biclique cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/packing-maximal-induced-matchings\">Maximal induced matching packing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/vertex-arboricity\">Vertex arboricity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-into-hereditary-graph-classes\">Hereditary graph partition</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">→ Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "covering-by-maximal-induced-bicliques": {
    "title": "Covering by maximal induced bicliques",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Covering by maximal induced bicliques</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Vertex covering by maximal induced bicliques)</span></h2>\n<p>For a graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, decide whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math></span> is the union of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> vertex sets, each inducing a maximal complete bipartite subgraph. Maximality means that no extra vertex can be added while preserving this property. The covering is on vertices; overlapping bicliques are permitted. Packing and exact partition variants impose disjointness.\nThe covering framework uses quantum QRAM time and classical RAM time, while fixed-cover-count algorithms use Boolean bit complexity.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.8554</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Gaspers–Li biclique covering, packing and partition algorithms.</strong> Maximal induced bicliques admit the restricted enumeration used by the framework, with base <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. The same theorem handles the three set-family variants once their covering, packing or partition feasibility condition is specified. Coherent preprocessing memory is assumed.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, inclusion–exclusion over the biclique family.</strong> For covering by a fixed number <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> of bicliques, a stronger bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. Two side-membership bits per vertex per biclique impose induced complete-bipartite adjacency through fixed-width clauses, together with a covering clause per vertex. Each covering biclique may be extended to a maximal one, so the encoding decides the formal covering problem. This extension preserves covering and the fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> comparison is stated for that variant.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup through a smaller exponential constant for the general family problems with unrestricted part count, using amplitude amplification. Fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> covering has the subexponential classical bound.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous enumeration-based quantum upper bound and partial evidence against the general classical framework.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial registers encode subsets, biclique sides and part counts. Exponential coherent table storage and long searches make useful cases fault-tolerant targets.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gaspers–Li biclique covering, packing and partition algorithms</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.08042\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gaspers and Li, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Graph Coloring and Other Partitioning, Covering, and Packing Problems</em> (ICALP 2024)</a>. Theorem 7, maximal-biclique application; Theorem 4 defines the covering, packing and partition routines and their representative-family condition.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D, applied to the two-side fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> covering encoding.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>: <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition\">Set cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/chromatic-number\">Chromatic number</a>, <a href=\"#entry/domatic-number\">Domatic number</a>, <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/set-packing\">Set packing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/exact-set-partition\">Exact set partition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/packing-maximal-induced-matchings\">Maximal induced matching packing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/vertex-arboricity\">Vertex arboricity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-into-hereditary-graph-classes\">Hereditary graph partition</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">→ Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "packing-maximal-induced-matchings": {
    "title": "Packing maximal induced matchings",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Packing maximal induced matchings</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Packing and partitioning maximal induced matchings)</span></h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math></span> contain all vertex sets <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math></span> such that every vertex of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> has degree one and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span> is inclusion-maximal with that property. Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> disjoint members of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math></span>, or a partition of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math></span> into <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> such members. Each member is the endpoint set of an induced matching.\nThe quantum comparison uses QRAM time and classical subset-transform time, with bit complexity for the fixed-part-count Boolean encoding.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.8934</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Gaspers–Li maximal-induced-matching framework.</strong> Restricted maximal-induced-matching enumeration has base <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. Combining it with partial subset preprocessing and quantum search gives the bound for packing or exact partition into an unrestricted number of members. Membership includes both induced matching and maximality.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, inclusion–exclusion and subset transforms.</strong> For every fixed number <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> of members, a stronger bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for each fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>. Vertex-membership and neighborhood-dominance bits encode degree one inside each member and exclusion of any further compatible edge. This uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> variables and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> long clauses, together with polynomially many fixed-width clauses. Long-clause shortening followed by tensor contraction gives the bound; disjointness and complete coverage specify packing and partition respectively.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup through a smaller exponential constant for unrestricted part count, arising from amplitude amplification. A fixed part count has the stronger subexponential classical comparator.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum upper bound follows the framework theorem and the maximal-induced-matching enumeration bound. The classical fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> conclusion follows the explicit Boolean encoding.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial active registers represent selected endpoints, local degree and maximality conditions, with exponential coherent preprocessing memory. Large useful instances require fault tolerance and long coherent searches.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gaspers–Li maximal-induced-matching algorithms</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.08042\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gaspers and Li, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Graph Coloring and Other Partitioning, Covering, and Packing Problems</em> (ICALP 2024)</a>. Theorem 7, maximal-induced-matching application, using the Gupta–Raman–Saurabh enumeration bound; Theorem 4 gives the packing and partition procedures.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/anthropics/formal-math/tree/main/eth\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Claude (Anthropic), <em>A <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> Time Algorithm for 3-SAT</em> (2026)</a>. Long-clause shortening; <a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em></a>, Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D: fixed-width counting after shortening.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>: <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition\">Set cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/chromatic-number\">Chromatic number</a>, <a href=\"#entry/domatic-number\">Domatic number</a>, <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/set-packing\">Set packing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/exact-set-partition\">Exact set partition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/covering-by-maximal-induced-bicliques\">Maximal induced biclique cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/vertex-arboricity\">Vertex arboricity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-into-hereditary-graph-classes\">Hereditary graph partition</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">→ Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "vertex-arboricity": {
    "title": "Vertex arboricity",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Vertex arboricity</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Vertex arboricity)</span></h2>\n<p>Find the smallest <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> for which <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace=\"0.22em\" rspace=\"0.22em\"><mrow><mover><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo>&#x002D9;</mo></mover></mrow></mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo lspace=\"0.22em\" rspace=\"0.22em\"><mrow><mover><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo>&#x002D9;</mo></mover></mrow></mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> and every <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> is a forest. This invariant is vertex arboricity. Equivalently, cover the vertex set by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> maximal induced forests: enlarge each forest for the forward implication and assign overlapping vertices to one covering forest for the reverse implication.\nThe quantum algorithm is measured in QRAM time and the classical subset algorithm in RAM time.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.9629</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Gaspers–Li induced-forest covering algorithm.</strong> The framework uses a maximal-induced-forest enumeration procedure with base <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.8527</mn></mrow></math></span>, combined with partial preprocessing and quantum search. Heredity permits deletion of overlaps from a forest covering to obtain a partition with the same number of parts. The input graph is explicit, and the number of parts is optimized.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, inclusion–exclusion over induced forests.</strong> Forest membership is polynomial-time decidable. Subset transforms aggregate admissible families and test covering feasibility; the covering-partition equivalence then gives the vertex-arboricity optimum. Classical memory is exponential at this time bound. Forest acyclicity is the defining global condition in this comparison.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>1.9629</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, based on amplitude amplification.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous quantum framework upper bound with partial evidence relative to the strongest displayed general classical subset method.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial active graph and subset registers require exponential coherent preprocessing storage. The modest reduction in the exponential base still leaves exponentially long coherent computation; useful implementations require fault tolerance.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gaspers–Li induced-forest covering algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.08042\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gaspers and Li, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Graph Coloring and Other Partitioning, Covering, and Packing Problems</em> (ICALP 2024)</a>. Theorem 7, maximal-induced-forest application; the preceding applications discussion gives the enumeration base and covering-partition equivalence, and Theorem 4 gives the quantum framework.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>: <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition\">Set cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/chromatic-number\">Chromatic number</a>, <a href=\"#entry/domatic-number\">Domatic number</a>, <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/set-packing\">Set packing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/exact-set-partition\">Exact set partition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/covering-by-maximal-induced-bicliques\">Maximal induced biclique cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/packing-maximal-induced-matchings\">Maximal induced matching packing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-into-hereditary-graph-classes\">Hereditary graph partition</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">→ Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "partition-into-hereditary-graph-classes": {
    "title": "Partition into hereditary graph classes",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Partition into hereditary graph classes</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Partition into a hereditary graph class)</span></h2>\n<p>For a fixed hereditary graph class <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">H</mi></mrow></math></span>, minimize <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace=\"0.22em\" rspace=\"0.22em\"><mrow><mover><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo>&#x002D9;</mo></mover></mrow></mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo lspace=\"0.22em\" rspace=\"0.22em\"><mrow><mover><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo>&#x002D9;</mo></mover></mrow></mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">H</mi></mrow></math></span>. For cluster graphs, every connected component of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> must be a clique, equivalently <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> contains no induced three-vertex path. This is a structured clustering or generalized coloring problem.\nThe unrestricted framework uses quantum QRAM time and classical RAM time, and fixed-part-count Boolean encodings use bit complexity.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.9149</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for cluster or triangle-free graphs, Gaspers–Li partition framework.</strong> The corresponding bases are <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.9365</mn></mrow></math></span> for cographs, claw-free graphs, trivially perfect graphs or threshold graphs; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.9494</mn></mrow></math></span> for split graphs; and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.9579</mn></mrow></math></span> for line graphs. Restricted enumeration of maximal induced members supplies the family routine. Heredity converts a covering into a partition by deleting overlaps. These bounds optimize the number of parts without treating it as a fixed constant.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, inclusion–exclusion and subset transforms.</strong> If the number of parts <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> is fixed and the class has a finite forbidden-induced-subgraph description of maximum size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math></span>, a stronger bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. One-hot variables assign a part to each vertex, and a clause forbids every monochromatic forbidden copy. This uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> variables, fixed width and polynomially many clauses. Exact counting and self-reduction recover the partition in polynomial space. This applies to the listed finite-forbidden-subgraph classes in the fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> regime.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedups through smaller exponential constants for unrestricted part count, from amplitude amplification. Fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> decision has the subexponential classical comparison above.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous enumeration-based quantum upper bounds, with partial evidence against the classical general partition framework.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial subset, part and forbidden-pattern registers accompany exponential coherent preprocessing memory. The largest forbidden-pattern size affects both enumeration cost and reversible membership testing.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gaspers–Li hereditary graph partition algorithms</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.08042\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gaspers and Li, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Graph Coloring and Other Partitioning, Covering, and Packing Problems</em> (ICALP 2024)</a>. Theorem 7 and its preceding list of forbidden-induced-subgraph classes; Theorem 4 defines the partition routine.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D, applied to the fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>, bounded-forbidden-pattern encoding.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>: <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition\">Set cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/chromatic-number\">Chromatic number</a>, <a href=\"#entry/domatic-number\">Domatic number</a>, <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/set-packing\">Set packing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/exact-set-partition\">Exact set partition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/covering-by-maximal-induced-bicliques\">Maximal induced biclique cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/packing-maximal-induced-matchings\">Maximal induced matching packing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/vertex-arboricity\">Vertex arboricity</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/gaspers-li\">→ Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "minimum-fill-in": {
    "title": "Minimum fill-in",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Minimum fill-in</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Minimum fill-in)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, minimize <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span> over added-edge sets <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> is chordal, meaning every cycle of length at least four has a chord. Minimum fill-in controls additional nonzero entries created by sparse elimination.\nThe quantum ordering bound uses QRAM time, while the classical structural algorithm uses RAM time on the explicit graph.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.817</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, quantum vertex-ordering dynamic programming.</strong> An elimination order determines a chordal completion by making each eliminated vertex&#x27;s remaining neighborhood a clique. The ordering recurrence charges the newly inserted edges and optimizes the total cost. Partial preprocessing and quantum minimum finding provide the stated bound with exponential coherent memory.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.7347</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, potential-maximal-clique dynamic programming.</strong> The algorithm enumerates potential maximal cliques and minimal-separator blocks, then selects compatible triangulations with minimum fill cost. Its time improves on the generic <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> elimination-order recurrence, and its space is exponential. The graph structure exploited by this method gives the smaller base in the overall comparison.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The classical <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>1.7347</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> bound is asymptotically smaller than the displayed quantum <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>1.817</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> bound. The quantum result accelerates the subset-ordering recurrence.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The ordering acceleration has a rigorous upper bound; the strongest displayed overall complexity bound is classical.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The quantum construction requires polynomial active subset and fill-cost registers with exponential QRAM. Useful depths require fault tolerance. The stronger classical structural method also uses exponential storage.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum vertex-ordering dynamic programming</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05209\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis et al., <em>Quantum Speedups for Exponential-Time Dynamic Programming Algorithms</em> (SODA 2019)</a>. Corollary 5 and equation (3): minimum-fill-in ordering application.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1309.1559\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Fomin, Todinca and Villanger, <em>Large Induced Subgraphs via Triangulations and CMSO</em> (SIAM Journal on Computing 2015)</a>. Proposition 5 states the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>1.7347</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> potential-maximal-clique enumeration bound; the triangulation preliminaries define minimal separators, full blocks and potential maximal cliques used in minimum-fill dynamic programming.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/vertex-ordering\">Quantum path finding in the Boolean hypercube</a>: <a href=\"#entry/graph-bandwidth\">Bandwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/vertex-ordering\">→ Quantum path finding in the Boolean hypercube</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "recursive-fourier-sampling": {
    "title": "Recursive Fourier sampling",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Recursive Fourier sampling</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Recursive Fourier sampling)</span></h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> be integers. A rooted tree of height <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math></span> has children <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> indexed by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. Each internal vertex <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math></span> has an unknown string <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and a Boolean function <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Its answer bit is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, and the promise is\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msup><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;internal&#x000A0;vertex&#x000A0;$v$&#x000A0;and&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;$x\\in\\{0,1\\</mtext><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>$</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mi>}</mi></mrow></math></span>\nGiven oracle access to the leaf bits and all the functions <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, output the root bit <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span> with probability at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>. A vertex is identified by its path from the root; the oracle address length is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. This promise problem was introduced to study separations between quantum and classical computation. Complexity is measured in oracle queries, including queries to the functions <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, recursive Bernstein–Vazirani Fourier sampling.</strong> The exact algorithm recursively recovers <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> with Hadamard transforms, queries <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, and uncomputes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>. Johnson&#x27;s clean implementation uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> queries. For the restriction in which all <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> equal a fixed nonparity function <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span>, Aaronson proves the lower bound <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> is its nonparity coefficient. The function <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02262;</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" /><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, yielding <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> queries and a matching <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> bound when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The non-oracle gate count has an additional factor polynomial in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, recursive classical Fourier sampling.</strong> At each vertex, recursively evaluating <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> linearly independent children recovers <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>; one query to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> then determines its answer. This uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> queries. For success probability <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math></span>, Johnson proves the lower bound <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> in the positive-factor range <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>. For constant <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, these bounds give randomized query complexity <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Subexponential to polynomial in the strongest comparison: throughout <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, the quantum cost is polynomial in the oracle address length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></math></span>, while the classical cost is superpolynomial. At larger depths within the stated lower-bound range, both costs become superpolynomial and the general bounds describe an intermediate improvement. Fixed depth gives a polynomial query advantage.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Unconditional randomized and quantum query lower bounds for the same oracle problem family establish the separation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The recursive circuit uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical qubits outside the oracle implementation and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> coherent oracle calls, with polynomial non-oracle gate overhead. Oracle implementation determines the additional workspace and gate cost.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Recursive Bernstein–Vazirani Fourier sampling</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1137/S0097539796300921\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bernstein and Vazirani, <em>Quantum Complexity Theory</em> (1997)</a>. Section 8.4 introduces recursive Fourier sampling and the quantum–classical oracle separation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=ee55b07d326ac3cb422fa755aae4484ab3b2ad2d&amp;repid=rep1&amp;type=pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Johnson, <em>Upper and Lower Bounds for Recursive Fourier Sampling</em>, Ph.D. thesis, University of California, Berkeley (2008)</a>. Section 1.2.3, starting on page 5, defines the generalized oracle problem; Algorithm 3, page 42, gives the clean quantum recursion; Algorithm 9, page 51, gives the classical recursion; Section 3.4, starting on page 59, gives the randomized query lower bound.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0209060\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Aaronson, <em>Quantum Lower Bound for Recursive Fourier Sampling</em> (2003)</a>. Section 1 and Lemma 1: arbitrary-depth problem and quantum recursion; Theorem 5 and Proposition 6: quantum lower bound.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/recursive-fourier\">→ Recursive Bernstein–Vazirani Fourier sampling</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q10": {
    "title": "Search with wildcards",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Search with wildcards</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Search with wildcards)</span></h2>\n<p>An unknown string <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> is accessed through queries <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, returning whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></span>. Recover <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> exactly with bounded error.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by the adversary-bound learning method; Ambainis–Montanaro give the earlier constructive <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> algorithm.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> wildcard queries in the worst case; singleton queries recover all bits.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Ambainis–Montanaro and Belovs wildcard learning</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.1148\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for search with wildcards and combinatorial group testing</em></a>. Wildcard algorithm; tight improvement summarized in TQC2026.3</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.6777\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithms for Learning Symmetric Juntas via the Adversary Bound</em></a>. Wildcard-search consequence of the adversary algorithm</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/wildcard-quantum-learning\">→ Ambainis–Montanaro and Belovs wildcard learning</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q11": {
    "title": "Combinatorial group testing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Combinatorial group testing</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Combinatorial group testing)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an unknown defective set <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> of size at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, a test on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> returns <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>. Recover <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span> with constant success probability; tests may be queried coherently.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Belovs&#x27; adversary-bound learner; efficient implementations have additional polynomial or near-linear dependence on the test description length.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> adaptive binary tests in the standard sparse-defective regime, by generalized binary splitting and information-theoretic lower bounds.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Belovs adversary algorithm for learning symmetric juntas</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.6777\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithms for Learning Symmetric Juntas via the Adversary Bound</em></a>. OR-function theorem; explicit implementation in Montanaro–Shao2011.08611</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/belovs-symmetric-junta-learning\">Belovs adversary algorithm for learning symmetric juntas</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q32\">Learning symmetric juntas</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/belovs-symmetric-junta-learning\">→ Belovs adversary algorithm for learning symmetric juntas</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q12": {
    "title": "Gapped group testing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Gapped group testing</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Gapped group testing)</span></h2>\n<p>An unknown <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> is promised to have <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>. Given subset-intersection tests <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>, distinguish the cases with bounded error.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Ambainis–Belovs–Regev–de Wolf, using a tailored adversary construction.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> tests in the corresponding nondegenerate parameter regime, by randomized testing and the paper&#x27;s lower bounds.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Ambainis–Belovs–Regev–de Wolf gapped group testing</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.03126\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Efficient Quantum Algorithms for (Gapped) Group Testing and Junta Testing</em></a>. Definitions3.1–3.2; Theorems3.3–3.5 and3.10</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/gapped-group-testing\">→ Ambainis–Belovs–Regev–de Wolf gapped group testing</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q13": {
    "title": "Junta testing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Junta testing</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Junta testing)</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Junta testing with membership queries.</strong> Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> by a coherent membership oracle, distinguish functions depending on at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> variables from functions at uniform Hamming distance at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> from every such function.</p><p><strong>Junta testing from quantum examples.</strong> Distinguish <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-juntas from functions <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>-far from every <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-junta using copies of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Classical access consists of independent uniform labeled examples.</p><p><strong>Distribution-free junta testing.</strong> Given membership queries to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> and sampling access to an unknown distribution <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span>, distinguish <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-juntas from functions with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> for every <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-junta <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span>. The quantum model coherently accesses the specified oracles.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <strong>Junta testing with membership queries.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math></span>, by the optimal Boolean-junta tester. In the range <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> this simplifies to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</strong> <strong>Junta testing from quantum examples.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum examples by Atici–Servedio Fourier sampling, testing whether observed Fourier supports fit within <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> coordinates.</p><p><strong>Distribution-free junta testing.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Belovs&#x27; distribution-free quantum junta tester.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <strong>Junta testing with membership queries.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical queries by junta testing or truth-table acquisition, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> required at constant distance.</strong> <strong>Junta testing from quantum examples.</strong> Passive classical examples require a number growing with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, even for small fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>; exhaustive empirical testing of all <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-coordinate functions uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> samples.</p><p><strong>Distribution-free junta testing.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> adaptive queries by classical distribution-free junta testing, with an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> lower bound at constant distance.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Optimal quantum junta testing; Atici–Servedio Fourier junta learning and testing; Belovs distribution-free quantum junta tester</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37926\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Optimal Quantum Junta Testing</em></a>. Theorems 4.2 and 4.8: complete Boolean-junta query bound</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.3479\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithms for Learning and Testing Juntas</em></a>. Junta-testing theorem and Fourier-sampling procedure</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.08462\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithm for Distribution-Free Junta Testing</em></a>. Main algorithm; CSR2019.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-fourier-junta-learning\">Atici–Servedio Fourier junta learning and testing</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q15\">Learning juntas from quantum examples</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/optimal-quantum-junta-testing\">→ Optimal quantum junta testing</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-fourier-junta-learning\">→ Atici–Servedio Fourier junta learning and testing</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/distribution-free-quantum-junta-testing\">→ Belovs distribution-free quantum junta tester</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q15": {
    "title": "Learning juntas from quantum examples",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Learning juntas from quantum examples</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Learning juntas from quantum examples)</span></h2>\n<p>Given uniform quantum examples of an unknown Boolean function depending on at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> coordinates, output a classical hypothesis with uniform error at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> and constant confidence.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum examples to identify relevant coordinates, plus <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical examples to learn the restricted truth table, by Atici–Servedio.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> uniform labeled examples suffice by finite-class learning. Classical search over relevant-coordinate sets can take <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time; the information-theoretic sample dependence on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> persists.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Each example occupies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> logical qubits. The algorithm additionally needs the indicated number of coherent examples and its measurement/postprocessing workspace; preparing these examples is part of the supplied-access model.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Atici–Servedio Fourier junta learning and testing</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.3479\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithms for Learning and Testing Juntas</em></a>. Learning theorem and algorithm</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-fourier-junta-learning\">Atici–Servedio Fourier junta learning and testing</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q13\">Junta testing</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-fourier-junta-learning\">→ Atici–Servedio Fourier junta learning and testing</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q16": {
    "title": "Learning DNF from quantum examples",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Learning DNF from quantum examples</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Learning DNF from quantum examples)</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Learning DNF under the uniform distribution.</strong> An unknown <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>-term DNF <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> supplies uniform quantum examples. Output a classical hypothesis <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> and confidence <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Complexity counts examples and computation.</p><p><strong>Learning DNF under product distributions.</strong> Given quantum examples of an unknown <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>-term DNF under a product distribution with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> for constant <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, output a hypothesis of error at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>. The marginal biases are known or learned as in the algorithm.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <strong>Learning DNF under the uniform distribution.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time and quantum examples by Bshouty–Jackson, combining Fourier sampling and boosting.</strong> <strong>Learning DNF under product distributions.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time for constant <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></span>, by Kanade–Rocchetto–Severini biased Fourier sampling and the product-distribution DNF learner.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <strong>Learning DNF under the uniform distribution.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by classical low-degree approximation and uniform-distribution DNF learning. Classical membership-query learning is polynomial in the stronger active-query model.</strong> <strong>Learning DNF under product distributions.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time by low-degree biased-Fourier approximation and classical regression; the general distribution-independent DNF learner has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time for polynomial-size DNF.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum Fourier-sampling DNF learning</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/S0097539795293123\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Learning DNF over the Uniform Distribution Using a Quantum Example Oracle</em></a>. Quantum Fourier-sampling weak learner and boosting construction</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.05690\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Learning DNFs under product distributions via mu-biased quantum Fourier sampling</em></a>. Biased Fourier sampler and DNF-learning theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-dnf-fourier-learning\">→ Quantum Fourier-sampling DNF learning</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q18": {
    "title": "Exact learning of Fourier-sparse Boolean functions",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Exact learning of Fourier-sparse Boolean functions</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Exact learning of Fourier-sparse Boolean functions)</span></h2>\n<p>Given uniform quantum examples of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> with at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> nonzero Fourier coefficients, recover <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math></span> exactly with constant success probability. The resource is copies of examples, with processing counted separately.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum examples by Ye&#x27;s 2026 support-covering and exact-recovery learner, using separate measurements; the earlier bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> uniform labeled samples in the admissible sparsity range, by sparse recovery and matching classical sample lower bounds.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Each example occupies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> logical qubits. The algorithm additionally needs the indicated number of coherent examples and its measurement/postprocessing workspace; preparing these examples is part of the supplied-access model.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Fourier-sparse Boolean-function learning</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17648\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Nearly Optimal Quantum Sample Complexity of Learning Fourier-Sparse Boolean Functions</em></a>. Main learning theorem and classical sample-complexity section</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/fourier-sparse-quantum-learning\">→ Fourier-sparse Boolean-function learning</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q19": {
    "title": "Agnostic learning of decision trees",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Agnostic learning of decision trees</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Agnostic learning of decision trees)</span></h2>\n<p>Given quantum examples of a distribution over <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> with uniform marginal on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span>, output a classical hypothesis with error at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>opt</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>opt</mo><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> is the best error of a size-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span> decision tree.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Chatterjee–SAPV–Bera, using a quantum Goldreich–Levin weak learner and agnostic boosting.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by classical agnostic decision-tree learning from examples, as in Blanc–Lange–Tan. Membership-query agnostic learners have polynomial running time with their stronger access.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Chatterjee–SAPV–Bera quantum agnostic learner</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v238/chatterjee24a/chatterjee24a.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Efficient Quantum Agnostic Improper Learning of Decision Trees</em></a>. Algorithms1–3; Theorems3–4</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-agnostic-decision-tree-learning\">→ Chatterjee–SAPV–Bera quantum agnostic learner</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q20": {
    "title": "Linearity testing of Boolean functions",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Linearity testing of Boolean functions</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Linearity testing of Boolean functions)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a coherent oracle for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>, distinguish linear functions <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> from functions at normalized Hamming distance at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> from every linear function.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by quantum linearity testing, amplifying detection of Fourier mass incompatible with linearity.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized queries by the Blum–Luby–Rubinfeld test and the corresponding distance-dependent lower bound.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum linearity and symmetry testing; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.6195\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Improved quantum test for linearity of a Boolean function</em></a>. Main algorithm; earlier construction: arXiv:1106.4831</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-boolean-property-tests\">Quantum linearity and symmetry testing</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-boolean-property-tests\">→ Quantum linearity and symmetry testing</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q21": {
    "title": "Symmetry testing of Boolean functions",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Symmetry testing of Boolean functions)</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Symmetry testing of Boolean functions.</strong> Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, distinguish functions depending only on Hamming weight from functions <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>-far from every such symmetric function. Here access is by coherent membership queries.</p><p><strong>Symmetry testing from quantum examples.</strong> Distinguish symmetric Boolean functions from functions <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>-far from symmetric using copies of the uniform quantum example state; symmetry means invariance under every permutation of coordinates.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <strong>Symmetry testing of Boolean functions.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by the quantum symmetry test, using the symmetric subspace and amplitude amplification.</strong> <strong>Symmetry testing from quantum examples.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum examples by Caro–Naik–Slote, independent of the Boolean dimension <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <strong>Symmetry testing of Boolean functions.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> membership queries by sampling pairs of inputs with equal Hamming weight and checking their values.</strong> <strong>Symmetry testing from quantum examples.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> passive uniform labeled samples are necessary at constant distance; classical tests compare sampled inputs of equal Hamming weight.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Each example occupies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> logical qubits. The algorithm additionally needs the indicated number of coherent examples and its measurement/postprocessing workspace; preparing these examples is part of the supplied-access model.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum linearity and symmetry testing; Grover search and amplitude amplification; Caro–Naik–Slote property testing from quantum data</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2013/232.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms to check Resiliency, Symmetry and Linearity of a Boolean function</em></a>. Symmetry-testing section; later extended manuscript.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol362-itcs2026/html/LIPIcs.ITCS.2026.34/LIPIcs.ITCS.2026.34.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Testing classical properties from quantum data</em></a>. Symmetry application.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-boolean-property-tests\">Quantum linearity and symmetry testing</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-data-property-testing\">Caro–Naik–Slote property testing from quantum data</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q22\">Monotonicity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q25\">Triangle-freeness of Boolean functions from examples</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-boolean-property-tests\">→ Quantum linearity and symmetry testing</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-data-property-testing\">→ Caro–Naik–Slote property testing from quantum data</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q22": {
    "title": "Monotonicity testing of Boolean functions",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Monotonicity testing of Boolean functions</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Monotonicity testing of Boolean functions)</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Monotonicity testing of Boolean functions.</strong> For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, distinguish <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> from distance at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> to every monotone Boolean function, using membership queries.</p><p><strong>Monotonicity testing from quantum examples.</strong> Distinguish monotone Boolean functions on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> from functions <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>-far from monotone using independent uniform quantum examples <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Classical access consists of passive uniform labeled samples.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <strong>Monotonicity testing of Boolean functions.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Belovs–Blais quantum monotonicity testing.</strong> <strong>Monotonicity testing from quantum examples.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum examples by Caro–Naik–Slote, with failure probability <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <strong>Monotonicity testing of Boolean functions.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by the directed-isoperimetric classical tester. At constant distance, adaptive classical lower bounds are <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>.</strong> <strong>Monotonicity testing from quantum examples.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> passive samples are necessary in the theorem&#x27;s parameter regime; learning the truth table gives an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>-sample upper bound.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Each example occupies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> logical qubits. The algorithm additionally needs the indicated number of coherent examples and its measurement/postprocessing workspace; preparing these examples is part of the supplied-access model.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Belovs–Blais quantum monotonicity tester; Caro–Naik–Slote property testing from quantum data</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://theoryofcomputing.org/articles/v011a016/v011a016.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithm for Monotonicity Testing</em></a>. Main theorem and Section3 algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol362-itcs2026/html/LIPIcs.ITCS.2026.34/LIPIcs.ITCS.2026.34.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Testing classical properties from quantum data</em></a>. Algorithm1 and Theorem9.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-data-property-testing\">Caro–Naik–Slote property testing from quantum data</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q25\">Triangle-freeness of Boolean functions from examples</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-monotonicity-tester\">→ Belovs–Blais quantum monotonicity tester</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-data-property-testing\">→ Caro–Naik–Slote property testing from quantum data</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q25": {
    "title": "Triangle-freeness of Boolean functions from examples",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Triangle-freeness of Boolean functions from examples</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Triangle-freeness of Boolean functions from examples)</span></h2>\n<p>For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, distinguish functions with no <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></span> satisfying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> from functions <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>-far from that property. Access consists of uniform quantum examples.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum examples by Caro–Naik–Slote, exploiting coherent access to the additive structure of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> passive labeled examples are required at constant distance in the classical model; sampling enough additive triples gives the corresponding finite-domain testing approach.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Each example occupies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> logical qubits. The algorithm additionally needs the indicated number of coherent examples and its measurement/postprocessing workspace; preparing these examples is part of the supplied-access model.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Caro–Naik–Slote property testing from quantum data</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol362-itcs2026/html/LIPIcs.ITCS.2026.34/LIPIcs.ITCS.2026.34.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Testing classical properties from quantum data</em></a>. Triangle-freeness application and classical sample lower bound.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-data-property-testing\">Caro–Naik–Slote property testing from quantum data</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q22\">Monotonicity testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-data-property-testing\">→ Caro–Naik–Slote property testing from quantum data</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q29": {
    "title": "Quantum coupon collection",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum coupon collection</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Quantum coupon collection)</span></h2>\n<p>An unknown <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-element subset <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> supplies copies of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Recover all elements of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span> with constant success probability. A second model also supplies reflection about <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> copies for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>; with state reflections, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries. These bounds are from Arunachalam–Belovs–Childs–Kothari–Rosmanis–de Wolf.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> independent uniform samples from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span> by classical coupon collection. The quantum benefit depends on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> and on whether reflections are supplied.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The copy-only advantage is logarithmic in the favorable support regime. With the supplied reflection oracle, the strongest regime has polynomial query advantage; both models remain explicit above.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum coupon-collector algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.07688\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Coupon Collector</em></a>. Table1; Theorems2,5,9–12.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-coupon-collector\">→ Quantum coupon-collector algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q32": {
    "title": "Learning symmetric juntas",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Learning symmetric juntas</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Learning symmetric juntas)</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Learning an exact-half junta.</strong> An unknown <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> has known even size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>. Query access is to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>. Recover <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span> with bounded error; each query can specify a superposition of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit inputs.</p><p><strong>Learning a majority junta.</strong> An unknown <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> of known size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> defines <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>, with the paper&#x27;s tie convention. Given membership queries, recover <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <strong>Learning an exact-half junta.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Belovs&#x27; adversary-bound algorithm, with efficient implementations described in the subsequent symmetric-junta learning work.</strong> <strong>Learning a majority junta.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Belovs&#x27; symmetric-junta adversary algorithm; time-efficient variants incur additional logarithmic and input-description factors.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <strong>Learning an exact-half junta.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical binary queries by information theory for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>; finite-class elimination provides a general upper bound and active learning exploits the symmetric predicate.</strong> <strong>Learning a majority junta.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical binary queries for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>, by distinguishing the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> candidate supports.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Belovs adversary algorithm for learning symmetric juntas</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.6777\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithms for Learning Symmetric Juntas via the Adversary Bound</em></a>. Exact-half application; implementation in arXiv:2011.08611.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.6777\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithms for Learning Symmetric Juntas via the Adversary Bound</em></a>. Majority application; implementation in arXiv:2011.08611.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/belovs-symmetric-junta-learning\">Belovs adversary algorithm for learning symmetric juntas</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q11\">Combinatorial group testing</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/belovs-symmetric-junta-learning\">→ Belovs adversary algorithm for learning symmetric juntas</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q34": {
    "title": "Identity and uniformity testing of finite distributions",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Identity and uniformity testing of finite distributions</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Identity and uniformity testing of finite distributions)</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Uniformity testing of a finite distribution.</strong> Given coherent access to a classical sampler for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>, distinguish <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span>. Classical access uses ordinary independent samples.</p><p><strong>Identity testing of a finite distribution.</strong> Given a known distribution <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> and coherent sampler access to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span>, distinguish <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>q</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>. The known probabilities of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> can be evaluated as required by the reduction.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <strong>Uniformity testing of a finite distribution.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> coherent sampler queries by Bravyi–Harrow–Hassidim and Chakraborty–Fischer–Matsliah–de Wolf; the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> dependence is optimal at constant distance.</strong> <strong>Identity testing of a finite distribution.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Chakraborty–Fischer–Matsliah–de Wolf via reduction to uniformity testing.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <strong>Uniformity testing of a finite distribution.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> samples by collision or chi-square uniformity testers in the standard nondegenerate range.</strong> <strong>Identity testing of a finite distribution.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> samples in the worst case over known reference distributions, with sharper instance-dependent bounds for a specified <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum finite-distribution identity and uniformity testing</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.3920\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for testing properties of distributions</em></a>. Uniformity algorithm; improved precision in arXiv:1005.0523 Section3.1.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.0523\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>New Results on Quantum Property Testing</em></a>. Theorem1.1, Section3.2.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-distribution-identity-testing\">→ Quantum finite-distribution identity and uniformity testing</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q36": {
    "title": "ℓ_1-closeness testing of finite distributions",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions)</span></h2>\n<p>Given coherent purified sample-preparation oracles for unknown <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> and their inverses, distinguish <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>q</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Both distributions have finite support.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Luo–Wang–Li, refining the purified-access distribution testers of Gilyen–Li.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> independent samples by optimal classical two-sample closeness testing.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing; Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.12916\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Succinct quantum testers for closeness and k-wise uniformity of probability distributions</em></a>. L1-closeness theorem; predecessor arXiv:1902.00814 Corollary17.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/purified-distribution-testing\">Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>: <a href=\"#entry/approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments\">Approximate counting</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/purified-distribution-testing\">→ Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">→ Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q37": {
    "title": "ℓ_2-closeness testing of finite distributions",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions)</span></h2>\n<p>Given purified coherent sample-preparation oracles for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> on a finite set and their inverses, distinguish <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>q</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Complexity counts preparation-oracle queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Luo–Wang–Li; the domain size does not enter this norm-sensitive bound.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> independent samples in the worst case by classical collision-based <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span> testing.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing; Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.12916\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Succinct quantum testers for closeness and k-wise uniformity of probability distributions</em></a>. L2-closeness theorem; robust variant in arXiv:1902.00814 Theorem14.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/purified-distribution-testing\">Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>: <a href=\"#entry/approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments\">Approximate counting</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/purified-distribution-testing\">→ Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">→ Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q38": {
    "title": "Independence testing of a finite joint distribution",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Independence testing of a finite joint distribution)</span></h2>\n<p>For an unknown <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>, distinguish <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>, using purified coherent sample preparation and its inverse. Assume <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Gilyen–Li, reducing to coherent closeness testing against the product of marginals.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> samples by optimal classical independence testing.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing; Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.00814\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Distributional property testing in a quantum world</em></a>. Definition6; Corollary18; AppendixC.2.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/purified-distribution-testing\">Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>: <a href=\"#entry/approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments\">Approximate counting</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/purified-distribution-testing\">→ Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">→ Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q39": {
    "title": "k-wise uniformity testing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing)</span></h2>\n<p>Given purified access to a distribution <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, test whether every marginal on at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> coordinates is uniform, versus being <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>-far in total variation from all such distributions. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> is fixed.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Luo–Wang–Li, estimating the low-degree Fourier moment condition.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> samples by the classical tester compared in that work; for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>, the cited sample lower bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing; Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.12916\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Succinct quantum testers for closeness and k-wise uniformity of probability distributions</em></a>. k-wise-uniformity theorem.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/purified-distribution-testing\">Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>: <a href=\"#entry/approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments\">Approximate counting</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/purified-distribution-testing\">→ Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">→ Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q40": {
    "title": "Shannon entropy of a finite distribution",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Shannon entropy of a finite distribution)</span></h2>\n<p>For a distribution <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>, estimate <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> within additive <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>. The input is a coherent distribution oracle; its precise access model is part of the complexity statement.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> purified-access queries by Li–Wu and subsequent entropy algorithms; Shin–Jeong obtain <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> in their quantum probability-oracle model.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> independent samples in the standard entropy-estimation regime, by polynomial approximation and bias-corrected estimators.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum Shannon-entropy estimation; Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07452\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near optimal quantum algorithm for estimating Shannon entropy</em></a>. Main theorem; established predecessor arXiv:1902.00814 Theorem12.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.06025\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Query Complexity of Entropy Estimation</em></a>. Shannon-entropy and support-estimation theorems</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>: <a href=\"#entry/approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments\">Approximate counting</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-finite-entropy-estimation\">→ Quantum Shannon-entropy estimation</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">→ Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q41": {
    "title": "Support-size estimation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Support-size estimation</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Support-size estimation)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a distribution <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> whose nonzero probabilities are at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span>, estimate <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span> to additive error <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span>. Access is through the coherent sampler used by Li–Wu.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum queries by Li–Wu, using amplitude estimation and polynomial approximations to the zero-probability threshold.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> independent samples for fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> in the standard minimum-mass model, by optimal unseen-species/support estimators.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Li–Wu support-size and support-coverage estimation; Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.06025\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Query Complexity of Entropy Estimation</em></a>. Algorithm9 and Theorem8.2.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-support-estimation\">Li–Wu support-size and support-coverage estimation</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>: <a href=\"#entry/approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments\">Approximate counting</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-support-estimation\">→ Li–Wu support-size and support-coverage estimation</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">→ Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q42": {
    "title": "Support-coverage estimation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Support-coverage estimation</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Support-coverage estimation)</span></h2>\n<p>For a finite distribution <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> and integer <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span>, estimate <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, the expected number of distinct values in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span> independent draws, to additive error <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span>. Access is via a coherent sampler.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Li–Wu&#x27;s support-coverage estimator.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical samples at fixed accuracy in the corresponding support-coverage regime, by optimal unseen-species estimators.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Li–Wu support-size and support-coverage estimation; Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.06025\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Query Complexity of Entropy Estimation</em></a>. Algorithm8 and Theorem8.1.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-support-estimation\">Li–Wu support-size and support-coverage estimation</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>: <a href=\"#entry/approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments\">Approximate counting</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-support-estimation\">→ Li–Wu support-size and support-coverage estimation</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">→ Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q43": {
    "title": "Disjoint-support testing of finite distributions",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Disjoint-support testing of finite distributions</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Disjoint-support testing of finite distributions)</span></h2>\n<p>Given coherent samplers for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>, distinguish <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>supp</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mo>supp</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math></span> from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>q</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>. This is the orthogonality-testing promise of Bravyi–Harrow–Hassidim.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries for constant <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> by coherent collision detection between samples from the two distributions.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> samples in the worst-case constant-gap setting, using cross-collisions and the classical collision lower bound.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Bravyi–Harrow–Hassidim orthogonality tester</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.3920\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for testing properties of distributions</em></a>. Orthogonality-testing algorithm.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-distribution-orthogonality\">→ Bravyi–Harrow–Hassidim orthogonality tester</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "mst": {
    "title": "Minimum spanning tree",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Minimum spanning tree</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Minimum spanning tree)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a connected undirected graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span>, and weights <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow></math></span>, output a spanning tree <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></span> minimizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. This is the minimum-cost network connecting all vertices.\nThe comparison counts weight-oracle queries; explicit-input classical time counts comparisons and RAM operations, and quantum gate cost includes reversible weight access.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> matrix queries or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> array queries, Dürr–Heiligman–Høyer–Mhalla.</strong> The adjacency array supplies each vertex degree and coherent access to its indexed weighted neighbors. Both bounds allow bounded error and have polylogarithmic computational overhead when graph access and weight comparison are efficient. The output consists of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> classical edges.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> expected time, Karger–Klein–Tarjan; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> matrix queries.</strong> The randomized linear-time algorithm uses an explicit edge list and weight comparisons. In the oracle representation, inspecting the edge weights requires <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries in the worst case. Thus the matrix comparison is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span> against <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>; the array advantage is a factor <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></span>. For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit weights, arithmetic and comparison costs must be included when converting either query bound to bit or gate complexity.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial: a factor <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></math></span> for matrix input and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></span> for array input. The quantum improvement comes from amplitude amplification and minimum finding inside Borůvka phases.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The classical and quantum query bounds are tight for their respective input representations.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant graph access is required at useful scale. Active search and weight registers use <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical qubits, in addition to reversible-oracle workspace and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical component records.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Dürr–Heiligman–Høyer–Mhalla minimum spanning tree</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0401091\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dürr, Heiligman, Høyer and Mhalla, <em>Quantum Query Complexity of Some Graph Problems</em> (2004/2006)</a>. Section 4, “Minimum Spanning Tree”, Theorem 8; Section 8, Theorem 24, for the lower bound.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/201019.201022\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Karger, Klein and Tarjan, <em>A Randomized Linear-Time Algorithm to Find Minimum Spanning Trees</em> (1995)</a>. The minimum-spanning-tree algorithm and its expected linear-time analysis.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "undirected-graph-connectivity": {
    "title": "Undirected graph connectivity",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Undirected graph connectivity</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Undirected graph connectivity)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an undirected graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span>, decide whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math></span> there is a path from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math></span>; optionally return its connected components and a spanning forest. Connectivity is a feasibility condition in network design.\nThe quantum and classical bounds count adjacency-matrix or neighbor-array queries; explicit graph traversal time is also stated.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> matrix queries or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> array queries, quantum graph exploration.</strong> The array model includes the vertex degrees and indexed coherent access to each neighbor list. The algorithm can also output connected components and a spanning forest with the same asymptotic query bound. The forest has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> edges.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> matrix queries; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> explicit-input time, breadth-first or depth-first search.</strong> The matrix lower bound follows from distinguishing two disconnected components from a graph with one hidden connecting edge. Array traversal visits every discovered vertex and its incident edges. The quantum linear-query result uses the symmetry of undirected neighbor access and the supplied degrees.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> in the matrix model and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> in the array comparison. Quantum search accelerates structured graph exploration.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>A rigorous query separation, with matching quantum upper and lower bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant implementation uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> active vertex registers, coherent neighbor-oracle workspace and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical forest records. Constructing or accessing the graph oracle contributes to the gate count.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum graph exploration</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0401091\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dürr, Heiligman, Høyer and Mhalla, <em>Quantum Query Complexity of Some Graph Problems</em> (2004/2006)</a>. Section 5, “Connectivity”, Theorems 9 and 11; Section 8, Theorem 23, for the matrix lower bound.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-graph-exploration\">Quantum graph exploration</a>: <a href=\"#entry/strong-connectivity-of-directed-graphs\">Strong connectivity</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-graph-exploration\">→ Quantum graph exploration</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "strong-connectivity-of-directed-graphs": {
    "title": "Strong connectivity of directed graphs",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Strong connectivity of directed graphs</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Strong connectivity of directed graphs)</span></h2>\n<p>For a directed graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, decide whether every ordered pair <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> is joined by a directed path. A constructive version returns the strongly connected components. Applications include reachability and recurrent states in transition systems.\nThe comparison counts adjacency queries, with classical RAM time stated for explicit adjacency lists.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> matrix queries and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> array queries, quantum directed graph exploration.</strong> The array bound has lower bound <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Classical discovery orders and component records support the quantum searches, and the output can list the strongly connected components. The graph representation supplies outgoing neighbors; this access model determines the array bound.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time, Tarjan or Kosaraju; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> matrix queries.</strong> Tarjan uses depth-first discovery indices and low-link information, while Kosaraju uses two directed traversals. Both produce an explicit component partition. With matrix input, deciding whether a hidden arc closes a one-way cut gives the quadratic classical query requirement.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial, from Grover search and quantum minimum finding. The dense matrix improvement is a factor <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></math></span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous oracle advantage. The matrix bound is tight, and the array bound is tight up to logarithmic factors.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant search uses logarithmic vertex-address registers and oracle workspace, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical component data. The resource requirement is dominated by repeated coherent access to the directed graph.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum directed graph exploration</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0401091\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dürr, Heiligman, Høyer and Mhalla, <em>Quantum Query Complexity of Some Graph Problems</em> (2004/2006)</a>. Section 6, “Strong Connectivity”, including Theorem 13; Section 8, Theorem 22.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1137/0201010\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Tarjan, <em>Depth-First Search and Linear Graph Algorithms</em> (1972)</a>. The strongly connected components algorithm.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-graph-exploration\">Quantum graph exploration</a>: <a href=\"#entry/undirected-graph-connectivity\">Undirected connectivity</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-graph-exploration\">→ Quantum graph exploration</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "single-source-shortest-paths": {
    "title": "Single-source shortest paths",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Single-source shortest paths</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Single-source shortest paths)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a directed graph with nonnegative edge lengths and a source <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>&#x02933;</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for all vertices, together with optional predecessor edges. Unreachable vertices receive distance <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math></span>.\nThe oracle comparison counts weighted adjacency queries; explicit-input times use the comparison-addition model, with bit or gate costs depending on weight precision.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> matrix queries or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> array queries, quantum shortest-path search.</strong> The corresponding lower bounds are <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The algorithms return all distances and can retain predecessor edges. Their computational overhead includes arithmetic on distances and reversible evaluation of the weighted graph oracle.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time, Duan–Mao–Mao–Shu–Yin; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> dense-matrix queries.</strong> The deterministic 2025 algorithm treats directed nonnegative real weights in the comparison-addition model. Taking its minimum with Dijkstra using Fibonacci heaps, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, gives a useful bound across graph densities. For matrix-oracle input, the tight classical query complexity remains quadratic, so the dense oracle comparison is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span> versus <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Weight precision determines the bit complexity of additions and comparisons.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial oracle speedup, originating in amplitude amplification and minimum finding. The explicit sparse-graph comparison uses the smaller of the two stated classical time bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous query separation, with quantum lower bounds matching the main powers of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault tolerance is required for long coherent searches. Active registers use <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical qubits for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit distance arithmetic, plus reversible arithmetic and oracle workspace; classical distance records occupy <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> bits.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Dürr–Heiligman–Høyer–Mhalla shortest paths</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0401091\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dürr, Heiligman, Høyer and Mhalla, <em>Quantum Query Complexity of Some Graph Problems</em> (2004/2006)</a>. Section 7, “Single Source Shortest Paths”, Theorem 18 and Table 1; Section 8, Theorem 24.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.17033\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Duan, Mao, Mao, Shu and Yin, <em>Breaking the Sorting Barrier for Directed Single-Source Shortest Paths</em> (2025)</a>. Theorem 1.1.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "maximum-cardinality-matching": {
    "title": "Maximum-cardinality matching",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Maximum-cardinality matching</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Maximum-cardinality matching)</span></h2>\n<p>Output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math></span> maximizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span>, subject to every vertex occurring in at most one edge of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math></span>. The input may be bipartite or general. Matching models assignment and resource allocation.\nThe comparison uses quantum time with coherent adjacency-array access and classical RAM or field-arithmetic time on explicit graphs.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for bipartite graphs; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for general graphs, Ambainis–Špalek.</strong> The matching is stored classically, and quantum access supports augmenting-path and blossom operations. These are time upper bounds for explicit classical output under coherent adjacency access.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for bipartite graphs, almost-linear maximum flow; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> for general graphs.</strong> Bipartite cardinality matching reduces to unit-capacity flow. For general graphs, Micali–Vazirani supplies the sparse combinatorial bound and randomized algebraic matching uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> field operations. The bound <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math></span> yields the displayed algebraic time for every fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>. The minimum of these classical bounds is asymptotically at least as strong as the stated quantum bounds.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The displayed quantum algorithms have no current worst-case time advantage over these classical algorithms.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The runtime upper bounds are rigorous; the present comparison assigns no quantum-speedup claim.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant coherent adjacency access is needed. Search registers are logarithmic in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, while matching, blossom and layered-graph records require polynomial classical storage with reversible query support.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Ambainis–Špalek quantum matching</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0508205\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis and Špalek, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Matching and Network Flows</em> (2005/2006)</a>. Theorems 3 and 4: bipartite and general matching.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.00671\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Chen et al., <em>Maximum Flow and Minimum-Cost Flow in Almost-Linear Time</em> (2022)</a>. Main maximum-flow and minimum-cost-flow theorem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Matrix-Multiplication-Nine-Fourths-October-2-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>An Upper Bound of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math></span> for the Matrix Multiplication Exponent</em> (2026)</a>. Main matrix-multiplication theorem; <a href=\"https://github.com/jzuiddam/omega-nine-quarters-all-fields\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Zuiddam, extension to every field (2026)</a>.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-matching\">→ Ambainis–Špalek quantum matching algorithms</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "integral-maximum-flow": {
    "title": "Integral maximum flow",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Integral maximum flow</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Integral maximum flow)</span></h2>\n<p>Given directed capacities <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> and terminals <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span>, maximize the net outflow from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> over <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, subject to flow conservation at <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. This is a central network optimization primitive.\nComplexity counts quantum time with coherent residual-graph access and classical RAM time for polynomially bounded integral capacities.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>U</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Ambainis–Špalek.</strong> This bound assumes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. The flow is returned as an explicit classical edge array. Residual-capacity updates and subsequent coherent queries are part of the implementation.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, Chen–Kyng–Liu–Peng–Probst Gutenberg–Sachdeva and deterministic van den Brand et al.</strong> The almost-linear bound applies to polynomially bounded integer capacities. Interior-point optimization is combined with dynamic graph data structures for finding improving flow directions. This bound is asymptotically stronger than the displayed quantum blocking-flow bound for the same capacity regime.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The current comparison supplies no general quantum time advantage.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Both algorithmic bounds are rigorous; the quantum theorem is an upper bound for a specific flow method.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant implementation uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> search and arithmetic registers plus oracle workspace. Residual-flow data occupy <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical bits and must support coherent queries after updates.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Ambainis–Špalek quantum blocking flow</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0508205\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis and Špalek, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Matching and Network Flows</em> (2005/2006)</a>. Theorem 6 and the blocking-flow procedure following it.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.00671\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Chen et al., <em>Maximum Flow and Minimum-Cost Flow in Almost-Linear Time</em> (2022)</a>. Main algorithmic theorem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.16629\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">van den Brand et al., <em>A Deterministic Almost-Linear Time Algorithm for Minimum-Cost Flow</em> (2023)</a>. Main minimum-cost-flow theorem.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-flow\">→ Ambainis–Špalek quantum blocking-flow algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "unweighted-triangle-finding": {
    "title": "Unweighted triangle finding",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Unweighted triangle finding</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Unweighted triangle finding)</span></h2>\n<p>Given oracle access to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for a simple undirected graph, find distinct <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>, or report that none exist. Triangles encode local clustering and small constraint witnesses.\nThe primary comparison counts adjacency-oracle queries; the explicit-input classical comparator counts arithmetic operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, Le Gall&#x27;s combinatorial quantum-walk algorithm.</strong> The algorithm outputs a triangle with bounded error whenever one exists. The query count includes sampled neighborhoods and nested quantum walks. A circuit implementation additionally pays for updating and querying the stored subsets and neighborhoods.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> entry queries; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> arithmetic operations, algebraic triangle detection.</strong> Reading the matrix attains the classical query upper bound, and a hidden isolated triangle gives a quadratic worst-case query lower bound. Explicit-input triangle detection uses fast matrix multiplication with exponent <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math></span>, for every fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>; sparse inputs permit additional combinatorial refinements.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial query improvement, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The mechanism combines quantum walks and combinatorial preprocessing.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous separation from optimal classical entry-query complexity.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant quantum walks require coherent subset registers and neighborhood data of polynomial size. A logical-qubit estimate depends on the stored-data representation and graph oracle; the query theorem itself fixes their query count.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Le Gall quantum triangle finding</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.0085\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Le Gall, <em>Improved Quantum Algorithm for Triangle Finding via Combinatorial Arguments</em> (2014)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and the algorithm in Sections 3–4.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Matrix-Multiplication-Nine-Fourths-October-2-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>An Upper Bound of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math></span> for the Matrix Multiplication Exponent</em> (2026)</a>. Main matrix-multiplication theorem.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/triangle-walk\">→ Le Gall quantum triangle-finding algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "graph-collision": {
    "title": "Graph collision",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Graph collision</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Graph collision)</span></h2>\n<p>A graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> is known in advance; oracle bits <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> mark vertices. Decide whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02203;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>, and find such an edge. The graph structure is free input, whereas marks are queried.\nComplexity counts queries to the mark bits; the known graph is available independently of this oracle.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, Cornelissen et al.; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for the independence-number refinement.</strong> The November 2026 algorithm splits high- and low-degree neighborhoods and combines quantum search with sampled graph structure. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> is the independence number. The earlier general quantum-walk construction uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries. The quantum lower bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> over an appropriate family of known graphs.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> mark queries, exhaustive reading and randomized search.</strong> Reading all marks permits direct checking of the known edge set. A star with its center marked embeds an unstructured search over the other marks, giving the linear classical lower bound. Graph preprocessing and checking a stored edge relation have additional time costs outside the mark-query measure.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> in the worst case. The algorithms use quantum search, quantum walks and graph structure.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum upper bound and classical lower bound give a rigorous query separation; the strongest cited bound is a November 2026 preprint.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant coherent processing is required. The <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> walk uses roughly <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> coherent bits for stored labels and marks. The <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></math></span> query exponent has its own implementation-dependent workspace costs.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Cornelissen et al. graph collision; Belovs graph-collision span program</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.05356\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Cornelissen et al., <em>Breaking the Quantum Barriers for Graph Collision</em> (2026)</a>. Theorems 1.1–1.2; Section 4, “An Improved Algorithm”; Section 6 for the lower bound.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1105.4024\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Belovs, <em>Span Programs for Functions with Constant-Sized 1-Certificates</em> (2012)</a>. The graph-collision application.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/graph-collision\">Cornelissen et al. graph-collision algorithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g43\">Boolean matrix multiplication</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/graph-collision\">→ Cornelissen et al. graph-collision algorithm</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/belovs-graph-collision\">→ Belovs graph-collision span program</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "testing-bipartiteness-in-bounded-degree-graphs": {
    "title": "Testing bipartiteness in bounded-degree graphs",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Testing bipartiteness in bounded-degree graphs</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Testing bipartiteness in bounded-degree graphs)</span></h2>\n<p>For a graph of maximum degree <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, distinguish a bipartite graph from one requiring more than <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> edge changes to become bipartite. Neighbor-list queries give the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math></span>th neighbor of a specified vertex. The promise permits sublinear property testing.\nComplexity counts neighbor-oracle queries and running time for fixed maximum degree and fixed distance parameter.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Ambainis–Childs–Liu bipartiteness testing.</strong> The theorem assumes fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Reversible random-walk generation and quantum collision finding locate opposite-parity walks with a common endpoint. The decision has bounded error, with one-sided acceptance for bipartite graphs.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Goldreich–Ron random-walk testing.</strong> The tester samples walks from chosen starting vertices and detects an odd-cycle certificate from conflicting parities. The lower bound applies to the same bounded-degree property-testing promise. Dependence on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> and confidence is restored when these parameters vary with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, through collision-finding quantum walks.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous advantage over a classical property-testing lower bound.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant neighbor queries and reversible walk generation are needed. A direct collision-finding implementation stores <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> bits of walk seeds and endpoints in the fixed-parameter regime, in addition to oracle workspace.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Ambainis–Childs–Liu bipartiteness testing</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1012.3174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis, Childs and Liu, <em>Quantum Property Testing for Bounded-Degree Graphs</em> (2011)</a>. Algorithm 2 and Theorem 10; Algorithm 1 and Theorem 7 give the classical comparator.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/s004930050059\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Goldreich and Ron, <em>A Sublinear Bipartiteness Tester for Bounded Degree Graphs</em> (1999)</a>. Random-walk bipartiteness tester and lower-bound construction.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/bipartiteness-test\">→ Ambainis–Childs–Liu bipartiteness tester</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "testing-graph-expansion": {
    "title": "Testing graph expansion",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Testing graph expansion</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Testing graph expansion)</span></h2>\n<p>For a bounded-degree graph, distinguish expansion at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A6;</mi></mrow></math></span> from being <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>-far from every graph with expansion at least a specified smaller threshold <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. The gap between <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A6;</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> is part of the testing promise. Expansion controls mixing and the robustness of cuts.\nComplexity counts neighbor queries and quantum or classical operations implementing the prescribed random-walk tester.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Apers&#x27;s fast-forwarding and seed-set tester.</strong> The proximity parameter and degree are fixed. The promise relates the accepting expansion threshold <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A6;</mi></mrow></math></span> to the smaller rejecting threshold as specified in the tester theorem. The bound combines an evolving-set construction with quantum fast-forwarding and estimates of the resulting walk distribution.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, classical random-walk expansion testing; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries at fixed parameters.</strong> The classical algorithm checks whether sampled walk endpoints exhibit the concentration expected in poorly expanding regions. Upper and lower bounds refer to bounded-degree oracle access with the stated gap between the two expansion thresholds.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, and quadratic improvement in the walk-time dependence <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. The mechanism is quantum fast-forwarding and amplitude estimation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous oracle advantage for the testing promise.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant coherent walk steps require logarithmic vertex registers, seed-set storage and estimation ancillas. The seed-set data structure supports coherent access; total depth depends on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> and the target confidence.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Apers expansion testing</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1907.02369\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Apers, <em>Expansion Testing Using Quantum Fast-Forwarding and Seed Sets</em> (2019)</a>. Algorithm 2 and Theorem 5; Theorem 1 states the main complexity bound.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/expansion-test\">→ Apers expansion tester</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "formula": {
    "title": "AND–OR formula and game-tree evaluation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>AND–OR formula and game-tree evaluation</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(AND–OR formula and game-tree evaluation)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a fixed read-once formula <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span> input leaves and gates AND, OR and NOT, query input bits and compute <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. For alternating AND–OR trees, this decides the winner of a finite perfect-information game with known tree and queried terminal outcomes.\nComplexity counts queries to the leaf bits; gate complexity additionally includes access to the preprocessed formula graph.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, quantum-walk and span-program formula evaluation.</strong> The bound applies to fixed read-once AND–OR formulas, allowing negations on inputs. Efficient implementations have nearly matching time after preprocessing the formula. The output is the Boolean value at the root.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for balanced binary trees, randomized alpha–beta pruning, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>33</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.753</mn></mrow></math></span>.</strong> The randomized algorithm evaluates children in random order and stops as soon as the parent value is determined. This is the optimal randomized classical bound for the balanced alternating binary tree. Arbitrary formulas have structure-dependent classical complexity and the general <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> leaf-reading upper bound.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>0.753</mn><mi>&#x02026;</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> on balanced binary trees. The mechanism is a quantum walk or span-program reflection algorithm.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous: the balanced-tree classical and quantum exponents have matching lower bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant evaluation uses logarithmic graph-address registers, phase-estimation ancillas and coherent access to the preprocessed formula. Regular balanced trees admit succinct graph descriptions.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum-walk and span-program formula evaluation</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://theoryofcomputing.org/articles/v008a013/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Reichardt and Špalek, <em>Span-Program-Based Quantum Algorithm for Evaluating Formulas</em> (2012)</a>. Formula-evaluation construction and the main algorithmic theorem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1005.1601\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Reichardt, <em>Reflections for Quantum Query Algorithms</em> (2011)</a>. Theorem 1.3 and Algorithms 1–3: conversion of span-program witnesses to query algorithms.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/formula-evaluation\">→ Quantum formula evaluation by span programs</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "matrix-product-verification-over-a-field": {
    "title": "Matrix-product verification over a field",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Matrix-product verification over a field</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Matrix-product verification over a field)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> through entry oracles, decide whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></span>, with field operations charged explicitly or treated as unit-cost operations. Matrix products encode path counts and algebraic certificates used throughout combinatorial algorithms.\nThe comparison counts matrix-entry queries and field operations; bit and gate costs also charge the representation and implementation of field arithmetic.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries and field-arithmetic time, Buhrman–Špalek quantum verification.</strong> The quantum walk combines selected-row and selected-column subproblems with randomized field checks. It accepts correct products and detects incorrect products with bounded error. The known quantum query lower bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> field operations and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> entry queries, Freivalds verification.</strong> A random vector <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math></span> tests the identity <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></math></span>. Repetition reduces the error probability logarithmically. The quadratic entry-query bound is optimal. For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit field elements, both algorithms incur the corresponding classical or reversible arithmetic cost.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, using quantum walks and randomized algebraic checks.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous separation from the tight classical entry-query bound.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The published quantum algorithm uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> stored field elements, or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical data qubits for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit elements, plus arithmetic and oracle workspace. Fault-tolerant coherent arithmetic is required at useful scale.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Buhrman–Špalek matrix-product verification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0409035\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Buhrman and Špalek, <em>Quantum Verification of Matrix Products</em> (2006)</a>. Figure 1, “Product Verification” and “Verify Once”; Theorem 6 gives time and space; Theorem 11 gives the lower bound.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.math.uwaterloo.ca/ anayak/papers/Nayak08.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Nayak, <em>Checking Matrix Identities</em> (2008)</a>. The exposition of Freivalds&#x27;s identity test.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/matrix-verification\">→ Buhrman–Špalek matrix-product verification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "boolean-matrix-product-verification": {
    "title": "Boolean matrix-product verification",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Boolean matrix-product verification</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Boolean matrix-product verification)</span></h2>\n<p>For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, decide whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x022C1;</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02227;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for all <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math></span>. This checks the claimed two-step reachability relation of a directed graph.\nThe main comparison counts Boolean matrix-entry queries; explicit classical computation also counts arithmetic or word operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>17</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, Gilani–Le Gall–Zhou.</strong> The 2026 algorithm reduces verification to Orthogonal Vectors and uses quantum search on structured subinstances. Its quantum lower bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Nested quantum search also supplies an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>-query construction with a simpler implementation.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> entry queries; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> arithmetic time for computing and checking the Boolean product.</strong> The query upper bound reads all three matrices. Exact Boolean multiplication can use algebraic matrix multiplication over a suitable field, with exponent <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math></span>, for every fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>. The problem uses Boolean-semiring multiplication; a zero output entry and a one output entry have different witness conditions.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial query improvement: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>17</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The method combines reductions, quantum search and quantum walks.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous separation from classical query complexity; the strongest cited bounds are a 2026 preprint.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant implementation needs coherent access to the matrices and to the records used by the Orthogonal Vectors procedure. Logical memory depends on that implementation; the simpler nested-search algorithm uses small index registers with reversible query workspace.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gilani–Le Gall–Zhou and Buhrman–Špalek Boolean matrix-product verification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.40180\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gilani, Le Gall and Zhou, <em>Improved Quantum Query Bounds for Boolean Matrix Product Verification</em> (2026)</a>. Theorems 1.1–1.2 and 3.5; Algorithms 2 and 6 and Theorems 4.1 and 4.6.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0409035\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Buhrman and Špalek, <em>Quantum Verification of Matrix Products</em> (2006)</a>. Theorem 13: Boolean verification by nested search.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/boolean-matrix-verification\">→ Gilani–Le Gall–Zhou Boolean matrix-product verification</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/boolean-nested-search\">→ Buhrman–Špalek nested-search Boolean verification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "promised-short-path-st-connectivity": {
    "title": "Promised short-path s–t connectivity",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Promised short-path <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>–<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span> connectivity</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Promised short-path <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>–<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span> connectivity)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an undirected graph and vertices <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span>, distinguish the existence of an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>–<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span> path of length at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span> from complete disconnection. The positive-instance path-length promise exposes how certificate structure affects quantum complexity.\nThe comparison counts adjacency-matrix queries and the quantum working space used between queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time, Belovs–Reichardt span-program connectivity.</strong> The promise is that connected terminals have a path of at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span> edges. The algorithm uses logarithmic quantum space. The case <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries. For very small <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>, direct specialized tests can improve the displayed general upper bound; for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> a single adjacency query suffices.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> matrix time and queries, breadth-first search; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with explicit adjacency lists.</strong> The unrestricted matrix-oracle problem has classical complexity <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The promise parameter must be retained when comparing algorithms on shorter-path subclasses. A classical traversal records discovered vertices and stops once <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span> is reached or its component has been exhausted.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial oracle speedup, including <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for the unrestricted path-length regime. The mechanism is a span program and quantum reflections.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous query advantage in the regime where the classical quadratic lower bound applies.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical working qubits up to precision ancillas, with coherent adjacency access. This space-efficient construction still needs fault tolerance for its long sequence of queries.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Belovs–Reichardt span-program connectivity</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2603\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Belovs and Reichardt, <em>Span Programs and Quantum Algorithms for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span>-Connectivity and Claw Detection</em> (2012)</a>. Theorem 3: query bound; Theorem 9: time-efficient implementation.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/span-connectivity\">Belovs–Reichardt span-program connectivity</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g36\">Cycle detection</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/span-connectivity\">→ Belovs–Reichardt span-program connectivity</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "global-minimum-cut": {
    "title": "Global minimum cut",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Global minimum cut</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Global minimum cut)</span></h2>\n<p>In a weighted undirected graph, output a nonempty proper subset <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02282;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math></span> minimizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span>. This identifies the cheapest edge failure that disconnects a network.\nThe quantum comparison counts weighted adjacency queries and running time; classical bounds refer to explicit weighted graphs.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> matrix time and queries, quantum minimum cut.</strong> Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></span> is the ratio of the largest to smallest positive weight; reading the matrix caps the bound at <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. In the adjacency-array model, the query bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and the time bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The algorithm returns the minimum-cut weight and shores. Approximate cuts can instead use quantum cut sparsification.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> expected time, randomized classical minimum cut; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> matrix queries.</strong> The explicit-input comparison includes randomized contraction and near-linear cut algorithms. The matrix quantum lower bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> in the nontrivial range <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, up to the precision and logarithmic conventions of the theorem.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial oracle improvement; for bounded weight ratio and dense graphs it is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous matrix-query separation with nearly matching quantum bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant search and sparsification require logarithmic vertex registers, weight registers and coherent graph-access workspace. A classical sparse graph and contracted partitions are stored during the computation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Apers–Lee quantum minimum cut</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2011.09823\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Apers and Lee, <em>Quantum Complexity of Minimum Cut</em> (2021)</a>. Theorems 3 and 5 for the matrix and adjacency-array algorithms; the named routines FindMinStar, Cut-Sparsifier, LearnCutAtoms and LearnContraction.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-mincut\">→ Apers–Lee quantum minimum cut</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "maxcut-approximation-with-qaoa": {
    "title": "MaxCut approximation with QAOA",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>MaxCut approximation with QAOA</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Maximum cut)</span></h2>\n<p>Given nonnegative weights <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span>, maximize <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span> over <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, or return a cut meeting a specified approximation ratio. Sparse regular graphs are a central QAOA benchmark.\nComplexity uses quantum gate and sample counts, classical arithmetic time for approximation, and classical bit complexity for exact finite-precision instances.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates per sample, depth-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> QAOA; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span> objective evaluations with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span> shots each.</strong> The cost includes the problem-phase and mixer layers; synthesis precision contributes additional factors. On 3-regular graphs, the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> analysis guarantees an approximation ratio of at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.6924</mn></mrow></math></span>. Results on higher-girth graphs and larger depth have their own graph and parameter assumptions. The complete variational optimization includes the evaluations and samples represented by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Polynomial time with ratio <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.87856</mn></mrow></math></span>, Goemans–Williamson; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for exact sparse unweighted MaxCut.</strong> The approximation guarantee applies to general nonnegative weights. For unweighted graphs with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, threshold counting and self-reduction give the exact subexponential bound. For arbitrary unweighted graphs and fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, sampling <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> edges and optimizing the sample gives value at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>OPT</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> with the same exponent. For constant <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span>, bounded degree and a fixed finite algebraic gate set, the complete QAOA output distribution can be sampled classically in expected <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time; compiled gate count controls the corresponding statement for synthesized rotations.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The general QAOA runtime and approximation theorems establish no asymptotic advantage over the applicable classical comparator.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Approximation guarantees are rigorous for specified graph classes and depths. Empirical comparisons are tied to their tested instances and classical solvers.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Small superconducting-processor demonstrations exist. A circuit uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> data qubits plus ancillas; useful large-depth operation needs accurate two-qubit gates, repeated sampling and parameter optimization. Fault-tolerant estimates also include rotation synthesis.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA)</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1411.4028\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Farhi, Goldstone and Gutmann, <em>A Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm</em> (2014)</a>. Section II, “Fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> Algorithm”; Section V, “MaxCut on 3-Regular Graphs”.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://math.mit.edu/ goemans/PAPERS/maxcut-jacm.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Goemans and Williamson, <em>Improved Approximation Algorithms for Maximum Cut and Satisfiability Problems Using Semidefinite Programming</em> (1995)</a>. The MaxCut semidefinite relaxation and randomized hyperplane rounding.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (25 October 2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and the sparse-instance bound; witness recovery by conditional counting.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1RzhP9ZMy1xkC22oqKmMHEOh7gG1cwkfA/view\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gheorghiu and Gupte, <em>Subexponential-Time Sampling of Quantum Circuits from Exact Counting</em> (2026)</a>. Main exact-sampling theorem for fixed finite algebraic gate sets.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.04197\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Harrigan et al., <em>Quantum Approximate Optimization of Non-Planar Graph Problems on a Planar Superconducting Processor</em> (2021)</a>. Experimental QAOA circuits and performance figures.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>: <a href=\"#entry/bounded-occurrence-max-e3lin2\">Max-E3LIN2</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-independent-set-in-rydberg-geometric-graphs\">Rydberg independent set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/sherringtonkirkpatrick-spin-glass-optimization\">SK spin-glass optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-k-cut-with-variational-quantum-circuits\">Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-Cut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-bisection-with-constraint-preserving-qaoa\">Maximum bisection</a>, <a href=\"#entry/job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs\">Job-shop scheduling</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-portfolio-rebalancing-with-constrained-mixers\">Discrete portfolio rebalancing</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">→ QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "bounded-occurrence-max-e3lin2": {
    "title": "Bounded-occurrence Max-E3LIN2",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Bounded-occurrence Max-E3LIN2</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Bounded-occurrence Max-E3LIN2)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> equations <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x02295;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>&#x02295;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> over <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>, with every variable appearing in at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span> equations, maximize the number satisfied. Random assignment satisfies half in expectation; the approximation question measures the additive improvement over <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>.\nComplexity counts quantum gates for QAOA, classical arithmetic time for approximation, and bit operations for exact bounded-degree instances.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates per sample, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> QAOA for bounded-occurrence Max-E3LIN2.</strong> For sufficiently large <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span>, the guaranteed satisfied fraction is at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>101</mn><msqrt><mi>D</mi></msqrt><mi>ln</mi><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The theorem chooses parameters from the objective analysis; shot counts for estimating the objective and success amplification contribute additional costs.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Polynomial time with fraction <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Barak et al.; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for exact optimization at fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> The classical approximation algorithm applies to odd-arity XOR constraints and improves the dependence on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span> in the QAOA guarantee. At fixed occurrence bound, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and the exact objective threshold has a linear-size Boolean circuit. Exact counting, binary search and witness recovery supply the displayed bit-complexity bound.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The approximation guarantee has a polynomial-time classical comparator with a stronger dependence on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span>. The stated quantum algorithm has no established runtime advantage.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The approximation theorems are rigorous; they yield no asymptotic quantum-speedup claim in this comparison.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>A QAOA circuit uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> data qubits and coherent three-body parity phases, typically decomposed into two-qubit gates. Modest instances are feasible on circuit hardware; scaling depends on connectivity, noise and sampling cost.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA)</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1412.6062\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Farhi, Goldstone and Gutmann, <em>A Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm Applied to a Bounded Occurrence Constraint Problem</em> (2014)</a>. Section II, “The General Case”, for the worst-case guarantee; Section III for typical instances.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1505.03424\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Barak et al., <em>Beating the Random Assignment on Constraint Satisfaction Problems of Bounded Degree</em> (2015)</a>. Theorem 1.1: odd-arity Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>XOR approximation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (25 October 2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and the sparse-instance bound; witness recovery by conditional counting.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>: <a href=\"#entry/maxcut-approximation-with-qaoa\">MaxCut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-independent-set-in-rydberg-geometric-graphs\">Rydberg independent set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/sherringtonkirkpatrick-spin-glass-optimization\">SK spin-glass optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-k-cut-with-variational-quantum-circuits\">Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-Cut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-bisection-with-constraint-preserving-qaoa\">Maximum bisection</a>, <a href=\"#entry/job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs\">Job-shop scheduling</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-portfolio-rebalancing-with-constrained-mixers\">Discrete portfolio rebalancing</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">→ QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "maximum-independent-set-in-rydberg-geometric-graphs": {
    "title": "Maximum independent set in Rydberg geometric graphs",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Maximum independent set in Rydberg geometric graphs</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Maximum independent set in unit-disk graphs)</span></h2>\n<p>Given points <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span> and a blockade radius <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math></span>, let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math></span> when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math></span>. Find the largest <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math></span> for distinct <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>. This geometric promise makes independent-set constraints directly implementable with atom interactions.\nThe experiment measures analog evolution time and repetitions to success; the exact classical comparison uses time on explicitly represented geometric graphs.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span> evolution time to success probability <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math></span>, Rydberg adiabatic and variational optimization.</strong> Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> is the measured single-run success probability and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span> the evolution duration. State preparation, readout and parameter tuning add to the total time. Experiments with up to 289 atoms reported favorable scaling against an optimized simulated-annealing comparator on the tested graph families.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for exact unit-disk independent set, geometric separators and dynamic programming.</strong> The geometric promise is essential: weighted separators and tree decompositions for similarly sized fat objects yield the subexponential exact algorithm in the plane. A polynomial-time approximation scheme is available for unit-disk graphs at fixed approximation error. Simulated annealing is the experimental comparator, while the geometric separator algorithm supplies the exact worst-case comparison.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Experimental improvement over a specified annealing solver; the cited results establish no general asymptotic advantage over exact geometric algorithms.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence from measured time-to-solution scaling on the tested instances and solver settings.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The 289-atom demonstration uses physical analog degrees of freedom. One atom represents one vertex; blockade geometry, calibration, coherence time and preparation/readout govern scaling.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Rydberg adiabatic optimization; QAOA</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.09372\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ebadi et al., <em>Quantum Optimization of Maximum Independent Set Using Rydberg Atom Arrays</em> (Science 2022)</a>. Main-text Figures 3–4; Supplementary Sections 4–5 define QAOA and variational adiabatic protocols; Sections 8–9 give the annealing comparison and success scaling.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1803.10633\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">de Berg et al., <em>A Framework for ETH-Tight Algorithms and Lower Bounds in Geometric Intersection Graphs</em> (2020)</a>. Theorem 14: Independent Set on a weighted tree decomposition; the geometric separator and decomposition theorems specialize to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>: <a href=\"#entry/maxcut-approximation-with-qaoa\">MaxCut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/bounded-occurrence-max-e3lin2\">Max-E3LIN2</a>, <a href=\"#entry/sherringtonkirkpatrick-spin-glass-optimization\">SK spin-glass optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-k-cut-with-variational-quantum-circuits\">Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-Cut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-bisection-with-constraint-preserving-qaoa\">Maximum bisection</a>, <a href=\"#entry/job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs\">Job-shop scheduling</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-portfolio-rebalancing-with-constrained-mixers\">Discrete portfolio rebalancing</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">→ QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/rydberg-optimization\">→ Rydberg adiabatic and variational optimization</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "sherringtonkirkpatrick-spin-glass-optimization": {
    "title": "Sherrington–Kirkpatrick spin-glass optimization",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Sherrington–Kirkpatrick spin-glass optimization</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Sherrington–Kirkpatrick spin-glass optimization)</span></h2>\n<p>For independent standard Gaussian couplings <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span>, minimize <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> over <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. One may request the exact ground state or an energy within <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> of it. Random fully connected spin glasses are an average-case optimization benchmark.\nQuantum circuits are measured in gates and samples; the Gaussian classical approximation theorem counts real-arithmetic operations, while exact discretized instances use bit complexity.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates per sample, depth-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> QAOA for the SK Hamiltonian.</strong> The infinite-size expected-energy analysis can be evaluated classically in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>16</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time for fixed parameters. It predicts the achieved energy density and guides angle selection. Exact optimization by generalized short-path algorithms uses additional overlap, spectral and landscape hypotheses, with runtime relative to an associated classical Markov-chain search.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span> arithmetic operations for an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-near optimum, Montanari&#x27;s incremental approximate message passing.</strong> The guarantee assumes full replica symmetry breaking and holds with high probability over Gaussian couplings. For exact optimization, enumeration supplies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> arithmetic time, with instance-specific branch-and-bound improvements. Discretizing dense couplings to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span> bits gives an energy-threshold circuit of size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>; sparse exact counting on that circuit costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. The smaller exact bound is chosen for the represented instance.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The QAOA energy-density results have a polynomial-time classical near-optimal comparator. A general asymptotic QAOA advantage is unestablished.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The limiting QAOA formulas and conditional classical approximation theorem are analytical results; empirical comparisons concern fixed depths and finite instances.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>QAOA uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> data qubits but <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> interactions per layer before compilation. Small processor experiments exist; dense routing, two-qubit errors and repeated shots determine practical cost.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: QAOA; generalized short-path algorithms</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1910.08187\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Farhi et al., <em>The Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm and the Sherrington–Kirkpatrick Model at Infinite Size</em> (2022)</a>. Section 2 defines QAOA; Section 4 states the limiting-energy result; Section 6 gives the general-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> calculation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1812.10897\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Montanari, <em>Optimization of the Sherrington–Kirkpatrick Hamiltonian</em> (2021)</a>. Assumption 1, Theorem 2, and Algorithm 1, “IAMP Algorithm to Optimize SK Hamiltonian”.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.23270\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Chakrabarti et al., <em>Generalized Short Path Algorithms</em> (2024/2025)</a>. Algorithms 1–2 and Theorem 3.5.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (25 October 2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and the sparse-instance bound; witness recovery by conditional counting.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>: <a href=\"#entry/maxcut-approximation-with-qaoa\">MaxCut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/bounded-occurrence-max-e3lin2\">Max-E3LIN2</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-independent-set-in-rydberg-geometric-graphs\">Rydberg independent set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-k-cut-with-variational-quantum-circuits\">Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-Cut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-bisection-with-constraint-preserving-qaoa\">Maximum bisection</a>, <a href=\"#entry/job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs\">Job-shop scheduling</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-portfolio-rebalancing-with-constrained-mixers\">Discrete portfolio rebalancing</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/short-path\">Short-path quantum optimization</a>: <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">→ QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/short-path\">→ Short-path quantum optimization</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths": {
    "title": "Exact bounded-locality spin optimization by short paths",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Exact bounded-locality spin optimization by short paths</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Exact fixed-arity spin optimization)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>\\arg</mi><mo>min</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> is fixed and coefficients are efficiently represented. QUBO is the case <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span> after changing binary variables to spins.\nComplexity compares quantum gate time with classical bit time for explicitly represented fixed-arity objective functions.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, Le Gall–Tamaki quantum conditioning-and-search under the low-energy-density promise.</strong> For a centered homogeneous degree-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> objective, let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> and suppose\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>z</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nFor fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, the exponent is\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>h</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nwhere <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math></span> is binary entropy. The algorithm combines amplitude amplification with minimum finding; the weighted formulation removes the known-optimum assumption with a <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> overhead. Dalzell et al.&#x27;s short-path bound under the corresponding promise has exponent <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>, for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>. The generalized short-path framework gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for its specified Markov-chain-search cost <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> under its overlap and spectral hypotheses. The subexponential factors in the conditioning-and-search bounds are displayed explicitly.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> under the same density promise, Le Gall–Tamaki; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, exact threshold counting.</strong> The first bound uses the same centered homogeneous objective and parameters as the quantum conditioning-and-search algorithm. For an objective with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> integer terms and coefficients of at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span> bits, a threshold circuit and its Boolean encoding have <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> variables and gates. Binary search and conditional counting recover an optimum at the displayed counting cost. The classical comparison takes the minimum of the applicable bounds, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> enumeration and specialized exact solvers. The density promise, term count and coefficient precision determine which bound is smallest.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The paired conditioning-and-search bounds have a quadratic time relationship up to subexponential factors. The overall comparison uses the smallest applicable classical bound. Generalized short paths give a stronger-than-quadratic improvement over their specified Markov-chain-search cost <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The low-energy-density, short-path and exact-counting theorems give rigorous parameter-dependent upper bounds. The paired algorithmic comparison has the same objective and density promise on both sides.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant implementation needs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> assignment qubits, polynomial reversible workspace and exponentially many coherent operations for the displayed fixed-positive-exponent bound. Cost precision and spectral resolution contribute to depth.</p><p><small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, the cited CSP comparisons had classical bounds <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> and quantum bounds <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for problem-dependent constants, and claimed an improvement in the exponential constant.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Short-path algorithms; Le Gall–Tamaki quantum conditioning-and-search</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2212.01513\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dalzell et al., <em>Mind the Gap: Achieving a Super-Grover Quantum Speedup by Jumping to the End</em> (2023/2026)</a>. Algorithm 1 and Theorems 1, 4, 6–8.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.23270\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Chakrabarti et al., <em>Generalized Short Path Algorithms</em> (2024/2025)</a>. Algorithm 2, Theorems 3.3–3.5, and Table 1.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.12131\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Le Gall and Tamaki, <em>Dequantizing Short-Path Quantum Algorithms</em> (2026)</a>. Theorems 1.1–1.4 and 4.1: matched classical and quantum bounds; Section 4.3: amplitude amplification and minimum finding; Appendices A–B: removing the known-optimum assumption.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (25 October 2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and the sparse-instance bound; witness recovery by conditional counting.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/short-path\">Short-path quantum optimization</a>: <a href=\"#entry/sherringtonkirkpatrick-spin-glass-optimization\">SK spin-glass optimization</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/short-path\">→ Short-path quantum optimization</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/conditioning-search\">→ Quantum conditioning-and-search</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting": {
    "title": "Partition functions and approximate combinatorial counting",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Partition functions and approximate combinatorial counting</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Partition-function estimation)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an energy <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> and inverse temperature <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></mrow></math></span>, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>Z</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mover><mi>Z</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. Suitable choices count independent sets, matchings and colorings; the zero-temperature limit counts optimal configurations.\nComplexity counts coherent or classical Markov-chain steps and energy evaluations, with arithmetic costs restored for numerical output.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><msqrt><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Cornelissen–Hamoudi partition-function estimation.</strong> Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math></span> bounds the spectral gaps of the supplied sampling chains. Montanaro&#x27;s estimator uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><msqrt><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for a supplied length-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></math></span> constant-relative-variance cooling schedule. In the independent-set uniqueness regime, appropriate Glauber dynamics yield a representative <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> bound. Schedule preparation and initial-state assumptions are part of these theorems.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Štefankovič–Vempala–Vigoda adaptive cooling and classical sampling.</strong> For explicit sparse graphs, independent-set, fixed-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> coloring and perfect-matching counts also admit exact <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time through sparse Boolean counting. For sparse constant-arity, constant-bit integer energies, counting each of the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> possible energies gives the density of states and hence <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi></mrow></math></span> with that exponent, plus numerical precision costs. These exact methods are useful when the supplied chain mixes slowly; in uniqueness regimes, polynomial-time correlation-decay and sampling algorithms provide classical alternatives.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement for the stated sampling model, including <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. Quantum amplitude estimation and quantum walks supply these factors.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous algorithmic comparison for the supplied chains and cooling assumptions. The explicit-instance comparator depends on the counting model and mixing regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>A configuration on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> bits needs at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> logical data qubits, plus reversible transition, energy and estimation workspace. Fault tolerance and coherent Markov-chain steps are required; depth depends on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Cornelissen–Hamoudi partition-function estimation; Montanaro quantum Monte Carlo</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2207.08643\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Cornelissen and Hamoudi, <em>A Sublinear-Time Quantum Algorithm for Approximating Partition Functions</em> (2023)</a>. Section 4 and Theorem 4.1; Algorithm 6 and Theorem 3.3 define the product estimator.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4614442/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Montanaro, <em>Quantum Speedup of Monte Carlo Methods</em> (2015)</a>. The partition-function approximation application and its cooling-schedule theorem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (25 October 2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and the sparse-instance bound; witness recovery by conditional counting.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>: <a href=\"#entry/approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments\">Approximate counting</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-sampling\">Wocjan–Abeyesinghe quantum sampling</a>: <a href=\"#entry/gibbs-sampling-and-quantum-simulated-annealing\">Gibbs sampling</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">→ Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-sampling\">→ Wocjan–Abeyesinghe quantum sampling</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "traversing-a-randomly-welded-pair-of-trees": {
    "title": "Traversing a randomly welded pair of trees",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Traversing a randomly welded pair of trees</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Welded-tree traversal)</span></h2>\n<p>Two depth-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math></span> binary trees have their leaves joined by a random alternating cycle; vertex names are random strings. Given the entrance root and an oracle returning neighbors, output the other root. The graph has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> vertices and constant degree. This is a graph-search oracle problem exhibiting structural exponential advantage.\nComplexity counts neighbor-oracle queries and quantum gates in the random-label oracle model.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries and gates, the Childs et al. continuous-time quantum walk.</strong> The graph has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> vertices, but its column symmetry confines the relevant evolution to a polynomial-dimensional effective path. The oracle implements sparse-graph evolution from the entrance, and measurement finds the exit with probability amplified to a constant.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> queries in the worst case, classical randomized oracle exploration.</strong> The original lower bound shows that an algorithm making at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> queries has exponentially small success probability. Breadth-first exploration gives an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> upper bound. Random labels and the hidden leaf welding prevent a classical query algorithm from reconstructing the useful column structure with polynomially many queries.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential to polynomial, through continuous-time quantum transport and interference.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Fully rigorous quantum–classical separation in the specified graph-oracle model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant digital simulation uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> vertex-label qubits plus sparse-Hamiltonian simulation and oracle workspace. A physical analog realization with one site per graph vertex instead needs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> sites, a distinct resource model.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Childs et al. continuous-time quantum walk</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0209131\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Childs et al., <em>Exponential Algorithmic Speedup by a Quantum Walk</em> (2003)</a>. Theorem 3: quantum traversal; Theorem 9: classical query lower bound.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/welded-tree-walk\">→ Continuous-time quantum walk on welded trees</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "gibbs-sampling-and-quantum-simulated-annealing": {
    "title": "Gibbs sampling and quantum simulated annealing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Gibbs sampling and quantum simulated annealing</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Gibbs sampling)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></mrow></math></span>, generate a sample within total-variation error <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. For an integer-valued objective with unit gap, choosing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> makes an optimum occur with probability at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B7;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Thus sampling can yield discrete optimization.\nThe chain-based comparison counts coherent or classical transition steps; explicit constraint-model sampling also has a classical bit-time bound.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> coherent walk steps, quantum simulated annealing and quantum sampling.</strong> The theorem assumes a supplied sequence of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span> reversible chains with gap at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math></span>, an efficiently prepared initial distribution and suitable overlap between adjacent stationary states. Accuracy and overlap parameters contribute logarithmic or explicit factors. Measuring the final coherent Gibbs state produces a sample.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> transition steps, sequential classical mixing; subexponential conditional counting for sparse explicit models.</strong> Warm starts and the smallest relevant stationary probability enter the mixing bound. Sparse fixed-width hard constraints admit exact uniform sampling in expected <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time by conditional counts. For sparse constant-arity, constant-bit integer energies, conditional counts at the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> possible energies yield Gibbs samples to total-variation error <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> with the same exponent and polynomial numerical overhead. The smaller applicable sampler determines the explicit-instance comparison.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Quadratic improvement in inverse spectral gap for the specified chain sequence, from quantum-walk phase detection and state transformations. An exponentially small gap gives an improvement in the exponential constant of that chain-based bound.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous relative-to-chain algorithmic speedup. Explicit-instance comparisons also include samplers obtained from counting.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>A configuration requires <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical data qubits plus reversible transition, energy and phase-estimation workspace. Fault-tolerant depth scales with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></msqrt></mrow></math></span> and can remain exponential for slowly mixing chains.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum simulated annealing; Wocjan–Abeyesinghe quantum sampling</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0804.1571\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Somma, Boixo, Barnum and Knill, <em>Quantum Simulations of Classical Annealing Processes</em> (2008)</a>. The quantum simulated annealing construction and its spectral-gap runtime analysis.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0804.4259\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Wocjan and Abeyesinghe, <em>Speedup via Quantum Sampling</em> (2008)</a>. Theorem 2: preparation along a sequence of reversible Markov chains.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (25 October 2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and the sparse-instance bound; witness recovery by conditional counting.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-sampling\">Wocjan–Abeyesinghe quantum sampling</a>: <a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-simulated-annealing\">→ Quantum simulated annealing</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-sampling\">→ Wocjan–Abeyesinghe quantum sampling</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "all-pairs-shortest-paths": {
    "title": "All-pairs shortest paths",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>All-pairs shortest paths</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(All-pairs shortest paths)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a directed graph with real edge weights and no negative cycle, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span>, the minimum path length from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math></span>, for every ordered pair. This full <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> distance table is central to routing and fine-grained complexity.\nThe general comparison uses quantum arithmetic time with coherent matrix access and classical real-RAM operations; bounded-integer and unweighted variants use their stated word-RAM models.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, quantum minimum finding for min-plus products and repeated squaring.</strong> Each distance-product entry is a minimum over <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> candidates, and the complete distance table is output classically. Arithmetic on real weights is treated as unit cost in this comparison; a finite-precision circuit charges the corresponding addition, comparison and access gates.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2.998</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> expected time, Alman–Vassilevska Williams Las Vegas APSP.</strong> The real-weight algorithm uses comparisons, additions and subtractions and also has a corresponding high-probability runtime guarantee. Polynomially bounded integer weights admit deterministic <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2.99942</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> word-RAM time. Directed unweighted APSP admits <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2.4999</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time via rectangular matrix multiplication. Each specialized comparator applies to its stated weight regime.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial for arbitrary real weights: approximately a factor <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>0.498</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> between the displayed upper bounds, with quantum logarithmic factors suppressed. The mechanism is minimum finding.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence from the gap between known general real-weight algorithms. The unweighted specialization has a stronger classical bound.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant implementation requires coherent access to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span> distance entries and reversible arithmetic. With <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit distances, the direct stored matrix occupies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> bits accessible to quantum queries, plus active index and arithmetic registers.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum min-plus products and repeated squaring</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1410.6220\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Nayebi and Vassilevska Williams, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Shortest Paths Problems in Structured Instances</em> (2014)</a>. Introduction: general <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> APSP baseline; structured algorithms in Theorems 1–5.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.06783\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Alman and Vassilevska Williams, <em>Truly Subquadratic 3SUM and Truly Subcubic APSP via Triangles in Sparse Lopsided Graphs</em> (2026)</a>. Theorems 22 and 35: integer and real-weight APSP.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.10494v2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Uznański, <em>Rectangular Matrix Multiplication from Polynomial Products</em> (2026)</a>. Section 7: directed unweighted APSP.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "optimal-polynomial-intersection-by-decoded-quantum-interferometry": {
    "title": "Optimal polynomial intersection by decoded quantum interferometry",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Optimal polynomial intersection by decoded quantum interferometry</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Optimal polynomial intersection)</span></h2>\n<p>Given prime <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span>, integer <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>, and explicit sets <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, find a polynomial <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, maximizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The approximation task asks for a specified fraction of the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> constraints. This connects finite-field fitting, list recovery and coding theory.\nThe explicit-input comparison counts quantum gates and classical finite-field operations; the folded-OPI separation counts membership-oracle queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates, decoded quantum interferometry for the stated balanced OPI regime.</strong> With <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span> and constant <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span>, DQI achieves fraction <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></span> asymptotically, using efficient decoding of the relevant Reed–Solomon dual code. Horinaga–Yamakawa give a worst-case polynomial-time quantum algorithm with full satisfaction for balanced sets at rate above <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math></span>. For the folded membership-oracle family at rate <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.3</mn></mrow></math></span>, the separation gives expected quantum score <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.85</mn></mrow></math></span> while a classical score exceeding <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.65</mn></mrow></math></span> by a constant requires superpolynomially many queries.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Polynomial-time interpolation with expected fraction <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Prange-type interpolation; exponential repetition to reach the studied DQI target.</strong> Complete enumeration costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The explicit balanced tables contain <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> bits. A direct Boolean objective circuit has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates, so applying subexponential exact counting to this circuit gives a bound whose exponent, at constant <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span>, exceeds the studied exponential-in-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> interpolation-repetition comparator. The oracle lower bound concerns unknown acceptance tables in the folded family.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Candidate exponential-to-polynomial advantage for the explicit random-instance approximation target; a superpolynomial query separation for the folded oracle family. The mechanism is decoded Fourier interference.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence for the explicit-input comparison, based on the analyzed classical algorithms. The folded oracle comparison has a rigorous classical query lower bound.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant DQI requires approximately <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical data qubits plus decoding workspace. A cited <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>521</mn></mrow></math></span> design uses about 8,850 logical qubits and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.01</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow></math></span> Toffoli gates; decoder construction and error correction determine physical scale.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Decoded Quantum Interferometry; Horinaga–Yamakawa optimal polynomial intersection</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.08292\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Jordan et al., <em>Optimization by Decoded Quantum Interferometry</em> (Nature 2025)</a>. Theorem 4.1; Section 5, “Optimal Polynomial Intersection”; Section 8, “Decoded Quantum Interferometry”; Section 16 for resources.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.14650\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Horinaga and Yamakawa, <em>Worst-Case Quantum Algorithm for Optimal Polynomial Intersection Beyond Decoded Quantum Interferometry</em> (2026)</a>. Theorems 1.1 and 6.1.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.02145\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kramer et al., <em>A Provable Quantum Advantage for Approximate Optimization via Decoded Quantum Interferometry</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1: folded-OPI quantum–classical query separation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (25 October 2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and the sparse-instance bound; witness recovery by conditional counting.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/dqi\">Decoded Quantum Interferometry</a>: <a href=\"#entry/yz-codeword-hash-search\">Codeword search with random hash constraints</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/dqi\">→ Decoded Quantum Interferometry</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/worst-case-opi\">→ Horinaga–Yamakawa optimal polynomial intersection algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "maximum-k-cut-with-variational-quantum-circuits": {
    "title": "Maximum k-cut with variational quantum circuits",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Maximum <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-cut with variational quantum circuits</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Maximum <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-cut)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an undirected graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, nonnegative rational edge weights <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span> and an integer <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> maximizing\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nThe approximation version requests a labeling with a specified value relative to the optimum or total edge weight. Write <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The problem models partitioning into several groups and extends the binary cut objective. Complexity counts quantum gates and measurement samples, classical arithmetic for approximation, and bit operations for exact finite-precision optimization.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates, one-hot QAOA with a parity-ring mixer; polynomial-size qudit QAOA with a Grover mixer.</strong> Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> is the number of alternating layers, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span> counts evaluated parameter settings and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span> is the number of samples per setting. The one-hot encoding uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> data qubits; its cost layer has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> two-qubit phases and its mixer has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> two-qubit rotations. Each sample is a classical <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-labeling. Binary encodings use <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>k</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span> data qubits and additional gates for unused labels. The classical parameter optimization and gate-synthesis precision contribute to total cost. For the qudit Grover-mixer algorithm on unweighted <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>-regular graphs of girth <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>, Apte et al. compute the expected cut fraction and identify <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math></span> comparisons in which depths starting at <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math></span> exceed the analyzed classical Local Vector guarantees. Their expectation calculation costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical time and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> memory, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span> entering the numerical values.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Polynomial time, Frieze–Jerrum approximation and the Local Vector algorithm; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for exact integer-weighted 2-CSP.</strong> Frieze–Jerrum rounding gives approximation ratio <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02243;</mo><mn>0.836008</mn></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span>. García-Soriano and Miyauchi give certified ratios for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math></span> and characterize the ratio for every <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>. On regular graphs with a girth promise, the Local Vector algorithm supplies the cut-fraction comparison. For exact optimization, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math></span> is the matrix-multiplication exponent and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math></span> bounds the scaled integer score. A threshold circuit with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span> gates gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time through exact 3-CNF counting; enumeration gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The smallest applicable bound is used. At fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, unit weights and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, threshold counting gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The established comparison concerns approximation quality at specified depth and graph promises. A runtime speedup for attaining the same target quality remains unestablished.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Analytical expectation formulas and the reported parameter comparisons support an advantage over the stated classical cut-fraction guarantees. The strongest general approximation and exact algorithms provide additional comparisons.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>A prospective implementation uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> one-hot logical qubits or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>k</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span> binary logical data qubits, plus encoding and synthesis workspace. Depth, graph routing and repeated sampling control the required fault-tolerant resources; the cited encoding and high-girth studies use classical simulations and analysis.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Qudit QAOA; Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.03489\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Hadfield et al., <em>From the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm to a Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</em> (2019)</a>. Section 3 and Appendix A.3.1: feasible-state encodings, mixers and Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-Cut mapping.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.01095\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Fuchs et al., <em>Efficient Encoding of the Weighted MAX <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-CUT on a Quantum Computer Using QAOA</em> (2021)</a>. Sections 3.1–3.3, Equations (27)–(28) and Table 1: one-hot/binary encodings and gate counts.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.39042\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Apte et al., <em>Provable Classical and Quantum Local Algorithms for Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-Cut and Quantum Advantage at Moderate Girth</em> (2026)</a>. Algorithm 1; Sections 4.1–4.2 define and evaluate qudit QAOA; Section 5 and Appendix A give the comparisons.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35453\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">García-Soriano and Miyauchi, <em>Hermite Brings a Laptop: Analyzing Frieze–Jerrum Rounding Yields Improved Approximations for Clustering Problems</em> (2026)</a>. Section 4.4, Theorems 4.8–4.12: the Frieze–Jerrum ratio and its certified evaluation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cs.helsinki.fi/u/mkhkoivi/publications/ipl-2005.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Koivisto, <em>Optimal 2-Constraint Satisfaction via Sum-Product Algorithms</em> (2006)</a>. Sections 2–4: sum-product reduction and exact weighted 2-CSP optimization.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1: sparse fixed-width exact counting; threshold circuits and witness recovery give the exact optimization bound.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>: <a href=\"#entry/maxcut-approximation-with-qaoa\">MaxCut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/bounded-occurrence-max-e3lin2\">Max-E3LIN2</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-independent-set-in-rydberg-geometric-graphs\">Rydberg independent set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/sherringtonkirkpatrick-spin-glass-optimization\">SK spin-glass optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-bisection-with-constraint-preserving-qaoa\">Maximum bisection</a>, <a href=\"#entry/job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs\">Job-shop scheduling</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-portfolio-rebalancing-with-constrained-mixers\">Discrete portfolio rebalancing</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">→ QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "maximum-bisection-with-constraint-preserving-qaoa": {
    "title": "Maximum bisection with constraint-preserving QAOA",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Maximum bisection with constraint-preserving QAOA</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Maximum bisection)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an undirected graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with even <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span> and nonnegative rational weights, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02282;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span> maximizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span>. The balance constraint models an equal-size partition of a network. Complexity counts quantum gates and samples, classical arithmetic for approximation, and bit operations for exact optimization.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates, the quantum alternating operator ansatz with an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math></span> parity-ring mixer.</strong> The initial computational basis state has Hamming weight <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>. Each of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> layers applies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> weighted <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></math></span> phases followed by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> two-qubit <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math></span> rotations arranged in parity layers. These rotations preserve Hamming weight, so every measured bit string describes a balanced partition. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span> is the number of parameter settings evaluated and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span> the samples per setting; the classical optimizer and rotation-synthesis accuracy supply further costs.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Polynomial time with ratio <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.8776</mn></mrow></math></span>, Austrin–Benabbas–Georgiou; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for exact weighted 2-CSP.</strong> The approximation algorithm rounds a semidefinite hierarchy solution with biased thresholds and restores exact balance. For exact optimization, a quadratic cardinality penalty produces a weighted 2-CSP, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math></span> includes the integer penalty and score range and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math></span> is the matrix-multiplication exponent. Threshold counting also gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for a size-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span> feasibility-and-score circuit; enumeration gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The smallest applicable bound is used. Sparse unweighted graphs admit <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, yielding <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The variational construction supplies feasible balanced solutions with the displayed evaluation cost. Its approximation and time-to-solution performance have no established asymptotic advantage over these classical algorithms.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Constraint preservation and gate counts are rigorous. Target-quality guarantees depend on the parameter and sampling strategy.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The circuit uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> data qubits. The parity-ring mixer has constant depth before routing; the cost layer has depth at most the graph&#x27;s maximum degree plus one with suitable connectivity. Protected implementations must also support parameter optimization, repeated measurements and phase synthesis.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz with an XY mixer</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.03489\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Hadfield et al., <em>From the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm to a Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</em> (2019)</a>. Appendix A.3.2, “Graph Partitioning (Minimum Bisection)”, and A.3.3, “Maximum Bisection”: complete mappings and resource counts.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.0458\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Austrin, Benabbas and Georgiou, <em>Better Balance by Being Biased: A 0.8776-Approximation for Max Bisection</em> (2013/2016)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and the rounding algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cs.helsinki.fi/u/mkhkoivi/publications/ipl-2005.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Koivisto, <em>Optimal 2-Constraint Satisfaction via Sum-Product Algorithms</em> (2006)</a>. Sections 2–4: exact weighted 2-CSP optimization.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1; sparse threshold-circuit encoding and conditional counting.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>: <a href=\"#entry/maxcut-approximation-with-qaoa\">MaxCut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/bounded-occurrence-max-e3lin2\">Max-E3LIN2</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-independent-set-in-rydberg-geometric-graphs\">Rydberg independent set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/sherringtonkirkpatrick-spin-glass-optimization\">SK spin-glass optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-k-cut-with-variational-quantum-circuits\">Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-Cut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs\">Job-shop scheduling</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-portfolio-rebalancing-with-constrained-mixers\">Discrete portfolio rebalancing</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">→ QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs": {
    "title": "Job-shop scheduling with delivery and changeover costs",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Job-shop scheduling with delivery and changeover costs</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Discrete production scheduling)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>J</mi></mrow></math></span> unit-time jobs, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math></span> machines, due dates <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, idle allowances <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, production groups <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span> and nonnegative costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, choose distinct integer slots <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> for the jobs on each machine. Require <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span> and that each machine&#x27;s occupied slots form one consecutive interval. If <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> is its <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math></span>th processed job, minimize\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><munder><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><munder><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></munderover><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02260;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nwhere <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. This scheduling model captures delivery penalties and production-group changes in steel manufacturing. Complexity counts quantum gates and measurement samples and classical bit operations for the time-indexed binary formulation.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates for hardware-efficient VQE and F-VQE; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for QAOA.</strong> The time-indexed QUBO has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> binary variables before presolving, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> nonzero quadratic terms. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> counts circuit layers, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span> counts primitive circuit evaluations and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span> the shots per evaluation. A computational-basis sample gives a schedule assignment and its classically evaluated cost. VQE minimizes the sampled energy or a conditional-value-at-risk objective; filtering VQE (F-VQE) reweights sampled energies and uses parameter-shift updates. Variational imaginary-time evolution (VarQITE) uses the same state family and estimates a parameter metric: with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span> parameters, a direct iteration needs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> measurement settings and an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical linear-system solve. These settings contribute to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span>. QAOA uses phases for the QUBO terms and a transverse-field mixer. Parameter optimization and feasibility checks are part of the hybrid computation.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, enumeration, weighted 2-CSP and exact threshold counting.</strong> Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math></span> bounds the scaled integer QUBO score, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math></span> is the matrix-multiplication exponent, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span> is the size of a score-and-feasibility circuit. Enumerating machine permutations and initial idle slots also gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>&#x00021;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>M</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time, with coefficient bit costs included in the polynomial. The smallest applicable bound is used. Classical branch-and-bound, constraint programming and mixed-integer solvers use scheduling-specific propagation and lower bounds. Presolving reduces the binary variable count before either classical or quantum optimization.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Circuit and sampling costs are parameterized by the optimization effort. The published study establishes empirical convergence and solution-quality comparisons among the variational methods.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The hardware experiments compare sampled energies and optimum frequencies on the tested instances; an asymptotic quantum speedup has not been established.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Amaro et al. compared the four methods on a five-qubit instance and ran F-VQE on instances with up to 23 physical superconducting qubits after presolving. VQE, F-VQE and QAOA use <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span> data qubits; the Hadamard-test implementation of VarQITE adds an ancilla. Logical implementations must protect the complete repeated-measurement computation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: QAOA; VQE; filtering VQE; variational imaginary-time evolution</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.03745\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Amaro et al., <em>A Case Study of Variational Quantum Algorithms for a Job Shop Scheduling Problem</em> (2022)</a>. Sections II.A–II.B and Appendix A define the scheduling problem and QUBO; Sections II.C.1–II.C.4 and Table II specify VQE, QAOA, VarQITE and F-VQE; Section III gives the experiments.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.10055\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Amaro et al., <em>Filtering Variational Quantum Algorithms for Combinatorial Optimization</em> (2022)</a>. Filtering objective, parameter-shift update and adaptive-filter prescription.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cs.helsinki.fi/u/mkhkoivi/publications/ipl-2005.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Koivisto, <em>Optimal 2-Constraint Satisfaction via Sum-Product Algorithms</em> (2006)</a>. Sections 2–4: exact optimization of integer-weighted pairwise objectives.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1; exact threshold counting and witness recovery.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>: <a href=\"#entry/maxcut-approximation-with-qaoa\">MaxCut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/bounded-occurrence-max-e3lin2\">Max-E3LIN2</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-independent-set-in-rydberg-geometric-graphs\">Rydberg independent set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/sherringtonkirkpatrick-spin-glass-optimization\">SK spin-glass optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-k-cut-with-variational-quantum-circuits\">Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-Cut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-bisection-with-constraint-preserving-qaoa\">Maximum bisection</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-portfolio-rebalancing-with-constrained-mixers\">Discrete portfolio rebalancing</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/vqe\">Variational quantum eigensolver and filtering VQE</a>: <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/two-community-modularity-maximization-by-variational-local-search\">Community modularity</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">→ QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/vqe\">→ Variational quantum eigensolver and filtering VQE</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/varqite\">→ Variational imaginary-time evolution (VarQITE)</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "discrete-portfolio-rebalancing-with-constrained-mixers": {
    "title": "Discrete portfolio rebalancing with constrained mixers",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Discrete portfolio rebalancing with constrained mixers</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Discrete portfolio rebalancing)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> assets, a positive-semidefinite rational covariance matrix <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A3;</mi></mrow></math></span>, expected returns <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math></span>, previous positions <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, a net investment <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> and transaction cost <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> satisfying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span> and minimizing\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>T</mi><munder><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02260;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nThe positions represent short, absent and long holdings in discrete lots. Complexity counts circuit gates and samples, with classical bit complexity depending on the coefficient precision.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates for dense covariance, the quantum alternating operator ansatz with paired parity mixers.</strong> The two-spin encoding writes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math></span> using <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> data qubits. An entangled initial state has the required difference between long and short occupations. Separate <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math></span> parity-ring mixers preserve their respective occupations and hence the investment constraint. A cost layer implements the risk, return and trading-cost phases; sampling and decoding return discrete portfolios. The parameters <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span> count alternating layers, evaluated parameter settings and shots per setting. Classical optimizer work and cost evaluation of the sampled portfolios add to the displayed gate count.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, enumeration, weighted 2-CSP and exact threshold counting.</strong> The investment equality can be imposed by a sufficiently large quadratic penalty. Unary transaction costs and pairwise risk terms then form a three-state 2-CSP. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math></span> includes the scaled integer coefficient and penalty ranges; the polynomial dependence on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math></span> makes the matrix-multiplication bound pseudopolynomial in coefficient bit length. The threshold-circuit bound instead uses the binary encoding size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span> of feasibility and objective evaluation. Mixed-integer quadratic programming with branch-and-bound supplies the practical classical solver; covariance structure and valid bounds determine its instance-specific search.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm preserves the investment constraint and produces sampled discrete portfolios. The established resource formula is a circuit-evaluation cost, with optimization effort specified by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The reported solution-quality results concern small simulated portfolios. A complexity separation for this rebalancing problem remains open.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The encoding requires <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> logical data qubits plus compilation workspace. The cited eight-asset study uses an ideal simulator. Dense covariance gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> interactions per cost layer, and routing, angle accuracy and repeated sampling dominate scaling on physical devices.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz with paired parity mixers</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.05296\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Hodson et al., <em>Portfolio Rebalancing Experiments Using the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</em> (2019)</a>. Equations (9)–(12): optimization problem; Section IV and Equations (13)–(24): encoding, constraints, initial state and paired parity mixers; Section V: simulated experiments.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cs.helsinki.fi/u/mkhkoivi/publications/ipl-2005.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Koivisto, <em>Optimal 2-Constraint Satisfaction via Sum-Product Algorithms</em> (2006)</a>. Sections 2–4, specialized to a three-element variable domain.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1; binary threshold-circuit encoding and witness recovery.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>: <a href=\"#entry/maxcut-approximation-with-qaoa\">MaxCut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/bounded-occurrence-max-e3lin2\">Max-E3LIN2</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-independent-set-in-rydberg-geometric-graphs\">Rydberg independent set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/sherringtonkirkpatrick-spin-glass-optimization\">SK spin-glass optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-k-cut-with-variational-quantum-circuits\">Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-Cut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-bisection-with-constraint-preserving-qaoa\">Maximum bisection</a>, <a href=\"#entry/job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs\">Job-shop scheduling</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">→ QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "two-community-modularity-maximization-by-variational-local-search": {
    "title": "Two-community modularity maximization by variational local search",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Two-community modularity maximization by variational local search</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Two-community modularity maximization)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a simple undirected graph with adjacency matrix <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> edges and degrees <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> maximizing\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nThe signs specify at most two communities; modularity measures their excess internal connectivity relative to the degree-based null model. Complexity counts quantum gates and samples for each local subproblem and classical graph-processing and bit-operation costs for the complete search.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates, variational quantum local search on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span>-vertex subproblems.</strong> In each of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math></span> local-search rounds, a classical routine chooses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span> vertices, fixes the remaining signs, and forms a quadratic objective with linear terms from the fixed vertices. A variational circuit samples candidate updates, and an improving update is accepted. The bound uses a dense depth-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> ansatz or cost circuit, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span> parameter evaluations per round and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span> shots per evaluation; the classical selection and update work is additional. Shaydulin et al. describe the gate-model solver as QAOA and implement an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> variational ansatz with COBYLA parameter optimization. The hardware runs use angles optimized on a simulator, whose cost is part of that experiment&#x27;s computation.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> exact global optimization, weighted 2-CSP; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> per exact local subproblem.</strong> Multiplying the objective by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span> gives integer pair coefficients <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> of polynomial magnitude. Shifting each pair&#x27;s score to be nonnegative permits the Williams–Koivisto weighted 2-CSP algorithm, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math></span> the matrix-multiplication exponent. A size-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span> Boolean threshold circuit also supplies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time; the smaller applicable bound is used. The published practical comparison uses Gurobi both for global optimization and for the same local subproblems. Local search with an exact subproblem solver optimizes the selected neighborhood at each round, while global search supplies an optimum certificate.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The hybrid method improves modularity by optimizing small neighborhoods. Its measured solution quality and iteration count provide an empirical comparison with classical local search.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The study reports comparable solution quality across the tested solvers. A same-task asymptotic runtime advantage is unestablished.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The gate-model experiment uses 16 physical superconducting qubits for local subproblems inside graphs with up to 410 vertices. A logical realization uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span> data qubits plus circuit workspace; the full graph and current partition reside in classical memory. The number of graph vertices is therefore distinct from the quantum register size.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Variational quantum local search</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.12484\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Shaydulin et al., <em>Network Community Detection on Small Quantum Computers</em> (2019)</a>. Equation (1): two-community modularity; Algorithm 1 and Equation (2): quantum local search and its subproblem; Section 3.2: gate-model implementation; Section 4: experiments.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://people.csail.mit.edu/rrw/2-csp-final.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Williams, <em>A New Algorithm for Optimal Constraint Satisfaction and Its Implications</em> (2005)</a>. Split-and-list reduction of 2-CSP to weighted triangles and matrix multiplication.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cs.helsinki.fi/u/mkhkoivi/publications/ipl-2005.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Koivisto, <em>Optimal 2-Constraint Satisfaction via Sum-Product Algorithms</em> (2006)</a>. Sections 2–4: improved weighted 2-CSP implementation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1; threshold-circuit encoding and conditional counting.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/vqe\">Variational quantum eigensolver and filtering VQE</a>: <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs\">Job-shop scheduling</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/vqe\">→ Variational quantum eigensolver and filtering VQE</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g01": {
    "title": "Element distinctness and k-distinctness",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Element distinctness and k-distinctness</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Element distinctness and k-distinctness)</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Element distinctness.</strong> Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>, decide whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> for some <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math></span>. The input is accessed by coherent indexed queries; collision detection is a primitive for data processing.</p><p><strong><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-distinctness.</strong> Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> and fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>, decide whether some value occurs at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> times. Complexity counts indexed input queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <strong>Element distinctness.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by the Ambainis Johnson-graph quantum walk, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time when the walk data structure supports coherent updates.</strong> <strong><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-distinctness.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Belovs&#x27; learning graph; the bound is for constant <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <strong>Element distinctness.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized queries; hashing gives expected <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> word-RAM time and sorting gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> comparison time.</strong> <strong><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-distinctness.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, with expected <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> word-RAM time by counting equal keys.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Ambainis–Childs–Eisenberg subset quantum walk; Belovs learning graph for k-distinctness</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0311001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum walk algorithm for element distinctness</em></a>. Main element-distinctness algorithm and theorem</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.1534\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Learning-Graph-Based Quantum Algorithm for k-distinctness</em></a>. Main theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/subset-quantum-walk\">Ambainis–Childs–Eisenberg subset quantum walk</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g03\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-sum</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/belovs-k-distinctness\">Belovs learning graph for k-distinctness</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g38\">Cycle finding from an edge list</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/subset-quantum-walk\">→ Ambainis–Childs–Eisenberg subset quantum walk</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/belovs-k-distinctness\">→ Belovs learning graph for k-distinctness</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g03": {
    "title": "k-sum",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-sum</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-sum)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> in a finite abelian group and a target <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span>, find distinct <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span>, or report that none exist. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> is constant and group elements are supplied by an indexed oracle.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> input queries by the subset quantum walk. This is tight for sufficiently large groups.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, followed by classical processing. Meet-in-the-middle gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time for fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>; query and arithmetic bounds measure different resources.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Ambainis–Childs–Eisenberg subset quantum walk</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0311038\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for subset finding</em></a>. General subset-finding algorithm; k-sum application</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6528\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Adversary lower bound for the k-sum problem</em></a>. Main theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/subset-quantum-walk\">Ambainis–Childs–Eisenberg subset quantum walk</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g01\">Element distinctness and k-distinctness</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/subset-quantum-walk\">→ Ambainis–Childs–Eisenberg subset quantum walk</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g04": {
    "title": "Ordered search",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Ordered search</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Ordered search)</span></h2>\n<p>Given oracle access to a nondecreasing bit string <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, determine its first <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>, including the all-zero case. Complexity counts exact comparison queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> exact queries by the Carolan–Childs algorithm.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span> exact queries by binary search; both bounds are <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>No established asymptotic speedup in the comparison stated above.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The leading query constant and the classical binary-search comparator are proved; both asymptotic orders are logarithmic.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Carolan–Childs optimal ordered search</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34293\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Optimal Quantum Algorithms for Ordered Search</em></a>. Main theorem and exact polynomial-program construction</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/optimal-ordered-search\">→ Carolan–Childs optimal ordered search</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g05": {
    "title": "Approximate median",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Approximate median</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Approximate median)</span></h2>\n<p>Given indexed access to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> totally ordered values and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>, output an element whose rank lies between <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>. The resource is bounded-error input queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by the Nayak–Wu approximate-selection algorithm.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries at constant confidence by random sampling and classical selection.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Nayak–Wu approximate selection; Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9804066\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The quantum query complexity of approximating the median and related statistics</em></a>. Approximate-median algorithm and theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>: <a href=\"#entry/approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments\">Approximate counting</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-approximate-selection\">→ Nayak–Wu approximate selection</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">→ Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g06": {
    "title": "Exact string matching",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Exact string matching</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Exact string matching)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a text <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and a pattern <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></math></span>, or report that no such position exists. The alphabet is finite and characters support indexed queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time and character queries by Ramesh–Vinay string matching, using periodicity preprocessing and quantum search.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case time and queries by Knuth–Morris–Pratt or other linear-time string matching algorithms.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Ramesh–Vinay quantum string matching; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0011049\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>String Matching in tilde O(sqrt(n)+sqrt(m)) Quantum Time</em></a>. Main algorithm; deterministic-sampling construction</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/ramesh-vinay-matching\">→ Ramesh–Vinay quantum string matching</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g07": {
    "title": "Average-case multidimensional pattern matching",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,112,112)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Average-case multidimensional pattern matching)</span></h2>\n<p>Given arrays <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi>&#x003A3;</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi>&#x003A3;</mi></mrow></math></span>, find an offset <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for every <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. The theorem assumes the paper&#x27;s random-input distribution and fixed dimension <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>; complexity counts queries and time.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Montanaro&#x27;s reduction to hidden shift, for the promised random arrays.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> in the corresponding average-case classical model.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,112,112)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential to subexponential in the strongest stated regime; the general parameter dependence is retained above.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Montanaro hidden-shift pattern matching; Hidden-shift sieves of Kuperberg and Regev; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.1816\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum pattern matching fast on average</em></a>. Theorems1–2; Sections4–5</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/kuperberg\">Hidden-shift sieves of Kuperberg and Regev</a>: <a href=\"#entry/vectorization-for-commutative-isogeny-actions\">Commutative isogeny vectorization</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/hidden-shift-pattern-matching\">→ Montanaro hidden-shift pattern matching</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/kuperberg\">→ Hidden-shift sieves of Kuperberg and Regev</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g08": {
    "title": "Longest common substring",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Longest common substring</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Longest common substring)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, output the maximum <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></math></span> for which <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> for some <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math></span>. Input characters support coherent random access.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Akmal–Jin, combining synchronizing sets with a quantum walk.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time on an integer alphabet by suffix-tree or suffix-array methods, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case character queries.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Akmal–Jin synchronizing-set quantum walk</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.09696\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal Quantum Algorithms for String Problems</em></a>. Section3; main LCS theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/synchronizing-set-quantum-walk\">→ Akmal–Jin synchronizing-set quantum walk</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g09": {
    "title": "Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, output an index minimizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> lexicographically. Complexity uses indexed character access.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time by the Akmal–Jin quantum string algorithm.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time and queries by Booth&#x27;s or Duval&#x27;s minimum-rotation algorithm.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Akmal–Jin quantum string selection; Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.09696\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal Quantum Algorithms for String Problems</em></a>. Theorem4.6</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/akmal-jin-string-selection\">Akmal–Jin quantum string selection</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/akmal-jin-string-selection\">→ Akmal–Jin quantum string selection</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g10": {
    "title": "Longest square substring",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Longest square substring</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Longest square substring)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, find the longest substring of the form <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow></math></span> for a nonempty string <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math></span>. Complexity counts character queries and random-access time.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Akmal–Jin, using periodicity and quantum search over candidate scales.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by classical periodicity and runs algorithms; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries are required in the worst case.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Akmal–Jin quantum string selection; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.09696\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal Quantum Algorithms for String Problems</em></a>. Theorem5.1</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/akmal-jin-string-selection\">Akmal–Jin quantum string selection</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/akmal-jin-string-selection\">→ Akmal–Jin quantum string selection</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g12": {
    "title": "Longest Lyndon substring",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Longest Lyndon substring</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Longest Lyndon substring)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> over an ordered alphabet, find its longest substring that is strictly lexicographically smaller than every nontrivial cyclic rotation of itself. Complexity uses indexed characters.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time by Akmal–Jin&#x27;s reduction to quantum minimum-rotation procedures.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time using Lyndon factorization and classical string preprocessing; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case queries.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Akmal–Jin quantum string selection; Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.09696\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal Quantum Algorithms for String Problems</em></a>. Theorem4.9</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/akmal-jin-string-selection\">Akmal–Jin quantum string selection</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/akmal-jin-string-selection\">→ Akmal–Jin quantum string selection</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g14": {
    "title": "Longest palindromic substring",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Longest palindromic substring</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Longest palindromic substring)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, find a longest interval <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> satisfying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> for every admissible <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span>. Complexity counts character queries and random-access time.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Le Gall–Seddighin, using periodicity and amplitude amplification.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Manacher&#x27;s algorithm, matching the worst-case character-query lower bound.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Le Gall–Seddighin palindrome search; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.12122\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Meets Fine-grained Complexity: Sublinear Time Quantum Algorithms for String Problems</em></a>. Theorem2; palindrome section</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-palindrome-search\">→ Le Gall–Seddighin palindrome search</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g15": {
    "title": "Approximate Ulam distance",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Approximate Ulam distance</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Approximate Ulam distance)</span></h2>\n<p>For two permutations <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math></span> of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>, approximate <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>LCS</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> within factor <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Complexity uses indexed access; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> is fixed in the displayed bounds.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Le Gall–Seddighin&#x27;s quantum Ulam-distance approximation.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Naumovitz–Saks–Seshadhri; exact reduction to longest increasing subsequence takes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> word-RAM time.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Le Gall–Seddighin Ulam-distance approximation; Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.12122\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Meets Fine-grained Complexity: Sublinear Time Quantum Algorithms for String Problems</em></a>. Theorem2.6 and Section6</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>: <a href=\"#entry/approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments\">Approximate counting</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-ulam-approximation\">→ Le Gall–Seddighin Ulam-distance approximation</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">→ Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g16": {
    "title": "Bounded edit distance",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Bounded edit distance</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Bounded edit distance)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>, compute their edit distance and an optimal edit sequence if the distance is at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, and otherwise report that it exceeds <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>. Insertions, deletions and substitutions have unit cost; characters are queried by index.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> character queries by Gibney–Jin–Kociumaka–Thankachan.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by the Landau–Vishkin bounded-distance method, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> character queries.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gibney–Jin–Kociumaka–Thankachan bounded edit distance; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01793\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal Quantum Algorithms for Bounded Edit Distance and Lempel-Ziv Factorization</em></a>. Main bounded-edit-distance theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-bounded-edit-distance\">→ Gibney–Jin–Kociumaka–Thankachan bounded edit distance</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g17": {
    "title": "Pattern matching with mismatches",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Pattern matching with mismatches</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Pattern matching with mismatches)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and threshold <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, identify occurrences whose Hamming distance from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math></span> is at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, using the paper&#x27;s compact representation of occurrence sets. Complexity counts character queries separately from processing and output.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Kociumaka–Nogler–Wellnitz. The query notation suppresses subpolynomial factors.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case character queries by reading both strings. Classical time can then use convolution and periodicity algorithms; the displayed separation is in the query model.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Kociumaka–Nogler–Wellnitz approximate pattern matching; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.06808\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal-Time Quantum Algorithms for Approximate Pattern Matching</em></a>. Main mismatch-matching theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-approximate-pattern-matching\">Kociumaka–Nogler–Wellnitz approximate pattern matching</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-approximate-pattern-matching\">→ Kociumaka–Nogler–Wellnitz approximate pattern matching</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g18": {
    "title": "Pattern matching with edits",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Pattern matching with edits</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Pattern matching with edits)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, identify substrings of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></span> at edit distance at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math></span>, represented compactly as in the algorithm. Complexity counts character queries and processing time.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Kociumaka–Nogler–Wellnitz.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case character queries by reading the input; classical dynamic programming and periodicity methods provide the subsequent processing.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Kociumaka–Nogler–Wellnitz approximate pattern matching; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.06808\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal-Time Quantum Algorithms for Approximate Pattern Matching</em></a>. Main edit-matching theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-approximate-pattern-matching\">Kociumaka–Nogler–Wellnitz approximate pattern matching</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-approximate-pattern-matching\">→ Kociumaka–Nogler–Wellnitz approximate pattern matching</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g19": {
    "title": "Lempel–Ziv factorization",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Lempel–Ziv factorization</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Lempel–Ziv factorization)</span></h2>\n<p>Given indexed access to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, output its LZ77 factorization into <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math></span> phrases, each extending the longest previously occurring prefix under the chosen LZ77 convention. The resource is character queries and random-access time.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time and queries by Gibney–Jin–Kociumaka–Thankachan&#x27;s quantum Lempel–Ziv construction.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> word-RAM time using suffix structures, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case queries.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gibney–Jin–Kociumaka–Thankachan LZ77 construction; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01793\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal Quantum Algorithms for Bounded Edit Distance and Lempel-Ziv Factorization</em></a>. Main Lempel–Ziv theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-lz77\">→ Gibney–Jin–Kociumaka–Thankachan LZ77 construction</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g20": {
    "title": "Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a string <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> with a unique end marker, output the run-length encoding of its Burrows–Wheeler transform, containing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math></span> runs. Complexity counts indexed character queries and time.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by the Gibney–Thankachan compressibility-aware quantum construction.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by classical suffix sorting and Burrows–Wheeler construction; reading all characters takes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gibney–Thankachan compressibility-aware string algorithms; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.07235\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Compressibility-Aware Quantum Algorithms on Strings</em></a>. RL-BWT construction result</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-compressed-bwt\">→ Gibney–Thankachan compressibility-aware string algorithms</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g22": {
    "title": "Longest common substring of run-length encoded strings",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Longest common substring of run-length encoded strings</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Longest common substring of run-length encoded strings)</span></h2>\n<p>Given two run-length encoded strings with at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> runs each and uncompressed length at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span>, find a longest common substring. Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span> be the number of runs spanned by an optimal match, with the convention of Lee–Lin. Queries return a run character and length.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>polylog</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Lee–Lin; the algorithm searches over possible match scales.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> encoded-input queries suffice to read the runs; classical compressed-string algorithms perform the remaining processing. This comparison measures oracle access rather than decompression time.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Lee–Lin run-length encoded substring algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.02421\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal Quantum Algorithm for Finding the Longest Common Substring between Run-Length Encoded Strings</em></a>. Main theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-rle-lcs\">→ Lee–Lin run-length encoded substring algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g23": {
    "title": "Star-free regular-language membership",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Star-free regular-language membership</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Star-free regular-language membership)</span></h2>\n<p>Fix a star-free regular language <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math></span>. Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> by character queries, decide <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span>. Star-free means definable from finite languages using union, concatenation and complement.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries for every fixed star-free language by Aaronson–Grier–Schaeffer. The full regular-language classification has constant, near-square-root, and linear query regimes.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time and queries by a fixed finite automaton; languages in the nontrivial star-free query class have <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized query complexity.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Aaronson–Grier–Schaeffer regular-language algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.04219\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A Quantum Query Complexity Trichotomy for Regular Languages</em></a>. Star-free recognition algorithm and classification theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/star-free-language-quantum-query\">→ Aaronson–Grier–Schaeffer regular-language algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g24": {
    "title": "Bounded-height Dyck language",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Bounded-height Dyck language</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Bounded-height Dyck language)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and height bound <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math></span>, decide whether every prefix has balance in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> and the final balance is zero. Complexity counts queries to parenthesis symbols.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Khadiev–Shen&#x27;s recursive minimal-unbalanced-substring search.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by a running balance counter for nontrivial heights.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Khadiev–Shen bounded-height Dyck algorithm; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02176\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Query Complexity of Dyck Languages with Bounded Height</em></a>. Main algorithm and query upper bound</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/bounded-dyck-quantum-search\">→ Khadiev–Shen bounded-height Dyck algorithm</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g25": {
    "title": "Common subsequence of length k",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>)</span></h2>\n<p>Given two strings of length at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> and a fixed positive integer <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, decide whether they have a common subsequence of length at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, and output one if it exists. Complexity counts character queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Childs and collaborators&#x27; quantum divide-and-conquer algorithm.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries to read both strings; classical dynamic programming then decides the subsequence condition.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum divide and conquer; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.06419\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum divide and conquer</em></a>. Theorem7; Section3.4</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-divide-conquer\">Quantum divide and conquer</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-divide-conquer\">→ Quantum divide and conquer</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g26": {
    "title": "Increasing subsequence of length k",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an indexed sequence <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> from a totally ordered finite domain and fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, decide whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span> exist. Complexity counts random-access time.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Allcock and collaborators&#x27; divide-and-conquer algorithm.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time for fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> by maintaining the smallest possible last element of each subsequence length; general patience sorting takes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum divide and conquer; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.16401\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>On the quantum time complexity of divide and conquer</em></a>. Theorem19; ICALP Theorem3</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-divide-conquer\">Quantum divide and conquer</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-divide-conquer\">→ Quantum divide and conquer</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g27": {
    "title": "Longest substring with distinct characters",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Longest substring with distinct characters</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Longest substring with distinct characters)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, find a longest contiguous interval in which all characters are pairwise distinct. Complexity uses indexed characters and coherent data-structure operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by the Allcock–Bao–Belovs–Lee–Santha quantum divide-and-conquer algorithm.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> expected word-RAM time by sliding windows with hashing; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case character queries.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum divide and conquer; Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.16401\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>On the quantum time complexity of divide and conquer</em></a>. Theorem39; ICALP Theorem4</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-divide-conquer\">Quantum divide and conquer</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-divide-conquer\">→ Quantum divide and conquer</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g31": {
    "title": "Maximum-sum submatrix",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Maximum-sum submatrix</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Maximum-sum submatrix)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> with word-sized entries, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></math></span> maximizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span>. Complexity counts RAM operations and coherent indexed access.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Allcock and collaborators&#x27; quantum divide-and-conquer algorithm.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> randomized time via the reduction to distance products and Williams&#x27; APSP algorithm; the standard row-compression dynamic program takes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum divide and conquer; Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.16401\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>On the quantum time complexity of divide and conquer</em></a>. Theorem6 in ICALP version; Maximum Submatrix section</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6680\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Faster All-Pairs Shortest Paths via Circuit Complexity</em></a>. Distance-product running time; applied through the maximum-submatrix reduction</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-divide-conquer\">Quantum divide and conquer</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-divide-conquer\">→ Quantum divide and conquer</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g32": {
    "title": "Euclidean closest pair",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Euclidean closest pair</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Euclidean closest pair)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> points in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Q</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> with finite word-sized coordinates and fixed dimension <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>, output two distinct points of minimum Euclidean distance. Complexity counts coordinate queries and RAM operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by the Aaronson–Chia–Lin–Wang–Zhang quantum walk with a spatial data structure.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> expected time on the randomized word-RAM with floor and hashing, or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> in the algebraic decision-tree model.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Aaronson–Chia–Lin–Wang–Zhang geometric quantum walk</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.01973\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>On the Quantum Complexity of Closest Pair and Related Problems</em></a>. Theorem1.3; geometric quantum walk</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/geometric-quantum-walk\">Aaronson–Chia–Lin–Wang–Zhang geometric quantum walk</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/geometric-quantum-walk\">→ Aaronson–Chia–Lin–Wang–Zhang geometric quantum walk</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g33": {
    "title": "Bichromatic closest pair",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Bichromatic closest pair</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Bichromatic closest pair)</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Bichromatic closest pair.</strong> Given red and blue point sets <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02282;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Q</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> of total size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math></span> minimizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>. Coordinates have finite encodings and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span> is fixed.</p><p><strong>Approximate bichromatic closest pair.</strong> For red and blue point sets of total size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Q</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, output a bichromatic pair whose distance is at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> times optimum. Complexity counts coordinate-access time, with fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <strong>Bichromatic closest pair.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time for every fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, by geometric quantum search.</strong> <strong>Approximate bichromatic closest pair.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by the approximate geometric quantum walk.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <strong>Bichromatic closest pair.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x02309;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time for every fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> by classical fixed-dimensional geometric methods; in the plane, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> suffices.</strong> <strong>Approximate bichromatic closest pair.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time for fixed dimension and approximation factor by classical approximate nearest-neighbor or geometric decomposition methods.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Aaronson–Chia–Lin–Wang–Zhang geometric quantum walk; Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.01973\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>On the Quantum Complexity of Closest Pair and Related Problems</em></a>. Theorem1.4; bichromatic closest-pair section</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.01973\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>On the Quantum Complexity of Closest Pair and Related Problems</em></a>. Theorem1.4; approximate bichromatic algorithm</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/geometric-quantum-walk\">Aaronson–Chia–Lin–Wang–Zhang geometric quantum walk</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g32\">Euclidean closest pair</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/geometric-quantum-walk\">→ Aaronson–Chia–Lin–Wang–Zhang geometric quantum walk</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g35": {
    "title": "Planarity",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Planarity</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Planarity)</span></h2>\n<p>Given the adjacency-matrix oracle of an undirected graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> vertices, decide whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> admits a planar embedding. Complexity counts adjacency queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by the Childs–Kothari algorithm for minor-closed graph properties.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> matrix queries in the worst case. Once the edges are listed, Hopcroft–Tarjan planarity testing takes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Childs–Kothari minor-closed graph-property algorithm; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.1443\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum query complexity of minor-closed graph properties</em></a>. Minor-closed classification; planarity specialization</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minor-closed-property-quantum-query\">→ Childs–Kothari minor-closed graph-property algorithm</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g36": {
    "title": "Cycle detection",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Cycle detection</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Cycle detection)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an undirected graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, decide whether it contains a cycle. Two input models are considered: an adjacency matrix and neighbor arrays with maximum degree <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>max</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> matrix-query time, or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>max</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> neighbor-array time, using <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> work qubits in the Cade–Montanaro–Belovs construction.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> matrix-query time or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> adjacency-list time by depth-first search.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The cited implementation uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> work qubits in addition to the supplied graph oracle, at the stated time cost.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Belovs–Reichardt span-program connectivity</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.00581\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Time and Space Efficient Quantum Algorithms for Detecting Cycles and Testing Bipartiteness</em></a>. Cycle-detection algorithms</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/span-connectivity\">Belovs–Reichardt span-program connectivity</a>: <a href=\"#entry/promised-short-path-st-connectivity\">Promised short-path <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>–<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span> connectivity</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/span-connectivity\">→ Belovs–Reichardt span-program connectivity</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g37": {
    "title": "Fixed-subgraph containment",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Fixed-subgraph containment</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Fixed-subgraph containment)</span></h2>\n<p>Fix a nonempty graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> vertices, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> edges and a vertex of degree <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>. Given adjacency queries to an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-vertex graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span>, decide whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> contains a subgraph isomorphic to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Lee–Magniez–Santha learning graphs, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The pattern is constant-sized.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized adjacency queries in the worst case; after acquiring the matrix, classical subgraph algorithms perform the computation.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Lee–Magniez–Santha fixed-subgraph learning graph</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.5135\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A learning graph based quantum query algorithm for finding constant-size subgraphs</em></a>. Theorem4.4</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/fixed-subgraph-learning-graph\">→ Lee–Magniez–Santha fixed-subgraph learning graph</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g38": {
    "title": "Cycle finding from an edge list",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Cycle finding from an edge list</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Cycle finding from an edge list)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an indexed edge list of an undirected graph with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> edges, maximum degree at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>, and fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>, decide whether the graph contains a simple <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-cycle. Complexity counts edge-list queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Gilani–Wang–Wu–Zhou&#x27;s adaptation of Belovs&#x27; learning graph. For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> this is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case edge-list queries, by reading the list and classical cycle testing. The degree promise permits the same linear-query lower bound.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Belovs learning graph for k-distinctness</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.17786\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithms on Edge Lists: Hiding, Shuffling, and Cycle Finding</em></a>. Theorem 6.16 and Corollary 6.17; Section 6.2</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/belovs-k-distinctness\">Belovs learning graph for k-distinctness</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g01\">Element distinctness and k-distinctness</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/belovs-k-distinctness\">→ Belovs learning graph for k-distinctness</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g42": {
    "title": "Distributed graph diameter",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Distributed graph diameter</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Distributed graph diameter)</span></h2>\n<p>In an unweighted connected <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-vertex network of diameter <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span>, compute <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>dist</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, with one processor per vertex and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>-qubit messages per edge per round. Complexity counts synchronous CONGEST rounds.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> rounds by Le Gall–Magniez distributed quantum search and distance estimation.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case rounds for exact diameter in classical CONGEST, including networks of small diameter.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Le Gall–Magniez distributed diameter algorithm; Grover search and amplitude amplification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.02917\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Sublinear-Time Quantum Computation of the Diameter in CONGEST Networks</em></a>. Theorem1; Section3.2</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/distributed-quantum-diameter\">→ Le Gall–Magniez distributed diameter algorithm</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g43": {
    "title": "Boolean matrix multiplication",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Boolean matrix multiplication</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Boolean matrix multiplication)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x022C1;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02227;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></math></span> be the number of nonzero output entries. Complexity counts time with coherent indexed input access and explicit sparse output.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Le Gall. The Jeffery–Kothari–Magniez graph-collision algorithm uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, in the output-size range covered by its matching lower bound.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> input queries. For time, output-sparse matrix multiplication gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>, by Abboud–Bringmann–Fischer–Kunnemann and Bennett–Gajulapalli–Golovnev–Warton. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> is the rectangular matrix-multiplication exponent; the integer product has the same support as the Boolean product.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Le Gall output-sensitive Boolean matrix multiplication; Cornelissen et al. graph-collision algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.6174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A Time-Efficient Output-Sensitive Quantum Algorithm for Boolean Matrix Multiplication</em></a>. Main theorem</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.5855\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Improving Quantum Query Complexity of Boolean Matrix Multiplication Using Graph Collision</em></a>. Main upper and lower bounds</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.10250\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Output-Sparse Matrix Multiplication Using Compressed Sensing</em></a>. Randomized output-sparse multiplication theorem and comparison with SODA 2024</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/graph-collision\">Cornelissen et al. graph-collision algorithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/graph-collision\">Graph collision</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/output-sensitive-boolean-product\">→ Le Gall output-sensitive Boolean matrix multiplication</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/graph-collision\">→ Cornelissen et al. graph-collision algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g44": {
    "title": "Random spanning-tree sampling",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Random spanning-tree sampling</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Random spanning-tree sampling)</span></h2>\n<p>Given adjacency-list access to a connected graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> vertices, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> edges and nonnegative finitely encoded weights, sample a tree with probability proportional to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, within total-variation error <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Unit weights give the uniform spanning-tree distribution.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time and adjacency-list queries by Apers–Gao–Ji–Liu. The quantum query lower bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> at constant error.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time at constant error by Anari–Liu–Oveis Gharan–Vinzant–Vuong&#x27;s down-up-walk sampler, with logarithmic dependence on the target accuracy. The general weighted adjacency-list problem requires <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical queries.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The implementation runs circuits on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> qubits and uses quantum-read/classical-write memory of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> bits, together with adjacency-list oracle access.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Apers–Gao–Ji–Liu spanning-tree sampler</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15603\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Speedup for Sampling Random Spanning Trees</em></a>. Main theorem; large-step up-down walk</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-spanning-tree-sampling\">→ Apers–Gao–Ji–Liu spanning-tree sampler</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g45": {
    "title": "Signed graph clusterability testing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Signed graph clusterability testing</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Signed graph clusterability testing)</span></h2>\n<p>For a bounded-degree graph with edges labeled <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></math></span> or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo></mrow></math></span>, distinguish graphs whose vertices can be partitioned so that positive edges lie inside parts and negative edges join distinct parts from graphs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>-far from that property. Distance counts edge-label or adjacency modifications.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> neighbor queries for fixed degree and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>, by Chen–Apers–Hsieh&#x27;s quantum signed-graph tester.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries for fixed degree and distance in the classical bounded-degree model.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Chen–Apers–Hsieh clusterability tester</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.10480\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>(Quantum) complexity of testing signed graph clusterability</em></a>. Theorems2 and6</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/signed-graph-clusterability-tester\">→ Chen–Apers–Hsieh clusterability tester</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g46": {
    "title": "Directed subgraph-freeness testing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Directed subgraph-freeness testing</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Directed subgraph-freeness testing)</span></h2>\n<p>Fix a directed pattern <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> source strongly connected components. Given outgoing-neighbor access to a bounded-outdegree <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-vertex digraph, distinguish <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>-free graphs from graphs requiring at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> edge changes to become <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>-free.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Apers–Magniez–Sen–Szabo for fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>, degree and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by the classical unidirectional tester; this exponent is necessary for the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-star subfamily.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Apers–Magniez–Sen–Szabo directed graph tester</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.05001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum property testing in sparse directed graphs</em></a>. Theorem13 in conference version (Theorem1 restatement)</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/directed-subgraph-quantum-tester\">→ Apers–Magniez–Sen–Szabo directed graph tester</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-g47": {
    "title": "Directed fixed-length path containment",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Directed fixed-length path containment</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Directed fixed-length path containment)</span></h2>\n<p>Fix a positive integer <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>. Given adjacency-matrix access to a directed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-vertex graph, decide whether it contains a simple directed path with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> edges. Complexity counts adjacency queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Cornelissen–Gilani–Patro, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>17</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>, with the exact recurrence in their path-containment theorem.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case adjacency queries for fixed nonempty paths; classical color coding or algebraic path detection follows input acquisition.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Cornelissen–Gilani–Patro path-containment algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.09017\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for path and cycle containment problems</em></a>. Main result 1.10; Corollary 4.5; Section 4</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-directed-path-containment\">→ Cornelissen–Gilani–Patro path-containment algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q30": {
    "title": "Learning a graph with edge-existence queries",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Learning a graph with edge-existence queries</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Learning a graph with edge-existence queries)</span></h2>\n<p>An unknown graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> on a known vertex set <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> is accessed by queries <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>. Recover its edges, under the matching, cycle, or star promises analyzed by Montanaro–Shao.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries for the stated matching/cycle classes; stars admit <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> in the corresponding promise regime.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> binary queries for the sparse matching families by counting possible graphs, with classical adaptive group-testing reconstruction within logarithmic factors.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Montanaro–Shao hidden-graph learning</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.08611\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for learning a hidden graph and beyond</em></a>. Table1 and OR-query section.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-hidden-graph-learning\">Montanaro–Shao hidden-graph learning</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q31\">Learning a graph with edge-parity queries</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-hidden-graph-learning\">→ Montanaro–Shao hidden-graph learning</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q31": {
    "title": "Learning a graph with edge-parity queries",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Learning a graph with edge-parity queries</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Learning a graph with edge-parity queries)</span></h2>\n<p>For an unknown graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>, a query on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> returns <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>. Recover the edge set. Complexity counts these parity queries rather than individual adjacency bits.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries for sparse graphs, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for maximum degree <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for the specified star/clique families, by Montanaro–Shao.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> binary-query information is needed over arbitrary <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span>-edge graphs; bounded-degree families give <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Classical parity-query reconstruction attains polynomial or logarithmically larger bounds through learning the quadratic form.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Montanaro–Shao hidden-graph learning</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.08611\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for learning a hidden graph and beyond</em></a>. Table1 and parity-query section.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-hidden-graph-learning\">Montanaro–Shao hidden-graph learning</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-q30\">Learning a graph with edge-existence queries</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-hidden-graph-learning\">→ Montanaro–Shao hidden-graph learning</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "spatial-lattice-search": {
    "title": "Spatial search on a lattice",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Spatial search on a lattice</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Spatial search on a lattice)</span></h2>\n<p>A <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>-dimensional lattice of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span> vertices has a unique marked vertex, recognized by an oracle. Find it with success at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>, using adjacent-vertex movements and queries at the walker&#x27;s position. Complexity counts local movement and oracle steps.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> steps for fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span> by Aaronson–Ambainis; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span> by Tulsi&#x27;s ancilla-controlled walk. The ancilla raises success to a constant.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical steps and queries, achieved by a systematic traversal and matched by unstructured-search query lower bounds. The 2026 electrostatic theorem gives a matching <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum local-step lower bound in two dimensions.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>A quadratic speedup for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>, and factor <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></span> in two dimensions.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The classical lower bound is unconditional in the model charging local movement and queries.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Digital position register: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>N</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span> qubits plus coin and ancilla; a physical lattice encoding uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span> sites.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Tulsi ancilla-controlled spatial search; Aaronson–Ambainis spatial search</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0801.0497\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Faster quantum walk algorithm for the two dimensional spatial search</em></a>. Ancilla-controlled walk and O(sqrt(N log N)) theorem</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://toc.ilab.sztaki.hu/articles/v001a004/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Search of Spatial Regions</em></a>. Section 7: O(sqrt n)-qubit disjointness protocol; spatial-search theorems</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23560\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Electrostatic Limits on Quantum Spatial Search</em></a>. Main local-step lower bound; October 2026 preprint</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/tulsi_spatial_search\">→ Tulsi ancilla-controlled spatial search</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/aaronson_ambainis_spatial_search\">→ Aaronson–Ambainis spatial search</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "factoring": {
    "title": "Integer factorization",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Integer factorization</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Integer factorization)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an odd composite integer <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span> in binary, output primes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> and positive integers <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></span>. Write <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>N</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span>. The balanced semiprime case underlies RSA. The explicit-integer comparison uses quantum gate complexity and classical bit complexity.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> total gates, Shor and Regev factoring.</strong> Shor&#x27;s order-finding algorithm uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates with elementary arithmetic and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with fast arithmetic; its logical space is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Regev uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates per circuit and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mi>&#x02309;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math></span> repetitions, followed by polynomial-time lattice postprocessing. The total quantum gate count is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, with shorter individual coherent computations. Pilatte establishes unconditional correctness for the recent factoring framework.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>9</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> heuristic time, general number field sieve.</strong> Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. This is the standard fastest heuristic comparator for general balanced RSA semiprimes. It includes smooth-relation collection and sparse linear algebra. Rudich–Rousseau also announce deterministic worst-case <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time using the Boolean tensor-contraction theorem. That result specifies a deterministic guarantee; its <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> exponent does not quantitatively rank it against heuristic GNFS.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Subexponential to polynomial in the input bit length, through Fourier sampling.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Strong evidence under the standard assumption that factoring is classically hard in polynomial time. The algorithms give upper bounds in the explicit-integer model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Small factoring circuits have been demonstrated. RSA-sized instances require fault tolerance and polynomial arithmetic workspace. Gidney&#x27;s RSA-2048 estimate uses about 1,409 logical qubits and projects fewer than one million physical qubits and an approximately one-week runtime under the specified architecture assumptions.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Shor and Regev factoring</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9508027\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Shor, <em>Polynomial-Time Algorithms for Prime Factorization and Discrete Logarithms on a Quantum Computer</em> (1997)</a>. Section 5: order finding and factor extraction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.06572\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Regev, <em>An Efficient Quantum Factoring Algorithm</em> (2023)</a>. Theorem 1.1: circuit cost and repetition count; Section 3: quantum procedure.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/forum-of-mathematics-pi/article/unconditional-correctness-of-recent-quantum-algorithms-for-factoring-and-computing-discrete-logarithms/3077F5BD48D1F62000B1ED800EB86308\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Pilatte, <em>Unconditional Correctness of Recent Quantum Algorithms for Factoring and Computing Discrete Logarithms</em> (2026)</a>. Main factoring correctness theorem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.15917\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gidney, <em>How to Factor 2048 Bit RSA Integers with Less Than a Million Noisy Qubits</em> (2025)</a>. Resource-estimate summary and architecture assumptions.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://hexagonmath.org/2610.00600v1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Rudich and Rousseau, <em>Deterministic Factoring in Time <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span></em> (2026)</a>. Main deterministic factoring statement.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithms-in-finite-fields\">Finite-field discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm\">Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a01\">Finite abelian-group decomposition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a02\">Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a05\">Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a06\">Ideal operations in finite rings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a08\">Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a10\">Semidirect discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q45\">Testing one-to-one periodicity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithm-concept-learning\">Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">→ Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/regev-factoring\">→ Regev quantum factoring</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "discrete-logarithms-in-finite-fields": {
    "title": "Discrete logarithms in finite fields",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Discrete logarithms in finite fields</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Discrete logarithms in finite fields)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a finite field <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, an element <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span> of order <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math></span>, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></math></span>. The input length is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> plus the field representation. Prime-field Diffie–Hellman is the principal cryptographic application. The explicit-field comparison uses quantum gate complexity and classical bit complexity; the black-box comparison counts group operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical qubits, Shor discrete logarithms.</strong> For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>q</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span>, this conventional bound includes reversible field exponentiation and Fourier sampling; fast multiplication improves the arithmetic cost. The group-operation query count is polynomial in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>. The output is the classical exponent <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span>, with constant success probability amplified by repetition.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>9</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> heuristic time in general large prime fields, number field sieve discrete logarithms.</strong> The bound includes relation collection, sparse linear algebra and individual-logarithm descent. Special field representations can improve the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span>-constant. For applicable families in small fixed characteristic, Barbulescu–Gaudry–Joux–Thomé and subsequent rigorous developments give quasi-polynomial time <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The characteristic and field representation therefore determine the matched classical comparator.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Subexponential to polynomial for large prime fields; quasi-polynomial to polynomial in the stated small-characteristic families. The quantum mechanism is abelian Fourier sampling.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Strong conditional evidence under the corresponding classical discrete-logarithm hardness assumption; generic-group lower bounds apply to the separately specified oracle model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Cryptographic fields require fault-tolerant reversible arithmetic. The data and arithmetic workspace use <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical qubits in space-efficient implementations, with polynomially many coherent field multiplications. Physical-qubit counts depend on the chosen arithmetic circuits and error-correction architecture.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Shor discrete logarithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9508027\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Shor, <em>Polynomial-Time Algorithms for Prime Factorization and Discrete Logarithms on a Quantum Computer</em> (1997)</a>. Section 6, equations (6.1)–(6.3): discrete-logarithm algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1306.4244\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Barbulescu, Gaudry, Joux and Thomé, <em>A Heuristic Quasi-Polynomial Algorithm for Discrete Logarithm in Finite Fields of Small Characteristic</em> (2014)</a>. Theorem 3: the quasi-polynomial bound for sparse medium subfield representations; descent construction in the following sections.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/factoring\">Integer factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm\">Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a01\">Finite abelian-group decomposition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a02\">Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a05\">Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a06\">Ideal operations in finite rings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a08\">Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a10\">Semidirect discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q45\">Testing one-to-one periodicity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithm-concept-learning\">Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">→ Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm": {
    "title": "Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an elliptic curve <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span> and prime-order group <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> of order <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math></span>, a nonzero point <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math></span>, and a point <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math></span>, find\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>such&#x000A0;that</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nThe curve and points are supplied by field coordinates; let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>p</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span>. This is the discrete-logarithm problem underlying prime-field elliptic-curve public-key cryptography.\nThe explicit-coordinate comparison uses quantum gate complexity and expected classical bit complexity; the generic-group comparison counts group-oracle queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical qubits, Shor&#x27;s discrete-logarithm algorithm.</strong> This conventional bound uses reversible elliptic-curve arithmetic; implementation-dependent logarithmic factors arise from modular inversion and circuit synthesis. The input and output are classical, and the success probability can be amplified by repetition. Fast arithmetic and optimized point-addition circuits improve the detailed gate counts.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> expected time, the Akshima et al. index-calculus algorithm.</strong> The result uses the Boolean tensor-contraction theorem in <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em>. It applies to explicit prime-field coordinates and extends to fixed cofactors. Its analysis balances relation-search and factor-base costs of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0224D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. In the generic-group oracle model, Pollard&#x27;s rho and the matching generic lower bound give <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>r</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical group operations.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Subexponential to polynomial for explicit prime-field curves, through Fourier sampling. The generic-group comparison is exponential to polynomial.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Strong conditional evidence for explicit curves under polynomial-time ECDLP hardness; a rigorous separation in the generic-group model. The subexponential coordinate algorithm is stated in a 2026 preprint and depends on the tensor-contraction theorem.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Cryptographic instances require fault tolerance. The Roetteler–Naehrig–Svore–Lauter construction for a 256-bit prime field uses 2,330 logical qubits and approximately <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.26</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mn>11</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> Toffoli gates. Its arithmetic workspace is polynomial in the field bit length.</p><p><small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, the known general-curve classical bound was <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> from Pollard&#x27;s rho, and Shor&#x27;s algorithm claimed an exponential-to-polynomial speedup.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Shor discrete logarithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9508027\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Shor, <em>Polynomial-Time Algorithms for Prime Factorization and Discrete Logarithms on a Quantum Computer</em> (1997)</a>. Section 6, equations (6.1)–(6.3): discrete-logarithm algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1706.06752\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Roetteler, Naehrig, Svore and Lauter, <em>Quantum Resource Estimates for Computing Elliptic Curve Discrete Logarithms</em> (ASIACRYPT 2017)</a>. Section 2.2 and Figures 1–2: ECDLP circuit; Section 4, Algorithm 1: point addition; Section 5, Table 2: resource counts.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/bochum-crypto/subexponential-ecdl/blob/main/subexponential-ecdl.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Akshima et al., <em>A Subexponential Time Algorithm for Elliptic Curve Discrete Logarithm</em>, v0.2 (29 October 2026)</a>. Theorem 4, p. 11; Section 3.4, pp. 15–17, and Figure 2, p. 16: full algorithm; Section 3.5: running time.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/factoring\">Integer factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithms-in-finite-fields\">Finite-field discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a01\">Finite abelian-group decomposition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a02\">Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a05\">Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a06\">Ideal operations in finite rings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a08\">Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a10\">Semidirect discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q45\">Testing one-to-one periodicity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithm-concept-learning\">Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">→ Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group": {
    "title": "Order finding in a black-box cyclic group",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Order finding in a black-box cyclic group</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Order finding in a black-box cyclic group)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an element <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span> in a uniquely encoded finite group and an upper bound <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math></span> on its order, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>:</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Multiplication and inversion are supplied as reversible group-operation oracles. Order finding is the algebraic primitive connecting Fourier sampling to factorization and discrete logarithms. Complexity is measured in group-oracle operations together with quantum gates and classical bit operations polynomial in the encoding length.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> group operations, Shor order finding.</strong> The auxiliary gate cost is polynomial in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></math></span> and the group-element encoding length. An <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>-qubit Fourier register supplies the required precision. Classical continued-fraction and least-common-multiple computations are polynomial in these parameters. For modular multiplication, the gate bounds specialize to entry 61.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>R</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> group operations, baby-step–giant-step and collision methods.</strong> Baby-step–giant-step uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>R</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> storage; collision methods supply low-memory alternatives in their applicable order-search settings. A tighter interval or a known factored multiple of the order can improve the bound. In explicit arithmetic groups, factorization and index calculus give additional instance-specific comparators. The displayed comparison supplies only the group-operation access and an order bound.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential to polynomial in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></math></span> relative to generic order-search algorithms. Fourier sampling extracts global periodic information from the group operation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence for a separation in this general formulation; the input promises and access model determine applicable lower bounds. The concrete cryptographic consequences occur in factoring and discrete logarithms.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The implementation needs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> control qubits plus group-element and reversible-arithmetic workspace. Explicit cryptographic groups lead to long fault-tolerant multiplication circuits; the number of logical qubits is polynomial in the input length.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Shor order finding; Watrous solvable-group order algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9508027\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Shor, <em>Polynomial-Time Algorithms for Prime Factorization and Discrete Logarithms on a Quantum Computer</em> (1997)</a>. Section 5: the order-finding computation and continued-fraction reconstruction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0011023\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Watrous, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Solvable Groups</em> (2001)</a>. Theorem 1: quantum computation of group order in the black-box group model; the cyclic case provides the order-finding setting.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/factoring\">Integer factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithms-in-finite-fields\">Finite-field discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm\">Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a01\">Finite abelian-group decomposition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a02\">Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a05\">Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a06\">Ideal operations in finite rings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a08\">Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a10\">Semidirect discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q45\">Testing one-to-one periodicity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithm-concept-learning\">Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/watrous-group-order\">Watrous solvable-group order algorithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-a24\">Zeta functions of curves over finite fields</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">→ Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/watrous-group-order\">→ Watrous solvable-group order algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "pell-s-equation-and-the-real-quadratic-regulator": {
    "title": "Pell's equation and the real quadratic regulator",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Pell&#x27;s equation and the real quadratic regulator</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Pell&#x27;s equation and the real quadratic regulator)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a nonsquare positive integer <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>, let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> be the least positive unit with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>. Compute the regulator <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> to prescribed polynomial-bit precision, or a compact representation of the fundamental solution. Compact output is the natural representation because <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></span> can have exponentially many bits in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>. Complexity is measured in quantum gates and classical bit operations, including the required output precision.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>precision</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Hallgren regulator and Pell algorithms.</strong> The bound uses compact output and includes ideal arithmetic, discretization of the real-periodic function and Fourier precision. A polynomial number of Fourier samples suffices to reconstruct the regulator to the specified accuracy. The compact representation describes the fundamental solution even when its fully expanded integers are exponentially long in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> heuristic time, Buchmann infrastructure and index calculus.</strong> The comparison uses the same discriminant-scale parameter and compact-output convention. Smooth relations among small prime ideals and integer linear algebra determine the regulator. The complexity assumes the standard smoothness and relation-generation hypotheses. Continued-fraction enumeration provides an alternative whose time depends on the length of the period of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Subexponential to polynomial in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>, through Fourier sampling of a periodic function over the reals.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence from sustained study of classical regulator algorithms and real-quadratic cryptosystems. The quantum polynomial-time bound is rigorous with its precision and output conventions.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomially many logical qubits in the input and precision lengths suffice. Reduced-ideal arithmetic, coherent function evaluation and Fourier precision require fault tolerance at cryptographic scale. The asymptotic algorithm supplies a polynomial-space bound; concrete counts depend on the arithmetic implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Hallgren regulator and Pell algorithms</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cse.psu.edu/ sjh26/pell.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Hallgren, <em>Polynomial-Time Quantum Algorithms for Pell&#x27;s Equation and the Principal Ideal Problem</em> (JACM 2007)</a>. Algorithm 2.1: reduced-ideal evaluation; Algorithm 3.1: approximate period finding for the regulator. The introduction gives the classical subexponential comparison.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/hallgren-regulator\">Hallgren real-period finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/the-principal-ideal-problem\">Principal ideal problem</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/hallgren-regulator\">→ Hallgren real-period finding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "the-principal-ideal-problem": {
    "title": "The principal ideal problem",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>The principal ideal problem</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(The principal ideal problem)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a number field <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span> by a defining polynomial and an integral ideal <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">O</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> by a basis, determine whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">O</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>; if so, output a compact representation of a generator <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow></math></span>. The relevant size parameters are the degree, logarithmic discriminant, ideal encoding length, and output precision. Complexity is measured in quantum gates and classical bit operations on the number-field and ideal encodings, with compact output.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: Polynomial time in the input parameters, Hallgren and Biasse–Song principal-ideal algorithms.</strong> Hallgren treats real quadratic fields. Biasse–Song give the arbitrary-degree result through their quantum <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>-unit algorithm, using compact generators and precision sufficient for the associated logarithmic lattice. The input parameters include degree, logarithmic discriminant and ideal encoding size. The principal-ideal theorem is the relevant search statement; the related class-group theorem has a GRH hypothesis.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> under the standard index-calculus hypotheses in quadratic fields, relation-collection algorithms.</strong> The classical work combines factor-base relations, unit computation and integer linear algebra. The quadratic-field family supplies the direct subexponential comparison for identical inputs and compact generators. In arbitrary degree, the strongest applicable classical bounds depend on the relationship between degree and discriminant; specialized field families also admit distinct relation-generation tradeoffs.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Subexponential to polynomial in the matched quadratic-field regime. Quantum Fourier methods supply the hidden-lattice information needed for the generator.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence for a classical separation, based on the established number-field algorithmic frontier. Polynomial quantum solvability follows from the cited theorems with their representation and precision assumptions.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial logical space and coherent number-field arithmetic are required. Cryptographic-size computations belong to the fault-tolerant regime, with logical-qubit counts determined by field degree, coefficient precision and the chosen reversible arithmetic.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Hallgren principal-ideal algorithm; Biasse–Song S-unit computation</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cse.psu.edu/ sjh26/pell.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Hallgren, <em>Polynomial-Time Quantum Algorithms for Pell&#x27;s Equation and the Principal Ideal Problem</em> (JACM 2007)</a>. Algorithm 4.1: computing the distance of an ideal and the principal-ideal procedure.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://fangsong.info/files/pubs/BS_SODA16.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Biasse and Song, <em>Efficient Quantum Algorithms for Computing Class Groups and Solving the Principal Ideal Problem in Arbitrary Degree Number Fields</em> (SODA 2016)</a>. Theorem 1.1: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>-units; Theorem 1.3 and Algorithm 2: principal ideal problem.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/hallgren-regulator\">Hallgren real-period finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/pell-s-equation-and-the-real-quadratic-regulator\">Pell equation and regulator</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/biasse-song-units\">Biasse–Song S-unit computation</a>: <a href=\"#entry/computing-ideal-class-groups\">Ideal class groups</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/hallgren-regulator\">→ Hallgren real-period finding</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/biasse-song-units\">→ Biasse–Song S-unit computation</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "computing-ideal-class-groups": {
    "title": "Computing ideal class groups",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Computing ideal class groups</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Computing ideal class groups)</span></h2>\n<p>For a number field <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span>, compute invariant factors and represented generators of the finite abelian ideal class group <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">O</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Equivalently, produce an explicit decomposition into cyclic groups together with ideals representing their generators. The class group parametrizes ideal classes and important isogeny group actions. Complexity is measured in quantum gates and classical bit operations on explicit number-field and ideal representations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: Polynomial time under GRH, Biasse–Song class-group computation.</strong> The parameters are the number-field degree, logarithmic discriminant and standard encoding lengths. The output gives invariant factors and represented ideal generators. The cost includes finding the relevant <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>-unit relation lattice and classical Smith normal form; GRH bounds the small-prime generating set used by the reduction.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> in imaginary quadratic fields, Hafner–McCurley and Buchmann index calculus.</strong> The stated bounds use the analytic and smoothness hypotheses of the corresponding relation-collection algorithm. Factor-base relations and integer linear algebra recover a presentation of the same finite abelian class group. For fields of arbitrary degree, the applicable bound varies with degree and discriminant; the imaginary-quadratic family is the explicit matched comparison here.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Subexponential to polynomial in the quadratic-field regime, through hidden-lattice Fourier methods.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence for separation. The quantum polynomial bound is conditional on GRH, and the classical subexponential algorithms have their stated analytic or smoothness assumptions.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial logical space supports coherent number-field arithmetic and high-precision Fourier sampling. Cryptographic instances require fault tolerance; concrete workspace and gate counts depend on the field family and arithmetic circuits.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Biasse–Song S-unit computation</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://fangsong.info/files/pubs/BS_SODA16.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Biasse and Song, <em>Efficient Quantum Algorithms for Computing Class Groups and Solving the Principal Ideal Problem in Arbitrary Degree Number Fields</em> (SODA 2016)</a>. Theorem 1.2: class groups under GRH; Algorithm 1: reduction of class-group computation to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>-units; Theorem 1.1: the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>-unit quantum subroutine.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/biasse-song-units\">Biasse–Song S-unit computation</a>: <a href=\"#entry/the-principal-ideal-problem\">Principal ideal problem</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/biasse-song-units\">→ Biasse–Song S-unit computation</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "vectorization-for-commutative-isogeny-actions": {
    "title": "Vectorization for commutative isogeny actions",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Vectorization for commutative isogeny actions</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Vectorization for commutative isogeny group actions)</span></h2>\n<p>Let a finite abelian group <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> act freely and transitively on a set <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math></span>. Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. For CRS and CSIDH, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> is an ideal class group and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math></span> consists of appropriately oriented elliptic curves. Write <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The group-action comparison counts action-oracle queries and auxiliary operations; the explicit-isogeny comparison includes quantum gate and classical bit costs of action evaluation.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msup><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time, queries and QROM complexity, Castryck–Vander Meeren hidden-shift algorithm.</strong> Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> is any fixed prime, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> are fixed nonnegative integers, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msup><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msup><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>g</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The bound assumes efficient representations and action evaluation, and uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> qubits. It is polynomial when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msup><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. For general <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span>, Kuperberg&#x27;s collimation sieve takes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum space and subexponential classical space; Regev&#x27;s polynomial-space variant takes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time. For explicit isogenies, Childs–Jao–Soukharev include class-group action evaluation and obtain overall <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum complexity under GRH.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> action evaluations, baby-step–giant-step or Pollard-rho vectorization.</strong> This is the generic freely transitive action comparison. Each action evaluation carries its implementation cost when curves and ideals are supplied explicitly. The action model represents the hidden-shift functions by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. Efficient classical structure specific to a curve family belongs in its full arithmetic comparison.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential to polynomial in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span> for generic actions with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msup><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> and fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>. General abelian hidden shifts give exponential-to-subexponential improvement, with power saving in the exponent. These comparisons use the action-oracle model; explicit isogeny costs include arithmetic evaluation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous quantum upper bounds and partial evidence relative to the best known classical vectorization algorithms. For explicit isogeny actions, classical arithmetic structure and action-evaluation cost enter the comparison.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The bounded-torsion algorithm uses polynomially many qubits and polynomial QROM when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msup><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. General-group variants include <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum space for collimation. Their subexponential state generation and classical storage, together with reversible isogeny arithmetic, make cryptographic-size execution a fault-tolerant workload.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Castryck–Vander Meeren and Childs–Jao–Soukharev isogeny algorithms; Kuperberg and Regev hidden-shift sieves</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/1366\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Castryck and Vander Meeren, <em>Two Remarks on the Vectorization Problem</em> (2022)</a>. Theorem 1: the bound in terms of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msup><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span> and polynomial quantum storage; Section 2: vectorization and hidden shift; Section 5: bounded-torsion hidden shifts.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1112.3333\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kuperberg, <em>Another Subexponential-Time Quantum Algorithm for the Dihedral Hidden Subgroup Problem</em> (TQC 2013)</a>. Algorithms 4.1 and 4.4: collimation and the collimation sieve.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0406151\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Regev, <em>A Subexponential Time Algorithm for the Dihedral Hidden Subgroup Problem with Polynomial Space</em> (2004)</a>. Polynomial-space sieve construction and its recursive combination procedure.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1012.4019\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Childs, Jao and Soukharev, <em>Constructing Elliptic Curve Isogenies in Quantum Subexponential Time</em> (2014)</a>. Algorithm 2 and Theorem 4.6: group-action evaluation; Algorithm 3 and Theorem 5.4: full isogeny algorithm; Theorems 5.1–5.2: hidden-shift subroutines.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/kuperberg\">Hidden-shift sieves of Kuperberg and Regev</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/kuperberg\">→ Hidden-shift sieves of Kuperberg and Regev</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "supersingular-isogeny-path-finding": {
    "title": "Supersingular isogeny path finding",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Supersingular isogeny path finding</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Supersingular isogeny path finding)</span></h2>\n<p>Given supersingular elliptic curves <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span>, output an explicitly evaluable isogeny <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. One may represent it as a composition of low-degree isogenies. This is the unrestricted path problem underlying several isogeny constructions. Complexity is measured in field-operation-scale time, with polynomial-bit arithmetic translated into quantum gates and classical bit operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time, Biasse–Jao–Sankar supersingular isogeny search.</strong> Amplitude amplification reaches the subgraph of curves defined over <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, after which the algorithm uses its abelian structure. Reversible isogeny evaluation and the endpoint-recognition predicate determine the arithmetic factors. The exponent absorbs the subexponential costs required by the second stage.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time and memory, Wesolowski supersingular isogeny algorithm.</strong> The result uses a heuristic assumption on smoothness of the relevant random integers. Non-scalar endomorphism computations and reductions between endomorphism and isogeny problems produce an explicitly evaluable path. The <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> exponent includes superpolynomial factors in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span>. For regimes using the random-walk approach, Delfs–Galbraith provides a <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> alternative with different memory requirements.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup through a smaller exponential constant in the input bit length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span>, from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math></span>. Amplitude amplification is an essential quantum component.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence for explicit curves. The classical <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> comparison is a 2026 preprint with a stated smoothness heuristic.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Curve registers have polynomial size in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span>. Reversible field arithmetic and exponentially many coherent operations require fault tolerance; runtime is the principal obstacle at cryptographic sizes.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Biasse–Jao–Sankar supersingular isogeny search</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://cacr.uwaterloo.ca/techreports/2014/cacr2014-24.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Biasse, Jao and Sankar, <em>A Quantum Algorithm for Computing Isogenies Between Supersingular Elliptic Curves</em> (INDOCRYPT 2014)</a>. Theorem 1: main complexity result; Algorithm 1: complete isogeny procedure; Algorithms 2–3: reaching and navigating the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> subgraph.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1486\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Wesolowski, <em>The Supersingular Isogeny Problem in Time and Memory <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span></em> (2026)</a>. Main complexity statement and smoothness assumption in the abstract.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/biasse-jao-sankar\">→ Biasse–Jao–Sankar supersingular isogeny algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "sidh-secret-isogeny-recovery": {
    "title": "SIDH secret-isogeny recovery",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>SIDH secret-isogeny recovery</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(SIDH secret-isogeny recovery with torsion images)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a supersingular starting curve <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>, a secret isogeny image <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, and the published images <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> of a basis of coprime torsion, recover the kernel of the secret isogeny <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> of prescribed smooth degree. This input is the public-key recovery problem of SIDH and SIKE. The comparison uses quantum isogeny-oracle calls and the resulting gate cost, and classical bit complexity on the full SIDH public key.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> isogeny-oracle calls, Tani claw finding applied to split isogenies.</strong> In the balanced SIDH parameter regime, the two half-isogeny lists contain about <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> candidates each. Quantum claw search identifies matching intermediate endpoints; the stated exponent counts isogeny evaluations with the required coherent list access. Arithmetic and memory-access factors enter the full gate cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Castryck–Decru and Robert SIDH key recovery.</strong> The full public key includes torsion images, which supply the extra information used by product-isogeny attacks. Robert&#x27;s higher-dimensional abelian-variety framework gives polynomial-time key recovery in the specified SIDH setting. The polynomial algorithm recovers the prescribed secret isogeny or equivalent secret-key information from the same explicit public input.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The problem has a polynomial-time classical solution; the exponential quantum claw bound has no speedup over this comparator.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The classical key-recovery theorem determines the comparison for SIDH public keys with their auxiliary torsion data.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The quantum claw approach requires exponentially large coherent data structures and deep reversible isogeny arithmetic. The polynomial-time attack uses classical computation. For the quantum construction, both coherent memory and total circuit depth determine the required fault-tolerant resources.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Tani quantum-walk claw finding</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0708.2584\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Tani, <em>Claw Finding Algorithms Using Quantum Walk</em> (2009)</a>. Theorem 8 and the <span class=\"small-caps\">ClawSearch</span> procedure: balanced claw-finding bound used for the split-isogeny search.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/975\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Castryck and Decru, <em>An Efficient Key Recovery Attack on SIDH</em> (2022)</a>. Main key-recovery construction using auxiliary torsion images.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.math.u-bordeaux.fr/ damienrobert/pro/publications/articles/breaking_sidh.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Robert, <em>Breaking SIDH in Polynomial Time</em> (EUROCRYPT 2023)</a>. Theorem 1.1: polynomial-time recovery through higher-dimensional isogenies.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/tani-claw\">Tani quantum-walk claw finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/claw\">Claw finding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/claw/double-encryption-key-recovery\">Double-encryption key recovery</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/tani-claw\">→ Tani quantum-walk claw finding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "the-euclidean-shortest-vector-problem": {
    "title": "The Euclidean shortest vector problem",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>The Euclidean shortest vector problem</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(The Euclidean shortest vector problem)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a basis <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> of a rank-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> lattice <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> minimizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>. Exact SVP is the central subroutine in block lattice reduction and lattice cryptanalysis. The comparison uses arithmetic or bit-operation time with polynomial factors in input precision suppressed, and quantum gate time in the stated coherent-memory model; enumeration additionally counts tree-oracle operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.247</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> rigorous time, Hhan–Kim–Kim–Laarhoven; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.2563</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> heuristic time, reusable quantum walks.</strong> The superlattice bound is stated in the November 2026 preprint and has a <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.247</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> quantum time-space bound. The heuristic Bonnetain et al. sieve uses coherent access to exponential lists and the standard random-vector assumptions of lattice sieving. Aono–Nguyen–Shen quantum enumeration instead takes roughly <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> operations for a search tree with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></span> nodes, using polynomial quantum workspace. Aggarwal–Chen–Kumar–Shen provide a separate rigorous memory tradeoff: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.950</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time with polynomial qubits and exponential classical memory, or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.835</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> with QRAM.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.409</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> rigorous time, Hhan–Kim–Kim–Laarhoven; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.2925</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> heuristic time, Becker–Ducas–Gama–Laarhoven sieving.</strong> The superlattice algorithm uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.259</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> space. The heuristic sieve uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.2075</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> space. These are distinct comparisons: the rigorous quantum exponent is paired with the rigorous classical exponent, and the heuristic pair shares its distribution assumptions. Classical enumeration costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></span> node operations for the specified tree. Input precision contributes polynomial factors to the displayed dimension exponents.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedups through smaller exponential constants in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>. Enumeration gives a quadratic improvement in tree size through quantum backtracking; quantum walks and amplification accelerate sieve subroutines.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence for a separation. Rigorous upper bounds and heuristic sieve estimates have separate assumptions; the superlattice results are stated in a 2026 preprint.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fast sieves need exponential coherent memory and fault-tolerant access to vector lists. Enumeration uses polynomial logical qubits at greater runtime. The polynomial-qubit BDD construction explicitly separates its quantum workspace from exponential classical memory and QRAM.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Hhan et al. superlattice SVP; reusable quantum walks; quantum lattice enumeration; quantum SVP via BDD</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/2761\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Hhan, Kim, Kim and Laarhoven, <em>Superlattice Methods for the Shortest Vector Problem and Discrete Gaussian Sampling</em> (2026)</a>. Main classical and quantum SVP complexity statements.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.14023\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bonnetain et al., <em>Finding Many Collisions via Reusable Quantum Walks: Application to Lattice Sieving</em> (EUROCRYPT 2023)</a>. Algorithm 2: reusable collision walk; Section 7.1: the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2563</mn></mrow></math></span> SVP exponent and the classical sieve comparator.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/546\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Aono, Nguyen and Shen, <em>Quantum Lattice Enumeration and Tweaking Discrete Pruning</em> (ASIACRYPT 2018)</a>. Algorithm 1 and Theorem 6: tree minimization; Section 4.2, Theorems 7–9: cylinder pruning; Section 7: quantum discrete pruning.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/35122/35122.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Aggarwal, Chen, Kumar and Shen, <em>Improved Classical and Quantum Algorithms for the Shortest Vector Problem via Bounded Distance Decoding</em> (SIAM J. Comput. 2025)</a>. Theorem 1.3: quantum time-space bounds; Section 6: the SVP algorithms.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/backtracking\">Quantum backtracking and branch and bound</a>: <a href=\"#entry/constraint-satisfaction-by-backtracking\">Constraint satisfaction by backtracking</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-optimization-by-branch-and-bound\">Branch and bound</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/knapsack-and-binary-integer-programming\">Knapsack</a>, <a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-closest-vector-problem\">Closest vector problem</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/superlattice-svp\">Hhan–Kim–Kim–Laarhoven quantum SVP</a>: <a href=\"#entry/isomorphism-to-the-standard-integer-lattice\">Isomorphism to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span></a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/reusable-collision-walks\">Reusable quantum walks for lattice sieving</a>: <a href=\"#entry/learning-with-errors-with-classical-samples\">Learning with errors</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/svp-via-bdd\">Aggarwal–Chen–Kumar–Shen quantum SVP via BDD</a>: <a href=\"#entry/isomorphism-to-the-standard-integer-lattice\">Isomorphism to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span></a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/backtracking\">→ Quantum backtracking and branch and bound</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/superlattice-svp\">→ Hhan–Kim–Kim–Laarhoven quantum SVP</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/reusable-collision-walks\">→ Reusable quantum walks for lattice sieving</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/svp-via-bdd\">→ Aggarwal–Chen–Kumar–Shen quantum SVP via BDD</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "isomorphism-to-the-standard-integer-lattice": {
    "title": "Isomorphism to the standard integer lattice",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Isomorphism to the standard integer lattice</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Isomorphism to the standard integer lattice)</span></h2>\n<p>Given the Gram matrix of a lattice <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span> promised isometric to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, recover an orthonormal integral lattice basis, equivalently an explicit isometry <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. This is the standard-integer-lattice case of lattice isomorphism, relevant to signature constructions such as HAWK. Complexity is measured in quantum gate time with the stated QRAM access and in classical bit-operation time, suppressing polynomial input-precision factors.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.1235</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time, Ducas reduction with the Hhan–Kim–Kim–Laarhoven SVP subroutine.</strong> The exponent is obtained by substituting the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.247</mn><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> quantum SVP bound into the reduction to dimension <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02243;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>. It is a direct consequence of the two results. The Aggarwal–Chen–Kumar–Shen implementation gives a separately quantified tradeoff: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.417</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.147</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> QRAM, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.25</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> classical storage and polynomial additional qubits. Both computations recover norm-one directions sufficient to reconstruct the integral isometry.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.2045</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time, the same reduction with classical superlattice SVP.</strong> Substitution of the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.409</mn><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> classical SVP theorem gives the displayed exponent. Classical discrete-Gaussian SVP with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time yields the alternative <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> bound. The reduction and output task are identical in both comparisons; the dimension-halving exponents retain the assumptions and storage costs of their chosen SVP subroutines.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup through a smaller exponential constant in the lattice rank. The quantitative comparison follows from the matched classical and quantum shortest-vector algorithms.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence for separation. The two fastest displayed exponents depend on the 2026 superlattice preprint; the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.417</mn></mrow></math></span> quantum implementation has a published rigorous resource analysis.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fault-tolerant computation with exponential storage is required for these algorithms. The published construction distinguishes polynomial logical workspace, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.147</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> QRAM cells and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.25</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> classical memory; the superlattice substitution inherits its own exponential time-space requirement.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Ducas lattice-isomorphism reduction with quantum SVP</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/35122/35122.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Aggarwal, Chen, Kumar and Shen, <em>Improved Classical and Quantum Algorithms for the Shortest Vector Problem via Bounded Distance Decoding</em> (2025)</a>. Section 8: reduction and quantum algorithm for isomorphism to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>; Theorem 1.3: its SVP subroutine.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/2761\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Hhan, Kim, Kim and Laarhoven, <em>Superlattice Methods for the Shortest Vector Problem and Discrete Gaussian Sampling</em> (2026)</a>. Main classical and quantum SVP results, substituted into the dimension-halving reduction.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/superlattice-svp\">Hhan–Kim–Kim–Laarhoven quantum SVP</a>: <a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-shortest-vector-problem\">Shortest vector problem</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/svp-via-bdd\">Aggarwal–Chen–Kumar–Shen quantum SVP via BDD</a>: <a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-shortest-vector-problem\">Shortest vector problem</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/superlattice-svp\">→ Hhan–Kim–Kim–Laarhoven quantum SVP</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/svp-via-bdd\">→ Aggarwal–Chen–Kumar–Shen quantum SVP via BDD</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "learning-parity-with-noisy-quantum-examples": {
    "title": "Learning parity with noisy quantum examples",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Learning parity with noisy quantum examples</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Learning parity with noisy quantum examples)</span></h2>\n<p>Recover <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span> from a noisy quantum example oracle preparing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math></span>, followed by a specified noise channel. The classical learner receives the same examples measured in the computational basis. For classification noise, the final bit is flipped independently with constant probability <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>, producing ordinary learning-parity-with-noise samples <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The comparison measures quantum example queries and elementary gates, and classical sample complexity and bit-operation time for measured examples.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> examples and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> processing, Cross–Smith–Smolin noisy parity learning.</strong> These bounds hold for fixed noise rate in the analyzed noisy quantum-example models, with failure probability at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Under classification noise alone, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> coherent examples suffice: the Hadamard-basis parity information survives a flip of the result qubit. The logarithmic dependence on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> covers bitwise reconstruction in the depolarizing-noise treatment. Noise parameters affect the constants.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time and samples at fixed noise, BKW-type LPN algorithms.</strong> The classical input consists of computational-basis measurements of the same examples, giving uniformly random labeled samples with independent classification noise. BKW combines samples to eliminate coordinate blocks, trading increased noise against sample count and memory. The fastest concrete attack depends on the sample budget and noise rate. These are classical noisy-sample costs for the stated access model.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Subexponential to polynomial in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, through Fourier interference in the quantum examples. The availability of coherent examples is part of the input model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Strong conditional evidence from classical LPN hardness. The quantum learning theorem is rigorous for its noise channel; the comparison invokes the hardness of learning from measured examples.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The data register uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> qubits and shallow Hadamard-basis processing. Ristè et al. demonstrated noisy parity learning with a small superconducting processor. This supports the small-instance category; scaling the supplied example oracle and controlling noise determine larger-instance feasibility.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Cross–Smith–Smolin noisy quantum parity learning</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5088\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Cross, Smith and Smolin, <em>Quantum Learning Robust Against Noise</em> (2015)</a>. Sections “Learning from ideal queries” and “Learning in the presence of noise”; equations (7)–(8): transformed examples and classification noise; the depolarizing-noise subsection gives reconstruction bounds.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1512.06069\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ristè et al., <em>Demonstration of Quantum Advantage in Machine Learning</em> (2017)</a>. Experimental learning circuits and sample-complexity results in the main text and figures.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/noisy-parity\">→ Cross–Smith–Smolin noisy quantum parity learning</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "the-euclidean-closest-vector-problem": {
    "title": "The Euclidean closest vector problem",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>The Euclidean closest vector problem</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(The Euclidean closest vector problem)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a rank-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> lattice <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and target <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span> minimizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>. CVP models noisy lattice decoding and underlies attacks on lattice cryptography. The tree comparison counts quantum and classical node-oracle operations; total algorithms use quantum gates and classical bit operations including lattice arithmetic.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> node operations, quantum lattice enumeration.</strong> Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></span> is the size of the classical enumeration tree for the chosen basis and search radius, and its depth is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>. Radius refinement and witness recovery introduce polynomial factors. The arithmetic cost also depends on basis bit length and precision. This bound gives a matched acceleration of the specified tree, while general exact CVP can also be solved by running the classical <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> algorithm on quantum hardware.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time and space, Aggarwal–Dadush–Stephens-Davidowitz exact CVP; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></span> nodes for the matched enumeration tree.</strong> The general exact algorithm uses discrete-Gaussian sampling and a reduction through clusters of approximate closest vectors. For the promise <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>dist</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1.039</mn><mi>&#x02026;</mi></mrow></math></span>, Hhan gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> classical time and space. Its lattice model and distance guarantee are part of that bound. The appropriate comparator is the minimum of the general, promised-distance and enumeration bounds applicable to the input.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Quadratic acceleration in enumeration-tree size. The total-runtime comparison with the best classical solver depends on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></span>, basis quality and the target-distance promise.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous quantum backtracking upper bounds; partial evidence for separation from optimized classical CVP algorithms on explicit lattices.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Quantum enumeration needs polynomial logical registers for coefficient prefixes and reversible arithmetic, but potentially exponential coherent depth. Discrete-Gaussian and list-based methods use exponential storage. Large cryptanalytic instances require fault tolerance.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Aono–Nguyen–Shen quantum lattice enumeration</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/546\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Aono, Nguyen and Shen, <em>Quantum Lattice Enumeration and Tweaking Discrete Pruning</em> (ASIACRYPT 2018)</a>. Sections 3.1–3.2: close-vector enumeration; Algorithm 1 and Theorem 6: quantum tree minimization; Section 5.3, Algorithm 2: close-vector discrete pruning.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1504.01995\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Aggarwal, Dadush and Stephens-Davidowitz, <em>Solving the Closest Vector Problem in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> Time—The Discrete Gaussian Strikes Again!</em> (FOCS 2015)</a>. Theorem 5.7: exact-CVP reduction; Theorem 6.4 and the resulting exact algorithm: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.02764\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Hhan, <em>Finding a Shortest Vector and More in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> Time Using <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>-ary Coset Difference Tree</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> CVP bound with a distance promise; Section 5, Algorithm 4: CVP with a distance guarantee.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/backtracking\">Quantum backtracking and branch and bound</a>: <a href=\"#entry/constraint-satisfaction-by-backtracking\">Constraint satisfaction by backtracking</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-optimization-by-branch-and-bound\">Branch and bound</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/knapsack-and-binary-integer-programming\">Knapsack</a>, <a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-shortest-vector-problem\">Shortest vector problem</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/backtracking\">→ Quantum backtracking and branch and bound</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "bounded-distance-decoding-on-periodic-lattices": {
    "title": "Bounded distance decoding on periodic lattices",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Bounded distance decoding on periodic lattices</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Bounded distance decoding on periodic integer lattices)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an integer lattice <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span> and a target <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span> promised to satisfy <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>dist</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>, recover its unique closest lattice vector. Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> be the minimum number of generators of the finite group <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>q</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. Complexity is measured in quantum gates and classical bit operations, polynomial in the lattice encoding and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time, Eldar–Hallgren periodic-lattice decoding.</strong> The decoding radius is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, with the explicit constants and parameter restrictions of the theorem. The algorithm uses the finite-group structure of the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>-periodic integer lattice and returns the closest vector under this promise. Basis encoding and the accuracy needed for quantum-state preparation enter the polynomial factors.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Deterministic polynomial time in the same radius regime, Ducas–van Woerden decoding as developed by Allen et al.</strong> The classical construction combines lattice reduction, duality, short full-rank vector sets and Babai nearest plane. The short-basis guarantee ensures correct rounding throughout the promised radius. Both bounds concern the same periodic-lattice parameters <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> and exact recovery within the stated distance.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial quantum and classical complexity in the matched regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous classical matching of the quantum decoding regime. The formal radius and input representation specify the scope of both polynomial-time statements.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The quantum construction uses polynomial logical space, coherent algebraic arithmetic and Fourier processing. Its implementation would require controlled state preparation and fault-tolerant arithmetic at large dimension. The classical reduced-basis method implements the same decoding guarantee on ordinary hardware.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Eldar–Hallgren periodic-lattice decoding</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.13450\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Eldar and Hallgren, <em>An Efficient Quantum Algorithm for Lattice Problems Achieving Subexponential Approximation Factor</em> (2022)</a>. Algorithm 22, “Algorithm Q”: bounded-distance decoding on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>-periodic lattices; Algorithms 15, 17 and 19: quantum-state and sampling subroutines.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.05019\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Allen et al., <em>Quantum and Classical Algorithms for Bounded Distance Decoding</em> (2022)</a>. Theorems 1.1–1.3: quantum promise and classical comparisons; the classical decoding development uses LLL and nearest plane.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/eldar-hallgren\">→ Eldar–Hallgren periodic-lattice decoding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "learning-with-errors-with-classical-samples": {
    "title": "Learning with errors with classical samples",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Learning with errors with classical samples</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Learning with errors with classical samples)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>e</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" /><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math></span> is uniform, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> follows a specified secret distribution, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math></span> follows a specified small-noise distribution, recover <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>. Modulus, noise width, sample count, and secret distribution are essential parameters. ML-KEM and related schemes use structured versions of this problem. The comparison uses classical bit-operation time and quantum gate time with the specified coherent-memory model; the samples themselves are classical.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.2563</mn><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> per heuristic SVP call, quantum sieving in lattice-reduction attacks.</strong> The block size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></mrow></math></span> is chosen by the primal, dual or hybrid attack for the given modulus, noise, dimension and sample count. Polynomial factors include reduction tours and matrix operations. Amplitude amplification can additionally accelerate guessed secret coordinates. The rigorous superlattice SVP alternative has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.247</mn><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time under the 2026 theorem; its use requires an attack reduction compatible with that subroutine.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: The minimum of optimized primal, dual, hybrid, BKW and applicable bounded-error algorithms; heuristic sieving costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.2925</mn><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> per SVP call.</strong> For prime <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, Lin–Vasudevan give expected time <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>ln</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. One regime has errors bounded by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>ln</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> samples. Another has secrets bounded by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, errors <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> and at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>ln</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> samples, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. For fixed module ranks <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></math></span> in power-of-two cyclotomic Module-LWE, Youmans gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time and space for every fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>, and matrix-independent errors have norm at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>polylog</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mrow></math></span> with probability <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The algorithm uses the given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></math></span> ring samples.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup through a smaller exponential constant for the matched lattice-reduction subroutines. In the bounded-error regimes, the subexponential classical algorithms enter the minimum for the complete attack.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence for quantum sieve speedups, with explicit random-vector and memory assumptions. The bounded-error classical statements are parameter-specific theorems; the module extension is a 2026 preprint.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Fast quantum sieving requires exponential coherent storage in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></mrow></math></span> and fault-tolerant list access. Enumeration trades memory for runtime and uses polynomial logical qubits. Cryptographic resource estimates must optimize the full attack parameters and include reversible arithmetic.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Reusable quantum walks for lattice sieving</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.14023\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bonnetain et al., <em>Finding Many Collisions via Reusable Quantum Walks: Application to Lattice Sieving</em> (2023)</a>. Section 7.1: quantum SVP subroutine used in lattice-reduction attacks.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2015/1222\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Albrecht et al., <em>On the Asymptotic Complexity of Solving LWE</em> (2015)</a>. Primal, dual and BKW attack analyses specify the parameter-dependent classical costs.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.13043\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Lin and Vasudevan, <em>Provably Subexponential Algorithms for Bounded-Error LWE with Few Samples</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1: both parameter regimes; Algorithm 2 and Proposition 2.3: formal decision algorithm; Section 2.5 and Proposition 2.7: search and resource bounds.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/2420\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Youmans, <em>Provable Subexponential Algorithms for Module-SIS and Module-LWE</em> (2026)</a>. Abstract: fixed-rank Module-LWE theorem, error promise and sample count.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/reusable-collision-walks\">Reusable quantum walks for lattice sieving</a>: <a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-shortest-vector-problem\">Shortest vector problem</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/reusable-collision-walks\">→ Reusable quantum walks for lattice sieving</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "short-integer-solutions-with-a-wide-public-matrix": {
    "title": "Short integer solutions with a wide public matrix",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Short integer solutions with a wide public matrix</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Short integer solutions with a wide public matrix)</span></h2>\n<p>For uniformly random <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></span>, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" /><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Chen–Liu–Zhandry study polynomial prime <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>, fixed constant <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>, and sufficiently large polynomial width, including <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>q</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Complexity is measured in quantum gates and classical bit operations on the explicit public matrix, including its dimensions and modulus length.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: Polynomial time in the public dimensions and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>, Chen–Liu–Zhandry quantum filtering.</strong> The guarantee holds for the stated wide random matrix, prime modulus and infinity-norm bound. Quantum filtering succeeds with the prescribed error-support and Fourier-coefficient conditions; preparation and processing of the LWE-like quantum states are included in the polynomial running time. The output is a classical nonzero short kernel vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time in a stronger regime, Kothari–O&#x27;Donnell–Wu.</strong> The deterministic classical result covers all the Chen–Liu–Zhandry SIS infinity-norm parameter regimes. In particular, for arbitrary input matrices and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math></span>, it finds a nonzero <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math></span> satisfying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" /><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x0230A;</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>&#x0230B;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></span> is an appropriate absolute constant. This worst-case wide-matrix result strengthens both the input distribution and the norm guarantee of the matched quantum regime.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial quantum and classical solvability in the specified wide-matrix regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous classical matching and stronger classical parameters. The matrix width and infinity-norm threshold define the algorithmic scope.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The quantum filtering construction uses polynomial logical space in the full public-matrix dimensions, Fourier transforms and coherent filtering. Large instances require fault-tolerant state preparation and arithmetic. The deterministic classical method gives a direct ordinary-hardware implementation for the same regime.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Chen–Liu–Zhandry quantum filtering</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2108.11015\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Chen, Liu and Zhandry, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Variants of Average-Case Lattice Problems via Filtering</em> (EUROCRYPT 2022)</a>. Theorem 2: SIS guarantee; quantum filtering algorithms and the LWE-state construction provide the associated subroutines.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.07515\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kothari, O&#x27;Donnell and Wu, <em>No Exponential Quantum Speedup for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></msub></mrow></math></span> Anymore</em> (STOC 2026)</a>. Theorem 1.5, proved in Section 4.4: classical infinity-norm algorithm; its <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span> specialization gives the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math></span> bound with cubic width.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/clz-filtering\">Chen–Liu–Zhandry quantum filtering</a>: <a href=\"#entry/vector-subset-sum-over-f3\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span>-subset sum</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/clz-filtering\">→ Chen–Liu–Zhandry quantum filtering</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "binary-syndrome-decoding": {
    "title": "Binary syndrome decoding",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Binary syndrome decoding</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Binary syndrome decoding)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a full-rank binary parity-check matrix <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></span>, a syndrome <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>, and weight <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span>. Random instances model generic attacks on code-based cryptography. Complexity is measured in quantum gate time and classical bit-operation time, with coherent list-access costs specified for quantum walks.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>n</mi><mi>w</mi></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></span>, amplified Prange; parameter-dependent improvements from quantum-walk ISD.</strong> For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>n</mi><mi>w</mi></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> is the success probability of one Prange information-set trial in the usual random-code model. Reversible elimination, the weight test and uncomputation enter its polynomial cost. Kachigar–Tillich and Kirshanova accelerate internal list matching as well as the outer search. The <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.05869</mn></mrow></math></span> exponent is the maximum over rates at Gilbert–Varshamov weight in the Kachigar–Tillich comparison; concrete attacks optimize the actual <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: The minimum of optimized BJMM/May–Ozerov ISD and applicable sparse-circuit counting; Prange costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> For dense random constant-rate codes, the classical comparator minimizes the parameter-dependent representation and nearest-neighbor costs of modern ISD. If <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span> has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math></span> nonzero entries, the predicate <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span> has an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>-gate Boolean circuit. Tensor contraction and witness recovery give deterministic time <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> this is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. Dense matrices have <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, where optimized ISD supplies the stronger displayed bound.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup for dense random codes: quadratic acceleration of Prange and further reductions in the exponential constant from quantum walks. Sparse parity checks have the stated subexponential classical comparator.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence for a total-runtime separation on explicit dense codes; amplification rigorously improves a specified trial success probability.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Reversible elimination uses polynomial logical space. Advanced walks trade runtime for exponentially large coherent list storage. Cryptographic instances require fault-tolerant arithmetic and many coherent trial evaluations.</p><p><small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, the classical sparse-code comparison used exponential-time ISD, and quantum amplification reduced a Prange trial cost from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mi>p</mi></msqrt></mrow></math></span>.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum Prange; Kachigar–Tillich and Kirshanova quantum-walk ISD</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1703.00263\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kachigar and Tillich, <em>Quantum Information Set Decoding Algorithms</em> (2017)</a>. Algorithm 2: ISD skeleton; quantum-walk analyses and rate optimization give the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.05869</mn></mrow></math></span> exponent.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1808.00714\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kirshanova, <em>Improved Quantum Information Set Decoding</em> (2018)</a>. Algorithm 1 and Theorem 1: quantum ISD and its runtime formula.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2112.06157\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Esser et al., <em>An Optimized Quantum Implementation of ISD on Scalable Quantum Resources</em> (2021)</a>. Algorithms 1–4: Prange, state preparation, linear-system solving and the combined quantum circuit.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1: Boolean tensor contraction; witness self-reduction following the theorem and the bounded-width reductions in Appendix D: explicit-circuit search bound.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-walk-isd\">Kachigar–Tillich and Kirshanova quantum-walk ISD</a>: <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-walk-isd\">→ Kachigar–Tillich and Kirshanova quantum-walk ISD</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords": {
    "title": "Minimum-weight nonzero codewords",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Minimum-weight nonzero codewords</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Minimum-weight nonzero codewords)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a full-rank generator matrix <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></span>, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> minimizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The minimum value is the code&#x27;s minimum distance. This is a discrete optimization problem related to syndrome decoding and the search for short dual codewords in statistical cryptanalysis. Complexity is measured in weight-oracle evaluations for the direct search, and quantum gates or classical bit operations for explicitly represented codes.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> weight evaluations, Dürr–Høyer minimum finding; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></span> for amplified ISD trials.</strong> The direct exact method has polynomial workspace and includes coherent computation of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span>, its Hamming weight and comparison with a threshold. For random codes, let an ISD trial cost <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></span> and find a nonzero word of weight at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span> with probability <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>. Amplification replaces the trial count by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, and quantum nearest-neighbor walks can reduce the internal cost. Full attacks optimize weight, representations and list sizes.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: The minimum of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> weight evaluations, optimized ISD and applicable sparse-circuit counting.</strong> Exhaustive enumeration can update codewords in Gray-code order. An ISD trial family gives expected work <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, with optimized representations and nearest-neighbor matching improving the random-code comparator. If a generator or parity-check matrix has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> nonzero entries, the threshold predicate has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> Boolean gates, yielding <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> deterministic classical time. Binary search over the weight adds a polynomial factor. A linear-size representation gives subexponential time in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>; dense constant-rate codes retain the stronger enumeration or ISD bound.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup from quadratic acceleration of exhaustive weight search or a specified ISD success probability, giving a smaller exponential constant in the dense-code comparison.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous oracle amplification; partial evidence for a full-runtime separation on explicit random codes. Sparse representations have the separate subexponential classical bound.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The direct circuit uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> message qubits, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> codeword qubits, a weight register and polynomial ancillas. Exponentially many fault-tolerant repetitions dominate its cost. Faster quantum-walk variants use exponential coherent lists.</p><p><small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, the generic exact comparison was <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical weight evaluations versus <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum evaluations, a quadratic speedup also applied to sparse representations.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Dürr–Høyer minimum finding; quantum information-set decoding</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9607014\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dürr and Høyer, <em>A Quantum Algorithm for Finding the Minimum</em> (1996)</a>. The minimum-finding algorithm and its expected <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> query theorem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1703.00263\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kachigar and Tillich, <em>Quantum Information Set Decoding Algorithms</em> (2017)</a>. Algorithm 2 and the quantum-walk cost analyses, applied at zero syndrome with the zero vector excluded.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1808.00714\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kirshanova, <em>Improved Quantum Information Set Decoding</em> (2018)</a>. Algorithm 1 and Theorem 1: the parameter-dependent ISD construction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1: Boolean tensor contraction; witness self-reduction following the theorem and the bounded-width reductions in Appendix D: explicit-circuit search bound.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-walk-isd\">Kachigar–Tillich and Kirshanova quantum-walk ISD</a>: <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-walk-isd\">→ Kachigar–Tillich and Kirshanova quantum-walk ISD</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "random-subset-sum-at-cryptographic-density": {
    "title": "Random subset sum at cryptographic density",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Random subset sum at cryptographic density</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Random subset sum at cryptographic density)</span></h2>\n<p>Given independent random integers <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> modulo a modulus <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math></span> of roughly <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> bits and a target <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" /><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Density and the distribution of solutions are fixed as in the representation-algorithm model. These instances appear in knapsack cryptanalysis and as subproblems in decoding. Complexity is measured in classical RAM time and quantum time with either quantum access to classical memory or quantum-accessible quantum memory, as stated.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.2182</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time with classical list heuristics, or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.2156</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with an additional update heuristic, Bonnetain–Bricout–Schrottenloher–Shen quantum walks.</strong> The first walk has memory exponent <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2182</mn></mrow></math></span>; the second has memory exponent <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2110</mn></mrow></math></span>. A quantum-search variant uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.2356</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time and classically stored lists with quantum random access. The walk variants use quantum-accessible quantum memory. Their representation lists, modular filters and update costs are analyzed for the random density-one distribution in the problem statement.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.283</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time and space, Bonnetain–Bricout–Schrottenloher–Shen classical representations.</strong> The result uses the standard random-instance list heuristics and representations with coefficients in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. For positive binary integers of polynomial bit length, the general worst-case subset-sum algorithm gives randomized <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.49</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> word-RAM time; the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.283</mn></mrow></math></span> exponent is the stronger bound in this random regime. A direct Boolean verifier for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> modular summands of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span> bits has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates. With <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, applying the explicit-circuit counting bound gives an exponent of order <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></span>, so the representation bound determines the displayed comparison.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup through a smaller exponential constant in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>. Both amplitude amplification and quantum walks contribute to the algorithms.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence under the stated random-instance and list assumptions. The <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2156</mn></mrow></math></span> result additionally assumes the quantum-walk update heuristic; the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2182</mn></mrow></math></span> result uses the classical heuristics.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>All fast variants are memory-intensive fault-tolerant workloads. The <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2356</mn></mrow></math></span> algorithm uses exponential classical storage with coherent access; the faster walks use exponential quantum-accessible quantum memory. The memory-access model determines the gate cost of a lookup.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Bonnetain–Bricout–Schrottenloher–Shen subset-sum algorithms</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.05276\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bonnetain, Bricout, Schrottenloher and Shen, <em>Improved Classical and Quantum Algorithms for Subset-Sum</em> (ASIACRYPT 2020)</a>. Section 2.5: classical <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.283</mn></mrow></math></span> exponent; Section 4.3: quantum-search <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2356</mn></mrow></math></span> exponent; Section 5.3: heuristic quantum walk; Section 6.4: walk without the additional update heuristic; Table 1: models and resource exponents.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>Subset Sum in Time <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.49</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span></em> (2026)</a>. Main randomized word-RAM theorem for worst-case inputs.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/random-subset-sum\">→ Bonnetain–Bricout–Schrottenloher–Shen subset-sum algorithms</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations": {
    "title": "Boolean multivariate quadratic equations",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Boolean multivariate quadratic equations</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Boolean multivariate quadratic equations)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> quadratic polynomials <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> for every <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math></span>, or report infeasibility. The square case <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> is a basic algebraic-cryptanalysis benchmark and a foundation for multivariate signature assumptions. Complexity is measured in elementary quantum gates and classical bit-operation time on the explicit polynomial coefficient list.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates, Grover search with reversible quadratic-equation evaluation.</strong> Schwabe–Westerbaan construct the equation-checking oracle with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> qubits; their space-efficient variant uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> qubits with the same asymptotic gate bound. QuantumBooleanSolve gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.462</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> expected gates for every fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> and the system is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.17</mn></mrow></math></span>-strong semiregular. Its deterministic variant gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.47</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> under <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.13</mn></mrow></math></span>-strong semiregularity. These bounds include Macaulay-system linear algebra and coherent testing of partial assignments.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.6943</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time, Dinur multiple parity counting; subexponential time for sparse systems, Boolean tensor contraction.</strong> Polynomial-time Gaussian elimination removes linearly dependent equations from the coefficient list; Dinur&#x27;s randomized search algorithm then uses parity counts of partial assignments. If <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and each equation has polylogarithmically many monomials, the counting reduction gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. With linear total variable–monomial incidence, an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>-size Boolean circuit gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. These sparse bounds take precedence for their input regimes. Dense systems satisfying the quantum semiregularity promise give the displayed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.6943</mn></mrow></math></span> versus <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.462</mn></mrow></math></span> comparison.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup through a smaller exponential constant for the dense promised regime, using amplitude amplification and algebraic preprocessing. Sparse explicit systems have subexponential classical bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence: the quantum algebraic exponent uses strong semiregularity, while the classical parity-counting bound is a general upper bound.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Reversible polynomial evaluation and Macaulay linear algebra need fault tolerance and substantial workspace. Direct Grover search uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> assignment qubits and polynomial ancillas; the algebraic algorithm has additional matrix-storage and reversible-linear-algebra costs.</p><p><small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, the general classical upper bound applied to sparse quadratic systems was <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>0.6943</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, compared with the claimed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.462</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum bound under strong semiregularity.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Grover search; QuantumBooleanSolve</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://cryptojedi.org/papers/mqgrover-20171130.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Schwabe and Westerbaan, <em>Solving Binary MQ with Grover&#x27;s Algorithm</em> (SPACE 2016; full version 2017)</a>. Section 2.1: problem definition; Sections 4–5: the two reversible equation-checking circuits and their gate and qubit costs.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1712.07211\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Faugère et al., <em>Fast Quantum Algorithm for Solving Multivariate Quadratic Equations</em> (2017)</a>. Theorems 5–7 and Section 4: quantum circuit and QuantumBooleanSolve complexity; Theorem 7 states the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.17</mn></mrow></math></span>-strong semiregular <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.462</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> bound.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2005.04800\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dinur, <em>Improved Algorithms for Solving Polynomial Systems over GF(2) by Multiple Parity-Counting</em> (2020)</a>. Theorem 1.1: search complexity; Section 2.2: elimination of linearly dependent equations; Algorithms 1–3: parity-counting procedures.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1: Boolean tensor contraction; witness self-reduction following the theorem and the bounded-width reductions in Appendix D: explicit-circuit search bound.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "collision": {
    "title": "Hash collisions",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hash collisions</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Hash collisions)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a random function <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit outputs and a sufficiently large domain, find distinct <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Collision resistance is the relevant property for many hash-based constructions and commitment schemes. The random-function comparison measures hash-oracle queries; total quantum time additionally counts coherent table access, and explicit circuits use gate or bit complexity.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries with a table of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math></span> values, Brassard–Høyer–Tapp collision search.</strong> The table parameter satisfies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. Optimizing it gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> and the tight <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum query bound. Near-matching time assumes efficient coherent lookup into the stored table. A gate implementation includes the lookup circuit and reversible hash cost. The random-function setting yields the matching quantum collision lower-bound scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, birthday search or Pollard-rho collision finding.</strong> Birthday storage is of order <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> entries; Pollard-rho offers low-memory collision finding under the usual random-function assumptions. For a hash given by a <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span>-gate circuit on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></math></span> input bits, the predicate <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates. Boolean tensor contraction and witness recovery give <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> classical time. A linear-size explicit family therefore has a subexponential comparator.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup of degree <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span> relative to the classical birthday query count. The BHT construction combines a stored table with Grover search. In output length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, the exponent changes from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span> without power saving.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Fully proved in the collision-oracle model with the stated domain promise; random-function collision bounds give the corresponding cryptographic query scale.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The time-efficient BHT implementation stores about <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> entries with coherent access and requires exponentially many fault-tolerant operations. Polynomial-qubit alternatives use different time-memory tradeoffs; the lookup model is a material resource assumption.</p><p><small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, the generic collision comparison used <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical versus <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum queries, an exponential-constant improvement also used for explicit hash circuits.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Brassard–Høyer–Tapp collision search</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9705002\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Brassard, Høyer and Tapp, <em>Quantum Algorithm for the Collision Problem</em> (1997)</a>. Theorem 1 and <span class=\"small-caps\">Collision</span><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>: table-based quantum collision search; Section 3: coherent table-access costs.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0112086\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Shi, <em>Quantum Lower Bounds for the Collision and the Element Distinctness Problems</em> (FOCS 2002)</a>. Theorem 1.1: tight cube-root collision query lower bound.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and witness self-reduction: search for a satisfying assignment of the explicit Boolean verification circuit.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/bht-collision\">→ Brassard–Høyer–Tapp collision search</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "claw": {
    "title": "Claw finding and double-encryption key recovery",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Claw finding and double-encryption key recovery</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Claw finding and double-encryption key recovery)</span></h2>\n<p>Given evaluation oracles <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></math></span>, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, or determine that none exists.</p><p><span class=\"problem-anchor\" id=\"double-encryption-key-recovery\"></span><strong>Double-encryption key recovery.</strong> Given sufficiently many known pairs satisfying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for independent <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit keys, recover <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The functions <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> give a balanced claw problem with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. Tuples of intermediate values isolate the intended key pair. Claws also arise when matching two halves of an isogeny computation.</p><p>The comparisons count function-evaluation queries or ideal-cipher encryption and decryption queries; explicit functions additionally use gate and bit complexity.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>M</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span>, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>M</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span>, quantum-walk claw finding.</strong> Tani&#x27;s construction handles both detection and finding. The balanced bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, giving <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> ideal-cipher queries for double encryption. A quantum walk maintains evaluated subsets of the two domains; coherent sorting or lookup and reversible function evaluation determine its gate cost. The time-efficient double-encryption construction uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> memory.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> worst-case queries, list matching; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time for double encryption, meet in the middle.</strong> Hashing gives expected linear-time list matching with suitable value representation; sorting adds a logarithmic overhead. Double encryption uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> storage and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> ideal-cipher queries. For explicit functions, let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> be the gate count of the complete claw-verification circuit and let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>N</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>M</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Boolean tensor contraction gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> classical search time. In double encryption, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> includes all known-pair checks. Balanced claws with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> have an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> classical bound; linear-size two-key verification gives subexponential time in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>A polynomial speedup of degree <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span> in the balanced query comparison and a quadratic search improvement in the highly unbalanced regime. Double encryption improves the exponential constant from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span> through the balanced claw-finding reduction.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Matching quantum upper and lower bounds hold for balanced claw queries and the stated ideal-cipher key-recovery task. The explicit-circuit comparisons include their representation-dependent classical algorithms.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Time-efficient walks require coherent stored values and superpolynomial memory in logarithmic domain size. Reversible encryption, decryption and walk updates require fault tolerance at cryptographic sizes; coherent list storage and depth dominate the logical resources.\n<small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, the balanced explicit-function comparison transferred the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical versus <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum query costs to function-evaluation time, including double-encryption meet in the middle.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Tani quantum-walk claw finding; quantum meet in the middle</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0708.2584\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Tani, <em>Claw Finding Algorithms Using Quantum Walk</em> (2009)</a>. Section 3: claw detection; Section 4, <span class=\"small-caps\">ClawSearch</span> and Theorem 8: finding a claw and the two domain-size regimes.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1410.1434\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kaplan, <em>Quantum Attacks Against Iterated Block Ciphers</em> (2015)</a>. Theorem 2: double-encryption algorithm and memory; Theorem 3: matching query lower bound; Theorem 4: the related fourfold-encryption construction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and witness self-reduction: search for a satisfying assignment of the explicit Boolean verification circuit.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/tani-claw\">Tani quantum-walk claw finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/sidh-secret-isogeny-recovery\">SIDH key recovery</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/tani-claw\">→ Tani quantum-walk claw finding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "generalized-birthday-search-random-k-xor": {
    "title": "Generalized birthday search: random k-XOR",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Generalized birthday search: random <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-XOR</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Generalized birthday search: random <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-XOR)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> lists <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> of uniformly random <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit strings, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02295;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02295;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>. The list lengths are part of the input. The single-solution regime uses lists of size about <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>; unrestricted-list variants permit larger lists. These problems model hash combiners, subset sums, and parts of decoding. Complexity is measured in classical list-processing time and quantum time or oracle queries, with coherent access to the prescribed random lists.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x0230A;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>k</mi><mi>&#x0230B;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time for unrestricted lists, quantum generalized birthday algorithms.</strong> The list sizes in this setting can be chosen to support the merging strategy. For four prescribed random lists of size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, Schrottenloher gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum time in the single-solution regime. Its asymmetric merges, quantum search and quantum walks exploit that specific list-size promise. The fastest time bounds depend on the available memory and coherent lookup model; random-oracle <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-XOR query lower bounds have scale <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x0230A;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>k</mi><mi>&#x0230B;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time with unrestricted lists, Wagner generalized birthday search.</strong> Wagner uses lists of the corresponding size and successive prefix constraints. For four prescribed lists of size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, pair-list meet-in-the-middle costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time. This is the comparator for the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow></math></span> quantum result. The unrestricted-list and prescribed-list problems have different input promises and are compared separately.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedups through smaller exponential constants in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> for fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>. Amplitude amplification and quantum walks both appear in the merging constructions.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence for time optimality, together with rigorous random-oracle query bounds. List size, number of solutions and memory model specify each comparison.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The fastest methods require exponentially large lists with coherent access and fault-tolerant merging operations. Polynomial-qubit variants trade greater runtime for less coherent memory. Explicitly supplied lists also incur their stated loading and storage costs.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum generalized birthday algorithms</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.iacr.org/archive/crypto2002/24420288/24420288.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Wagner, <em>A Generalized Birthday Problem</em> (CRYPTO 2002)</a>. Generalized birthday algorithm and its <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-list complexity analysis.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/501\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Naya-Plasencia and Schrottenloher, <em>Optimal Merging in Quantum <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-XOR and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SUM Algorithms</em> (EUROCRYPT 2020)</a>. Quantum merging-tree framework and optimization of single-solution instances.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/31603/31603.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Schrottenloher, <em>Improved Quantum Algorithms for the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-XOR Problem</em> (SAC 2021; proceedings 2022)</a>. Section 5: single-solution merging; Section 6, Theorem 4 and Algorithm 4: quantum-walk construction; Section 6.3: the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> four-list exponent.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-kxor-merging\">→ Quantum k-XOR and k-SUM merging</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "even": {
    "title": "Even–Mansour recovery with quantum queries",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Even–Mansour recovery with quantum queries</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Even–Mansour key recovery with quantum chosen plaintexts)</span></h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math></span> be a public random permutation on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, and let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02295;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02295;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>. Given coherent oracle access to both <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math></span>, recover <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>. The coherent chosen-plaintext access is part of this cryptographic model, commonly called Q2. The comparison counts coherent or classical oracle queries to the encryption and public permutation, with polynomial auxiliary quantum-gate and classical-bit costs.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> coherent queries and polynomial auxiliary time, Kuwakado–Morii and Simon period finding.</strong> The function <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02295;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> has period <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>. The bounded-extra-collision analysis for a random public permutation supplies the success guarantee for Simon sampling; polynomial-time linear algebra recovers the period. One additional evaluation gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>. Coherent evaluation of both <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math></span> is available in the stated Q2 model, so each sample has polynomial additional gates and two oracle evaluations.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> public-permutation work with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span> classical encryption queries, generic Even–Mansour attacks.</strong> The time–data tradeoff has scale <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0224D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and total work including data acquisition. Minimizing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></span> gives the square-root total-work scale. The lower bound concerns the public random permutation and secret-key encryption access specified by the idealized construction.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential to polynomial in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, from Simon period finding. This uses the XOR period created by the Even–Mansour construction and its coherent chosen-plaintext access.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Fully proved in the stated random-permutation oracle model, with the bounded-extra-collision analysis supporting the period-recovery algorithm.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm needs polynomial logical qubits for the input, output and period equations, plus workspace for the public permutation and coherent encryption device. The depth is polynomial in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> times the oracle depth. Realizing the supplied coherent secret-key oracle is part of the hardware model.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Kuwakado–Morii attack with Simon period finding</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1602.05973\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kaplan, Leurent, Leverrier and Naya-Plasencia, <em>Breaking Symmetric Cryptosystems Using Quantum Period Finding</em> (CRYPTO 2016)</a>. Theorem 1: Simon sampling with an approximate promise; Section 3.2, “Application to the Even-Mansour construction”: the Kuwakado–Morii attack and its extra-collision analysis; Figure 6: the period function.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.12439\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bonnetain et al., <em>Quantum Attacks Without Superposition Queries: The Offline Simon&#x27;s Algorithm</em> (ASIACRYPT 2019; full version 2020)</a>. Section 5.1 and Table 2: classical Even–Mansour time–data tradeoff.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/simon\">Simon period finding and offline Simon</a>: <a href=\"#entry/evenmansour-recovery-with-classical-queries\">Even–Mansour with classical queries</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a40\">Distinguishing three-round Feistel networks</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a41\">CBC-MAC forgery under superposition access</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/simon\">→ Simon period finding and offline Simon</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "evenmansour-recovery-with-classical-queries": {
    "title": "Even–Mansour recovery with classical queries",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Even–Mansour recovery with classical queries</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Even–Mansour key recovery with classical queries)</span></h2>\n<p>Recover the keys of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02295;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02295;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span> when the attacker may query <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></math></span> only on classical inputs and may compute the public permutation <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math></span> coherently. This is the Q1 version of entry 88, with a materially different access model and complexity. The Q1 comparison counts classical encryption queries, coherent public-permutation evaluations and quantum gates; explicit permutations also admit classical bit-complexity bounds.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> offline quantum time with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span> classical encryption queries, offline Simon.</strong> For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, Bonnetain et al. obtain the tradeoff <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0224D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> up to polynomial factors in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>. Total time includes data acquisition and has scale <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Reusable quantum states support the period tests with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical qubits and polynomial classical memory. Only the public permutation is evaluated coherently; encryption queries are classical.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> offline work with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span> encryption queries, generic classical Even–Mansour attacks.</strong> The tradeoff <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0224D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> gives total work <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical storage. For a public permutation implemented by a <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>-gate circuit, enough known pairs give an explicit key-verification circuit of size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, up to the fixed number of pairs. Tensor contraction yields <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> classical key-recovery time. Families with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> therefore have a subexponential classical comparator.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Quadratic improvement of offline work at fixed data volume in the generic Q1 model, combining Grover search with offline Simon tests. Optimizing total time over <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span> gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum versus <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical work, a polynomial speedup of degree <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous algorithmic improvement over the matching generic classical time-data bound. Explicit permutations are additionally compared with their circuit-based classical algorithms.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The construction uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical qubits and polynomial classical memory while processing <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span> classical encryption queries. The offline computation takes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time; cryptographic-scale coherent depth requires fault tolerance.</p><p><small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, the explicit-permutation comparison used classical <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> balanced work versus <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> offline Simon time.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Offline Simon</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.12439\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bonnetain, Hosoyamada, Naya-Plasencia, Sasaki and Schrottenloher, <em>Quantum Attacks Without Superposition Queries: The Offline Simon&#x27;s Algorithm</em> (ASIACRYPT 2019; full version 2020)</a>. Section 3.2 and Algorithms 2–4: asymmetric shift search, period test and reusable database preparation; Section 5.1: Q1 Even–Mansour tradeoffs and the balanced <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> attack.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and witness self-reduction: search on the explicit key-verification circuit.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/simon\">Simon period finding and offline Simon</a>: <a href=\"#entry/even\">Even–Mansour with quantum queries</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a40\">Distinguishing three-round Feistel networks</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a41\">CBC-MAC forgery under superposition access</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/simon\">→ Simon period finding and offline Simon</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis": {
    "title": "Differential and linear cryptanalysis",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Differential and linear cryptanalysis</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Differential and linear cryptanalysis)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a keyed permutation <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, exploit either a differential <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02295;</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02295;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02207;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> or a linear approximation <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> to distinguish it from random and recover key bits. The differential probability, linear bias, queried rounds, and guessed subkey size specify the instance. Complexity is measured in classical samples or oracle calls and coherent quantum evaluations for the statistical primitives, and in gate or bit complexity for complete key recovery.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> differential tests and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> coherent bias-estimation evaluations, amplitude amplification and estimation.</strong> A differential right-pair search with success probability <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> has the first bound. Estimating a linear bias to constant relative precision has the second. A search over <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> subkeys uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> verification steps, with the data-processing cost of each step included. Kaplan–Leurent–Leverrier–Naya-Plasencia analyze how these components combine for simple and truncated differentials and for linear cryptanalysis. The query model determines whether data acquisition itself can use coherent access.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> differential trials, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> bias-estimation samples and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> exhaustive subkey checks.</strong> These are matched primitive costs. A complete classical attack minimizes over characteristics, data volume, guessed subkeys and other attacks on the same cipher and round count. If <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> key bits are checked by a <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span>-gate explicit verification circuit, Boolean tensor contraction adds <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> to that classical minimum. Linear-size circuit families have subexponential complete key-recovery time.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Quadratic improvements in the specified sampling and search components, from amplitude amplification or estimation. The exponent of a complete attack depends on the selected characteristic and key-recovery strategy.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous primitive bounds and partial cipher-specific evidence for a total-runtime separation. The full comparison uses the strongest complete classical attack with the same data access.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial registers suffice for reversible cipher rounds and subkeys. Coherent-query attacks require the specified quantum access; Q1 attacks process stored classical data. Cryptanalytic-scale coherent depth requires fault tolerance, and memory depends on the data-processing strategy.</p><p><small class=\"historical-note\">[Before “ETH is false”, the generic complete-key-search baseline was <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical versus <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> amplified trials; differential and linear attacks optimized their own exponential components.]</small></p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum differential and linear cryptanalysis with amplitude amplification and estimation</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1510.05836\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kaplan, Leurent, Leverrier and Naya-Plasencia, <em>Quantum Differential and Linear Cryptanalysis</em> (ToSC 2016)</a>. Sections 4.1–4.2: classical and quantum differential attacks; Sections 5.1–5.2: truncated differentials; Sections 7.1–7.2: linear cryptanalysis; Theorems 1–3: search and counting subroutines.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0005055\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Brassard, Høyer, Mosca and Tapp, <em>Quantum Amplitude Amplification and Estimation</em> (2002)</a>. Section 2: trial amplification; Theorem 12: amplitude estimation and its error bound.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and witness self-reduction: search on the explicit key-verification circuit.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>: <a href=\"#entry/approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments\">Approximate counting</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">→ Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "vector-subset-sum-over-f3": {
    "title": "Vector subset sum over F3",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Vector subset sum over <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math></span></h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Average-case <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span>-Subset-Sum)</span></h2>\n<p>Given independent uniform vectors <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span>, find a nonempty <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>. The coefficients are restricted to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. This is a finite-field subset-sum problem related to restricted short integer solutions. Complexity is measured in quantum gates and classical bit operations on the explicit input.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time, Kothari–Metger–O&#x27;Donnell–Shutty–Wu.</strong> For every fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, success probability is at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. The algorithm combines quantum filtering with a deterministic binary-error LWE decoder.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time in the Wagner regime; polynomial time above the classical vector-count threshold.</strong> For <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math></span> below the known polynomial-time thresholds, Wagner-type algorithms merge lists of partial sums while imposing zero coordinates. For bounded <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, known algorithms have <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time. Kothari–O&#x27;Donnell–Wu give a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm, valid even for worst-case inputs, when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> for an absolute constant <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></span>. Kothari–Metger–O&#x27;Donnell–Shutty–Wu further announce a polynomial-time threshold <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022C6;</mo></msub><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span>, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022C6;</mo></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>8</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>36</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.1022</mn></mrow></math></span>; Section 1.3 presents this as an announced construction with its formal proof deferred. The classical comparison takes the fastest applicable algorithm at the given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Subexponential to polynomial in the strongest comparison. The quantum tradeoff reaches polynomial time throughout <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, extending below the smallest reported classical polynomial-time vector-count threshold. The general bounds also describe intermediate improvements when both algorithms have superpolynomial time; their degree depends on the input density <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Partial evidence from the classical frontier; the quantum running-time theorem is rigorous. The tradeoff is a September 2026 preprint.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The Fourier construction has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> qutrit registers, together with the workspace for a reversible decoder. The gate count and decoder workspace follow the parameter-dependent running time. The polynomial-time regime has polynomial logical resources; larger decoding costs require correspondingly longer fault-tolerant computations.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Kothari–Metger–O&#x27;Donnell–Shutty–Wu vector subset sum</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.40321v1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kothari, Metger, O&#x27;Donnell, Shutty and Wu, <em>Exponential Quantum Speedup for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span>-Subset-Sum? Or, Rigorous Classical Algorithms for Binary-Error LWE</em> (2026)</a>. Problem 1.1; Theorem 1.3 and Corollary 1.4: quantum tradeoff; Section 1.1: classical comparison; Theorem 3.1: quantum reduction; Definition 4.3 and Section 4.2: quotient decoder.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.11015\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Chen, Liu and Zhandry, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Variants of Average-Case Lattice Problems via Filtering</em> (EUROCRYPT 2022)</a>. Quantum filtering and the decoding-to-short-solution reduction underlying the quantum construction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.07515v3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kothari, O&#x27;Donnell and Wu, <em>No Exponential Quantum Speedup for SIS<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi /><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></msup></mrow></math></span> Anymore</em> (STOC 2026)</a>. Theorem 1.2 and Section 2: deterministic polynomial-time subset sum above the classical vector-count threshold.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/ daw/papers/genbday.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Wagner, <em>A Generalized Birthday Problem</em> (CRYPTO 2002)</a>. Section 2 of the short version: list merging and the generalized-birthday algorithm; the finite-field subset-sum specialization is compared in Kothari et al., Section 1.1.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/clz-filtering\">Chen–Liu–Zhandry quantum filtering</a>: <a href=\"#entry/short-integer-solutions-with-a-wide-public-matrix\">Wide-matrix SIS</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/clz-filtering\">→ Chen–Liu–Zhandry quantum filtering</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a01": {
    "title": "Finite abelian-group decomposition",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Finite abelian-group decomposition</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Finite abelian-group decomposition)</span></h2>\n<p>Given generators and uniquely encoded reversible group operations for a finite abelian group <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span>, output elements <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> and orders <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>. Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> bound the encoding length and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Cheung–Mosca. Luo–Li–Le Gall improve the implementation: for operation cost <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> distinct circuits of size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, each repeated <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> times, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> qubits and polynomial classical postprocessing.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> generic group operations by Sutherland&#x27;s Monte Carlo structure algorithm, with representation-dependent bit costs. Groups supplied with an explicit decomposition admit faster classical processing.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The Luo–Li–Le Gall implementation uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical qubits and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> total quantum gates, with the circuit repetitions specified above.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Cheung–Mosca and Luo–Li–Le Gall abelian-group decomposition; Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36480\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Decomposing Finite Abelian Groups; Improved Quantum Algorithms for Black-Box Abelian Group Decomposition</em></a>. 2026 Theorem 1.1 and Section 5; original: https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/0101004</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.3413\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Structure computation and discrete logarithms in finite abelian p-groups</em></a>. Monte Carlo structure-computation theorem</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/0101004\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Decomposing finite abelian groups</em></a>. Decomposition algorithm</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/factoring\">Integer factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithms-in-finite-fields\">Finite-field discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm\">Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a02\">Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a05\">Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a06\">Ideal operations in finite rings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a08\">Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a10\">Semidirect discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q45\">Testing one-to-one periodicity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithm-concept-learning\">Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/abelian-group-decomposition\">→ Cheung–Mosca and Luo–Li–Le Gall abelian-group decomposition</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">→ Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a02": {
    "title": "Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups)</span></h2>\n<p>Given black-box groups <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span> promised to have decompositions <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022CA;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math></span> abelian and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>, decide whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x02245;</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span> and construct an isomorphism. Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>; input generators and operations have polylogarithmic descriptions.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum time by Le Gall, through abelian decomposition and simultaneous matrix-conjugacy calculations.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time by Le Gall&#x27;s classical algorithm for the same group family.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Le Gall coprime group-isomorphism algorithm; Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.0608\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>An Efficient Quantum Algorithm for some Instances of the Group Isomorphism Problem</em></a>. Theorems 1.2 and 1.3; STACS 2010 https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.STACS.2010.2484</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/factoring\">Integer factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithms-in-finite-fields\">Finite-field discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm\">Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a01\">Finite abelian-group decomposition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a05\">Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a06\">Ideal operations in finite rings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a08\">Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a10\">Semidirect discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q45\">Testing one-to-one periodicity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithm-concept-learning\">Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/coprime-group-isomorphism\">→ Le Gall coprime group-isomorphism algorithm</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">→ Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a04": {
    "title": "Group commutativity",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Group commutativity</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Group commutativity)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> generators of a black-box group, decide whether the generated subgroup is abelian, equivalently whether every pair of generators commutes. Complexity counts group-operation oracle calls.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> group queries by Magniez–Nayak&#x27;s quantum walk; the lower bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized group queries by Pak&#x27;s commutativity test.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Magniez–Nayak commutativity quantum walk</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0506265\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Complexity of Testing Group Commutativity</em></a>. Main upper/lower-bound theorems; author final PDF https://www.irif.fr/ magniez/PAPIERS/mn-algorithmica07.pdf</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/group-commutativity-quantum-walk\">→ Magniez–Nayak commutativity quantum walk</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a05": {
    "title": "Basis representation of a finite black-box ring",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Basis representation of a finite black-box ring)</span></h2>\n<p>Given generators of a finite ring <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math></span> and uniquely encoded addition and multiplication oracles, compute an additive invariant-factor basis and the multiplication structure constants in that basis. Complexity is in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span> and the generator-description length.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum time by Arvind–Das–Mukhopadhyay, using abelian-group decomposition and quantum discrete logarithms.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Generic additive-group structure and discrete-logarithm algorithms give <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> ring operations, with polynomially many basis products. If structure constants are supplied initially, the basis-conversion work is already present in the input.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Arvind–Das–Mukhopadhyay finite-ring basis algorithm; Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/11809678_15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Complexity of Black-Box Ring Problems</em></a>. Basis-representation construction; source PDF https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=8c18a1a053fc1317bdc53810c4ebe29aaa1bc799   repid=rep1   type=pdf</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.3413\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Structure computation and discrete logarithms in finite abelian p-groups</em></a>. Generic structure and discrete-logarithm algorithms applied to the additive group</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/factoring\">Integer factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithms-in-finite-fields\">Finite-field discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm\">Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a01\">Finite abelian-group decomposition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a02\">Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a06\">Ideal operations in finite rings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a08\">Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a10\">Semidirect discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q45\">Testing one-to-one periodicity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithm-concept-learning\">Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/black-box-ring-basis\">→ Arvind–Das–Mukhopadhyay finite-ring basis algorithm</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">→ Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a06": {
    "title": "Ideal operations in finite rings",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Ideal operations in finite rings</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Ideal operations in finite rings)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a uniquely encoded finite black-box ring <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math></span>, generators of ideals <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></math></span>, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math></span>, compute an additive basis of an ideal, decide <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math></span>, or compute generators of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></math></span> and annihilators as specified in the paper. Complexity counts reversible ring operations and gates.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Wocjan–Jordan–Ahmadi–Brennan, using uniform ideal states and abelian hidden-subgroup computations.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Once an additive basis and multiplication constants are available, Smith-normal-form and finite-module algorithms run in polynomial bit complexity. For a generic black-box input, constructing the needed coordinates has the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> generic-group bound.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Wocjan–Jordan–Ahmadi–Brennan ideal algorithms; Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.0022\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Efficient quantum processing of ideals in finite rings</em></a>. Ideal basis algorithm and applications sections.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/factoring\">Integer factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithms-in-finite-fields\">Finite-field discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm\">Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a01\">Finite abelian-group decomposition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a02\">Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a05\">Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a08\">Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a10\">Semidirect discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q45\">Testing one-to-one periodicity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithm-concept-learning\">Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-finite-ring-ideals\">→ Wocjan–Jordan–Ahmadi–Brennan ideal algorithms</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">→ Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a07": {
    "title": "Multilinear identity testing over a finite ring",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Multilinear identity testing over a finite ring</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Multilinear identity testing over a finite ring)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a finite ring <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math></span>, an ideal <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math></span> generated additively by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> elements, and oracle access to a multilinear polynomial map <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math></span>, decide whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math></span> vanishes on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. Complexity counts ring and evaluation queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Arvind–Mukhopadhyay when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. The quantum walk searches tuples of generating-set combinations.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized queries via ring identity testing; direct testing of generator tuples uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> evaluations. For fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span>, the former is linear in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Arvind–Mukhopadhyay multilinear identity test</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0807.1412\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Query Complexity of Multilinear Identity Testing</em></a>. Main algorithm theorem; ECCC https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2008/086/</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/ring-identity-quantum-walk\">→ Arvind–Mukhopadhyay multilinear identity test</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a08": {
    "title": "Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> in a uniquely encoded finite semigroup <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span>, or decide that none exists. Powers have an eventual tail and cycle; the resource is oracle operations and gates.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum time by Childs–Ivanyos, using period finding to recover the tail and cycle and then solving a cyclic-group logarithm.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> generic semigroup operations by baby-step–giant-step methods for the tail/cycle and the resulting group discrete logarithm. Structured platforms inherit their faster group-logarithm algorithms.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Childs–Ivanyos semigroup discrete logarithm; Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.6238\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum computation of discrete logarithms in semigroups</em></a>. Sections 2–3; independent reduction https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.7903</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/factoring\">Integer factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithms-in-finite-fields\">Finite-field discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm\">Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a01\">Finite abelian-group decomposition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a02\">Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a05\">Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a06\">Ideal operations in finite rings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a10\">Semidirect discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q45\">Testing one-to-one periodicity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithm-concept-learning\">Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/semigroup-discrete-logarithm\">→ Childs–Ivanyos semigroup discrete logarithm</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">→ Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a09": {
    "title": "Constructive membership in a commutative semigroup",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Constructive membership in a commutative semigroup</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Constructive membership in a commutative semigroup)</span></h2>\n<p>Given commuting elements <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> of a finite semigroup <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>, find nonnegative exponents <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></span>, or report failure. Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span> and fix <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> semigroup queries by Childs–Ivanyos; the paper proves a matching quantum lower bound up to logarithmic factors.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> operations by exploring the generated subsemigroup and recording predecessor products. Classical baby-step–giant-step techniques improve special cancellative or group instances.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Childs–Ivanyos commutative-semigroup membership</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.6238\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum computation of discrete logarithms in semigroups</em></a>. Section 5.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/commutative-semigroup-membership\">→ Childs–Ivanyos commutative-semigroup membership</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a10": {
    "title": "Semidirect discrete logarithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Semidirect discrete logarithm</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Semidirect discrete logarithm)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a finite group <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span>, an efficiently computable automorphism <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi></mrow></math></span>, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022CA;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math></span>. The theorem covers the solvable and matrix-group families specified by Imran–Ivanyos.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum time for the covered platform families, through reductions to group-action orbit and discrete-logarithm problems.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>r</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> group-action operations and storage by semidirect baby-step–giant-step, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math></span> is the orbit period; platform-specific reductions to ordinary discrete logarithms can be faster.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Imran–Ivanyos semidirect discrete logarithm; Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.14028\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Efficient quantum algorithms for some instances of the semidirect discrete logarithm problem</em></a>. Main theorems; extensions https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/905.pdf; current classical analysis https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.05048</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/factoring\">Integer factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithms-in-finite-fields\">Finite-field discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm\">Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a01\">Finite abelian-group decomposition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a02\">Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a05\">Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a06\">Ideal operations in finite rings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a08\">Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q45\">Testing one-to-one periodicity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithm-concept-learning\">Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/semidirect-discrete-logarithm\">→ Imran–Ivanyos semidirect discrete logarithm</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">→ Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a11": {
    "title": "Polynomial factorization over a finite field",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Polynomial factorization over a finite field</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Polynomial factorization over a finite field)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> of degree <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, output its monic irreducible factors and multiplicities. The field and coefficients have explicit finite encodings; complexity counts bit operations.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> expected time by Doliskani; typical factor-degree patterns admit <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized time by Kedlaya–Umans modular-composition factorization. The improvement depends on the relative growth of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Doliskani quantum finite-field factorization</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.09675\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Toward an Optimal Quantum Algorithm for Polynomial Factorization over Finite Fields</em></a>. Theorem 1; distinct-degree algorithm; classical https://doi.org/10.1137/08073408X; update https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.10851</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/doliskani-finite-field-factorization\">→ Doliskani quantum finite-field factorization</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a12": {
    "title": "Polynomial interpolation over finite fields",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Polynomial interpolation over finite fields</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Polynomial interpolation over finite fields)</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Univariate polynomial interpolation over finite fields.</strong> Given evaluation-oracle access to an unknown polynomial <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>, output its coefficients. Complexity counts oracle evaluations; success probabilities depend on the field-size regime.</p><p><strong>Multivariate polynomial interpolation over finite fields.</strong> Given an oracle for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> of total degree at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>, output its <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> coefficients. Complexity counts oracle evaluations in the sufficiently large-field regime.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <strong>Univariate polynomial interpolation over finite fields.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span> queries with the success probability in Childs–van Dam–Hung–Shparlinski; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span> queries achieve success approaching one as <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</strong> <strong>Multivariate polynomial interpolation over finite fields.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span> queries by Chen–Childs–Hung, with success <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> as <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math></span> under the paper&#x27;s interpolation conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <strong>Univariate polynomial interpolation over finite fields.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> evaluations are necessary and sufficient for exact interpolation by Lagrange or fast interpolation. The gain is a factor approaching two in queries.</strong> <strong>Multivariate polynomial interpolation over finite fields.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math></span> evaluations suffice by multivariate interpolation and are information-theoretically necessary for exact reconstruction. The query ratio is approximately <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum polynomial interpolation</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.09271\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Optimal quantum algorithm for polynomial interpolation</em></a>. Main interpolation theorem; ICALP https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ICALP.2016.16</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.03990\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithm for multivariate polynomial interpolation</em></a>. Finite-field theorem; full text https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5806014/</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-polynomial-interpolation\">→ Quantum polynomial interpolation</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a15": {
    "title": "Shifted multiplicative characters",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Shifted multiplicative characters</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Shifted multiplicative characters)</span></h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C7;</mi></mrow></math></span> be a known nontrivial multiplicative character of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, extended by <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C7;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>. Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C7;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> by coherent queries, recover the shift <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>. The Legendre character is the principal example.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates and constantly many oracle calls by van Dam–Hallgren–Ip, using Fourier transforms and Gauss-sum phases.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by testing candidate shifts against <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> sampled evaluations; the query information can be collected with polynomially many calls. The difficulty is computational recovery from those evaluations.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: van Dam–Hallgren–Ip multiplicative-character hidden shift</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0211140\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithms for some Hidden Shift Problems</em></a>. Shifted Legendre-symbol and multiplicative-character algorithms.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/multiplicative-character-hidden-shift\">→ van Dam–Hallgren–Ip multiplicative-character hidden shift</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a16": {
    "title": "Hidden shifts of bent Boolean functions",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hidden shifts of bent Boolean functions</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Hidden shifts of bent Boolean functions)</span></h2>\n<p>Given Boolean phase oracles for a bent function <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, recover <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span>. Bent means <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>f</mi></msup></mrow><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for every <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></span>; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> is even.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: Constantly many queries with access to the dual bent-function oracle, or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries in the corresponding ordinary-oracle formulation, by Rotteler&#x27;s Fourier hidden-shift algorithms.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Exponential classical query lower bounds hold for the oracle families constructed in the paper; querying the full truth tables uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> calls. Explicit formulas for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math></span> may give additional classical structure.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Rotteler bent-function hidden shift</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0811.3208\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for highly non-linear Boolean functions</em></a>. Main bent-function algorithms; Theorems 7 and lower-bound section.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/bent-function-hidden-shift\">→ Rotteler bent-function hidden shift</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a17": {
    "title": "Random Boolean hidden shift",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Random Boolean hidden shift</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Random Boolean hidden shift)</span></h2>\n<p>For a uniformly random known function <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, receive oracle access to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and identify the uniformly random shift <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>. The known-function description may be used freely in query complexity.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: Two queries achieve success tending to one by the Childs–Kothari–Ozols–Rotteler pretty-good measurement. Implementing that measurement can require large circuits for an arbitrary <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> one-bit oracle evaluations identify the shift with high probability when unlimited classical computation over the known truth table is free.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The query-optimal measurement can require exponentially large preprocessing or circuits for a generic supplied truth table.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Pretty-good measurement for Boolean hidden shift</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.4642\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Easy and hard functions for the Boolean hidden shift problem</em></a>. Random-function and pretty-good-measurement results.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/boolean-hidden-shift-pgm\">→ Pretty-good measurement for Boolean hidden shift</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a18": {
    "title": "Generalized hidden shift",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Generalized hidden shift</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Generalized hidden shift)</span></h2>\n<p>Given injective functions <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span> for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> satisfying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, recover <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>. Access to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> is coherent.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Childs–van Dam when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> for a fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, using a pretty-good measurement and a lattice-point subroutine.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Collision-based classical hidden-shift recovery uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> oracle calls; distinct oracle labels hide the shift until cross-function collisions occur.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Childs–van Dam generalized hidden-shift algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0507190\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithm for a generalized hidden shift problem</em></a>. Main efficient-algorithm theorem.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/generalized-hidden-shift-pgm\">→ Childs–van Dam generalized hidden-shift algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a19": {
    "title": "Hidden subgroups of selected semidirect-product groups",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hidden subgroups of selected semidirect-product groups</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Hidden subgroups of selected semidirect-product groups)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a known finite group <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022CA;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math></span> from the families in Bacon–Childs–van Dam and an oracle constant and distinct on left cosets of a hidden subgroup <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>, output generators of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>. Families include specified metacyclic groups and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>&#x022CA;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> for fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time when the associated matrix-sum problem has the efficient solution proved for the selected family; the algorithm implements the optimal coset-state measurement.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Generic coset-label sampling and collision search use polynomially many operations in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span>; the included abelian hidden-subgroup cases have exponential classical query lower bounds in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Bacon–Childs–van Dam semidirect-product HSP algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0504083\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>From optimal measurement to efficient quantum algorithms for the hidden subgroup problem over semidirect product groups</em></a>. Main metacyclic and fixed-r semidirect-product results.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/semidirect-hsp-pgm\">→ Bacon–Childs–van Dam semidirect-product HSP algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a20": {
    "title": "Hidden subgroups of nilpotency-class-two groups",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hidden subgroups of nilpotency-class-two groups</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Hidden subgroups of nilpotency-class-two groups)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a uniquely encoded black-box group <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and a function constant and distinct on cosets of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span>, find generators of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>. Complexity counts oracle queries and gates in the group encoding length.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum time by Ivanyos–Sanselme–Santha, using commutator structure and reductions to abelian hidden-subgroup instances.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Full coset-label acquisition takes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries. Abelian subfamilies include Simon-type instances requiring <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> classical queries.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Ivanyos–Sanselme–Santha nilpotency-two HSP algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.1260\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>An efficient quantum algorithm for the hidden subgroup problem in nil-2 groups</em></a>. Main theorem; extraspecial precursor https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0701235</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/nilpotency-two-hsp\">→ Ivanyos–Sanselme–Santha nilpotency-two HSP algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a21": {
    "title": "Normal hidden subgroup",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Normal hidden subgroup</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Normal hidden subgroup)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a finite group <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> and an oracle constant and distinct on cosets of a normal subgroup <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x022B4;</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span>, recover <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>. More generally, the procedure identifies the normal core of an arbitrary hidden subgroup.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> Fourier samples by Hallgren–Russell–Ta-Shma; efficient gate complexity additionally requires an efficient quantum Fourier transform and efficient kernel-intersection processing for the chosen group.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by complete coset-label acquisition, while included abelian oracle families require <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> randomized queries.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Hallgren–Russell–Ta-Shma normal-subgroup Fourier sampling</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cs.tau.ac.il/ amnon/Papers/HRT.siam02.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Hidden Subgroup Problem and Quantum Computation Using Group Representations</em></a>. Theorem 1.3 and normal-subgroup corollary.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/normal-hsp-fourier-sampling\">→ Hallgren–Russell–Ta-Shma normal-subgroup Fourier sampling</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a22": {
    "title": "Hidden quadratic level sets",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hidden quadratic level sets</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Hidden quadratic level sets)</span></h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> for fixed characteristic <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span>. Given an oracle on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> exactly when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for an unknown polynomial <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math></span> of degree at most two, recover a polynomial defining the same level-set partition. Coefficients are determined up to the partition-preserving equivalence.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum time over constant-characteristic fields, by Decker–Draisma–Ivanyos–Santha&#x27;s reduction from quadratic level sets to hidden symmetry and hidden subgroups.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Classical recovery can enumerate the finite coefficient space in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time. Collision lower bounds for the hidden-label oracle give a positive-power-of-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> obstruction for its hard subfamilies.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Decker–Draisma–Ivanyos–Santha hidden-symmetry algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1107.2189\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Hidden Symmetry Subgroup Problems</em></a>. Theorem 3 and Section 4.3: multivariate quadratic hidden polynomials over constant-characteristic fields</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/hidden-symmetry-subgroup-reduction\">→ Decker–Draisma–Ivanyos–Santha hidden-symmetry algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a23": {
    "title": "Hidden quadratic and cubic polynomial graphs",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hidden quadratic and cubic polynomial graphs</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Hidden quadratic and cubic polynomial graphs)</span></h2>\n<p>For a polynomial <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>:</mi><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> of fixed degree two or three, an oracle <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> has equal outputs exactly when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> agrees. Recover the nonconstant coefficients of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math></span>; the constant term is absorbed by output relabeling.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time for fixed degree and number of variables by Decker–Wocjan&#x27;s polynomial-graph Fourier algorithm.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>q</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical oracle queries are required for the hard fixed-degree families; polynomially many queries in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> suffice by acquiring enough labeled points and interpolating.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Decker–Wocjan hidden-polynomial-graph algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0703195\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Efficient Quantum Algorithm for Hidden Quadratic and Cubic Polynomial Function Graphs</em></a>. Quadratic/cubic hidden-graph algorithms.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0706.1219\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Efficient Quantum Algorithm for Identifying Hidden Polynomials</em></a>. Classical query lower bound and hidden-polynomial identification</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/hidden-polynomial-graph\">→ Decker–Wocjan hidden-polynomial-graph algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a24": {
    "title": "Zeta functions of curves over finite fields",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Zeta functions of curves over finite fields</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Zeta functions of curves over finite fields)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a smooth projective geometrically irreducible genus-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span> curve <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> in the polynomial-size representation of Kedlaya, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> satisfying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Its coefficients determine point counts over all finite extensions.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Kedlaya, using class-group order computations and reconstruction from cyclic resultants.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Schoof–Pila algorithms take <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time for fixed genus, with exponential dependence on genus in general. In small characteristic, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span>-adic cohomology algorithms have polynomial dependence on genus and field-extension degree, with a separate dependence on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Kedlaya quantum zeta-function algorithm; Watrous solvable-group order algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0411623\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum computation of zeta functions of curves</em></a>. Theorem 1; Sections 6–9.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/watrous-group-order\">Watrous solvable-group order algorithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/kedlaya-curve-zeta\">→ Kedlaya quantum zeta-function algorithm</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/watrous-group-order\">→ Watrous solvable-group order algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a39": {
    "title": "Multicollisions in a random function",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Multicollisions in a random function</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Multicollisions in a random function)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a random function <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> with sufficiently large domain and fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span>, find distinct <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> satisfying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Complexity counts random-oracle queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, from the Hosoyamada–Sasaki–Tani–Xagawa recursive algorithm and Liu–Zhandry lower bound.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x02113;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical random-function queries by generalized birthday sampling.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Hosoyamada–Sasaki–Tani–Xagawa multicollision algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.08097\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Improved Quantum Multicollision-Finding Algorithm; On Finding Quantum Multi-collisions</em></a>. Main multicollision theorem; matching lower bound https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/1096.pdf</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-multicollision\">→ Hosoyamada–Sasaki–Tani–Xagawa multicollision algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a40": {
    "title": "Distinguishing three-round Feistel networks",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Distinguishing three-round Feistel networks</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Distinguishing three-round Feistel networks)</span></h2>\n<p>Given coherent chosen-plaintext access to a <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit permutation, distinguish a three-round Feistel network built from independent random round functions from a uniformly random permutation. Complexity counts superposition oracle calls.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> calls by Santoli–Schaffner, who derive a periodic Boolean-vector function and apply Simon&#x27;s algorithm.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical queries in the birthday regime for distinguishing three-round random Feistel from a random permutation.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Simon period finding and offline Simon</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.07856\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Using Simon&#x27;s Algorithm to Attack Symmetric-Key Cryptographic Primitives</em></a>. Three-round Feistel distinguisher section.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/simon\">Simon period finding and offline Simon</a>: <a href=\"#entry/even\">Even–Mansour with quantum queries</a>, <a href=\"#entry/evenmansour-recovery-with-classical-queries\">Even–Mansour with classical queries</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a41\">CBC-MAC forgery under superposition access</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/simon\">→ Simon period finding and offline Simon</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a41": {
    "title": "CBC-MAC forgery under superposition access",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>CBC-MAC forgery under superposition access</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(CBC-MAC forgery under superposition access)</span></h2>\n<p>For the fixed-length CBC-MAC construction attacked by Santoli–Schaffner, given coherent access to the tag function, output a fresh message and a valid tag. The block length is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> and the underlying block cipher is modeled as an ideal permutation.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> coherent tag queries by the Simon-based forgery, which finds an XOR relation between message blocks.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Classical fixed-length CBC-MAC has birthday-scale generic forgery attacks and security bounds, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> tag queries for the relevant collision attacks. The bound concerns the specified secure fixed-length construction and message-query model.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The stated complexity or cryptographic implication provides strong evidence for the separation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Simon period finding and offline Simon</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.07856\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Using Simon&#x27;s Algorithm to Attack Symmetric-Key Cryptographic Primitives</em></a>. CBC-MAC forgery section.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/simon\">Simon period finding and offline Simon</a>: <a href=\"#entry/even\">Even–Mansour with quantum queries</a>, <a href=\"#entry/evenmansour-recovery-with-classical-queries\">Even–Mansour with classical queries</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a40\">Distinguishing three-round Feistel networks</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/simon\">→ Simon period finding and offline Simon</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a42": {
    "title": "Hidden radius over a finite field",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hidden radius over a finite field</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Hidden radius over a finite field)</span></h2>\n<p>In the Childs–Schulman–Vazirani shifted-subset model over <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span>, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span> odd and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></span> odd, the hidden set is the sphere <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Determine the quadratic character <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C7;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> from the randomized hiding oracle.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum time by Fourier sampling of hidden sphere states and estimation of their character-dependent statistics.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Classical oracle-query complexity is exponential in the encoded dimension/field-size regime of the hiding construction; exhaustive access gives a <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> upper bound.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Childs–Schulman–Vazirani hidden-radius algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.2784\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for hidden nonlinear structures</em></a>. Section 3, Theorems 1–3.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/hidden-radius-fourier\">→ Childs–Schulman–Vazirani hidden-radius algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "yz-codeword-hash-search": {
    "title": "Codeword search with random hash constraints",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Codeword search with random hash constraints</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Codeword search with random hash constraints)</span></h2>\n<p>For security parameter <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></span>, fix the folded Reed–Solomon code <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> from Lemma 4.2, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. Given oracle access to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> satisfying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> for every coordinate <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math></span>. This NP search relation has a publicly checkable witness. Complexity uses random-oracle queries and gates; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> denotes output bit <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates and at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></span> quantum queries, by Yamakawa–Zhandry, with success <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>negl</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> over random <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>. Prepare the code superposition and coordinate states supported on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Fourier transform both, coherently add and decode the dual code, then inverse transform. Verification uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> classical hash queries and polynomial code-membership work. A salted two-message construction handles nonuniform provers.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: For fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> in the code construction, every computationally unbounded classical algorithm using at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> oracle queries succeeds with probability at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> (Lemma 6.9). Random codeword trials provide an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>-query exponential comparator on typical random oracles. The keyless bound concerns uniform algorithms; the keyed construction treats oracle-dependent advice. Substituting an explicit cryptographic hash makes hardness an assumption about that family.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Subexponential-or-exponential classical oracle complexity to polynomial quantum queries and gates.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The random-oracle query separation is unconditional. Concrete-hash implementations use a separate hardness assumption.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> data qubits plus polynomial reversible-decoding and hash workspace; fault-tolerant operation is the relevant scale.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Yamakawa–Zhandry Fourier-decoding algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.02063v3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Verifiable Quantum Advantage without Structure</em></a>. Lemma 4.2; Section 6, Theorem 6.1 and Lemmas 6.3, 6.9; Figure 1; Section 7</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/dqi\">Decoded Quantum Interferometry</a>: <a href=\"#entry/optimal-polynomial-intersection-by-decoded-quantum-interferometry\">Optimal polynomial intersection</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/dqi\">→ Decoded Quantum Interferometry</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "trapdoor-claw-proof-quantumness": {
    "title": "Interactive proofs of quantumness",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Interactive proofs of quantumness</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Interactive proofs of quantumness)</span></h2>\n<p>A classical verifier supplies a public key for a trapdoor claw-free pair <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The prover commits to an image <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></span>, then answers a random challenge with a preimage or a prescribed measurement relation between its two preimages. The objective is an acceptance probability above the efficient-classical-prover threshold. Complexity uses gates, communication, and security parameter <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> prover gates and verifier time. Coherent function evaluation and image measurement retain a superposition of the two preimages. BCMVV uses complementary-basis challenges. The computational Bell protocol achieves <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>; Rabin-function implementations use modular squaring.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Under claw-freeness, classical polynomial-time provers satisfy <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>negl</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. For the factoring-based instantiation, factoring the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit modulus gives an attack with heuristic number-field-sieve time <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The LWE instantiation uses its lattice-attack parameters and adaptive-hardcore-bit assumption.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial quantum proving versus superpolynomial classical attacks under the selected cryptographic assumption.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Exceeding the classical soundness threshold yields a cryptographic attack.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial qubits, reversible arithmetic, and mid-circuit measurement with feed-forward; costs depend on the trapdoor family.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: BCMVV test of quantumness; Computational Bell-test protocol</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.00640\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A Cryptographic Test of Quantumness and Certifiable Randomness from a Single Quantum Device</em></a>. Test-of-quantumness and randomness-generation protocols; noisy trapdoor claw-free functions; LWE construction</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-022-01643-7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Classically verifiable quantum advantage from a computational Bell test</em></a>. Interactive protocol; Theorems 1 and 2; Table 1; modular-squaring circuits</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/bcmvv_protocol\">→ BCMVV test of quantumness</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/computational_bell_test\">→ Computational Bell-test protocol</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "discrete-logarithm-concept-learning": {
    "title": "Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts)</span></h2>\n<p>For prime <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span>, generator <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math></span>, and hidden <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>, let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> when <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> lies in the cyclic interval of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></span> exponents starting at <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> otherwise. Given uniform classical labeled examples, learn a classifier with error at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></math></span>. Complexity uses gates and training/inference time; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>p</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time at constant accuracy by support-vector classification with quantum kernel estimation. Kernels are squared overlaps of modular-exponentiation feature states. Alternatively, Shor&#x27;s algorithm maps examples to exponent intervals for classical learning.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Achieving accuracy <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gives an efficient classical discrete-logarithm algorithm. A direct attack computes logarithms with the number field sieve and learns the interval, with heuristic time <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for prime fields, up to polynomial training overhead.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Subexponential known classical preprocessing to polynomial quantum learning.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>An efficient classical learner with inverse-polynomial prediction advantage would solve discrete logarithms.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial logical qubits and modular arithmetic; kernel shots and classical support-vector training enter the cost.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Support-vector classification with quantum kernel estimation; Shor order finding and discrete logarithms</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.02174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A rigorous and robust quantum speed-up in supervised machine learning</em></a>. Theorems 1 and 2; Appendix B, Definition 6 and Theorem 8; Appendix C.3</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/factoring\">Integer factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithms-in-finite-fields\">Finite-field discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm\">Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a01\">Finite abelian-group decomposition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a02\">Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a05\">Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a06\">Ideal operations in finite rings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a08\">Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a10\">Semidirect discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q45\">Testing one-to-one periodicity</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/svm_quantum_kernel\">→ Support-vector classification with quantum kernel estimation</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">→ Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "local": {
    "title": "Local minima on the Boolean hypercube",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Local minima on the Boolean hypercube</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Local search on the Boolean hypercube)</span></h2>\n<p>Given value-oracle access to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow></math></span>, output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> such that <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02295;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for every coordinate <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math></span>, with probability at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>. Values have a finite comparison encoding. This models single-bit local improvement in combinatorial optimization. Complexity is measured in value-oracle queries for both quantum and randomized classical algorithms.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, quantum sampling and local descent.</strong> Minimum finding selects a low-valued starting point, and quantum search finds improving neighbors. The bound is tight. On a fixed-dimensional grid with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span> vertices and dimension at least six, the optimal quantum query complexity is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, randomized sampling and local descent.</strong> The Aldous-type algorithm samples vertices, chooses the lowest sampled value, and follows improving neighbors. For a fixed-dimensional grid of dimension at least four, the tight randomized bound is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup in the domain size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, with a smaller exponential constant in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>. Minimum finding and amplitude amplification are algorithmic components.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Matching quantum and randomized query lower bounds establish the separation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>A fault-tolerant implementation needs an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-qubit vertex register, value and comparison workspace, and coherent neighborhood access. Exponentially many queries dominate the runtime; the evaluator determines the additional logical-qubit count.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum sampling and local descent</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0504085\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Zhang, <em>(Almost) Tight Bounds for Randomized and Quantum Local Search on Hypercubes and Grids</em> (2005 preprint; journal version, 2009)</a>. Introduction, Theorems 1–2 and the discussion of matching sampling/descent upper bounds; Section 3 for the hypercube lower bound.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0307149\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Aaronson, <em>Lower Bounds for Local Search by Quantum Arguments</em> (2003/2006)</a>. Proposition 2 for the quantum algorithm and the preceding paragraph for randomized sampling and descent.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>: <a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>, <a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>, <a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>, <a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/minimum-finding\">→ Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "total-boolean-decision-problems-a-fourth-power-bound": {
    "title": "Total Boolean decision problems: a fourth-power bound",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Total Boolean decision problems: a fourth-power bound</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Total Boolean function evaluation)</span></h2>\n<p>For a total function <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, determine <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> by accessing individual bits of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span>. Every <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span>-bit string is a valid input. Threshold decision versions of explicitly encoded optimization problems are examples. Complexity is measured in input-bit oracle queries, with deterministic classical and bounded-error quantum algorithms distinguished.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, an optimal bounded-error quantum decision algorithm.</strong> The theorem relates this resource to deterministic query complexity for every total Boolean function. In particular, every nonconstant monotone graph property on an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-vertex adjacency matrix has quantum query complexity <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries, deterministic decision-tree evaluation.</strong> A decision tree achieving this bound exists for every total <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math></span>. Randomized query complexity satisfies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Known total functions attain the fourth-power deterministic-versus-quantum relationship up to logarithmic factors. The resource being bounded is the number of queried input bits; computation on the answers is free in this model.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>A polynomial upper bound on the possible query advantage, including separations greater than quadratic. The fourth-power relationship concerns deterministic classical complexity.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Unconditional query-complexity theorems, with the fourth-power exponent attained up to logarithmic factors.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Not applicable: this entry is a theorem relating query-complexity measures over all total Boolean functions. Hardware resources belong to a chosen function and its decision circuit.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Bounded-error quantum query algorithms</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.13231\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Aaronson, Ben-David, Kothari and Tal, <em>Quantum Implications of Huang&#x27;s Sensitivity Theorem</em> (2020)</a>. Theorem 1 for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>; Theorem 3 for monotone graph properties. Sections 3–4 give the corresponding arguments.</li>\n</ul></section>"
  },
  "symmetric-promise-problems-and-graph-properties": {
    "title": "Symmetric promise problems and graph properties",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Symmetric promise problems and graph properties</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Symmetric decision problems and graph property testing)</span></h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003A3;</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> be invariant under every permutation of the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span> input positions. Given oracle access to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></math></span>, determine <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. For graph properties, require invariance under vertex relabeling and specify either adjacency-matrix or adjacency-list access. In the latter model, let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> be the graph family of maximum degree five defined by Ben-David et al., Section 6, with tree-depth parameter <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>. Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> and a neighbor oracle <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x022A5;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> for an undirected multigraph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span>, distinguish\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>from</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace=\"0.22em\" rspace=\"0.22em\"><mrow><mi>&#x025B3;</mi></mrow></mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>N</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nwith error at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>. Edge edits count multiplicity; self-loops and parallel edges are allowed. These models describe the role of symmetry and input access in quantum speedups. Complexity is measured in the corresponding input-symbol, adjacency-matrix, or neighbor-oracle queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> queries for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, quantum-walk property testing; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for the symmetric decision problems.</strong> The tester identifies weld vertices by quantum walks and checks the graph structure locally. It has perfect completeness and rejects <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>-far inputs with probability at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> denote bounded-error quantum and randomized query complexity; each comparison uses the same promise and oracle representation.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries required for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> at a fixed constant <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> sufficient by reading the graph; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for full permutation symmetry, Chailloux&#x27;s simulation.</strong> The lower bound holds for every bounded-error randomized tester. Reading the adjacency lists permits exact membership testing with unrestricted offline computation. For adjacency-matrix graph properties, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>6</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>; for fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math></span>-uniform hypergraphs, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential to polynomial query complexity in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> for adjacency-list testing of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>. Full permutation symmetry and adjacency-matrix graph symmetry impose the stated polynomial limits.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Unconditional classical query lower bounds establish the adjacency-list separation; classical simulation theorems establish the limits in the other models.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Circuit-based assessment: demanding coherent data access, comparable to the welded-tree traversal and graph-query algorithms. A vertex register uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>N</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span> logical qubits; the bounded-degree walk uses a constant-size neighbor-index register, further vertex registers, and reversible-oracle and walk-control workspace. The <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> coherent neighbor-query sequence requires fault-tolerant control at useful scales. For an explicitly stored graph, coherent lookup circuitry or memory routing is the main hardware burden; a succinct reversible neighbor circuit reduces this burden.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Ben-David et al. adjacency-list graph-property tester</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ITCS.2019.19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Chailloux, <em>A Note on the Quantum Query Complexity of Permutation Symmetric Functions</em> (2019)</a>. Theorem 3; Section 3, especially Section 3.1, for the classical simulation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1137/23M1573975\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ben-David et al., <em>Symmetries, Graph Properties, and Quantum Speedups</em> (2024)</a>. Theorem 1.2 and Corollary 4.14: matrix-model limits. Section 6, pp. FOCS20-393–394: formal definition of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> and edge-edit distance. Theorem 6.1 and Sections 6.1–6.3: quantum tester and classical lower bound.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/symmetric-graph-tester\">→ Ben-David et al. adjacency-list graph-property tester</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "exact-counting-and-parity-of-feasible-solutions": {
    "title": "Exact counting and parity of feasible solutions",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Exact counting and parity of feasible solutions</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Exact counting and parity-SAT)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a predicate <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, compute <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> exactly, or compute this integer modulo two. The predicate is supplied either as an oracle or as an explicit CNF formula. These tasks count feasible solutions and determine the parity of their number. Complexity is measured in predicate-oracle queries for the oracle formulation and in classical bit operations for explicitly encoded formulas.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span> queries for parity and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for exact counting, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>.</strong> The parity algorithm uses phase kickback on pairs of input bits. The query bounds apply to bounded-error computation of the exact answer. For explicit fixed-width CNF input, a quantum computer can also execute the classical counting algorithm below.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span> queries for oracle parity and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for oracle counting; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time for explicit fixed-width CNF, tensor-contraction counting.</strong> Reading and accumulating the truth-table entries attains the oracle bounds. For a fixed-width <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-CNF with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> variables and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> clauses, exact counting takes\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nFor <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> with fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></span>, the time is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. Reducing the count modulo two gives parity-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT at the same cost.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Constant-factor oracle improvement for parity; equal asymptotic oracle complexity for exact counting. Explicit fixed-width counting has a subexponential classical upper bound without power saving.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Matching oracle lower bounds and rigorous counting upper bounds for the explicit representation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Small parity circuits are shallow implementations. General oracle counting requires exponentially many calls and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> address/count registers in addition to evaluator workspace.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Pairwise quantum parity algorithm; exact counting</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9802049\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Beals et al., <em>Quantum Lower Bounds by Polynomials</em> (1998/2001)</a>. Section 6, Proposition 6.4 and the preceding pairwise parity algorithm; Table 1 summarizes the tight bounds.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI, <em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 for fixed-density counting and Appendix D for the general fixed-width consequence.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/parity\">→ Pairwise quantum parity algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems": {
    "title": "Fine-grained complexity of satisfiability and string problems",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Fine-grained complexity of satisfiability and string problems</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Succinct truth-table properties)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a Boolean representation <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> inputs, compute a specified truth-table property, such as <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x022C1;</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02A01;</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The representation class is fixed-width CNF, unrestricted-width CNF, a branching program, or a circuit. Fine-grained reductions connect these tasks to edit distance and longest common subsequence. Complexity is measured in running time on the explicit representation, using quantum gates and classical bit or word operations as specified by each reduction.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for satisfiability by Grover search; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for fixed-width CNF by classical counting.</strong> Buhrman–Patro–Speelman&#x27;s basic QSETH concerns unrestricted-width CNF and postulates a <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> lower bound. Their broader framework specifies both the representation class and the truth-table property. The edit-distance and longest-common-subsequence consequences give conditional <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> quantum lower bounds for length-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span> strings under the corresponding compression-obliviousness assumptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for fixed-width CNF counting; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for the two string problems by dynamic programming.</strong> Sparse fixed-width counting improves to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. The Satisfiability Hypothesis (SH) is equivalent to Weak ETH: for every fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, 3-SAT has no <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> classical algorithm, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math></span> is input length. If a reduction maps 3-SAT size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> to target size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, SH excludes target time <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for every fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>. Counting versions satisfy the analogous equivalence.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>These are upper bounds and conditional lower-bound implications. The fixed-width counting upper bound is subexponential without power saving; SH addresses a fixed-power reduction inside the exponent.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The reduction theorems are rigorous. The target lower bounds are conditional on the explicitly named SH or representation-specific QSETH assumptions.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Circuit-based assessment: large coherent resources, on the same scale as amplified SAT search. For a size-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> Boolean verification circuit on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> assignment bits, straightforward reversible evaluation and uncomputation use <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical qubits and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates per Grover iteration. A worst-case search uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> coherent iterations, giving <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates. The dominant difficulty is sustaining an exponentially long fault-tolerant computation with polynomial active workspace.</p><p>[Before “ETH is false”, the general classical 3-SAT bound was <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.30704</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, and amplified PPSZ claimed a quadratic improvement to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>1.1433</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.]</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Grover satisfiability search; fixed-width CNF counting</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.STACS.2021.19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Buhrman, Patro and Speelman, <em>A Framework of Quantum Strong Exponential-Time Hypotheses</em> (2021)</a>. Sections 1–2 for basic QSETH and the general framework; Corollaries 11–12 for consequences; Appendix B for example lower bounds.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D for fixed-width counting.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02650\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">MIT Hardness Group et al., <em>ETH is Dead, Long Live SH</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 2.4 and Corollary 2.5 for decision/counting equivalences; Theorem 3.1 for transfer through reductions.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>: <a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>, <a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>, <a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>, <a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/grover\">→ Grover search and amplitude amplification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "stoquastic-frustration-free-constraint-satisfaction": {
    "title": "Stoquastic frustration-free constraint satisfaction",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Stoquastic frustration-free constraint satisfaction</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Stoquastic <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> qubits, with each positive semidefinite <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> acting on at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> qubits and having real nonpositive off-diagonal entries, distinguish <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Zero energy means that the local constraints share a zero vector. Complexity is measured in classical running time and quantum evolution time or simulation gates, with the path and spectral-gap promises specified below.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: Polynomial evolution time for a suitably gapped, initialized path, stoquastic frustration-free adiabatic evolution.</strong> The polynomial guarantee uses the smoothness, inverse-polynomial gap and initialization assumptions (A0)–(A3) of Bravyi–Terhal. For unrestricted instances, stoquastic 6-SAT is MA-complete: a polynomial-time quantum solution would imply <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math></span> and hence <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time, Bravyi–Terhal sampling under the same path assumptions.</strong> The simulator samples a distribution within total variation distance <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math></span> of the final ground-state measurement distribution. Its nonnegative-amplitude random walks track the path. Stoquastic <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT is in MA for each fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, with a classical randomized polynomial-time witness verifier.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial classical simulation in the specified adiabatic regime; MA-completeness characterizes the unrestricted decision problem.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous simulation, containment and completeness theorems.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The ideal analog system uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> qubits; a digital realization adds Hamiltonian-simulation ancillas. Polynomial evolution requires the stated gap and initialization conditions.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Stoquastic frustration-free adiabatic evolution</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0806.1746\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bravyi and Terhal, <em>Complexity of Stoquastic Frustration-Free Hamiltonians</em> (2008/2009)</a>. Definitions 1.6–1.8 for stoquastic <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT; assumptions (A0)–(A3) and Theorem 1.4 for classical sampling; Section 5 for the sampler; Theorem 1.9 and Section 6 for MA verification and completeness.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/stoquastic-adiabatic\">→ Stoquastic frustration-free adiabatic evolution</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "local-hamiltonian-ground-energy-optimization": {
    "title": "Local Hamiltonian ground-energy optimization",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Local Hamiltonian ground-energy optimization</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Local Hamiltonian)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> qubits, with constant-local terms of polynomially bounded norm, distinguish <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></span> from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span>, promised <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Diagonal terms encode classical constraint optimization; noncommuting terms permit quantum witnesses. Complexity is measured in quantum gates or block-encoding queries and classical arithmetic operations at the required precision.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates, energy filtering and amplitude amplification at inverse-polynomial precision.</strong> A purification of the maximally mixed state supplies ground-subspace overlap at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. In a guided-state regime, a preparable state with ground-state overlap amplitude <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B3;</mi></mrow></math></span>, gap lower bound <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi></mrow></math></span>, and block encoding of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow></math></span> allows ground-state preparation in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> block-encoding queries. The 2-local decision problem is QMA-complete, so a polynomial-time quantum solution would imply <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> arithmetic operations, randomized sparse-matrix Lanczos.</strong> Each matrix-vector product costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> operations; inverse-polynomial accuracy contributes polynomial overhead. State-vector memory is exponential. For the diagonal subfamily with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> constant-arity terms and constant-bit integer weights, a linear-size threshold circuit and sparse counting instead give exact minimization in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> time.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial speedup through a smaller exponential constant for the generic noncommuting comparison. Guided-state preparation is polynomial when its overlap, gap and block encoding have polynomial costs.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Proved algorithmic upper bounds and QMA-completeness; the generic runtime advantage is a comparison with known classical algorithms.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>A fault-tolerant implementation uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> qubits for the mixed-state purification, plus filtering and arithmetic ancillas. The generic method has exponential depth; guided-state resources depend on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B3;</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi></mrow></math></span> and precision.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Lin–Tong ground-state filtering and energy estimation</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.12508\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Lin and Tong, <em>Near-optimal Ground State Preparation</em> (2020)</a>. Theorem 6 for preparation with a ground-energy bound; Algorithm 1 and Theorem 8 for ground-energy estimation and binary search.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1137/0613066\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kuczyński and Woźniakowski, <em>Estimating the Largest Eigenvalue by the Power and Lanczos Algorithms with a Random Start</em> (1992)</a>. The Lanczos analysis for randomized extremal-eigenvalue estimation; apply it to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0406180\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kempe, Kitaev and Regev, <em>The Complexity of the Local Hamiltonian Problem</em> (2004/2006)</a>. Theorem 1 for 2-local QMA-completeness.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">OpenAI (2026)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix C for the diagonal circuit/counting specialization.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/ground-state-filtering\">→ Lin–Tong ground-state filtering and energy estimation</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters": {
    "title": "Global optimization of variational-circuit parameters",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Global optimization of variational-circuit parameters</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Variational quantum circuit optimization)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a parameterized circuit <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, an observable <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>, a parameter domain <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> on which the minimum is attained, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, find <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi></mrow></math></span> such that\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x02020;</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi></mrow></msub><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x02020;</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nThis is the outer optimization in VQE and variational combinatorial algorithms, including QAOA. Complexity is measured in circuit samples or coherent evaluation-oracle queries for objective evaluation, together with the classical or quantum time for the outer optimization.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> circuit samples or <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> coherent oracle uses for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span> objective evaluations, sampling or amplitude estimation.</strong> The objective is normalized to a bounded observable and each evaluation has additive accuracy <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math></span> with constant success probability. Simultaneous high confidence adds logarithmic factors. The global optimizer determines <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></math></span>. Bittel–Kliesch establish NP-hard parameter-optimization families even when the expectations are efficiently classically evaluable.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> expected time per exact sample, Gheorghiu–Gupte counting-based simulation of linear-size circuits.</strong> This bound holds for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-qubit circuits with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates from a fixed finite set of bounded-arity algebraic gates. Sampling within total variation distance <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi></mrow></math></span> takes worst-case time <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Estimating a sampled observable multiplies this cost by the sample count. Gate count is measured after compilation to the specified finite set. The specialized <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> method, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> Hadamards and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> diagonal gates, has exact worst-case time <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. Classical grid search supplies a global upper bound exponential in the parameter dimension for a compact domain and a known Lipschitz bound.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Coherent evaluation has a quadratic precision advantage. Global optimization complexity depends on the circuit family, landscape and outer optimizer.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The evaluation bounds, classical sampling bounds and NP-hardness results are rigorous. A general global-optimization speedup has no established evidence.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Small noisy circuits implement the sampling loop. Coherent amplitude estimation needs controlled state preparation and its inverse, with fault-tolerant depth. Logical storage consists of the ansatz register and evaluation ancillas.</p><p>[Before “ETH is false”, generic state-vector simulation of linear-size circuits took <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time; variational speedup claims often used this exponential evaluation baseline.]</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: QAOA and VQE objective evaluation; quantum amplitude estimation</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2101.07267\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bittel and Kliesch, <em>Training Variational Quantum Algorithms Is NP-Hard</em> (2021)</a>. Problem 4 and Theorem 1 for VQA optimization; Problem 6 and Theorem 3 for QAOA.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0005055\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Brassard et al., <em>Quantum Amplitude Amplification and Estimation</em> (2002)</a>. Section 4, <span class=\"small-caps\">Est_Amp</span> and Theorem 12.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1RzhP9ZMy1xkC22oqKmMHEOh7gG1cwkfA/view\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gheorghiu and Gupte, <em>Subexponential-Time Sampling of Quantum Circuits from Exact Counting</em> (2026 note)</a>. Theorems 1–2; Sections 3–5 for conditional sampling, finite algebraic gates and the Hadamard/diagonal specialization.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>: <a href=\"#entry/approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments\">Approximate counting</a>, <a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>: <a href=\"#entry/maxcut-approximation-with-qaoa\">MaxCut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/bounded-occurrence-max-e3lin2\">Max-E3LIN2</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-independent-set-in-rydberg-geometric-graphs\">Rydberg independent set</a>, <a href=\"#entry/sherringtonkirkpatrick-spin-glass-optimization\">SK spin-glass optimization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-k-cut-with-variational-quantum-circuits\">Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-Cut</a>, <a href=\"#entry/maximum-bisection-with-constraint-preserving-qaoa\">Maximum bisection</a>, <a href=\"#entry/job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs\">Job-shop scheduling</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-portfolio-rebalancing-with-constrained-mixers\">Discrete portfolio rebalancing</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/vqe\">Variational quantum eigensolver and filtering VQE</a>: <a href=\"#entry/job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs\">Job-shop scheduling</a>, <a href=\"#entry/two-community-modularity-maximization-by-variational-local-search\">Community modularity</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/amplitude-estimation\">→ Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/qaoa\">→ QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/vqe\">→ Variational quantum eigensolver and filtering VQE</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "adiabatic-optimization-spectral-gap-costs-and-oracle-bounds": {
    "title": "Adiabatic optimization: spectral-gap costs and oracle bounds",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Adiabatic optimization: spectral-gap costs and oracle bounds</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Adiabatic ground-state optimization)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a smooth Hamiltonian path <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>, with known initial ground state and final diagonal Hamiltonian <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math></span>, prepare a final ground state and measure a minimizer of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></span>. Write <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for the ground-state spectral gap. Complexity is measured in continuous evolution time with a fixed oracle energy scale; digital realizations additionally count Hamiltonian-simulation gates, and the classical comparison counts cost-oracle queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> evolution time for unstructured marked-item search, locally adiabatic search; gap-dependent time for a general path.</strong> For a nondegenerate ground state, a rigorous adiabatic error bound is proportional to\n<span class=\"math-block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0222B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>&#x02033;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></span>\nDerivative and gap bounds therefore determine a sufficient evolution time <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></span>. Roland–Cerf&#x27;s locally adjusted schedule attains the square-root bound for a marked item among <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span> configurations, with constant success probability and fixed energy normalization.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> cost queries, exhaustive minimum finding or unstructured search.</strong> The classical procedure evaluates the costs and retains the smallest value. Structured cost functions have the problem-specific classical bounds given in the corresponding catalogue entries. Continuous-time oracle lower bounds of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> match quantum unstructured search and apply to scrambled-cost constructions in adiabatic optimization.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Tight square-root oracle improvement for unstructured search; the general-path runtime depends on the gap and derivatives. The search schedule realizes the adiabatic form of Grover&#x27;s improvement.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Rigorous adiabatic error bounds and unstructured oracle lower bounds establish the stated comparisons.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The system register has <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>N</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span> qubits, with simulation ancillas in a digital implementation. Search takes exponential evolution time in the register size; coherent control and precision contribute to implementation cost.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Roland–Cerf local adiabatic search</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0107015\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Roland and Cerf, <em>Quantum Search by Local Adiabatic Evolution</em> (2002)</a>. Equations (16)–(20), especially the schedule in (17)–(18).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0603175\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Jansen, Ruskai and Seiler, <em>Bounds for the Adiabatic Approximation with Applications to Quantum Computation</em> (2007)</a>. Section II, Theorem 3, for the explicit derivative/gap error bound.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0512159\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Farhi et al., <em>How to Make the Quantum Adiabatic Algorithm Fail</em> (2005/2008)</a>. Section III, Theorem 2, for the scrambled-cost construction and oracle runtime bound.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/local-adiabatic-search\">→ Roland–Cerf local adiabatic search</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a31": {
    "title": "Jones polynomial approximation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Jones polynomial approximation</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Jones polynomial approximation)</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Jones polynomial of a plat closure.</strong> Given an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-strand braid <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math></span> of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span>, a permitted root of unity <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, estimate the Jones polynomial of its plat closure to the additive scale <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Complexity is in the finite braid description.</p><p><strong>Jones polynomial of a trace closure.</strong> Given a braid and a permitted fixed root of unity, estimate the Jones polynomial of its trace closure to inverse-polynomial additive error after the Markov-trace normalization. Input and output are finite braid data and a numerical estimate.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <strong>Jones polynomial of a plat closure.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum time by Aharonov–Jones–Landau, using the path-model braid representation and a Hadamard test.</strong> <strong>Jones polynomial of a trace closure.</strong> <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time in the one-clean-qubit model by Shor–Jordan, using normalized-trace estimation of the path-model braid representation.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <strong>Jones polynomial of a plat closure.</strong> Classical tensor contraction or state-vector evaluation takes <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time, with width-dependent improvements. At universal fixed roots and inverse-polynomial normalized error, efficient classical approximation would imply <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">Q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">P</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math></span>. For an implemented <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>-qubit, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span>-gate circuit over a fixed finite algebraic gate set, classical simulation also gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span> is a contraction width and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> is the size of the sparse bounded-fan-in Boolean encoding of scalar path counts at the required precision; gate synthesis and precision contribute to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> <strong>Jones polynomial of a trace closure.</strong> General exact contraction is exponential in braid width. A polynomial-time classical algorithm for the complete normalized approximation regime would place <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">Q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">C</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></math></span> inside <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math></span>. For an implemented <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>-qubit, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span>-gate circuit over a fixed finite algebraic gate set, classical simulation also gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span> is a contraction width and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> is the size of the sparse bounded-fan-in Boolean encoding of scalar path counts at the required precision; gate synthesis and precision contribute to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The stated complexity or cryptographic implication provides strong evidence for the separation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Aharonov–Jones–Landau Jones-polynomial approximation; One-clean-qubit Jones and HOMFLY approximation</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0511096\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A Polynomial Quantum Algorithm for Approximating the Jones Polynomial</em></a>. Theorems 1.2–1.3; Section 3 algorithms. Modern fifth-root normalization: https://arxiv.org/html/2503.05625v2, Section II.1.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em></a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D; applied to sparse Boolean encodings of scalar path counts</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.2831\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Estimating Jones polynomials is a complete problem for one clean qubit</em></a>. DQC1 algorithm and completeness theorem.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/one-clean-qubit-link-invariants\">One-clean-qubit Jones and HOMFLY approximation</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-a33\">HOMFLY polynomial approximation</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/ajl-jones-approximation\">→ Aharonov–Jones–Landau Jones-polynomial approximation</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/one-clean-qubit-link-invariants\">→ One-clean-qubit Jones and HOMFLY approximation</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a33": {
    "title": "HOMFLY polynomial approximation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>HOMFLY polynomial approximation</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(HOMFLY polynomial approximation)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a braid and fixed admissible root-of-unity parameters for a Hecke-algebra representation, estimate the normalized HOMFLY invariant of its trace closure within additive <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>. The normalization is the representation&#x27;s normalized Markov trace.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates by Jordan–Wocjan normalized-trace estimation in the one-clean-qubit model, for the representation families constructed in the paper.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Classical evaluation by Hecke-algebra state propagation is exponential in the representation size parameter. The Jones specialization contains the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">Q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">C</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></math></span>-complete additive-approximation regime. For an implemented <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>-qubit, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span>-gate circuit over a fixed finite algebraic gate set, classical simulation also gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span> is a contraction width and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> is the size of the sparse bounded-fan-in Boolean encoding of scalar path counts at the required precision; gate synthesis and precision contribute to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The stated complexity or cryptographic implication provides strong evidence for the separation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The one-clean-qubit model uses one initialized control qubit together with a polynomial-size maximally mixed register; coherent controlled representation gates and repeated measurements implement the estimator.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: One-clean-qubit Jones and HOMFLY approximation</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0807.4688\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Jones polynomial: quantum algorithms and applications in quantum complexity theory; Estimating Jones and HOMFLY polynomials with One Clean Qubit</em></a>. HOMFLY algorithm; precursor https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0603069</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em></a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D; applied to sparse Boolean encodings of scalar path counts</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/one-clean-qubit-link-invariants\">One-clean-qubit Jones and HOMFLY approximation</a>: <a href=\"#entry/prior-a31\">Jones polynomial approximation</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/one-clean-qubit-link-invariants\">→ One-clean-qubit Jones and HOMFLY approximation</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a34": {
    "title": "Additive approximation of the planar Tutte polynomial",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Additive approximation of the planar Tutte polynomial</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Additive approximation of the planar Tutte polynomial)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a planar graph <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with specified edge weights, estimate its multivariate Tutte polynomial <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> to additive <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> times the tensor-network operator-norm scale of Aharonov–Arad–Eban–Landau.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum time for the efficiently representable parameter regimes, using a Temperley–Lieb representation and an overlap-estimation circuit.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> state-sum evaluation, or width-based tensor contraction, applies generally. The specified complete approximation regimes are <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">Q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math></span>-complete. For an implemented <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>-qubit, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span>-gate circuit over a fixed finite algebraic gate set, classical simulation also gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span> is a contraction width and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> is the size of the sparse bounded-fan-in Boolean encoding of scalar path counts at the required precision; gate synthesis and precision contribute to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The stated complexity or cryptographic implication provides strong evidence for the separation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Aharonov–Arad–Eban–Landau Tutte approximation</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0702008\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Polynomial Quantum algorithms for additive approximations of the Potts model and other points of the Tutte plane</em></a>. Definition 2.1; algorithm section and BQP-hardness results.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em></a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D; applied to sparse Boolean encodings of scalar path counts</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-tutte-approximation\">→ Aharonov–Arad–Eban–Landau Tutte approximation</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a35": {
    "title": "Turaev–Viro invariant",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Turaev–Viro invariant</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Turaev–Viro invariant)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a closed 3-manifold by a genus-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span> Heegaard splitting and a length-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> mapping-class word, estimate its Turaev–Viro invariant for a fixed allowed category to additive error <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"script\">D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">D</mi></mrow></math></span> the total quantum dimension.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates for failure probability <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math></span> by Alagic–Jordan–Konig–Reichardt, through mapping-class representations.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Classical state-sum or tensor-network contraction is exponential in the presentation width; the normalized approximation is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">Q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math></span>-complete for the specified <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> theories. For an implemented <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>-qubit, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span>-gate circuit over a fixed finite algebraic gate set, classical simulation also gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span> is a contraction width and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> is the size of the sparse bounded-fan-in Boolean encoding of scalar path counts at the required precision; gate synthesis and precision contribute to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The stated complexity or cryptographic implication provides strong evidence for the separation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Alagic–Jordan–Konig–Reichardt Turaev–Viro estimator</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.0923\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Approximating Turaev-Viro 3-manifold invariants is universal for quantum computation</em></a>. Theorem 1.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em></a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D; applied to sparse Boolean encodings of scalar path counts</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/turaev-viro-quantum-estimation\">→ Alagic–Jordan–Konig–Reichardt Turaev–Viro estimator</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a36": {
    "title": "Witten–Reshetikhin–Turaev invariant",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Witten–Reshetikhin–Turaev invariant</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Witten–Reshetikhin–Turaev invariant)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a 3-manifold by a genus-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span> Heegaard splitting and mapping-class word <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span>, estimate its normalized Witten–Reshetikhin–Turaev invariant, represented as the matrix element <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math></span> after the standard <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"script\">D</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> prefactor. The level is fixed and admissible.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: Polynomial time in the word length, genus and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> by Garnerone–Marzuoli–Rasetti&#x27;s quantum recoupling algorithm.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Classical propagation through the full conformal-block basis is exponential in genus for fixed nontrivial level; contraction methods exploit smaller presentation width when available. For an implemented <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>-qubit, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span>-gate circuit over a fixed finite algebraic gate set, classical simulation also gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span> is a contraction width and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> is the size of the sparse bounded-fan-in Boolean encoding of scalar path counts at the required precision; gate synthesis and precision contribute to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The quantum bound improves a published classical algorithm in the stated regime; the comparison is between algorithmic upper bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Garnerone–Marzuoli–Rasetti quantum recoupling</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0703037\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Efficient quantum processing of 3-manifold topological invariants</em></a>. Quantum algorithm sections; Heegaard normalization also Eq. (3) in https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.0923</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em></a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D; applied to sparse Boolean encodings of scalar path counts</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/wrt-quantum-recoupling\">→ Garnerone–Marzuoli–Rasetti quantum recoupling</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a37": {
    "title": "Link invariants from quantum doubles of finite groups",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Link invariants from quantum doubles of finite groups</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Link invariants from quantum doubles of finite groups)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a braid with strands colored by irreducible representations of the quantum double <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, estimate the normalized invariant of its plat or trace closure to additive error <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>. The input specifies finite-group operations and the representation labels.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time by Krovi–Russell whenever the required centralizer Fourier transforms and representation operations are efficient.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: For fluxon labels <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, Krovi–Russell give a classical algorithm with the same polynomial bound by sampling permutation-matrix entries. General representation labels can require exponentially large representation matrices. For an implemented <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>-qubit, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span>-gate circuit over a fixed finite algebraic gate set, classical simulation also gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span> is a contraction width and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> is the size of the sparse bounded-fan-in Boolean encoding of scalar path counts at the required precision; gate synthesis and precision contribute to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>The fluxon additive-approximation problem has polynomial quantum and classical algorithms. For general representation labels, the classical comparison depends on representation and contraction complexity.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The paper supplies a direct classical randomized algorithm for the fluxon subfamily; its BPP-completeness supports that comparison.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Krovi–Russell quantum-double link estimation</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.1550\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Fourier Transforms and the Complexity of Link Invariants for Quantum Doubles of Finite Groups</em></a>. QFT construction, link-invariant algorithm and hardness sections.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em></a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D; applied to sparse Boolean encodings of scalar path counts</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum-double-link-estimation\">→ Krovi–Russell quantum-double link estimation</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-a38": {
    "title": "Quadratically signed weight enumerators",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quadratically signed weight enumerators</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Quadratically signed weight enumerators)</span></h2>\n<p>Given binary matrices <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math></span> and rational parameters <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></span>, evaluate or decide the promised sign of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>B</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. The quantum result uses the matrix structure, parameters and sign-gap promise of Knill–Laflamme.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> quantum time for the complete promised sign problem, by encoding a real quantum circuit into the enumerator.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> direct summation, improved to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mi>ker</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow></msup><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> by enumerating the kernel. The promised complete regime is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">Q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math></span>-complete. For an implemented <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>-qubit, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span>-gate circuit over a fixed finite algebraic gate set, classical simulation also gives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Here <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span> is a contraction width and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> is the size of the sparse bounded-fan-in Boolean encoding of scalar path counts at the required precision; gate synthesis and precision contribute to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The stated complexity or cryptographic implication provides strong evidence for the separation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Knill–Laflamme weight-enumerator algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9909094\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Computation and Quadratically Signed Weight Enumerators</em></a>. Promised signed-weight-enumerator completeness results.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em></a>. Theorem 1.1 and Appendix D; applied to sparse Boolean encodings of scalar path counts</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quadratically-signed-weight-enumerator\">→ Knill–Laflamme weight-enumerator algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q01": {
    "title": "Deutsch–Jozsa promise problem",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Deutsch–Jozsa promise problem</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Deutsch–Jozsa promise problem)</span></h2>\n<p>Given oracle access to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, promised to be constant or balanced, decide which case holds. The exact and bounded-error query models give different comparisons.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: One exact oracle query and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> additional gates by the Deutsch–Jozsa Hadamard-transform circuit.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> worst-case queries for exact classical decision; bounded-error randomized testing uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> samples at constant confidence.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential to polynomial for exact worst-case decision; constant-error randomized testing has constant query complexity.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The transform circuit uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical qubits and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> elementary gates around its oracle calls; implementing the oracle determines the full device cost. Small circuit demonstrations are feasible.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1992.0167\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Rapid solution of problems by quantum computation</em></a>. Main algorithm, pp.553–558</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/deutsch-jozsa\">→ Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q04": {
    "title": "Forrelation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Forrelation</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Forrelation)</span></h2>\n<p>Given phase oracles <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, distinguish the promised large and small values of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msup><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. The <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-fold version alternates <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span> phase functions and Hadamard transforms.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates for two-function Forrelation; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-fold Forrelation, by Aaronson–Ambainis.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized queries for the two-function promise, with an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical algorithm. These bounds are tight up to logarithmic factors.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The transform circuit uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical qubits and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> elementary gates around its oracle calls; implementing the oracle determines the full device cost. Small circuit demonstrations are feasible.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Forrelation Hadamard-transform algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.5729\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Forrelation: A Problem that Optimally Separates Quantum from Classical Computing</em></a>. Main separation theorem and k-fold BQP-completeness construction</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/forrelation\">→ Forrelation Hadamard-transform algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q05": {
    "title": "Fourier sampling of a Boolean function",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Fourier sampling of a Boolean function</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Fourier sampling of a Boolean function)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> by a phase oracle, sample <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> from a distribution within constant total-variation distance of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mover><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span>, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: One oracle call and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> Hadamard gates prepare and measure the Fourier-sampling distribution.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized queries in the worst case at the specified constant total-variation accuracy, by the Fourier-sampling lower bound of Aaronson–Chen.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The transform circuit uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> logical qubits and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> elementary gates around its oracle calls; implementing the oracle determines the full device cost. Small circuit demonstrations are feasible.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Boolean Fourier sampling</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.05903\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Complexity-Theoretic Foundations of Quantum Supremacy Experiments</em></a>. Fourier Sampling lower bound; Section7.4 for succinct-oracle implications</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/boolean-fourier-sampling\">→ Boolean Fourier sampling</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q08": {
    "title": "Full oracle interrogation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Full oracle interrogation</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Full oracle interrogation)</span></h2>\n<p>Given indexed bit-query access to an unknown <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, output the entire string with probability at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>. Complexity counts oracle queries; the output contains <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span> bits.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by van Dam&#x27;s oracle-interrogation algorithm, using a superposition over low-Hamming-weight query patterns.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries are necessary at constant success probability; reading every bit uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span>. Both complexities are <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>No established asymptotic speedup in the comparison stated above.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The near-factor-two reduction and the linear classical query lower bound are proved.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: van Dam oracle interrogation</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9805006\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Oracle Interrogation: Getting all information for almost half the price</em></a>. Full-interrogation algorithm</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/van-dam-oracle-interrogation\">→ van Dam oracle interrogation</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q09": {
    "title": "Oracle identification",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Oracle identification</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Oracle identification)</span></h2>\n<p>A known concept set <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> has size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math></span>. Given bit queries to an unknown <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></span>, identify <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span>. Complexity is worst case over concept sets with the given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math></span>, allowing unrestricted processing of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>M</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span>, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, by Kothari&#x27;s optimal identification algorithm.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized queries in the worst case over concept sets, by classical elimination and the corresponding hard classes.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial improvement in the stated resource and parameter regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Kothari oracle identification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.7685\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>An optimal quantum algorithm for the oracle identification problem</em></a>. Proposition2 and Theorem2; learning algorithm and composition theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/kothari-oracle-identification\">→ Kothari oracle identification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q26": {
    "title": "Boolean-function isomorphism under coordinate permutations",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Boolean-function isomorphism under coordinate permutations</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Boolean-function isomorphism under coordinate permutations)</span></h2>\n<p>Given oracle access to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>, distinguish whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for some permutation <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> from the case where every such permutation leaves disagreement at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries by Harrow–Lin–Montanaro&#x27;s sequential-measurement property test.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized queries are necessary, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> suffice at constant distance.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Harrow–Lin–Montanaro sequential-measurement tester</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.03236\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Sequential measurements, disturbance and property testing</em></a>. Theorem2; Boolean-function-isomorphism application.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/sequential-measurement-property-testing\">→ Harrow–Lin–Montanaro sequential-measurement tester</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q45": {
    "title": "Testing one-to-one periodicity",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Testing one-to-one periodicity</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Testing one-to-one periodicity)</span></h2>\n<p>Given <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>, distinguish functions whose values repeat injectively with a period in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span> from functions <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>-far from every such periodic function. Complexity counts value queries.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> oracle queries and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> additional processing by Chakraborty–Fischer–Matsliah–de Wolf, using Fourier period testing.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized queries are necessary; a classical tester can use collisions to infer candidate periods and verify them.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The algorithm requires coherent input access and fault-tolerant execution of the stated procedure; its circuit and workspace requirements depend on that access implementation.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum one-to-one periodicity tester; Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.0523\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>New Results on Quantum Property Testing</em></a>. Theorem1.4 and Section8.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<aside class=\"related-uses\"><h3>Also used in</h3><ul>\n<li><a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>: <a href=\"#entry/factoring\">Integer factorization</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithms-in-finite-fields\">Finite-field discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm\">Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a01\">Finite abelian-group decomposition</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a02\">Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a05\">Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a06\">Ideal operations in finite rings</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a08\">Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</a>, <a href=\"#entry/prior-a10\">Semidirect discrete logarithm</a>, <a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithm-concept-learning\">Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</a>.</li>\n</ul></aside>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/one-to-one-periodicity-testing\">→ Quantum one-to-one periodicity tester</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/shor-order\">→ Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "prior-q46": {
    "title": "PAC learning in the large-error regime",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>PAC learning in the large-error regime</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(PAC learning in the large-error regime)</span></h2>\n<p>For a constructive <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> satisfying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, learn the concept class constructed by Oliveira from uniform quantum examples: output a classical circuit agreeing with the target on at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> of inputs with probability at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, for fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> time and examples by correlation-biased sampling of quadratic hypotheses and boosting. The general learner using <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> examples achieves advantage <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, failure <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> and time <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: For the constructed class, no polynomial-time randomized membership-query learner attains even agreement <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> with success above <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> on every target. Querying the complete effective truth table uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries up to polynomial factors from the construction.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential or subexponential to polynomial in the strongest stated regime.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The cited results establish a classical lower bound separating the stated quantum resource bound in the specified model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Each example occupies <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> logical qubits. The algorithm additionally needs the indicated number of coherent examples and its measurement/postprocessing workspace; preparing these examples is part of the supplied-access model.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Oliveira correlation-biased quadratic-hypothesis learner</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23268\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Unconditional Quantum PAC Learning Advantage in the Large Error Regime</em></a>. Theorem1.2 gives the algorithmic tradeoff; Theorems1.4–1.5 establish separations.</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/correlation-biased-quadratic-learning\">→ Oliveira correlation-biased quadratic-hypothesis learner</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "classical-verification-bqp": {
    "title": "Classical verification of quantum computation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Classical verification of quantum computation</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Classical verification of quantum computation)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a quantum circuit deciding a promise problem with completeness at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span> and soundness at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>, construct an interactive protocol with a classical polynomial-time verifier and a quantum polynomial-time honest prover certifying the decision. Security is against efficient quantum cheating provers. Complexity uses gates, verifier time, and classical communication.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates for circuit size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math></span> and security parameter <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></span>, using Mahadev&#x27;s four-message protocol. Cryptographic commitments enforce computational- or Hadamard-basis measurements; the verifier checks a computation-verification test.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: The verifier runs in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> classical time. A classical honest prover for every BQP computation would require classical algorithms for those computations; generic simulation and problem-specific algorithms supply their respective comparators. Protocol soundness reduces to quantum hardness of LWE.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Efficient classical verification of BQP computation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Completeness and computational soundness are proved under quantum hardness of LWE.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial quantum space, coherent cryptographic evaluation, and repeated measurements; fault-tolerant implementation is the relevant regime.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Mahadev verification protocol</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.01082\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Classical Verification of Quantum Computations</em></a>. Main verification theorem; measurement protocol; construction from noisy trapdoor claw-free functions</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/mahadev_verification\">→ Mahadev verification protocol</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "certified-randomness": {
    "title": "Certified randomness from an untrusted quantum device",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Certified randomness from an untrusted quantum device</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Certified randomness from an untrusted quantum device)</span></h2>\n<p>A classical verifier interacts with an untrusted device and outputs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi></mrow></math></span> with conditional smooth min-entropy <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>, for the side information <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></math></span> admitted by the protocol. A seeded extractor gives nearly uniform bits. Complexity counts prover gates, communication, verification, and certified entropy.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: BCMVV uses polynomially many cryptographic rounds to certify entropy under LWE-based assumptions. A separate random-circuit protocol executes challenged circuits and classically checks selected probabilities. Its 2025 experiment certified <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>71</mn><mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow><mn>313</mn></mrow></math></span> entropy bits with 56 trapped-ion qubits under a restricted-adversary model.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: A classical device can retain every output, yielding zero conditional entropy against its own side information. The circuit-based protocol bounds the number of samples obtainable by a time-limited classical adversary; its verification used combined sustained performance <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.1</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mn>18</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> floating-point operations per second.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Certifiable entropy under an explicit adversarial model; verification work enters the resource tradeoff.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>LWE-based certification has a cryptographic proof; the circuit experiment has a security analysis for its restricted adversary.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>The circuit protocol was demonstrated on 56 trapped-ion qubits; trapdoor-function protocols require coherent cryptographic circuits.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: BCMVV certified-randomness protocol; Random-circuit certified-randomness protocol</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.00640\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A Cryptographic Test of Quantumness and Certifiable Randomness from a Single Quantum Device</em></a>. Test-of-quantumness and randomness-generation protocols; noisy trapdoor claw-free functions; LWE construction</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.20498\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Certified randomness using a trapped-ion quantum processor</em></a>. Protocol, restricted-adversary security model, entropy certification, and 56-qubit experiment</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/bcmvv_randomness\">→ BCMVV certified-randomness protocol</a>\n<a href=\"#algorithm/rcs_certified_randomness\">→ Random-circuit certified-randomness protocol</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "random-circuit-sampling": {
    "title": "Random circuit sampling",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Random circuit sampling</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Random circuit sampling)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-qubit circuit <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span> gates from a specified ensemble, sample <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> from <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span>, exactly or within total variation distance <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span>. Geometry, gate distribution, and noise define the instance family. Complexity uses gates and classical sampling time.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> operations per ideal sample by direct circuit execution and measurement. Finite-gate-set compilation adds its precision overhead.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Use the minimum of state-vector simulation <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>, tensor contraction <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> at contraction width <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span>, and fixed-algebraic-gate path counting <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> for a sparse Boolean encoding of size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> of the required marginal. Sequential conditional probabilities give samples with polynomial overhead. Constant-rate noisy random circuits in the anticoncentration regime admit polynomial-time approximate classical sampling.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Ideal sampling compares polynomial quantum cost with the best applicable exponential or subexponential generic classical simulator.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial approximate classical sampling implies a polynomial-hierarchy collapse under the corresponding average-case hardness and anticoncentration assumptions.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Implemented on superconducting processors, including 53-qubit Sycamore; asymptotic comparisons specify target noise and accuracy.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Random-circuit execution</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.04402\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>On the complexity and verification of quantum random circuit sampling</em></a>. RCS definition; average-case hardness theorem; verification discussion</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-019-1666-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum supremacy using a programmable superconducting processor</em></a>. 53-qubit experiment; supplementary simulation methods</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0511069\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Simulating quantum computation by contracting tensor networks</em></a>. Main circuit-treewidth simulation theorem</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em></a>. Theorem 1.1; Appendix D; sparse fixed-width counting</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.03999\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A polynomial-time classical algorithm for noisy random circuit sampling</em></a>. Main theorem for constant-rate noise in the anticoncentration regime</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/random_circuit_execution\">→ Random-circuit execution</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "fock-boson-sampling": {
    "title": "Fock-state boson sampling",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Fock-state boson sampling</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Fock-state boson sampling)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> interferometer <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math></span> and single photons in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> specified inputs, sample <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">N</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>, with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>Per</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x00021;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Matrix <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> uses occupied input columns and repeats row <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math></span> exactly <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> times. Complexity uses optical operations and classical arithmetic time at specified precision.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> two-mode optical elements implement a general interferometer, followed by photon-number detection. Scattershot boson sampling varies the occupied input modes.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Clifford–Clifford exact sampling costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Their collision-aware algorithm has Haar-average bound <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. For fixed nonzero uniform loss, the November 2026 loss theorem gives polynomial approximate sampling with total-variation error averaged over Haar interferometers.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Ideal sampling compares polynomial optical resources with exponential classical bounds, keeping photon and mode counts independent.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Efficient exact sampling implies polynomial-hierarchy collapse; approximate hardness adds permanent average-case hardness and anticoncentration conjectures.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Requires <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> indistinguishable photons, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> modes, and photon-number-resolving detectors; experiments implement finite lossy instances.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Linear-optical boson sampling</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.3245\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Computational Complexity of Linear Optics</em></a>. BosonSampling definition; exact- and approximate-sampling hardness</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.01260\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Classical Complexity of Boson Sampling</em></a>. Main exact-sampling algorithm and complexity theorem</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.04214\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Faster classical Boson Sampling</em></a>. Algorithm A; Theorem 5, general average-case bound</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01434\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Boson sampling is fragile to loss</em></a>. Section IV.1; average-case total-variation sampling theorem; November 2026 preprint</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/linear_optical_boson_sampling\">→ Linear-optical boson sampling</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "gaussian-boson-sampling": {
    "title": "Gaussian boson sampling",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Gaussian boson sampling</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Gaussian boson sampling)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a complex symmetric <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math></span> with singular values below one, sample <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">N</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>&#x02020;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>Haf</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x00021;</mo></mrow></math></span>, where <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> repeats row and column <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math></span> exactly <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> times. This is discrete photon-number sampling from a pure zero-mean Gaussian state. Complexity uses optical operations and classical sampling time.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> optical elements, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> squeezed input modes, and photon counting implement a general instance. Total detected photon count <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> is a random output parameter controlled by squeezing.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Conditional heterodyne sampling and loop-hafnian evaluation cost <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> for an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></span>-photon output, with the numerical cutoff specified. Polynomial classical samplers cover the nonnegative graph distributions in Anand et al. Equal bounded squeezing and fixed uniform loss admit polynomial sampling with Haar-average total-variation accuracy by the 2026 loss theorem.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>General ideal instances compare polynomial optical resources with exponential hafnian-based sampling time; structure and loss determine the comparator.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Ideal bipartite GBS has approximate-sampling hardness when the mode count is quadratic in photon count, under permanent conjectures and noncollapse of the polynomial hierarchy.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Squeezed-light processors and photon detectors implement this model; resources include modes, squeezing, loss, and detected photons.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Gaussian boson sampling</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.01199\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Gaussian Boson Sampling</em></a>. Photon-count probabilities and GBS protocol</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.15595\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quadratic speedup for simulating Gaussian boson sampling</em></a>. Section III algorithm and O(mN^3 2^(N/2)) bound</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.06964\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Complexity of Bipartite Gaussian Boson Sampling</em></a>. Approximate-sampling hardness under permanent conjectures</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16558v3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Simulating Gaussian boson sampling on graphs in polynomial time</em></a>. Theorem 1.1 and Section 4: graph-application distributions</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01434\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Boson sampling is fragile to loss</em></a>. Section IV.1; average-case total-variation sampling theorem; November 2026 preprint</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/gaussian_boson_sampling\">→ Gaussian boson sampling</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "iqp-sampling": {
    "title": "Sampling commuting quantum circuits",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Sampling commuting quantum circuits</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Sampling commuting quantum circuits)</span></h2>\n<p>Given a circuit <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math></span> of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span> gates diagonal in the Pauli-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math></span> basis, sample <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> with probability <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span>. Standard IQP families use a fixed finite gate set; approximation error is total variation distance. Complexity uses gates and classical sampling time.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates and measurements. Equivalently, apply Hadamards, commuting diagonal phase gates, Hadamards, then computational-basis measurement.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Take the best of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> state-vector simulation, <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> contraction at width <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math></span>, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> sparse-Boolean-encoding path counting for finite-algebraic-gate marginals. Clifford-only circuits admit polynomial stabilizer simulation.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial quantum sampling versus the best applicable exponential or subexponential classical simulator.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(85,85,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial classical sampling within <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>192</mn></mrow></math></span> in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span> distance gives a third-level polynomial-hierarchy collapse under either average-case conjecture in Bremner–Montanaro–Shepherd.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> qubits and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span> commuting gates; non-Clifford operations and approximation accuracy determine implementation demands.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: IQP circuit sampling</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.07999\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Average-case complexity versus approximate simulation of commuting quantum computations</em></a>. Theorem 1; Conjectures 2 and 3; IQP definition</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.1407\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Classical simulation of commuting quantum computations implies collapse of the polynomial hierarchy</em></a>. Postselection argument and weak-simulation hardness</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0511069\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Simulating quantum computation by contracting tensor networks</em></a>. Main circuit-treewidth simulation theorem</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em></a>. Theorem 1.1; Appendix D; sparse fixed-width counting</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/iqp_circuit_sampling\">→ IQP circuit sampling</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "dqc1-trace-estimation": {
    "title": "Normalized trace estimation for a quantum circuit",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Normalized trace estimation for a quantum circuit</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Normalized trace estimation for a quantum circuit)</span></h2>\n<p>Given an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-qubit unitary circuit <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math></span> of size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></span>, estimate both components of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>Tr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> to additive error <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> with failure probability <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math></span>. The input is a succinct circuit. Complexity uses gates and classical time; the problem characterizes DQC1.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates by Knill–Laflamme. A clean control applies controlled-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>; its <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math></span> expectations give the trace.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Random-vector trace estimation with state-vector circuit application costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Tensor contraction provides width-dependent alternatives. For fixed finite algebraic gates, trace path counting also costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>poly</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> in the size <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span> of its sparse Boolean encoding.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Polynomial quantum gates versus the best applicable exponential or subexponential generic classical trace computation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(170,170,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Inverse-polynomial-accuracy trace estimation is DQC1-complete; efficient classical estimation would simulate DQC1.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>One clean and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> maximally mixed qubits plus controlled-circuit workspace; repetitions scale as <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Knill–Laflamme normalized-trace algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802037\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>On the Power of One Bit of Quantum Information</em></a>. One-clean-qubit model and normalized-trace circuit</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0608132\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Computation with Unitaries and One Pure Qubit</em></a>. Normalized trace estimation and completeness for the one-pure-qubit complexity class</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0511069\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Simulating quantum computation by contracting tensor networks</em></a>. Main circuit-treewidth simulation theorem</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False</em></a>. Theorem 1.1; Appendix D; sparse fixed-width counting</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/knill_laflamme_trace\">→ Knill–Laflamme normalized-trace algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "two-dimensional-hidden-linear-function": {
    "title": "Two-dimensional hidden linear function",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Two-dimensional hidden linear function</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Two-dimensional hidden linear function)</span></h2>\n<p>On an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-vertex square grid, input bits <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> define <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" /><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Let <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02295;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span>. Output <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span> with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> for every <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>. Complexity counts bounded-fan-in circuit depth.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> depth and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> gates on a nearest-neighbor grid: apply <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02297;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, the Clifford phase <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>, and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02297;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>. Every measured output satisfies the relation. An encoded construction extends the separation to a related noisy three-dimensional task.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> depth for bounded-fan-in randomized classical circuits solving all instances with the stipulated success probability. Stabilizer simulation or finite-field linear algebra gives polynomial sequential time; parallel linear algebra gives polylogarithmic depth.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Constant quantum depth versus logarithmic classical depth; this is a parallel-computation separation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The classical bounded-fan-in depth lower bound is unconditional.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> qubits and constant-depth local gates; the noisy extension uses three-dimensional encoded states.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Bravyi–Gosset–König shallow-circuit algorithm</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.00690\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum advantage with shallow circuits</em></a>. Section 3, Eq. (14), 2D Hidden Linear Function definition; main depth-separation theorem</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.01502\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum advantage with noisy shallow circuits</em></a>. Noisy three-dimensional relation and main theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/bravyi_gosset_koenig\">→ Bravyi–Gosset–König shallow-circuit algorithm</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "equality-quantum-fingerprinting": {
    "title": "Equality in simultaneous-message communication",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Equality in simultaneous-message communication</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Equality in simultaneous-message communication)</span></h2>\n<p>Alice receives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and Bob receives <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span>. Each sends one message to a referee deciding <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></span> with error at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>. The senders have independent private randomness and no shared random key or prior entanglement. Complexity counts transmitted bits or qubits.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> qubits by quantum fingerprinting. An error-correcting code maps inputs to quantum states; the referee uses a SWAP test. Error <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math></span> costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> qubits.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> bits for bounded-error private-coin simultaneous-message protocols. Classical fingerprints sample codeword positions; matching lower bounds apply in the same model.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> reduction in communication, conventionally called an exponential separation in message length.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Matching classical and quantum communication bounds hold without shared randomness or entanglement.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> transmitted qubits; preparation reads the classical input. Optical protocols implement related encodings.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Quantum fingerprinting</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0102001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum fingerprinting</em></a>. Fingerprint states, SWAP test, and simultaneous-message equality bounds</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum_fingerprinting\">→ Quantum fingerprinting</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "hidden-matching-communication": {
    "title": "Hidden matching in one-way communication",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hidden matching in one-way communication</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Hidden matching in one-way communication)</span></h2>\n<p>Alice holds <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and Bob a perfect matching <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></span>. After one message from Alice, Bob outputs an edge <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math></span> and the bit <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02295;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>, with success at least <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>. Complexity counts one-way communicated bits or qubits.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span> qubits with exact success. Alice sends <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></math></span>; Bob measures in the basis <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math></span> for edges in <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math></span>.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized classical bits are necessary. Shared randomness enables a matching <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> birthday-paradox protocol; sending sampled indices explicitly costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> with private randomness.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> communication reduction against the public-coin classical optimum.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The classical lower bound holds even with shared randomness.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> transmitted qubits ideally; coherent-state optical variants have experimental demonstrations.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Hidden Matching quantum communication protocol</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://webee.technion.ac.il/people/zivby/papers/qcc/qcc.journal.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Exponential Separation of Quantum and Classical One-Way Communication Complexity</em></a>. Hidden Matching relation; quantum protocol; randomized classical lower bound</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-019-12139-z\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Experimental demonstration of quantum advantage for one-way communication complexity surpassing best-known classical protocol</em></a>. Hidden Matching and Sampling Matching protocols; optical experiment</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/hidden_matching_protocol\">→ Hidden Matching quantum communication protocol</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "set-disjointness-communication": {
    "title": "Set disjointness in two-party communication",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Set disjointness in two-party communication</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Set disjointness in two-party communication)</span></h2>\n<p>Alice and Bob hold <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> and decide whether <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span>, with error at most <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></span>. Complexity counts two-way communication; local computation is free.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> communicated qubits. Aaronson–Ambainis distributed search finds an index with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> and removes the logarithmic overhead of straightforward index-register communication.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized classical bits, achieved by sending one input and matched by lower bounds. The quantum <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> lower bound also holds with prior entanglement.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,224,224)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>A tight quadratic communication improvement arising from quantum search.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Both classical and quantum bounded-error communication complexities have matching lower bounds.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Coherent quantum links and distributed search registers; total communication is <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> qubits.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Aaronson–Ambainis disjointness protocol</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://toc.ilab.sztaki.hu/articles/v001a004/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Search of Spatial Regions</em></a>. Section 7: O(sqrt n)-qubit disjointness protocol; spatial-search theorems</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0204025\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum communication complexity of symmetric predicates</em></a>. Set-disjointness lower bound and symmetric-predicate theorem</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/aaronson_ambainis_disjointness\">→ Aaronson–Ambainis disjointness protocol</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "general-hsp-query-identification": {
    "title": "Hidden subgroup identification in a general finite group",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hidden subgroup identification in a general finite group</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Hidden subgroup identification in a general finite group)</span></h2>\n<p>Given finite <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span> and an oracle <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></math></span> constant on, and distinct between, the left cosets of unknown <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></math></span>, output generators of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>. Complexity counts oracle queries; <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math></span>.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries with error <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math></span> by the Ettinger–Høyer–Knill subgroup-state test. An exact construction uses <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries with arbitrary one-qubit gates. General subgroup discrimination and preprocessing require exponential resources.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Repeated birthday-collision sampling finds generators with <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> queries in the worst case; equal oracle values give elements of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>. Simon-type order-two subgroups require <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> randomized queries. For finite abelian groups, subgroup-sensitive identification costs <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> deterministic queries.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,0,0)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>Exponential classical queries to polynomial quantum queries.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The upper bound holds for every finite group; oracle lower bounds give an exponential worst-case classical separation.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(191,255,191)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Polynomially many coset-state registers suffice; general joint measurements and subgroup enumeration have exponential implementation cost.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Ettinger–Høyer–Knill subgroup identification</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0401083\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The quantum query complexity of the hidden subgroup problem is polynomial</em></a>. Theorems 1 and 2; Sections 2.1 and 2.2</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.14436\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Deterministic Algorithms for the Hidden Subgroup Problem</em></a>. Section 5: birthday sampling; Section 6: finding the subgroup</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.00827\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Deterministic Algorithms for the Hidden Subgroup Problem</em></a>. Finite abelian identification upper bound</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/ettinger_hoyer_knill\">→ Ettinger–Høyer–Knill subgroup identification</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "anonymous-leader-election": {
    "title": "Exact leader election in anonymous networks",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Exact leader election in anonymous networks</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Exact leader election in anonymous networks)</span></h2>\n<p>In a connected synchronous network of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> identical processors without unique identifiers, each knows <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>. Output bits summing to exactly one, in bounded time with zero error, on every topology. Complexity counts rounds and transmitted bits or qubits.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> rounds and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> communication for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math></span> links by Kobayashi–Matsumoto–Tani. Coherent symmetric-function computation enables exact symmetry breaking.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Exact bounded-time leader election is impossible on general anonymous classical networks even with known n. Symmetric executions on symmetric topologies keep processors indistinguishable.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>An exact-solvability separation in the anonymous-network model.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>Quantum solvability and classical impossibility are unconditional.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(128,255,128)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Quantum links and polynomial coherent local resources are required.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Kobayashi–Matsumoto–Tani leader election</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0712.4213\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Exact Quantum Algorithms for the Leader Election Problem</em></a>. Exact anonymous leader-election protocols and classical impossibility</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.5307\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Computing on Anonymous Quantum Network</em></a>. Symmetric-function reduction and O(n)-round leader-election corollary</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/quantum_anonymous_leader_election\">→ Kobayashi–Matsumoto–Tani leader election</a>\n</nav>"
  },
  "magic-square-game": {
    "title": "Mermin–Peres magic-square game",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Mermin–Peres magic-square game</h1>\n<span class=\"label-cells\"><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span><span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></span></div>\n<section class=\"document-problem\">\n<h2>Problem <span>(Mermin–Peres magic-square game)</span></h2>\n<p>A referee independently samples row <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> for Alice and column <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math></span> for Bob. Alice outputs three signs of product <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> and Bob three of product <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>; their intersection values must agree. No communication follows the inputs. Complexity uses winning probability and shared entanglement.</p></section>\n<section><h2>Quantum speedup</h2>\n<p><strong>Quantum complexity: Success <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> with two EPR pairs and constant-size local measurements. Commuting Mermin–Peres observables supply the required parities and agreement.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Classical complexity: Optimal classical success is exactly <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math></span>, allowing shared randomness and arbitrary local computation. A deterministic assignment satisfying eight of nine input pairs attains it.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Strength <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(255,255,255)\" title=\"Strength\" aria-label=\"Strength\"></span></h3><p>A nonlocal success-probability separation at zero communication.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Evidence <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(0,0,255)\" title=\"Evidence\" aria-label=\"Evidence\"></span></h3><p>The exact classical and quantum winning probabilities are proved.</p></div>\n<div class=\"document-dimension\"><h3>Hardware <span class=\"category-cell\" style=\"background:rgb(64,255,64)\" title=\"Hardware\" aria-label=\"Hardware\"></span></h3><p>Two EPR pairs suffice; photonic experiments use polarization and orbital-angular-momentum entanglement.</p></div>\n</section><section><h2>Algorithm: Mermin–Peres magic-square strategy</h2><ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.12042\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Experimental Demonstration of Quantum Pseudotelepathy</em></a>. Magic-square game, quantum strategy, and experiment</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41534-024-00874-1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Beating one bit of communication with and without quantum pseudo-telepathy</em></a>. Magic-square classical and quantum values</li>\n</ul></section>\n<nav class=\"entry-navigation\" aria-label=\"Algorithm pages\">\n<a href=\"#algorithm/magic_square_strategy\">→ Mermin–Peres magic-square strategy</a>\n</nav>"
  }
};
window.ALGORITHM_PAGES = {
  "grover": {
    "title": "Grover search and amplitude amplification",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Grover search and amplitude amplification</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9605043\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Grover, <em>A Fast Quantum Mechanical Algorithm for Database Search</em> (STOC 1996)</a>. Section 3, “Algorithm”, preprint p. 3, steps (i)–(iii).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0005055\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Brassard, Høyer, Mosca and Tapp, <em>Quantum Amplitude Amplification and Estimation</em> (2002)</a>. Section 2, Theorem 2 for known success probability; Theorem 3 and <span class=\"small-caps\">QSearch</span> for unknown success probability, preprint pp. 7–9.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cs.utep.edu/vladik/2005/tr05-26.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dantsin, Kreinovich and Wolpert, <em>On Quantum Versions of Record-Breaking Algorithms for SAT</em>, SIGACT News 36(4), 103–108 (2005)</a>. Section 1: “Ambainis&#x27; observation” and “The fastest algorithm for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT”, author-preprint pp. 2–3.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.eecs.harvard.edu/ michaelm/CS223/schoning99.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Schöning, <em>A Probabilistic Algorithm for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT and Constraint Satisfaction Problems</em> (FOCS 1999)</a>. Section 2, “The Algorithm”, p. 410. <a href=\"https://theoretics.episciences.org/13222/pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Scheder, <em>PPSZ Is Better Than You Think</em> (TheoretiCS 2024)</a>: Section 1.1, Algorithm 1, p. 3.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0510084\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dörn, <em>Quantum Complexity Bounds for Independent Set Problems</em> (2005)</a>. Algorithm 2 and Theorem 6: maximum independent set.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2112.06157\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Esser et al., <em>An Optimized Quantum Implementation of ISD on Scalable Quantum Resources</em> (2021)</a>. Algorithms 1–4: Prange, state preparation, linear-system solving and the combined quantum circuit.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1712.07211\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Faugère et al., <em>Fast Quantum Algorithm for Solving Multivariate Quadratic Equations</em> (2017)</a>. Theorems 5–7 and Section 4: quantum circuit and QuantumBooleanSolve complexity; Theorem 7 states the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.17</mn></mrow></math></span>-strong semiregular <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.462</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math></span> bound.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1510.05836\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kaplan, Leurent, Leverrier and Naya-Plasencia, <em>Quantum Differential and Linear Cryptanalysis</em> (ToSC 2016)</a>. Sections 4.1–4.2: classical and quantum differential attacks; Sections 5.1–5.2: truncated differentials; Sections 7.1–7.2: linear cryptanalysis; Theorems 1–3: search and counting subroutines.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/search\">Unstructured search</a>. Used directly to find a marked input.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/fixed-width-boolean-satisfiability\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT</a>. Used as a subroutine to amplify successful Schöning and PPSZ trials.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/independent-set-clique-and-vertex-cover\">Independent set, clique and vertex cover</a>. Used as a subroutine to amplify the randomized branching procedure for independent set.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>. Used as a subroutine to amplify successful conditioning-and-search trials.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>. Used as a subroutine to amplify Prange information-set decoding trials.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>. Used as a subroutine to amplify information-set trials producing a nonzero low-weight codeword.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/boolean-multivariate-quadratic-equations\">Boolean quadratic equations</a>. Used directly with a reversible verifier for the quadratic equations, and as a subroutine in QuantumBooleanSolve.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/search/ideal-cipher-key-recovery\">Ideal-cipher key recovery</a>. Used directly to search for a key consistent with the plaintext–ciphertext pairs.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/search/hash-preimages\">Hash preimages</a>. Used directly to find a preimage or a distinct input with the prescribed hash value.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>. Used as a subroutine to find pairs satisfying a differential condition.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/search/oracle-np-search\">Oracle NP search</a>. Used directly to search the witness space with the supplied verifier.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/fine-grained-complexity-of-satisfiability-and-string-problems\">Fine-grained satisfiability and strings</a>. Used as the satisfiability upper bound in the fine-grained comparison.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>. Searches candidate matches after exploiting pattern periodicity.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>. Amplifies the probability of detecting a linearity violation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>. Used as a search or amplitude-amplification subroutine inside the stated algorithm.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "minimum-finding": {
    "title": "Dürr–Høyer minimum finding",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Dürr–Høyer minimum finding</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9607014\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dürr and Høyer, <em>A Quantum Algorithm for Finding the Minimum</em> (1996/1999)</a>. Theorem 1 and the numbered minimum-finding procedure, preprint pp. 1–2; the linear scan is the classical comparison in the introduction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0401091\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dürr, Heiligman, Høyer and Mhalla, <em>Quantum Query Complexity of Some Graph Problems</em> (2004/2006)</a>. Section 4, “Minimum Spanning Tree”, Theorem 8; Section 8, Theorem 24, for the lower bound. Section 7, “Single Source Shortest Paths”, Theorem 18 and Table 1; Section 8, Theorem 24.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1410.6220\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Nayebi and Vassilevska Williams, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Shortest Paths Problems in Structured Instances</em> (2014)</a>. Introduction: general <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> APSP baseline; structured algorithms in Theorems 1–5.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0504085\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Zhang, <em>(Almost) Tight Bounds for Randomized and Quantum Local Search on Hypercubes and Grids</em> (2005 preprint; journal version, 2009)</a>. Introduction, Theorems 1–2 and the discussion of matching sampling/descent upper bounds; Section 3 for the hypercube lower bound.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0307149\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Aaronson, <em>Lower Bounds for Local Search by Quantum Arguments</em> (2003/2006)</a>. Proposition 2 for the quantum algorithm and the preceding paragraph for randomized sampling and descent.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/black-box-discrete-minimization\">Black-box minimization</a>. Used directly to minimize an oracle-supplied objective.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>. Used as a subroutine to minimize over partial tours and their decompositions.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>. Used as a subroutine to minimize over terminal-set splits and attachment choices.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>. Used as a subroutine to select an optimal vertex-ordering decomposition.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>. Used as a subroutine to minimize the largest cut along a vertex order.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>. Used as a subroutine to minimize the additive ordering cost.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>. Used as a subroutine to minimize over splits of the vertex ordering.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>. Used as a subroutine to minimize fill costs in the ordering recurrence.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>. Used as a subroutine to minimize completion costs in the ordering recurrence.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/mst\">Minimum spanning tree</a>. Used as a subroutine to find light edges leaving the current components.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/single-source-shortest-paths\">Single-source shortest paths</a>. Used as a subroutine to select candidate distance relaxations.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>. Used as a subroutine in the quantum conditioning-and-search algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/all-pairs-shortest-paths\">All-pairs shortest paths</a>. Used as a subroutine to compute each entry of a min-plus product.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>. Used directly to minimize the weight over nonzero encoded messages.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/local\">Hypercube local search</a>. Used as a subroutine to select a low-value starting point before local descent.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>. Selects the best candidate among recursively generated or geometrically defined alternatives.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>. Selects the best candidate among recursively generated or geometrically defined alternatives.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>. Selects the best candidate among recursively generated or geometrically defined alternatives.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>. Selects the best candidate among recursively generated or geometrically defined alternatives.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>. Selects the best candidate among recursively generated or geometrically defined alternatives.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "amplitude-estimation": {
    "title": "Quantum amplitude estimation and counting",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum amplitude estimation and counting</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0005055\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Brassard, Høyer, Mosca and Tapp, <em>Quantum Amplitude Amplification and Estimation</em> (2002)</a>. Section 4, <span class=\"small-caps\">Est Amp</span> and Theorem 12; Theorem 13 defines <span class=\"small-caps\">Count</span>, and Theorem 15 gives relative approximation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4614442/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Montanaro, <em>Quantum Speedup of Monte Carlo Methods</em> (2015)</a>. The partition-function approximation application and its cooling-schedule theorem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2207.08643\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Cornelissen and Hamoudi, <em>A Sublinear-Time Quantum Algorithm for Approximating Partition Functions</em> (2023)</a>. Section 4 and Theorem 4.1; Algorithm 6 and Theorem 3.3 define the product estimator.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1510.05836\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kaplan, Leurent, Leverrier and Naya-Plasencia, <em>Quantum Differential and Linear Cryptanalysis</em> (ToSC 2016)</a>. Sections 4.1–4.2: classical and quantum differential attacks; Sections 5.1–5.2: truncated differentials; Sections 7.1–7.2: linear cryptanalysis; Theorems 1–3: search and counting subroutines.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/approximate-counting-of-feasible-assignments\">Approximate counting</a>. Used directly to estimate the fraction of satisfying assignments.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>. Used as the estimation subroutine in quantum Monte Carlo and cooling-schedule partition-function algorithms.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/differential-and-linear-cryptanalysis\">Differential and linear cryptanalysis</a>. Used as a subroutine to estimate a linear-cryptanalysis bias.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>. Used as a subroutine to estimate a normalized variational objective coherently.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>. Estimates the fraction of entries below a proposed pivot.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>. Estimates a probability or Fourier statistic within the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>. Estimates a probability or Fourier statistic within the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>. Estimates a probability or Fourier statistic within the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>. Estimates a probability or Fourier statistic within the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>. Estimates a probability or Fourier statistic within the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>. Estimates a probability or Fourier statistic within the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>. Estimates a probability or Fourier statistic within the stated algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>. Estimates a probability or Fourier statistic within the stated algorithm.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "backtracking": {
    "title": "Quantum backtracking and branch and bound",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum backtracking and branch and bound</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1509.02374\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Montanaro, <em>Quantum Walk Speedup of Backtracking Algorithms</em> (Theory of Computing 2018)</a>. Theorems 1–2; Algorithm 1 defines classical backtracking, Algorithm 2 defines quantum detection, and the section on finding a marked vertex gives witness recovery.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1906.10375\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Montanaro, <em>Quantum Speedup of Branch-and-Bound Algorithms</em> (Physical Review Research 2020)</a>. Algorithm 2 and Theorem 1.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2210.03210\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Chakrabarti, Minssen, Yalovetzky and Pistoia, <em>Universal Quantum Speedup for Branch-and-Bound, Branch-and-Cut, and Tree-Search Algorithms</em> (2022)</a>. Theorem 1.3 and Section 2.4, Algorithm 2, “Incremental-Quantum-Branch-and-Bound”; Section 1 defines the classical heuristic conditions.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1612.06203\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Moylett, Linden and Montanaro, <em>Quantum Speedup of the Traveling-Salesman Problem for Bounded-Degree Graphs</em> (2017)</a>. Theorem 1; Theorems 4–5 give the degree-three and degree-four algorithms and their classical search subroutines.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41534-025-01097-8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A Quantum Algorithm for Solving 0-1 Knapsack Problems</em> (npj Quantum Information 2025)</a>. The <span class=\"small-caps\">QuantumTreeGenerator</span> construction and the numerical comparison with COMBO search trees.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/546\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Aono, Nguyen and Shen, <em>Quantum Lattice Enumeration and Tweaking Discrete Pruning</em> (ASIACRYPT 2018)</a>. Algorithm 1 and Theorem 6: tree minimization; Section 4.2, Theorems 7–9: cylinder pruning; Section 7: quantum discrete pruning. Sections 3.1–3.2: close-vector enumeration; Algorithm 1 and Theorem 6: quantum tree minimization; Section 5.3, Algorithm 2: close-vector discrete pruning.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/constraint-satisfaction-by-backtracking\">Constraint satisfaction by backtracking</a>. Used directly to search the tree defined by the branching and pruning predicates.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/discrete-optimization-by-branch-and-bound\">Branch and bound</a>. Used as the tree-search subroutine in Montanaro&#x27;s branch-and-bound algorithm and Incremental Quantum Branch and Bound.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>. Used as a subroutine in the bounded-degree travelling-salesman algorithms.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/knapsack-and-binary-integer-programming\">Knapsack</a>. Used as the tree-search subroutine in the QuantumTreeGenerator knapsack construction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-shortest-vector-problem\">Shortest vector problem</a>. Used as the tree-search subroutine in Aono–Nguyen–Shen shortest-vector enumeration.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-closest-vector-problem\">Closest vector problem</a>. Used as the tree-search subroutine in Aono–Nguyen–Shen closest-vector enumeration.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "subset-dp": {
    "title": "Quantum subset dynamic programming",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum subset dynamic programming</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05209\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis et al., <em>Quantum Speedups for Exponential-Time Dynamic Programming Algorithms</em> (SODA 2019)</a>. Theorem 8 and Algorithm 2; the TSP section states the classical Held–Karp recurrence. Section 7 and Corollary 9: directed feedback arc set and its split recurrence; the construction uses the quantum dynamic-programming routine of Algorithm 2. Theorem 10 and Algorithm 3: Set Cover.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.03581\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Miyamoto, Iwamura, Kise and Le Gall, <em>Quantum Speedup for the Minimum Steiner Tree Problem</em> (2019/2020)</a>. Theorem 1; Section 3.1 defines Dreyfus–Wagner, Section 3.2 gives the splitting recurrence, and Section 4 gives the quantum algorithm. Table 1 and its cited classical papers specify the weight-dependent comparisons.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman\">Travelling salesman</a>. Used directly to combine precomputed partial tours through quantum minimum finding.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition\">Set cover</a>. Used directly to combine partial covers of subsets of the universe.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/minimum-steiner-tree\">Steiner tree</a>. Used directly to optimize terminal-set splitting recurrences.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/travelling-salesman/directed-feedback-arc-set\">Directed feedback arc set</a>. Used directly to optimize the split recurrence for a directed vertex ordering.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "lattice-dp": {
    "title": "Quantum dynamic programming on lattice graphs",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum dynamic programming on lattice graphs</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2104.14384\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Glos, Kokainis, Mori and Vihrovs, <em>Quantum Speedups for Dynamic Programming on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span>-Dimensional Lattice Graphs</em> (MFCS 2021)</a>. Algorithm 1 and Theorem 5: lattice search; Sections 6–7 and Theorem 9: QRAM implementation and repeated-set multicover.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/set-multicover-with-repeated-sets\">Set multicover</a>. Used directly on the lattice of residual demand vectors.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "gaspers-li": {
    "title": "Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Gaspers–Li partition, cover and packing algorithms</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.08042\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gaspers and Li, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Graph Coloring and Other Partitioning, Covering, and Packing Problems</em> (ICALP 2024)</a>. Theorem 4 states the representative-family conditions and the general algorithm; Theorems 5–6 and Corollary 1 give the refined small-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></span> bounds. Theorem 8 and the coloring construction following it; appendix Algorithms 1–3 give the preprocessing and recursive procedures. The preliminary covering formulation gives the classical inclusion–exclusion comparison.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition\">Set cover</a>. Used directly with the eligible set-family enumeration routine.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/chromatic-number\">Chromatic number</a>. Used as a subroutine in the coloring algorithm, with additional small-color routines.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/domatic-number\">Domatic number</a>. Used directly with restricted enumeration of minimal dominating sets.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/set-packing\">Set packing</a>. Used directly to find disjoint members of the represented set family.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/set-cover-set-packing-and-set-partition/exact-set-partition\">Exact set partition</a>. Used directly to partition the universe into members of the represented family.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/covering-by-maximal-induced-bicliques\">Maximal induced biclique cover</a>. Used directly with the maximal-induced-biclique enumeration routine.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/packing-maximal-induced-matchings\">Maximal induced matching packing</a>. Used directly with the maximal-induced-matching enumeration routine.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/vertex-arboricity\">Vertex arboricity</a>. Used directly with the maximal-induced-forest enumeration routine.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/partition-into-hereditary-graph-classes\">Hereditary graph partition</a>. Used directly with enumeration for the specified hereditary graph class.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "treewidth-dp": {
    "title": "Quantum treewidth algorithms",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum treewidth algorithms</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.08186\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kļevickis, Prūsis and Vihrovs, <em>Quantum Speedups for Treewidth</em> (TQC 2022)</a>. Theorems 21, 22 and 24 give the three tradeoffs; Algorithm 4 gives the fastest construction. Algorithm 1 states the classical polynomial-space procedure.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/exact-treewidth\">Treewidth</a>. Used directly through the stated polynomial-space and QRAM time–space variants.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "vertex-ordering": {
    "title": "Quantum path finding in the Boolean hypercube",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum path finding in the Boolean hypercube</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.05209\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis et al., <em>Quantum Speedups for Exponential-Time Dynamic Programming Algorithms</em> (SODA 2019)</a>. Corollary 5 and equation (3): quantum ordering recurrence and cutwidth application. Section 4, Theorem 6: bandwidth algorithm; the section specifies the classical recurrence and cites the Cygan–Pilipczuk <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>4.383</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> algorithm.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/graph-bandwidth\">Bandwidth</a>. Used as the search subroutine in bandwidth dynamic programming.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/pathwidth-and-vertex-separation\">Pathwidth</a>. Used through its weighted dynamic-programming extension for vertex separation and pathwidth.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Cutwidth</a>. Used through its weighted dynamic-programming extension to minimize the largest prefix cut.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/minimum-linear-arrangement\">Minimum linear arrangement</a>. Used through its weighted dynamic-programming extension to minimize the sum of prefix-cut sizes.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/minimum-fill-in\">Minimum fill-in</a>. Used through its weighted dynamic-programming extension to minimize fill in an elimination order.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/minimum-linear-arrangement/interval-completion\">Interval completion</a>. Used through its weighted dynamic-programming extension to minimize interval-completion cost.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "arvind-schuler": {
    "title": "Arvind–Schuler binary integer-programming search",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Arvind–Schuler binary integer-programming search</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0212048\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Arvind and Schuler, <em>The Quantum Query Complexity of 0-1 Knapsack and Associated Claw Problems</em> (ISAAC 2003)</a>. Section 2, Theorem 1 and steps 1–6, pp. 3–4: partial-sum preprocessing, range-tree queries and quantum search; Corollary 2 gives threshold optimization.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/worst-case-subset-sum\">Worst-case subset sum</a>. Used directly for the subset-sum equality in the earlier meet-in-the-middle construction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/knapsack-and-binary-integer-programming\">Knapsack</a>. Used directly for capacity and value-threshold inequalities.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/knapsack-and-binary-integer-programming/binary-integer-programming\">Binary integer programming</a>. Used directly for the system of binary-variable inequalities.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "cklm-subset-sum": {
    "title": "Chukhin–Kulikov–Levitskii–Mihajlin subset-sum algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Chukhin–Kulikov–Levitskii–Mihajlin subset-sum algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.07309\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Chukhin, Kulikov, Levitskii and Mihajlin, <em>Improved Quantum Algorithms for Subset Sum and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SUM</em> (2026)</a>. Corollary 1.3; Theorem 1.2 and Algorithm 1 give the underlying <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SUM routine; the Subset Sum application gives the block reduction.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/worst-case-subset-sum\">Worst-case subset sum</a>. Used directly through a block reduction to the quantum k-SUM routine.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "recursive-fourier": {
    "title": "Recursive Bernstein–Vazirani Fourier sampling",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Recursive Bernstein–Vazirani Fourier sampling</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1137/S0097539796300921\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bernstein and Vazirani, <em>Quantum Complexity Theory</em> (1997)</a>. Section 8.4 introduces recursive Fourier sampling and the quantum–classical oracle separation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=ee55b07d326ac3cb422fa755aae4484ab3b2ad2d&amp;repid=rep1&amp;type=pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Johnson, <em>Upper and Lower Bounds for Recursive Fourier Sampling</em>, Ph.D. thesis, University of California, Berkeley (2008)</a>. Section 1.2.3, starting on page 5, defines the generalized oracle problem; Algorithm 3, page 42, gives the clean quantum recursion; Algorithm 9, page 51, gives the classical recursion; Section 3.4, starting on page 59, gives the randomized query lower bound.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/recursive-fourier-sampling\">Recursive Fourier sampling</a>. Used directly to recover recursively hidden parity information.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-graph-exploration": {
    "title": "Quantum graph exploration",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum graph exploration</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0401091\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dürr, Heiligman, Høyer and Mhalla, <em>Quantum Query Complexity of Some Graph Problems</em> (2004/2006)</a>. Section 5, “Connectivity”, Theorems 9 and 11; Section 8, Theorem 23, for the matrix lower bound. Section 6, “Strong Connectivity”, including Theorem 13; Section 8, Theorem 22.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/undirected-graph-connectivity\">Undirected connectivity</a>. Used directly to explore an undirected connected component.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/strong-connectivity-of-directed-graphs\">Strong connectivity</a>. Used directly in directed explorations testing strong connectivity.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-matching": {
    "title": "Ambainis–Špalek quantum matching algorithms",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Ambainis–Špalek quantum matching algorithms</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0508205\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis and Špalek, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Matching and Network Flows</em> (2005/2006)</a>. Theorems 3 and 4: bipartite and general matching.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/maximum-cardinality-matching\">Maximum-cardinality matching</a>. Used directly through the bipartite augmenting-path and general-graph blossom variants.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-flow": {
    "title": "Ambainis–Špalek quantum blocking-flow algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Ambainis–Špalek quantum blocking-flow algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0508205\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis and Špalek, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Matching and Network Flows</em> (2005/2006)</a>. Theorem 6 and the blocking-flow procedure following it.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/integral-maximum-flow\">Maximum flow</a>. Used directly to construct and update blocking flows in the residual network.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "triangle-walk": {
    "title": "Le Gall quantum triangle-finding algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Le Gall quantum triangle-finding algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.0085\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Le Gall, <em>Improved Quantum Algorithm for Triangle Finding via Combinatorial Arguments</em> (2014)</a>. Theorem 1.1 and the algorithm in Sections 3–4.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/unweighted-triangle-finding\">Triangle finding</a>. Used directly to find a triangle through combinatorial preprocessing and quantum walks.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "graph-collision": {
    "title": "Cornelissen et al. graph-collision algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Cornelissen et al. graph-collision algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.05356\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Cornelissen et al., <em>Breaking the Quantum Barriers for Graph Collision</em> (2026)</a>. Theorems 1.1–1.2; Section 4, “An Improved Algorithm”; Section 6 for the lower bound.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/graph-collision\">Graph collision</a>. Used directly to locate an edge with two marked endpoints in the known graph.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g43\">Boolean matrix multiplication</a>. Computes batches of nonzero entries through dense graph-collision instances.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "belovs-graph-collision": {
    "title": "Belovs graph-collision span program",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Belovs graph-collision span program</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1105.4024\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Belovs, <em>Span Programs for Functions with Constant-Sized 1-Certificates</em> (2012)</a>. The graph-collision application.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/graph-collision\">Graph collision</a>. Used directly in the earlier span-program approach to graph collision.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "bipartiteness-test": {
    "title": "Ambainis–Childs–Liu bipartiteness tester",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Ambainis–Childs–Liu bipartiteness tester</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1012.3174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ambainis, Childs and Liu, <em>Quantum Property Testing for Bounded-Degree Graphs</em> (2011)</a>. Algorithm 2 and Theorem 10; Algorithm 1 and Theorem 7 give the classical comparator.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/testing-bipartiteness-in-bounded-degree-graphs\">Bipartiteness testing</a>. Used directly to detect an odd-cycle witness from sampled random walks.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "expansion-test": {
    "title": "Apers expansion tester",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Apers expansion tester</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1907.02369\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Apers, <em>Expansion Testing Using Quantum Fast-Forwarding and Seed Sets</em> (2019)</a>. Algorithm 2 and Theorem 5; Theorem 1 states the main complexity bound.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/testing-graph-expansion\">Expansion testing</a>. Used directly through quantum fast-forwarding and seed-set tests.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "formula-evaluation": {
    "title": "Quantum formula evaluation by span programs",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum formula evaluation by span programs</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://theoryofcomputing.org/articles/v008a013/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Reichardt and Špalek, <em>Span-Program-Based Quantum Algorithm for Evaluating Formulas</em> (2012)</a>. Formula-evaluation construction and the main algorithmic theorem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1005.1601\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Reichardt, <em>Reflections for Quantum Query Algorithms</em> (2011)</a>. Theorem 1.3 and Algorithms 1–3: conversion of span-program witnesses to query algorithms.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/formula\">AND–OR formula evaluation</a>. Used directly to evaluate an AND–OR formula from its queried leaves.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "matrix-verification": {
    "title": "Buhrman–Špalek matrix-product verification",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Buhrman–Špalek matrix-product verification</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0409035\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Buhrman and Špalek, <em>Quantum Verification of Matrix Products</em> (2006)</a>. Figure 1, “Product Verification” and “Verify Once”; Theorem 6 gives time and space; Theorem 11 gives the lower bound.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/matrix-product-verification-over-a-field\">Field matrix-product verification</a>. Used directly to test a field matrix-product identity with a quantum walk.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "boolean-matrix-verification": {
    "title": "Gilani–Le Gall–Zhou Boolean matrix-product verification",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Gilani–Le Gall–Zhou Boolean matrix-product verification</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.40180\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Gilani, Le Gall and Zhou, <em>Improved Quantum Query Bounds for Boolean Matrix Product Verification</em> (2026)</a>. Theorems 1.1–1.2 and 3.5; Algorithms 2 and 6 and Theorems 4.1 and 4.6.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/boolean-matrix-product-verification\">Boolean matrix-product verification</a>. Used directly through the stated reductions, quantum searches and quantum walks.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "boolean-nested-search": {
    "title": "Buhrman–Špalek nested-search Boolean verification",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Buhrman–Špalek nested-search Boolean verification</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0409035\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Buhrman and Špalek, <em>Quantum Verification of Matrix Products</em> (2006)</a>. Theorem 13: Boolean verification by nested search.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/boolean-matrix-product-verification\">Boolean matrix-product verification</a>. Used directly in the earlier Boolean matrix-product verification construction.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "span-connectivity": {
    "title": "Belovs–Reichardt span-program connectivity",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Belovs–Reichardt span-program connectivity</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2603\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Belovs and Reichardt, <em>Span Programs and Quantum Algorithms for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span>-Connectivity and Claw Detection</em> (2012)</a>. Theorem 3: query bound; Theorem 9: time-efficient implementation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.00581\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Time and Space Efficient Quantum Algorithms for Detecting Cycles and Testing Bipartiteness</em></a>. Cycle-detection algorithms</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/promised-short-path-st-connectivity\">Promised short-path <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math></span>–<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></span> connectivity</a>. Used directly to decide whether the promised short s–t path exists.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g36\">Cycle detection</a>. Applied to a graph transformation whose connectivity encodes the existence of a cycle.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-mincut": {
    "title": "Apers–Lee quantum minimum cut",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Apers–Lee quantum minimum cut</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2011.09823\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Apers and Lee, <em>Quantum Complexity of Minimum Cut</em> (2021)</a>. Theorems 3 and 5 for the matrix and adjacency-array algorithms; the named routines FindMinStar, Cut-Sparsifier, LearnCutAtoms and LearnContraction.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/global-minimum-cut\">Global minimum cut</a>. Used directly through cut sparsification, contraction and minimum-cut recovery.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "qaoa": {
    "title": "QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>QAOA and the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1411.4028\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Farhi, Goldstone and Gutmann, <em>A Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm</em> (2014)</a>. Section II, “Fixed <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> Algorithm”; Section V, “MaxCut on 3-Regular Graphs”.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1412.6062\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Farhi, Goldstone and Gutmann, <em>A Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm Applied to a Bounded Occurrence Constraint Problem</em> (2014)</a>. Section II, “The General Case”, for the worst-case guarantee; Section III for typical instances.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1910.08187\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Farhi et al., <em>The Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm and the Sherrington–Kirkpatrick Model at Infinite Size</em> (2022)</a>. Section 2 defines QAOA; Section 4 states the limiting-energy result; Section 6 gives the general-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math></span> calculation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.03489\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Hadfield et al., <em>From the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm to a Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</em> (2019)</a>. Section 3 and Appendix A.3.1: feasible-state encodings, mixers and Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-Cut mapping. Appendix A.3.2, “Graph Partitioning (Minimum Bisection)”, and A.3.3, “Maximum Bisection”: complete mappings and resource counts.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.05296\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Hodson et al., <em>Portfolio Rebalancing Experiments Using the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz</em> (2019)</a>. Equations (9)–(12): optimization problem; Section IV and Equations (13)–(24): encoding, constraints, initial state and paired parity mixers; Section V: simulated experiments.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.39042\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Apte et al., <em>Provable Classical and Quantum Local Algorithms for Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-Cut and Quantum Advantage at Moderate Girth</em> (2026)</a>. Algorithm 1; Sections 4.1–4.2 define and evaluate qudit QAOA; Section 5 and Appendix A give the comparisons.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/maxcut-approximation-with-qaoa\">MaxCut</a>. Used directly with the MaxCut cost Hamiltonian and transverse-field mixer.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/bounded-occurrence-max-e3lin2\">Max-E3LIN2</a>. Used directly with parity phases for the Max-E3LIN2 constraints.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/maximum-independent-set-in-rydberg-geometric-graphs\">Rydberg independent set</a>. Used directly in the variational pulse sequences studied for Rydberg independent set.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/sherringtonkirkpatrick-spin-glass-optimization\">SK spin-glass optimization</a>. Used directly with the Sherrington–Kirkpatrick cost Hamiltonian.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>. Provides the parameterized circuit families whose outer parameter optimization is the target problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/maximum-k-cut-with-variational-quantum-circuits\">Max-<span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-Cut</a>. Used directly with qudit or feasible color encodings and their specified mixers, graph promises and depth.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/maximum-bisection-with-constraint-preserving-qaoa\">Maximum bisection</a>. Used directly with an XY mixer preserving the balanced Hamming weight.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs\">Job-shop scheduling</a>. Used directly on the scheduling QUBO as one of the compared variational methods.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/discrete-portfolio-rebalancing-with-constrained-mixers\">Discrete portfolio rebalancing</a>. Used directly with paired parity mixers preserving the investment constraint.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "rydberg-optimization": {
    "title": "Rydberg adiabatic and variational optimization",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Rydberg adiabatic and variational optimization</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.09372\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ebadi et al., <em>Quantum Optimization of Maximum Independent Set Using Rydberg Atom Arrays</em> (Science 2022)</a>. Main-text Figures 3–4; Supplementary Sections 4–5 define QAOA and variational adiabatic protocols; Sections 8–9 give the annealing comparison and success scaling.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/maximum-independent-set-in-rydberg-geometric-graphs\">Rydberg independent set</a>. Used directly by encoding independent sets through the Rydberg blockade and optimizing the pulse schedule.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "short-path": {
    "title": "Short-path quantum optimization",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Short-path quantum optimization</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2212.01513\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dalzell et al., <em>Mind the Gap: Achieving a Super-Grover Quantum Speedup by Jumping to the End</em> (2023/2026)</a>. Algorithm 1 and Theorems 1, 4, 6–8.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.23270\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Chakrabarti et al., <em>Generalized Short Path Algorithms</em> (2024/2025)</a>. Algorithm 2, Theorems 3.3–3.5, and Table 1.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/sherringtonkirkpatrick-spin-glass-optimization\">SK spin-glass optimization</a>. Used directly for exact SK optimization under the specified landscape and spectral hypotheses.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>. Used directly in the original and generalized constructions under their stated overlap, landscape and spectral conditions.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "conditioning-search": {
    "title": "Quantum conditioning-and-search",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum conditioning-and-search</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.12131\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Le Gall and Tamaki, <em>Dequantizing Short-Path Quantum Algorithms</em> (2026)</a>. Theorems 1.1–1.4 and 4.1: matched classical and quantum bounds; Section 4.3: amplitude amplification and minimum finding; Appendices A–B: removing the known-optimum assumption.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/exact-bounded-locality-spin-optimization-by-short-paths\">Fixed-locality spin optimization</a>. Used directly by amplifying successful conditioned trials and minimizing over recovered assignments.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "welded-tree-walk": {
    "title": "Continuous-time quantum walk on welded trees",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Continuous-time quantum walk on welded trees</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0209131\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Childs et al., <em>Exponential Algorithmic Speedup by a Quantum Walk</em> (2003)</a>. Theorem 3: quantum traversal; Theorem 9: classical query lower bound.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/traversing-a-randomly-welded-pair-of-trees\">Welded-tree traversal</a>. Used directly to propagate from the entrance to the exit of the oracle graph.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-simulated-annealing": {
    "title": "Quantum simulated annealing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum simulated annealing</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0804.1571\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Somma, Boixo, Barnum and Knill, <em>Quantum Simulations of Classical Annealing Processes</em> (2008)</a>. The quantum simulated annealing construction and its spectral-gap runtime analysis.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/gibbs-sampling-and-quantum-simulated-annealing\">Gibbs sampling</a>. Used directly to prepare a low-temperature distribution along the supplied chain sequence.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-sampling": {
    "title": "Wocjan–Abeyesinghe quantum sampling",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Wocjan–Abeyesinghe quantum sampling</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0804.4259\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Wocjan and Abeyesinghe, <em>Speedup via Quantum Sampling</em> (2008)</a>. Theorem 2: preparation along a sequence of reversible Markov chains.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/partition-functions-and-approximate-combinatorial-counting\">Partition functions</a>. Used as a state-preparation component of Markov-chain-based partition-function estimation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/gibbs-sampling-and-quantum-simulated-annealing\">Gibbs sampling</a>. Used directly to prepare successive coherent stationary distributions.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "dqi": {
    "title": "Decoded Quantum Interferometry",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Decoded Quantum Interferometry</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.08292\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Jordan et al., <em>Optimization by Decoded Quantum Interferometry</em> (Nature 2025)</a>. Theorem 4.1; Section 5, “Optimal Polynomial Intersection”; Section 8, “Decoded Quantum Interferometry”; Section 16 for resources.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.02145\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kramer et al., <em>A Provable Quantum Advantage for Approximate Optimization via Decoded Quantum Interferometry</em> (2026)</a>. Theorem 1: folded-OPI quantum–classical query separation.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.02063v3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Verifiable Quantum Advantage without Structure</em></a>. Lemma 4.2; Section 6, Theorem 6.1 and Lemmas 6.3, 6.9; Figure 1; Section 7</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/optimal-polynomial-intersection-by-decoded-quantum-interferometry\">Optimal polynomial intersection</a>. Used directly to construct high-scoring polynomials using Fourier interference and code decoding.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/yz-codeword-hash-search\">Codeword search with random hash constraints</a>. Uses Fourier transformation and dual-code decoding to construct a codeword satisfying all hash constraints; this is the Yamakawa–Zhandry construction of decoded quantum interferometry.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "worst-case-opi": {
    "title": "Horinaga–Yamakawa optimal polynomial intersection algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Horinaga–Yamakawa optimal polynomial intersection algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.14650\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Horinaga and Yamakawa, <em>Worst-Case Quantum Algorithm for Optimal Polynomial Intersection Beyond Decoded Quantum Interferometry</em> (2026)</a>. Theorems 1.1 and 6.1.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/optimal-polynomial-intersection-by-decoded-quantum-interferometry\">Optimal polynomial intersection</a>. Used directly to achieve full satisfaction in the stated worst-case balanced-set regime.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "vqe": {
    "title": "Variational quantum eigensolver and filtering VQE",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Variational quantum eigensolver and filtering VQE</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.03745\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Amaro et al., <em>A Case Study of Variational Quantum Algorithms for a Job Shop Scheduling Problem</em> (2022)</a>. Sections II.A–II.B and Appendix A define the scheduling problem and QUBO; Sections II.C.1–II.C.4 and Table II specify VQE, QAOA, VarQITE and F-VQE; Section III gives the experiments.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.10055\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Amaro et al., <em>Filtering Variational Quantum Algorithms for Combinatorial Optimization</em> (2022)</a>. Filtering objective, parameter-shift update and adaptive-filter prescription.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.12484\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Shaydulin et al., <em>Network Community Detection on Small Quantum Computers</em> (2019)</a>. Equation (1): two-community modularity; Algorithm 1 and Equation (2): quantum local search and its subproblem; Section 3.2: gate-model implementation; Section 4: experiments.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/global-optimization-of-variational-circuit-parameters\">Variational parameter optimization</a>. Provides the variational objective whose global parameter minimum is sought.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs\">Job-shop scheduling</a>. Used directly as VQE or F-VQE to optimize the diagonal scheduling objective through sampling and parameter updates.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/two-community-modularity-maximization-by-variational-local-search\">Community modularity</a>. Used as an <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> variational subproblem solver inside classical local search over community assignments.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "varqite": {
    "title": "Variational imaginary-time evolution (VarQITE)",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Variational imaginary-time evolution (VarQITE)</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.03745\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Amaro et al., <em>A Case Study of Variational Quantum Algorithms for a Job Shop Scheduling Problem</em> (2022)</a>. Sections II.A–II.B and Appendix A define the scheduling problem and QUBO; Sections II.C.1–II.C.4 and Table II specify VQE, QAOA, VarQITE and F-VQE; Section III gives the experiments.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/job-shop-scheduling-with-delivery-and-changeover-costs\">Job-shop scheduling</a>. Used directly by estimating the parameter metric and energy derivatives for scheduling optimization.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "shor-order": {
    "title": "Shor order finding and discrete logarithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Shor order finding and discrete logarithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9508027\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Shor, <em>Polynomial-Time Algorithms for Prime Factorization and Discrete Logarithms on a Quantum Computer</em> (1997)</a>. Section 5: the order-finding computation and continued-fraction reconstruction. Section 6, equations (6.1)–(6.3): discrete-logarithm algorithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.02174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A rigorous and robust quantum speed-up in supervised machine learning</em></a>. Theorems 1 and 2; Appendix B, Definition 6 and Theorem 8; Appendix C.3</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/factoring\">Integer factorization</a>. Used as a subroutine to obtain a multiplicative order from which classical processing extracts a factor.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithms-in-finite-fields\">Finite-field discrete logarithm</a>. Used directly with reversible finite-field group operations.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm\">Elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</a>. Used directly with reversible elliptic-curve group operations.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>. Used directly to determine the order of the supplied cyclic-group element.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a01\">Finite abelian-group decomposition</a>. Determines cyclic relations and orders during decomposition.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a02\">Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</a>. Provides finite-group order, period, or abelian hidden-subgroup computations within the reduction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a05\">Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</a>. Finds additive orders and coordinates of ring products.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a06\">Ideal operations in finite rings</a>. Provides finite-group order, period, or abelian hidden-subgroup computations within the reduction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a08\">Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</a>. Finds periods and solves the cyclic part of the logarithm.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a10\">Semidirect discrete logarithm</a>. Provides finite-group order, period, or abelian hidden-subgroup computations within the reduction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q45\">Testing one-to-one periodicity</a>. Provides the Fourier period-finding component of the tester.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithm-concept-learning\">Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</a>. Computes discrete logarithms as a preprocessing module for interval learning.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "regev-factoring": {
    "title": "Regev quantum factoring",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Regev quantum factoring</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.06572\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Regev, <em>An Efficient Quantum Factoring Algorithm</em> (2023)</a>. Theorem 1.1: circuit cost and repetition count; Section 3: quantum procedure.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/forum-of-mathematics-pi/article/unconditional-correctness-of-recent-quantum-algorithms-for-factoring-and-computing-discrete-logarithms/3077F5BD48D1F62000B1ED800EB86308\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Pilatte, <em>Unconditional Correctness of Recent Quantum Algorithms for Factoring and Computing Discrete Logarithms</em> (2026)</a>. Main factoring correctness theorem.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/factoring\">Integer factorization</a>. Used directly through multidimensional quantum sampling and classical lattice reconstruction.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "watrous-group-order": {
    "title": "Watrous solvable-group order algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Watrous solvable-group order algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0011023\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Watrous, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Solvable Groups</em> (2001)</a>. Theorem 1: quantum computation of group order in the black-box group model; the cyclic case provides the order-finding setting.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/order-finding-in-a-black-box-cyclic-group\">Cyclic-group order finding</a>. Used in its cyclic-group specialization to compute the group order.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a24\">Zeta functions of curves over finite fields</a>. Computes finite class-group information used to recover the zeta polynomial.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "hallgren-regulator": {
    "title": "Hallgren real-period finding",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hallgren real-period finding</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cse.psu.edu/ sjh26/pell.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Hallgren, <em>Polynomial-Time Quantum Algorithms for Pell&#x27;s Equation and the Principal Ideal Problem</em> (JACM 2007)</a>. Algorithm 2.1: reduced-ideal evaluation; Algorithm 3.1: approximate period finding for the regulator. The introduction gives the classical subexponential comparison. Algorithm 4.1: computing the distance of an ideal and the principal-ideal procedure.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/pell-s-equation-and-the-real-quadratic-regulator\">Pell equation and regulator</a>. Used directly to recover the real-quadratic regulator and compact Pell solution.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/the-principal-ideal-problem\">Principal ideal problem</a>. Used in the real-quadratic principal-ideal procedure to recover periods, ideal distances and a compact generator.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "biasse-song-units": {
    "title": "Biasse–Song S-unit computation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Biasse–Song S-unit computation</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://fangsong.info/files/pubs/BS_SODA16.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Biasse and Song, <em>Efficient Quantum Algorithms for Computing Class Groups and Solving the Principal Ideal Problem in Arbitrary Degree Number Fields</em> (SODA 2016)</a>. Theorem 1.1: <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>-units; Theorem 1.3 and Algorithm 2: principal ideal problem. Theorem 1.2: class groups under GRH; Algorithm 1: reduction of class-group computation to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>-units; Theorem 1.1: the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math></span>-unit quantum subroutine.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/the-principal-ideal-problem\">Principal ideal problem</a>. Used as a subroutine to recover a generator of a principal ideal.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/computing-ideal-class-groups\">Ideal class groups</a>. Used as a subroutine to compute relations and the ideal class group.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "kuperberg": {
    "title": "Hidden-shift sieves of Kuperberg and Regev",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hidden-shift sieves of Kuperberg and Regev</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1112.3333\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kuperberg, <em>Another Subexponential-Time Quantum Algorithm for the Dihedral Hidden Subgroup Problem</em> (TQC 2013)</a>. Algorithms 4.1 and 4.4: collimation and the collimation sieve.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0406151\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Regev, <em>A Subexponential Time Algorithm for the Dihedral Hidden Subgroup Problem with Polynomial Space</em> (2004)</a>. Polynomial-space sieve construction and its recursive combination procedure.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/1366\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Castryck and Vander Meeren, <em>Two Remarks on the Vectorization Problem</em> (2022)</a>. Theorem 1: the bound in terms of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msup><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math></span> and polynomial quantum storage; Section 2: vectorization and hidden shift; Section 5: bounded-torsion hidden shifts.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1012.4019\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Childs, Jao and Soukharev, <em>Constructing Elliptic Curve Isogenies in Quantum Subexponential Time</em> (2014)</a>. Algorithm 2 and Theorem 4.6: group-action evaluation; Algorithm 3 and Theorem 5.4: full isogeny algorithm; Theorems 5.1–5.2: hidden-shift subroutines.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/vectorization-for-commutative-isogeny-actions\">Commutative isogeny vectorization</a>. Used as hidden-shift subroutines after the Childs–Jao–Soukharev or Castryck–Vander Meeren vectorization reductions, with their respective torsion conditions and space tradeoffs.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>. Solves the hidden-shift instances created from local pattern windows.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "biasse-jao-sankar": {
    "title": "Biasse–Jao–Sankar supersingular isogeny algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Biasse–Jao–Sankar supersingular isogeny algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://cacr.uwaterloo.ca/techreports/2014/cacr2014-24.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Biasse, Jao and Sankar, <em>A Quantum Algorithm for Computing Isogenies Between Supersingular Elliptic Curves</em> (INDOCRYPT 2014)</a>. Theorem 1: main complexity result; Algorithm 1: complete isogeny procedure; Algorithms 2–3: reaching and navigating the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> subgraph.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/supersingular-isogeny-path-finding\">Supersingular isogeny paths</a>. Used directly to reach and navigate the specified finite-field subgraph.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "tani-claw": {
    "title": "Tani quantum-walk claw finding",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Tani quantum-walk claw finding</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0708.2584\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Tani, <em>Claw Finding Algorithms Using Quantum Walk</em> (2009)</a>. Section 3: claw detection; Section 4, <span class=\"small-caps\">ClawSearch</span> and Theorem 8: finding a claw and the two domain-size regimes.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/sidh-secret-isogeny-recovery\">SIDH key recovery</a>. Used as a subroutine to match the endpoints of two half-isogeny paths.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/claw\">Claw finding</a>. Used directly to find equal outputs of the two supplied functions.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/claw/double-encryption-key-recovery\">Double-encryption key recovery</a>. Used as a subroutine to match forward and backward encryption computations.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "superlattice-svp": {
    "title": "Hhan–Kim–Kim–Laarhoven quantum SVP",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hhan–Kim–Kim–Laarhoven quantum SVP</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/2761\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Hhan, Kim, Kim and Laarhoven, <em>Superlattice Methods for the Shortest Vector Problem and Discrete Gaussian Sampling</em> (2026)</a>. Main classical and quantum SVP complexity statements.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-shortest-vector-problem\">Shortest vector problem</a>. Used directly to find a shortest vector through the superlattice construction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/isomorphism-to-the-standard-integer-lattice\">Isomorphism to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span></a>. Used as the SVP subroutine in the dimension-halving lattice-isomorphism reduction.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "reusable-collision-walks": {
    "title": "Reusable quantum walks for lattice sieving",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Reusable quantum walks for lattice sieving</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.14023\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bonnetain et al., <em>Finding Many Collisions via Reusable Quantum Walks: Application to Lattice Sieving</em> (EUROCRYPT 2023)</a>. Algorithm 2: reusable collision walk; Section 7.1: the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2563</mn></mrow></math></span> SVP exponent and the classical sieve comparator.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-shortest-vector-problem\">Shortest vector problem</a>. Used as the collision-search component of the heuristic shortest-vector sieve.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/learning-with-errors-with-classical-samples\">Learning with errors</a>. Used as the block-SVP component of primal, dual and hybrid lattice attacks.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "svp-via-bdd": {
    "title": "Aggarwal–Chen–Kumar–Shen quantum SVP via BDD",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Aggarwal–Chen–Kumar–Shen quantum SVP via BDD</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/35122/35122.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Aggarwal, Chen, Kumar and Shen, <em>Improved Classical and Quantum Algorithms for the Shortest Vector Problem via Bounded Distance Decoding</em> (SIAM J. Comput. 2025)</a>. Theorem 1.3: quantum time-space bounds; Section 6: the SVP algorithms.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/the-euclidean-shortest-vector-problem\">Shortest vector problem</a>. Used directly through bounded-distance-decoding calls.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/isomorphism-to-the-standard-integer-lattice\">Isomorphism to <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span></a>. Used as an SVP subroutine in the stated lattice-isomorphism construction.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "noisy-parity": {
    "title": "Cross–Smith–Smolin noisy quantum parity learning",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Cross–Smith–Smolin noisy quantum parity learning</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5088\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Cross, Smith and Smolin, <em>Quantum Learning Robust Against Noise</em> (2015)</a>. Sections “Learning from ideal queries” and “Learning in the presence of noise”; equations (7)–(8): transformed examples and classification noise; the depolarizing-noise subsection gives reconstruction bounds.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/learning-parity-with-noisy-quantum-examples\">Noisy quantum parity learning</a>. Used directly to extract and denoise parity information from quantum examples.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "eldar-hallgren": {
    "title": "Eldar–Hallgren periodic-lattice decoding",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Eldar–Hallgren periodic-lattice decoding</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.13450\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Eldar and Hallgren, <em>An Efficient Quantum Algorithm for Lattice Problems Achieving Subexponential Approximation Factor</em> (2022)</a>. Algorithm 22, “Algorithm Q”: bounded-distance decoding on <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math></span>-periodic lattices; Algorithms 15, 17 and 19: quantum-state and sampling subroutines.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/bounded-distance-decoding-on-periodic-lattices\">Periodic-lattice BDD</a>. Used directly to recover the unique lattice vector within the promised radius.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "clz-filtering": {
    "title": "Chen–Liu–Zhandry quantum filtering",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Chen–Liu–Zhandry quantum filtering</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2108.11015\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Chen, Liu and Zhandry, <em>Quantum Algorithms for Variants of Average-Case Lattice Problems via Filtering</em> (EUROCRYPT 2022)</a>. Theorem 2: SIS guarantee; quantum filtering algorithms and the LWE-state construction provide the associated subroutines.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.40321v1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kothari, Metger, O&#x27;Donnell, Shutty and Wu, <em>Exponential Quantum Speedup for <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span>-Subset-Sum? Or, Rigorous Classical Algorithms for Binary-Error LWE</em> (2026)</a>. Problem 1.1; Theorem 1.3 and Corollary 1.4: quantum tradeoff; Section 1.1: classical comparison; Theorem 3.1: quantum reduction; Definition 4.3 and Section 4.2: quotient decoder.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/short-integer-solutions-with-a-wide-public-matrix\">Wide-matrix SIS</a>. Used directly to produce a short nonzero kernel vector for the stated wide random matrix.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/vector-subset-sum-over-f3\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math></span>-subset sum</a>. Used with the Kothari–Metger–O&#x27;Donnell–Shutty–Wu binary-error LWE decoder to produce the required binary vector relation.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-walk-isd": {
    "title": "Kachigar–Tillich and Kirshanova quantum-walk ISD",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Kachigar–Tillich and Kirshanova quantum-walk ISD</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1703.00263\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kachigar and Tillich, <em>Quantum Information Set Decoding Algorithms</em> (2017)</a>. Algorithm 2: ISD skeleton; quantum-walk analyses and rate optimization give the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.05869</mn></mrow></math></span> exponent.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1808.00714\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kirshanova, <em>Improved Quantum Information Set Decoding</em> (2018)</a>. Algorithm 1 and Theorem 1: quantum ISD and its runtime formula.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/binary-syndrome-decoding\">Syndrome decoding</a>. Used directly to accelerate the list-matching stages of information-set decoding.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/minimum-weight-nonzero-codewords\">Minimum-weight codewords</a>. Used directly at zero syndrome to search for a nonzero low-weight codeword.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "random-subset-sum": {
    "title": "Bonnetain–Bricout–Schrottenloher–Shen subset-sum algorithms",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Bonnetain–Bricout–Schrottenloher–Shen subset-sum algorithms</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.05276\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bonnetain, Bricout, Schrottenloher and Shen, <em>Improved Classical and Quantum Algorithms for Subset-Sum</em> (ASIACRYPT 2020)</a>. Section 2.5: classical <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.283</mn></mrow></math></span> exponent; Section 4.3: quantum-search <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.2356</mn></mrow></math></span> exponent; Section 5.3: heuristic quantum walk; Section 6.4: walk without the additional update heuristic; Table 1: models and resource exponents.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/random-subset-sum-at-cryptographic-density\">Random subset sum</a>. Used directly through the quantum-search and quantum-walk variants of the representation method.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "bht-collision": {
    "title": "Brassard–Høyer–Tapp collision search",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Brassard–Høyer–Tapp collision search</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9705002\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Brassard, Høyer and Tapp, <em>Quantum Algorithm for the Collision Problem</em> (1997)</a>. Theorem 1 and <span class=\"small-caps\">Collision</span><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span>: table-based quantum collision search; Section 3: coherent table-access costs.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/collision\">Hash collisions</a>. Used directly by building a value table and quantum-searching for an additional colliding input.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-kxor-merging": {
    "title": "Quantum k-XOR and k-SUM merging",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum k-XOR and k-SUM merging</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/501\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Naya-Plasencia and Schrottenloher, <em>Optimal Merging in Quantum <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-XOR and <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SUM Algorithms</em> (EUROCRYPT 2020)</a>. Quantum merging-tree framework and optimization of single-solution instances.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/31603/31603.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Schrottenloher, <em>Improved Quantum Algorithms for the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-XOR Problem</em> (SAC 2021; proceedings 2022)</a>. Section 5: single-solution merging; Section 6, Theorem 4 and Algorithm 4: quantum-walk construction; Section 6.3: the <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span> four-list exponent.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/generalized-birthday-search-random-k-xor\">Random <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-XOR</a>. Used directly through optimized merging trees and the quantum-walk variant.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "simon": {
    "title": "Simon period finding and offline Simon",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Simon period finding and offline Simon</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1602.05973\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Kaplan, Leurent, Leverrier and Naya-Plasencia, <em>Breaking Symmetric Cryptosystems Using Quantum Period Finding</em> (CRYPTO 2016)</a>. Theorem 1: Simon sampling with an approximate promise; Section 3.2, “Application to the Even-Mansour construction”: the Kuwakado–Morii attack and its extra-collision analysis; Figure 6: the period function.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.12439\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bonnetain, Hosoyamada, Naya-Plasencia, Sasaki and Schrottenloher, <em>Quantum Attacks Without Superposition Queries: The Offline Simon&#x27;s Algorithm</em> (ASIACRYPT 2019; full version 2020)</a>. Section 3.2 and Algorithms 2–4: asymmetric shift search, period test and reusable database preparation; Section 5.1: Q1 Even–Mansour tradeoffs and the balanced <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> attack.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.07856\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Using Simon&#x27;s Algorithm to Attack Symmetric-Key Cryptographic Primitives</em></a>. Three-round Feistel distinguisher section.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/even\">Even–Mansour with quantum queries</a>. Used directly on the period function obtained from the Even–Mansour construction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/evenmansour-recovery-with-classical-queries\">Even–Mansour with classical queries</a>. Used as the period-testing subroutine in offline Simon, with classical encryption data, reusable state preparation and asymmetric quantum search.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a40\">Distinguishing three-round Feistel networks</a>. Recovers the XOR period exposed by the Feistel construction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a41\">CBC-MAC forgery under superposition access</a>. Finds a hidden XOR relation in the tag oracle and uses it to construct a forgery.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "symmetric-graph-tester": {
    "title": "Ben-David et al. adjacency-list graph-property tester",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Ben-David et al. adjacency-list graph-property tester</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1137/23M1573975\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Ben-David et al., <em>Symmetries, Graph Properties, and Quantum Speedups</em> (2024)</a>. Theorem 1.2 and Corollary 4.14: matrix-model limits. Section 6, pp. FOCS20-393–394: formal definition of <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math></span> and edge-edit distance. Theorem 6.1 and Sections 6.1–6.3: quantum tester and classical lower bound.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/symmetric-promise-problems-and-graph-properties\">Symmetric promise problems</a>. Used directly on the promised graph family to obtain the stated query separation.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "parity": {
    "title": "Pairwise quantum parity algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Pairwise quantum parity algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9802049\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Beals et al., <em>Quantum Lower Bounds by Polynomials</em> (1998/2001)</a>. Section 6, Proposition 6.4 and the preceding pairwise parity algorithm; Table 1 summarizes the tight bounds.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/exact-counting-and-parity-of-feasible-solutions\">Exact counting and parity</a>. Used directly to recover the parity of two queried bits per phase-kickback step.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "stoquastic-adiabatic": {
    "title": "Stoquastic frustration-free adiabatic evolution",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Stoquastic frustration-free adiabatic evolution</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0806.1746\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Bravyi and Terhal, <em>Complexity of Stoquastic Frustration-Free Hamiltonians</em> (2008/2009)</a>. Definitions 1.6–1.8 for stoquastic <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-SAT; assumptions (A0)–(A3) and Theorem 1.4 for classical sampling; Section 5 for the sampler; Theorem 1.9 and Section 6 for MA verification and completeness.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/stoquastic-frustration-free-constraint-satisfaction\">Stoquastic frustration-free constraints</a>. Used directly along the initialized, gapped path in the stated simulation theorem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "ground-state-filtering": {
    "title": "Lin–Tong ground-state filtering and energy estimation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Lin–Tong ground-state filtering and energy estimation</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.12508\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Lin and Tong, <em>Near-optimal Ground State Preparation</em> (2020)</a>. Theorem 6 for preparation with a ground-energy bound; Algorithm 1 and Theorem 8 for ground-energy estimation and binary search.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/local-hamiltonian-ground-energy-optimization\">Local Hamiltonian ground energy</a>. Used directly through energy filters, amplitude amplification and threshold search.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "local-adiabatic-search": {
    "title": "Roland–Cerf local adiabatic search",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Roland–Cerf local adiabatic search</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0107015\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Roland and Cerf, <em>Quantum Search by Local Adiabatic Evolution</em> (2002)</a>. Equations (16)–(20), especially the schedule in (17)–(18).</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/adiabatic-optimization-spectral-gap-costs-and-oracle-bounds\">Adiabatic optimization</a>. Used directly to find the marked item with the locally chosen evolution schedule.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "subset-quantum-walk": {
    "title": "Ambainis–Childs–Eisenberg subset quantum walk",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Ambainis–Childs–Eisenberg subset quantum walk</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0311001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum walk algorithm for element distinctness</em></a>. Main element-distinctness algorithm and theorem</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0311038\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for subset finding</em></a>. General subset-finding algorithm; k-sum application</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g01\">Element distinctness and k-distinctness</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g03\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-sum</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "belovs-k-distinctness": {
    "title": "Belovs learning graph for k-distinctness",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Belovs learning graph for k-distinctness</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.1534\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Learning-Graph-Based Quantum Algorithm for k-distinctness</em></a>. Main theorem</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.17786\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithms on Edge Lists: Hiding, Shuffling, and Cycle Finding</em></a>. Theorem 6.16 and Corollary 6.17; Section 6.2</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g01\">Element distinctness and k-distinctness</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g38\">Cycle finding from an edge list</a>. The learning-graph construction is adapted from equal symbols to incident edges forming a fixed-length cycle.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "optimal-ordered-search": {
    "title": "Carolan–Childs optimal ordered search",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Carolan–Childs optimal ordered search</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34293\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Optimal Quantum Algorithms for Ordered Search</em></a>. Main theorem and exact polynomial-program construction</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g04\">Ordered search</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-approximate-selection": {
    "title": "Nayak–Wu approximate selection",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Nayak–Wu approximate selection</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9804066\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The quantum query complexity of approximating the median and related statistics</em></a>. Approximate-median algorithm and theorem</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g05\">Approximate median</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "ramesh-vinay-matching": {
    "title": "Ramesh–Vinay quantum string matching",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Ramesh–Vinay quantum string matching</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0011049\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>String Matching in tilde O(sqrt(n)+sqrt(m)) Quantum Time</em></a>. Main algorithm; deterministic-sampling construction</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g06\">Exact string matching</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "hidden-shift-pattern-matching": {
    "title": "Montanaro hidden-shift pattern matching",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Montanaro hidden-shift pattern matching</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.1816\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum pattern matching fast on average</em></a>. Theorems1–2; Sections4–5</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g07\">Average-case multidimensional pattern matching</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "synchronizing-set-quantum-walk": {
    "title": "Akmal–Jin synchronizing-set quantum walk",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Akmal–Jin synchronizing-set quantum walk</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.09696\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal Quantum Algorithms for String Problems</em></a>. Section3; main LCS theorem</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g08\">Longest common substring</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "akmal-jin-string-selection": {
    "title": "Akmal–Jin quantum string selection",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Akmal–Jin quantum string selection</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.09696\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal Quantum Algorithms for String Problems</em></a>. Theorem4.6</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g09\">Lexicographically minimal cyclic rotation</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g10\">Longest square substring</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g12\">Longest Lyndon substring</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-palindrome-search": {
    "title": "Le Gall–Seddighin palindrome search",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Le Gall–Seddighin palindrome search</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.12122\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Meets Fine-grained Complexity: Sublinear Time Quantum Algorithms for String Problems</em></a>. Theorem2; palindrome section</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g14\">Longest palindromic substring</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-ulam-approximation": {
    "title": "Le Gall–Seddighin Ulam-distance approximation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Le Gall–Seddighin Ulam-distance approximation</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.12122\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Meets Fine-grained Complexity: Sublinear Time Quantum Algorithms for String Problems</em></a>. Theorem2.6 and Section6</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g15\">Approximate Ulam distance</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-bounded-edit-distance": {
    "title": "Gibney–Jin–Kociumaka–Thankachan bounded edit distance",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Gibney–Jin–Kociumaka–Thankachan bounded edit distance</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01793\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal Quantum Algorithms for Bounded Edit Distance and Lempel-Ziv Factorization</em></a>. Main bounded-edit-distance theorem</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g16\">Bounded edit distance</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-approximate-pattern-matching": {
    "title": "Kociumaka–Nogler–Wellnitz approximate pattern matching",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Kociumaka–Nogler–Wellnitz approximate pattern matching</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.06808\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal-Time Quantum Algorithms for Approximate Pattern Matching</em></a>. Main mismatch-matching theorem</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g17\">Pattern matching with mismatches</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g18\">Pattern matching with edits</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-lz77": {
    "title": "Gibney–Jin–Kociumaka–Thankachan LZ77 construction",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Gibney–Jin–Kociumaka–Thankachan LZ77 construction</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01793\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal Quantum Algorithms for Bounded Edit Distance and Lempel-Ziv Factorization</em></a>. Main Lempel–Ziv theorem</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g19\">Lempel–Ziv factorization</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-compressed-bwt": {
    "title": "Gibney–Thankachan compressibility-aware string algorithms",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Gibney–Thankachan compressibility-aware string algorithms</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.07235\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Compressibility-Aware Quantum Algorithms on Strings</em></a>. RL-BWT construction result</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g20\">Run-length Burrows–Wheeler transform</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-rle-lcs": {
    "title": "Lee–Lin run-length encoded substring algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Lee–Lin run-length encoded substring algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.02421\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near-Optimal Quantum Algorithm for Finding the Longest Common Substring between Run-Length Encoded Strings</em></a>. Main theorem</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g22\">Longest common substring of run-length encoded strings</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "star-free-language-quantum-query": {
    "title": "Aaronson–Grier–Schaeffer regular-language algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Aaronson–Grier–Schaeffer regular-language algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.04219\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A Quantum Query Complexity Trichotomy for Regular Languages</em></a>. Star-free recognition algorithm and classification theorem</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g23\">Star-free regular-language membership</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "bounded-dyck-quantum-search": {
    "title": "Khadiev–Shen bounded-height Dyck algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Khadiev–Shen bounded-height Dyck algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02176\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Query Complexity of Dyck Languages with Bounded Height</em></a>. Main algorithm and query upper bound</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g24\">Bounded-height Dyck language</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-divide-conquer": {
    "title": "Quantum divide and conquer",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum divide and conquer</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.06419\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum divide and conquer</em></a>. Theorem7; Section3.4</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.16401\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>On the quantum time complexity of divide and conquer</em></a>. Theorem19; ICALP Theorem3</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g25\">Common subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g26\">Increasing subsequence of length <span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span></a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g27\">Longest substring with distinct characters</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g31\">Maximum-sum submatrix</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "geometric-quantum-walk": {
    "title": "Aaronson–Chia–Lin–Wang–Zhang geometric quantum walk",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Aaronson–Chia–Lin–Wang–Zhang geometric quantum walk</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.01973\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>On the Quantum Complexity of Closest Pair and Related Problems</em></a>. Theorem1.3; geometric quantum walk</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g32\">Euclidean closest pair</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g33\">Bichromatic closest pair</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "minor-closed-property-quantum-query": {
    "title": "Childs–Kothari minor-closed graph-property algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Childs–Kothari minor-closed graph-property algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.1443\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum query complexity of minor-closed graph properties</em></a>. Minor-closed classification; planarity specialization</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g35\">Planarity</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "fixed-subgraph-learning-graph": {
    "title": "Lee–Magniez–Santha fixed-subgraph learning graph",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Lee–Magniez–Santha fixed-subgraph learning graph</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.5135\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A learning graph based quantum query algorithm for finding constant-size subgraphs</em></a>. Theorem4.4</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g37\">Fixed-subgraph containment</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "distributed-quantum-diameter": {
    "title": "Le Gall–Magniez distributed diameter algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Le Gall–Magniez distributed diameter algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.02917\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Sublinear-Time Quantum Computation of the Diameter in CONGEST Networks</em></a>. Theorem1; Section3.2</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g42\">Distributed graph diameter</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "output-sensitive-boolean-product": {
    "title": "Le Gall output-sensitive Boolean matrix multiplication",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Le Gall output-sensitive Boolean matrix multiplication</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.6174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A Time-Efficient Output-Sensitive Quantum Algorithm for Boolean Matrix Multiplication</em></a>. Main theorem</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g43\">Boolean matrix multiplication</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-spanning-tree-sampling": {
    "title": "Apers–Gao–Ji–Liu spanning-tree sampler",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Apers–Gao–Ji–Liu spanning-tree sampler</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15603\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Speedup for Sampling Random Spanning Trees</em></a>. Main theorem; large-step up-down walk</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g44\">Random spanning-tree sampling</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "signed-graph-clusterability-tester": {
    "title": "Chen–Apers–Hsieh clusterability tester",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Chen–Apers–Hsieh clusterability tester</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.10480\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>(Quantum) complexity of testing signed graph clusterability</em></a>. Theorems2 and6</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g45\">Signed graph clusterability testing</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "directed-subgraph-quantum-tester": {
    "title": "Apers–Magniez–Sen–Szabo directed graph tester",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Apers–Magniez–Sen–Szabo directed graph tester</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.05001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum property testing in sparse directed graphs</em></a>. Theorem13 in conference version (Theorem1 restatement)</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g46\">Directed subgraph-freeness testing</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-directed-path-containment": {
    "title": "Cornelissen–Gilani–Patro path-containment algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Cornelissen–Gilani–Patro path-containment algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.09017\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for path and cycle containment problems</em></a>. Main result 1.10; Corollary 4.5; Section 4</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-g47\">Directed fixed-length path containment</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "abelian-group-decomposition": {
    "title": "Cheung–Mosca and Luo–Li–Le Gall abelian-group decomposition",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Cheung–Mosca and Luo–Li–Le Gall abelian-group decomposition</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36480\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Decomposing Finite Abelian Groups; Improved Quantum Algorithms for Black-Box Abelian Group Decomposition</em></a>. 2026 Theorem 1.1 and Section 5; original: https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/0101004</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a01\">Finite abelian-group decomposition</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "coprime-group-isomorphism": {
    "title": "Le Gall coprime group-isomorphism algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Le Gall coprime group-isomorphism algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.0608\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>An Efficient Quantum Algorithm for some Instances of the Group Isomorphism Problem</em></a>. Theorems 1.2 and 1.3; STACS 2010 https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.STACS.2010.2484</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a02\">Isomorphism of coprime abelian-by-cyclic groups</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "group-commutativity-quantum-walk": {
    "title": "Magniez–Nayak commutativity quantum walk",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Magniez–Nayak commutativity quantum walk</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0506265\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Complexity of Testing Group Commutativity</em></a>. Main upper/lower-bound theorems; author final PDF https://www.irif.fr/ magniez/PAPIERS/mn-algorithmica07.pdf</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a04\">Group commutativity</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "black-box-ring-basis": {
    "title": "Arvind–Das–Mukhopadhyay finite-ring basis algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Arvind–Das–Mukhopadhyay finite-ring basis algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/11809678_15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Complexity of Black-Box Ring Problems</em></a>. Basis-representation construction; source PDF https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=8c18a1a053fc1317bdc53810c4ebe29aaa1bc799   repid=rep1   type=pdf</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a05\">Basis representation of a finite black-box ring</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-finite-ring-ideals": {
    "title": "Wocjan–Jordan–Ahmadi–Brennan ideal algorithms",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Wocjan–Jordan–Ahmadi–Brennan ideal algorithms</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.0022\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Efficient quantum processing of ideals in finite rings</em></a>. Ideal basis algorithm and applications sections.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a06\">Ideal operations in finite rings</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "ring-identity-quantum-walk": {
    "title": "Arvind–Mukhopadhyay multilinear identity test",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Arvind–Mukhopadhyay multilinear identity test</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0807.1412\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Query Complexity of Multilinear Identity Testing</em></a>. Main algorithm theorem; ECCC https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2008/086/</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a07\">Multilinear identity testing over a finite ring</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "semigroup-discrete-logarithm": {
    "title": "Childs–Ivanyos semigroup discrete logarithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Childs–Ivanyos semigroup discrete logarithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.6238\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum computation of discrete logarithms in semigroups</em></a>. Sections 2–3; independent reduction https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.7903</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a08\">Discrete logarithms in finite semigroups</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "commutative-semigroup-membership": {
    "title": "Childs–Ivanyos commutative-semigroup membership",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Childs–Ivanyos commutative-semigroup membership</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.6238\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum computation of discrete logarithms in semigroups</em></a>. Section 5.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a09\">Constructive membership in a commutative semigroup</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "semidirect-discrete-logarithm": {
    "title": "Imran–Ivanyos semidirect discrete logarithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Imran–Ivanyos semidirect discrete logarithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.14028\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Efficient quantum algorithms for some instances of the semidirect discrete logarithm problem</em></a>. Main theorems; extensions https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/905.pdf; current classical analysis https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.05048</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a10\">Semidirect discrete logarithm</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "doliskani-finite-field-factorization": {
    "title": "Doliskani quantum finite-field factorization",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Doliskani quantum finite-field factorization</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.09675\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Toward an Optimal Quantum Algorithm for Polynomial Factorization over Finite Fields</em></a>. Theorem 1; distinct-degree algorithm; classical https://doi.org/10.1137/08073408X; update https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.10851</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a11\">Polynomial factorization over a finite field</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-polynomial-interpolation": {
    "title": "Quantum polynomial interpolation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum polynomial interpolation</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.09271\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Optimal quantum algorithm for polynomial interpolation</em></a>. Main interpolation theorem; ICALP https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ICALP.2016.16</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.03990\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithm for multivariate polynomial interpolation</em></a>. Finite-field theorem; full text https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5806014/</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a12\">Polynomial interpolation over finite fields</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "multiplicative-character-hidden-shift": {
    "title": "van Dam–Hallgren–Ip multiplicative-character hidden shift",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>van Dam–Hallgren–Ip multiplicative-character hidden shift</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0211140\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithms for some Hidden Shift Problems</em></a>. Shifted Legendre-symbol and multiplicative-character algorithms.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a15\">Shifted multiplicative characters</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "bent-function-hidden-shift": {
    "title": "Rotteler bent-function hidden shift",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Rotteler bent-function hidden shift</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0811.3208\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for highly non-linear Boolean functions</em></a>. Main bent-function algorithms; Theorems 7 and lower-bound section.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a16\">Hidden shifts of bent Boolean functions</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "boolean-hidden-shift-pgm": {
    "title": "Pretty-good measurement for Boolean hidden shift",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Pretty-good measurement for Boolean hidden shift</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.4642\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Easy and hard functions for the Boolean hidden shift problem</em></a>. Random-function and pretty-good-measurement results.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a17\">Random Boolean hidden shift</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "generalized-hidden-shift-pgm": {
    "title": "Childs–van Dam generalized hidden-shift algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Childs–van Dam generalized hidden-shift algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0507190\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithm for a generalized hidden shift problem</em></a>. Main efficient-algorithm theorem.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a18\">Generalized hidden shift</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "semidirect-hsp-pgm": {
    "title": "Bacon–Childs–van Dam semidirect-product HSP algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Bacon–Childs–van Dam semidirect-product HSP algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0504083\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>From optimal measurement to efficient quantum algorithms for the hidden subgroup problem over semidirect product groups</em></a>. Main metacyclic and fixed-r semidirect-product results.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a19\">Hidden subgroups of selected semidirect-product groups</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "nilpotency-two-hsp": {
    "title": "Ivanyos–Sanselme–Santha nilpotency-two HSP algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Ivanyos–Sanselme–Santha nilpotency-two HSP algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.1260\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>An efficient quantum algorithm for the hidden subgroup problem in nil-2 groups</em></a>. Main theorem; extraspecial precursor https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0701235</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a20\">Hidden subgroups of nilpotency-class-two groups</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "normal-hsp-fourier-sampling": {
    "title": "Hallgren–Russell–Ta-Shma normal-subgroup Fourier sampling",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hallgren–Russell–Ta-Shma normal-subgroup Fourier sampling</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cs.tau.ac.il/ amnon/Papers/HRT.siam02.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Hidden Subgroup Problem and Quantum Computation Using Group Representations</em></a>. Theorem 1.3 and normal-subgroup corollary.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a21\">Normal hidden subgroup</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "hidden-symmetry-subgroup-reduction": {
    "title": "Decker–Draisma–Ivanyos–Santha hidden-symmetry algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Decker–Draisma–Ivanyos–Santha hidden-symmetry algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1107.2189\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Hidden Symmetry Subgroup Problems</em></a>. Theorem 3 and Section 4.3: multivariate quadratic hidden polynomials over constant-characteristic fields</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a22\">Hidden quadratic level sets</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "hidden-polynomial-graph": {
    "title": "Decker–Wocjan hidden-polynomial-graph algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Decker–Wocjan hidden-polynomial-graph algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0703195\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Efficient Quantum Algorithm for Hidden Quadratic and Cubic Polynomial Function Graphs</em></a>. Quadratic/cubic hidden-graph algorithms.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a23\">Hidden quadratic and cubic polynomial graphs</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "kedlaya-curve-zeta": {
    "title": "Kedlaya quantum zeta-function algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Kedlaya quantum zeta-function algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0411623\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum computation of zeta functions of curves</em></a>. Theorem 1; Sections 6–9.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a24\">Zeta functions of curves over finite fields</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "ajl-jones-approximation": {
    "title": "Aharonov–Jones–Landau Jones-polynomial approximation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Aharonov–Jones–Landau Jones-polynomial approximation</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0511096\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A Polynomial Quantum Algorithm for Approximating the Jones Polynomial</em></a>. Theorems 1.2–1.3; Section 3 algorithms. Modern fifth-root normalization: https://arxiv.org/html/2503.05625v2, Section II.1.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a31\">Jones polynomial approximation</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "one-clean-qubit-link-invariants": {
    "title": "One-clean-qubit Jones and HOMFLY approximation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>One-clean-qubit Jones and HOMFLY approximation</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.2831\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Estimating Jones polynomials is a complete problem for one clean qubit</em></a>. DQC1 algorithm and completeness theorem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0807.4688\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Jones polynomial: quantum algorithms and applications in quantum complexity theory; Estimating Jones and HOMFLY polynomials with One Clean Qubit</em></a>. HOMFLY algorithm; precursor https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0603069</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a31\">Jones polynomial approximation</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a33\">HOMFLY polynomial approximation</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-tutte-approximation": {
    "title": "Aharonov–Arad–Eban–Landau Tutte approximation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Aharonov–Arad–Eban–Landau Tutte approximation</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0702008\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Polynomial Quantum algorithms for additive approximations of the Potts model and other points of the Tutte plane</em></a>. Definition 2.1; algorithm section and BQP-hardness results.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a34\">Additive approximation of the planar Tutte polynomial</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "turaev-viro-quantum-estimation": {
    "title": "Alagic–Jordan–Konig–Reichardt Turaev–Viro estimator",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Alagic–Jordan–Konig–Reichardt Turaev–Viro estimator</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.0923\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Approximating Turaev-Viro 3-manifold invariants is universal for quantum computation</em></a>. Theorem 1.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a35\">Turaev–Viro invariant</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "wrt-quantum-recoupling": {
    "title": "Garnerone–Marzuoli–Rasetti quantum recoupling",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Garnerone–Marzuoli–Rasetti quantum recoupling</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0703037\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Efficient quantum processing of 3-manifold topological invariants</em></a>. Quantum algorithm sections; Heegaard normalization also Eq. (3) in https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.0923</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a36\">Witten–Reshetikhin–Turaev invariant</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-double-link-estimation": {
    "title": "Krovi–Russell quantum-double link estimation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Krovi–Russell quantum-double link estimation</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.1550\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Fourier Transforms and the Complexity of Link Invariants for Quantum Doubles of Finite Groups</em></a>. QFT construction, link-invariant algorithm and hardness sections.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a37\">Link invariants from quantum doubles of finite groups</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quadratically-signed-weight-enumerator": {
    "title": "Knill–Laflamme weight-enumerator algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Knill–Laflamme weight-enumerator algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9909094\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Computation and Quadratically Signed Weight Enumerators</em></a>. Promised signed-weight-enumerator completeness results.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a38\">Quadratically signed weight enumerators</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-multicollision": {
    "title": "Hosoyamada–Sasaki–Tani–Xagawa multicollision algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hosoyamada–Sasaki–Tani–Xagawa multicollision algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.08097\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Improved Quantum Multicollision-Finding Algorithm; On Finding Quantum Multi-collisions</em></a>. Main multicollision theorem; matching lower bound https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/1096.pdf</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a39\">Multicollisions in a random function</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "hidden-radius-fourier": {
    "title": "Childs–Schulman–Vazirani hidden-radius algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Childs–Schulman–Vazirani hidden-radius algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.2784\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for hidden nonlinear structures</em></a>. Section 3, Theorems 1–3.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-a42\">Hidden radius over a finite field</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "deutsch-jozsa": {
    "title": "Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1992.0167\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Rapid solution of problems by quantum computation</em></a>. Main algorithm, pp.553–558</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q01\">Deutsch–Jozsa promise problem</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "forrelation": {
    "title": "Forrelation Hadamard-transform algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Forrelation Hadamard-transform algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.5729\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Forrelation: A Problem that Optimally Separates Quantum from Classical Computing</em></a>. Main separation theorem and k-fold BQP-completeness construction</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q04\">Forrelation</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "boolean-fourier-sampling": {
    "title": "Boolean Fourier sampling",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Boolean Fourier sampling</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.05903\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Complexity-Theoretic Foundations of Quantum Supremacy Experiments</em></a>. Fourier Sampling lower bound; Section7.4 for succinct-oracle implications</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q05\">Fourier sampling of a Boolean function</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "van-dam-oracle-interrogation": {
    "title": "van Dam oracle interrogation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>van Dam oracle interrogation</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9805006\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Oracle Interrogation: Getting all information for almost half the price</em></a>. Full-interrogation algorithm</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q08\">Full oracle interrogation</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "kothari-oracle-identification": {
    "title": "Kothari oracle identification",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Kothari oracle identification</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.7685\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>An optimal quantum algorithm for the oracle identification problem</em></a>. Proposition2 and Theorem2; learning algorithm and composition theorem</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q09\">Oracle identification</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "wildcard-quantum-learning": {
    "title": "Ambainis–Montanaro and Belovs wildcard learning",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Ambainis–Montanaro and Belovs wildcard learning</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.1148\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for search with wildcards and combinatorial group testing</em></a>. Wildcard algorithm; tight improvement summarized in TQC2026.3</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q10\">Search with wildcards</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "belovs-symmetric-junta-learning": {
    "title": "Belovs adversary algorithm for learning symmetric juntas",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Belovs adversary algorithm for learning symmetric juntas</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.6777\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithms for Learning Symmetric Juntas via the Adversary Bound</em></a>. OR-function theorem; explicit implementation in Montanaro–Shao2011.08611</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q11\">Combinatorial group testing</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q32\">Learning symmetric juntas</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "gapped-group-testing": {
    "title": "Ambainis–Belovs–Regev–de Wolf gapped group testing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Ambainis–Belovs–Regev–de Wolf gapped group testing</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.03126\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Efficient Quantum Algorithms for (Gapped) Group Testing and Junta Testing</em></a>. Definitions3.1–3.2; Theorems3.3–3.5 and3.10</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q12\">Gapped group testing</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "optimal-quantum-junta-testing": {
    "title": "Optimal quantum junta testing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Optimal quantum junta testing</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37926\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Optimal Quantum Junta Testing</em></a>. Theorems 4.2 and 4.8: complete Boolean-junta query bound</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q13\">Junta testing</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-fourier-junta-learning": {
    "title": "Atici–Servedio Fourier junta learning and testing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Atici–Servedio Fourier junta learning and testing</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.3479\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithms for Learning and Testing Juntas</em></a>. Junta-testing theorem and Fourier-sampling procedure</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q13\">Junta testing</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q15\">Learning juntas from quantum examples</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "distribution-free-quantum-junta-testing": {
    "title": "Belovs distribution-free quantum junta tester",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Belovs distribution-free quantum junta tester</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.08462\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithm for Distribution-Free Junta Testing</em></a>. Main algorithm; CSR2019.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q13\">Junta testing</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-dnf-fourier-learning": {
    "title": "Quantum Fourier-sampling DNF learning",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum Fourier-sampling DNF learning</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/S0097539795293123\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Learning DNF over the Uniform Distribution Using a Quantum Example Oracle</em></a>. Quantum Fourier-sampling weak learner and boosting construction</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.05690\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Learning DNFs under product distributions via mu-biased quantum Fourier sampling</em></a>. Biased Fourier sampler and DNF-learning theorem</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q16\">Learning DNF from quantum examples</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "fourier-sparse-quantum-learning": {
    "title": "Fourier-sparse Boolean-function learning",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Fourier-sparse Boolean-function learning</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17648\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Nearly Optimal Quantum Sample Complexity of Learning Fourier-Sparse Boolean Functions</em></a>. Main learning theorem and classical sample-complexity section</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q18\">Exact learning of Fourier-sparse Boolean functions</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-agnostic-decision-tree-learning": {
    "title": "Chatterjee–SAPV–Bera quantum agnostic learner",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Chatterjee–SAPV–Bera quantum agnostic learner</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v238/chatterjee24a/chatterjee24a.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Efficient Quantum Agnostic Improper Learning of Decision Trees</em></a>. Algorithms1–3; Theorems3–4</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q19\">Agnostic learning of decision trees</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-boolean-property-tests": {
    "title": "Quantum linearity and symmetry testing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum linearity and symmetry testing</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.6195\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Improved quantum test for linearity of a Boolean function</em></a>. Main algorithm; earlier construction: arXiv:1106.4831</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2013/232.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms to check Resiliency, Symmetry and Linearity of a Boolean function</em></a>. Symmetry-testing section; later extended manuscript.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q20\">Linearity testing of Boolean functions</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-data-property-testing": {
    "title": "Caro–Naik–Slote property testing from quantum data",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Caro–Naik–Slote property testing from quantum data</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol362-itcs2026/html/LIPIcs.ITCS.2026.34/LIPIcs.ITCS.2026.34.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Testing classical properties from quantum data</em></a>. Symmetry application.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q21\">Symmetry testing of Boolean functions</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q22\">Monotonicity testing of Boolean functions</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q25\">Triangle-freeness of Boolean functions from examples</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-monotonicity-tester": {
    "title": "Belovs–Blais quantum monotonicity tester",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Belovs–Blais quantum monotonicity tester</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://theoryofcomputing.org/articles/v011a016/v011a016.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Algorithm for Monotonicity Testing</em></a>. Main theorem and Section3 algorithm.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q22\">Monotonicity testing of Boolean functions</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "sequential-measurement-property-testing": {
    "title": "Harrow–Lin–Montanaro sequential-measurement tester",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Harrow–Lin–Montanaro sequential-measurement tester</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.03236\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Sequential measurements, disturbance and property testing</em></a>. Theorem2; Boolean-function-isomorphism application.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q26\">Boolean-function isomorphism under coordinate permutations</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-coupon-collector": {
    "title": "Quantum coupon-collector algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum coupon-collector algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.07688\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Coupon Collector</em></a>. Table1; Theorems2,5,9–12.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q29\">Quantum coupon collection</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-hidden-graph-learning": {
    "title": "Montanaro–Shao hidden-graph learning",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Montanaro–Shao hidden-graph learning</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.08611\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for learning a hidden graph and beyond</em></a>. Table1 and OR-query section.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q30\">Learning a graph with edge-existence queries</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q31\">Learning a graph with edge-parity queries</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-distribution-identity-testing": {
    "title": "Quantum finite-distribution identity and uniformity testing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum finite-distribution identity and uniformity testing</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.3920\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for testing properties of distributions</em></a>. Uniformity algorithm; improved precision in arXiv:1005.0523 Section3.1.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.0523\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>New Results on Quantum Property Testing</em></a>. Theorem1.1, Section3.2.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q34\">Identity and uniformity testing of finite distributions</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "purified-distribution-testing": {
    "title": "Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Gilyen–Li and Luo–Wang–Li distribution testing</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.12916\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Succinct quantum testers for closeness and k-wise uniformity of probability distributions</em></a>. L1-closeness theorem; predecessor arXiv:1902.00814 Corollary17.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.00814\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Distributional property testing in a quantum world</em></a>. Definition6; Corollary18; AppendixC.2.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q36\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q37\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></span>-closeness testing of finite distributions</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q38\">Independence testing of a finite joint distribution</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q39\"><span class=\"math-inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math></span>-wise uniformity testing</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-finite-entropy-estimation": {
    "title": "Quantum Shannon-entropy estimation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum Shannon-entropy estimation</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07452\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Near optimal quantum algorithm for estimating Shannon entropy</em></a>. Main theorem; established predecessor arXiv:1902.00814 Theorem12.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q40\">Shannon entropy of a finite distribution</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-support-estimation": {
    "title": "Li–Wu support-size and support-coverage estimation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Li–Wu support-size and support-coverage estimation</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.06025\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Query Complexity of Entropy Estimation</em></a>. Algorithm9 and Theorem8.2.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q41\">Support-size estimation</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q42\">Support-coverage estimation</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum-distribution-orthogonality": {
    "title": "Bravyi–Harrow–Hassidim orthogonality tester",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Bravyi–Harrow–Hassidim orthogonality tester</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.3920\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum algorithms for testing properties of distributions</em></a>. Orthogonality-testing algorithm.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q43\">Disjoint-support testing of finite distributions</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "one-to-one-periodicity-testing": {
    "title": "Quantum one-to-one periodicity tester",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum one-to-one periodicity tester</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.0523\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>New Results on Quantum Property Testing</em></a>. Theorem1.4 and Section8.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q45\">Testing one-to-one periodicity</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "correlation-biased-quadratic-learning": {
    "title": "Oliveira correlation-biased quadratic-hypothesis learner",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Oliveira correlation-biased quadratic-hypothesis learner</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23268\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Unconditional Quantum PAC Learning Advantage in the Large Error Regime</em></a>. Theorem1.2 gives the algorithmic tradeoff; Theorems1.4–1.5 establish separations.</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/prior-q46\">PAC learning in the large-error regime</a>. Used directly for the stated problem.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "bcmvv_protocol": {
    "title": "BCMVV test of quantumness",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>BCMVV test of quantumness</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.00640\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A Cryptographic Test of Quantumness and Certifiable Randomness from a Single Quantum Device</em></a>. Test-of-quantumness and randomness-generation protocols; noisy trapdoor claw-free functions; LWE construction</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/trapdoor-claw-proof-quantumness\">Interactive proofs of quantumness</a>. Uses trapdoor claw-free functions and complementary measurements.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "computational_bell_test": {
    "title": "Computational Bell-test protocol",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Computational Bell-test protocol</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-022-01643-7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Classically verifiable quantum advantage from a computational Bell test</em></a>. Interactive protocol; Theorems 1 and 2; Table 1; modular-squaring circuits</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/trapdoor-claw-proof-quantumness\">Interactive proofs of quantumness</a>. Uses a claw-state commitment and a CHSH-type challenge.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "mahadev_verification": {
    "title": "Mahadev verification protocol",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Mahadev verification protocol</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.01082\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Classical Verification of Quantum Computations</em></a>. Main verification theorem; measurement protocol; construction from noisy trapdoor claw-free functions</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/classical-verification-bqp\">Classical verification of quantum computation</a>. Certifies delegated computation using a classical cryptographic measurement protocol.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "bcmvv_randomness": {
    "title": "BCMVV certified-randomness protocol",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>BCMVV certified-randomness protocol</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.00640\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A Cryptographic Test of Quantumness and Certifiable Randomness from a Single Quantum Device</em></a>. Test-of-quantumness and randomness-generation protocols; noisy trapdoor claw-free functions; LWE construction</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/certified-randomness\">Certified randomness from an untrusted quantum device</a>. Turns unpredictable measurements into certified entropy.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "rcs_certified_randomness": {
    "title": "Random-circuit certified-randomness protocol",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Random-circuit certified-randomness protocol</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.20498\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Certified randomness using a trapped-ion quantum processor</em></a>. Protocol, restricted-adversary security model, entropy certification, and 56-qubit experiment</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/certified-randomness\">Certified randomness from an untrusted quantum device</a>. Uses challenged samples and classical verification to certify entropy.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "random_circuit_execution": {
    "title": "Random-circuit execution",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Random-circuit execution</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.04402\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>On the complexity and verification of quantum random circuit sampling</em></a>. RCS definition; average-case hardness theorem; verification discussion</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-019-1666-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum supremacy using a programmable superconducting processor</em></a>. 53-qubit experiment; supplementary simulation methods</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/random-circuit-sampling\">Random circuit sampling</a>. Executes the circuit and measures the output.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "linear_optical_boson_sampling": {
    "title": "Linear-optical boson sampling",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Linear-optical boson sampling</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.3245\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The Computational Complexity of Linear Optics</em></a>. BosonSampling definition; exact- and approximate-sampling hardness</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/fock-boson-sampling\">Fock-state boson sampling</a>. Interferes single photons and measures occupations.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "gaussian_boson_sampling": {
    "title": "Gaussian boson sampling",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Gaussian boson sampling</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.01199\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Gaussian Boson Sampling</em></a>. Photon-count probabilities and GBS protocol</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/gaussian-boson-sampling\">Gaussian boson sampling</a>. Prepares a Gaussian optical state and measures discrete occupations.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "iqp_circuit_sampling": {
    "title": "IQP circuit sampling",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>IQP circuit sampling</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.07999\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Average-case complexity versus approximate simulation of commuting quantum computations</em></a>. Theorem 1; Conjectures 2 and 3; IQP definition</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.1407\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Classical simulation of commuting quantum computations implies collapse of the polynomial hierarchy</em></a>. Postselection argument and weak-simulation hardness</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/iqp-sampling\">Sampling commuting quantum circuits</a>. Executes commuting phase gates between Hadamard layers.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "knill_laflamme_trace": {
    "title": "Knill–Laflamme normalized-trace algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Knill–Laflamme normalized-trace algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802037\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>On the Power of One Bit of Quantum Information</em></a>. One-clean-qubit model and normalized-trace circuit</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/dqc1-trace-estimation\">Normalized trace estimation for a quantum circuit</a>. Reads the normalized trace from controlled-unitary interference.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "bravyi_gosset_koenig": {
    "title": "Bravyi–Gosset–König shallow-circuit algorithm",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Bravyi–Gosset–König shallow-circuit algorithm</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.00690\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum advantage with shallow circuits</em></a>. Section 3, Eq. (14), 2D Hidden Linear Function definition; main depth-separation theorem</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/two-dimensional-hidden-linear-function\">Two-dimensional hidden linear function</a>. Samples a solution of the hidden linear relation using a Clifford circuit.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum_fingerprinting": {
    "title": "Quantum fingerprinting",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Quantum fingerprinting</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0102001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum fingerprinting</em></a>. Fingerprint states, SWAP test, and simultaneous-message equality bounds</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/equality-quantum-fingerprinting\">Equality in simultaneous-message communication</a>. Encodes strings in logarithmic-size quantum messages and compares their fingerprints.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "hidden_matching_protocol": {
    "title": "Hidden Matching quantum communication protocol",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Hidden Matching quantum communication protocol</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://webee.technion.ac.il/people/zivby/papers/qcc/qcc.journal.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Exponential Separation of Quantum and Classical One-Way Communication Complexity</em></a>. Hidden Matching relation; quantum protocol; randomized classical lower bound</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/hidden-matching-communication\">Hidden matching in one-way communication</a>. Phase-encodes Alice&#x27;s bits and measures an edge parity in Bob&#x27;s basis.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "aaronson_ambainis_disjointness": {
    "title": "Aaronson–Ambainis disjointness protocol",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Aaronson–Ambainis disjointness protocol</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://toc.ilab.sztaki.hu/articles/v001a004/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Search of Spatial Regions</em></a>. Section 7: O(sqrt n)-qubit disjointness protocol; spatial-search theorems</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/set-disjointness-communication\">Set disjointness in two-party communication</a>. Uses distributed quantum search to detect an intersection.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "svm_quantum_kernel": {
    "title": "Support-vector classification with quantum kernel estimation",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Support-vector classification with quantum kernel estimation</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.02174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>A rigorous and robust quantum speed-up in supervised machine learning</em></a>. Theorems 1 and 2; Appendix B, Definition 6 and Theorem 8; Appendix C.3</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/discrete-logarithm-concept-learning\">Learning discrete-logarithm interval concepts</a>. Learns through modular-exponentiation feature-state overlaps.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "tulsi_spatial_search": {
    "title": "Tulsi ancilla-controlled spatial search",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Tulsi ancilla-controlled spatial search</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0801.0497\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Faster quantum walk algorithm for the two dimensional spatial search</em></a>. Ancilla-controlled walk and O(sqrt(N log N)) theorem</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/spatial-lattice-search\">Spatial search on a lattice</a>. Uses a controlled walk to find a marked grid vertex.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "aaronson_ambainis_spatial_search": {
    "title": "Aaronson–Ambainis spatial search",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Aaronson–Ambainis spatial search</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://toc.ilab.sztaki.hu/articles/v001a004/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Quantum Search of Spatial Regions</em></a>. Section 7: O(sqrt n)-qubit disjointness protocol; spatial-search theorems</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/spatial-lattice-search\">Spatial search on a lattice</a>. Provides local search on higher-dimensional grids.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "ettinger_hoyer_knill": {
    "title": "Ettinger–Høyer–Knill subgroup identification",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Ettinger–Høyer–Knill subgroup identification</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0401083\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>The quantum query complexity of the hidden subgroup problem is polynomial</em></a>. Theorems 1 and 2; Sections 2.1 and 2.2</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/general-hsp-query-identification\">Hidden subgroup identification in a general finite group</a>. Collects coset states and jointly tests candidate subgroups.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "quantum_anonymous_leader_election": {
    "title": "Kobayashi–Matsumoto–Tani leader election",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Kobayashi–Matsumoto–Tani leader election</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.5307\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Computing on Anonymous Quantum Network</em></a>. Symmetric-function reduction and O(n)-round leader-election corollary</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/anonymous-leader-election\">Exact leader election in anonymous networks</a>. Uses coherent distributed symmetric-function evaluation to select one leader.</li>\n</ul>"
  },
  "magic_square_strategy": {
    "title": "Mermin–Peres magic-square strategy",
    "html": "<div class=\"document-heading\">\n<h1>Mermin–Peres magic-square strategy</h1></div>\n<ul class=\"algorithm-index\">\n<li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.12042\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Experimental Demonstration of Quantum Pseudotelepathy</em></a>. Magic-square game, quantum strategy, and experiment</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41534-024-00874-1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><em>Beating one bit of communication with and without quantum pseudo-telepathy</em></a>. Magic-square classical and quantum values</li>\n</ul><ul class=\"algorithm-applications\">\n<li><a href=\"#entry/magic-square-game\">Mermin–Peres magic-square game</a>. Uses entangled measurements to satisfy row, column, and intersection constraints.</li>\n</ul>"
  }
};
